KANT_TR['refl/guyer'] = {"refl:1619":["The common understanding is that which in all its thoughts and judgments at the same time takes regard of the universal judgment of others, in order through agreement with the same to become intelligible and convincing to itself. That which pays no heed to this at all falls into enthusiasm. The universal |\nexternal judgment is a [mark] touchstone of the illusion that befalls us when we judge merely from the private situation in which we are, and in the former we always have a guiding thread for testing our own judgment: I hold not the result of my judgments, but rather my reflections, up to the procedure of the common understanding, whether I am intelligible to everyone.",[]],"refl:1620":["L Ia:\nYou have until now thought much, but presumably not thought about your thinking.\nJust so you have spoken for many years, but not thought about language.* (g Speaking. Thinking aloud.)\n*(s One learns many things, e.g., walking, without having been taught them.\nThinking one cannot learn from others, any more than walking, but one can indeed: bring one's faculty of thinking under rules.)\nNevertheless in learning a dead language you have found [have] that it is bound (g to) certain constant rules, without which it could not be language, i.e., communication of one's thoughts. (g Thus you have followed the rules of a language without being able to name these rules themselves. (s Molière — bourgeois gentleman — speaking prose — thus speaking grammatically.))\nLanguage, however, is communication of thoughts. Thus thinking too will be bound to certain rules in all human beings. (g Which he also cannot name, although he has long followed them.)\n[Some of these rules of thinking are universal without distinction of the objects, i.e., without which one cannot think at all, and these rules are necessary.]\n(g It is, however, needful to know these rules if one wants to progress to artificial thinking in [de] sciences; — in common life one needs only practice. Walking, dancing, speaking, talking.)\nA general doctrine of thought [makes] is [it thus also] thus possible*, and from it there also follows a general doctrine of language. *Grammatica universalis* (universal grammar). Thus one teaches, according to the chief arrangement of Latin grammar, the French, German language as well.\n*(s Whether needful: is another question. For the common use of the understanding presumably not, any more than learning to walk, for which practice is enough.)\nThis general doctrine of thinking is logic. All thinking happens through the understanding; thus logic is general doctrine of the understanding. (s Science of the rules of thinking in general.)\nIf you thus want to think about your thinking, you must make the beginning with a logic [as a]. (s Natural logic is that which is acquired through use of the understanding. Logic as science.)\n*The understanding is the faculty of rules. A general doctrine of the understanding thus presents only the necessary rules of thinking without distinction of the objects, i.e., of the matter**, about which there is thinking, thus only the form of thinking in general and the rules without which there can be no thinking at all.\n*(s [The necessary rules of the understanding are universal rules, and the rules of the understanding universal in every consideration are merely [rules] formal rules, i.e., go to no determinate object.] The universal rules |\nare necessary: either only with a particular aim toward the cognition of certain objects, or with every aim, so that without them thinking in general is impossible.)\n**(g Not that according to which we cognize objects, but think in general, which is only a part of cognizing.)\nTo choose oneself objects of thinking is contingent; but the rules of thinking in general are necessary.\nThere are thus rules of thinking that are 1. necessary only with a certain aim (g in relation to a certain object), or those that 2. are necessary with every aim (g form of thinking — rules that consider in it not the content of cognition but the mere form of thinking (not of intuiting)). (Only the last belong to logic.) without which no understanding at all can be used.\nNecessary rules must be able to be had insight into independently of all experience, i.e., *a priori*. although experience can first give us examples of the use of these rules. We have the form of the understanding beforehand (s *a priori*), the objects with which it occupies itself, i.e., the matter of thinking, experience gives us.\n(s Logic abstracts from all content of cognition.)\n(g Rules) Must be able to be demonstrated. Not from empirical psychology.*\n*(g Thereby the boundaries of logic are determined (*1mo* no doctrine of idealism, *2do* not of the origin of ideas, *3tio* not of prejudices). Philosophy must in this follow behind almost all other sciences (natural right, religion, as consisting of theology and morals, each of which has its boundaries).)\nLogic. Science of the universal rules of the use of the understanding in general; thus not of the pure understanding, also not of the use in particular sciences, |\nL II: |\n[also] not of the learned, nor of the common use, but of all use. Yet |\nthe examples are taken from the common use of the understanding, which yet is afterwards itself corrected through these rules.",[]],"refl:1621":["Philosophy is an absolute unity. One cannot say that someone understands one part of it well and is weak in the rest. The spirit of philosophy must show itself in every part.",[]],"refl:1622":["The grammarians were the first logicians.",[]],"refl:1623":["Logic is the general *ars cogitandi* (art of thinking).",[]],"refl:1624":["Logic, defined as general doctrine of the understanding, could yet still seem also to contain the (g pure) concepts of the understanding of objects (categories); consequently it would not abstract from all content of thinking. Thus the definition [as] of it as a science which contains merely the formal rules of thinking is better.",[]],"refl:1625":["We begin a science which promises that it will teach thinking. You have, however, until now (g on your own about) thought much (g things), [without] only presumably not thought about your thinking.",[]],"refl:1626":["What I am to have insight into I must cognize as necessary, consequently *a priori*. If I am to comprehend it, then I must also breaks off.",[]],"refl:1627":["General logic goes merely to the form of thinking, not the matter (the object). It abstracts from all content of cognition. — Precisely for that reason it is no *organon*, but yet demonstrated doctrine, thus canon. Not: according to which rules we think, — but rather ought to think. [We ca] Not psychology. But we yet take these rules from the form of thinking.",[]],"refl:1628":["I have said: everything that happens in the world happens according to rules; the understanding thus also carries out its business according to rules.\n(s Following of rules without knowing them *in abstracto*. In walking. *Logica naturalis* (natural logic).)\nThinking is the business of the understanding.\nBut according as the objects are different, there must also be different rules of thinking, e.g., other rules in the object of experience than in the object of mere reason (virtue), other rules of the understanding for outer experience than for inner. Each science has its particular rules.\nBut there must yet also be one that precedes all sciences and contains the rules of thinking in general. Here there must be abstraction from all distinction of the objects.\nFrom all matter of cognition.\nThus such a science will (g merely) bring the form of thinking under rules. E.g., What is a concept? what is a distinct concept? what is a judgment, a determination? These rules are necessary (s without which there can be no thinking at all, thus abstract from the distinction of the objects) and essential ones of thinking in general. Logic. It is the propaedeutic of all sciences and critique of the healthy understanding. It is, however, not grounded on empirical (s psychological) principles; otherwise it could not contain necessary rules for every understanding. It needs no investigation of the origin of concepts; it speaks of concepts in general, not whereby they are generated in us, but what they are. Empirical psychology does not lie at its ground.\nIts use is critique* of the understanding in general (s *ars cogitandi* (art of thinking)) (s but not merely critique), but distinguishes itself from the critique of taste in that it [has a canon lying at its ground] is at the same time doctrine, i.e., contains principles *a priori* which can be demonstrated because they are necessary (g taste not). For the understanding proceeds according to rules; but it also itself determines in general what belongs thereto in order that something be a rule.\n*(s Since the (s deed) estimation precedes the rules. Doctrine: Since the rules must precede the (s deed) estimation.)\n(s Critique (g art of estimation according to subjective principles or objective) or doctrine, the latter is canon or organon.)\n(s Critique is opposed to doctrine. It is either *ars* or [*canon*] *doctrina diiudicationis* (art or doctrine of judging) (g the former according to norm, the latter according to canon). The understanding needs principles, taste models, of which the archetype is in us.\nCritical art or science; the latter is doctrine, not art.)\n(s *Ars critica*, not *cogitandi*. Doctrine permits *scientiam* (science).)\n(s Canon of doctrine is opposed to the *exemplar* (Polyclitus's rule in art).)\n*It is no organon. Through it we are not in a position to invent cognitions as to content, because there is abstraction from all matter, i.e., from the objects. Through mere grammar one cannot learn a language.\n*(g Canon is a demonstrated [principle of critique] doctrine. (s which serves for critique.))\nIt is only a canon of estimation [of a criteri], not an instrument of invention. It teaches not to make cognition agree with the object, but with the universal laws of thinking in general. Only that the understanding in thinking agree with itself and with its universal rules.\nIt investigates not how the understanding thinks and what happens, but (g teaches) what ought to happen, i.e., how it ought to think. The former psychology or anthropology does, and its remarks are contingent. The latter is necessary and *a priori*.\n(s The former, e.g., doctrine of prejudices, errors, must come into the scholia.)\nLogic is the science of the universal rules of the use of the understanding.",[]],"refl:1629":["denomination. *Logica*. Science of reason. *Scientia rationalis* (rational science) not (s merely) as to form, but as to object: reason itself. (g Of empirical rules there is no science.) As to content:\n1. Logic of universal (g human) reason: *logica* [*sensus communis*] *universalis*.\n2. Logic of the sciences. General logic goes to both.\nAs to method:\n1. Scholastic. This goes first.\n2. popular (s — The former first).\n(s not psychology: how one thinks, but: ought to think.)\n(s As to origin [and the]: *logica naturalis* as natural predisposition or *artificialis* as science. The former is falsely called logic; for it is no knowledge, but merely routine and practice.)\n[*Logica*. 1. *pura*. 2. *applicata*. The former] General logic is merely canon and to that extent (s doctrine) analytic. When it is used as organon, it is called dialectic (s art of presenting something in conformity with the form of truth). Logic of truth and of illusion. Analytic as general logic abstracts from every object and considers only the form of thinking. [Truth, however, consists only] The agreement with the laws of the understanding is the formal element of truth.\nBut this canon used as organon and the formal principles without the material (of the object) [used] used for the estimation of objects, is a mere illusion. For the material element of truth consists in agreement with the object, just as the formal in agreement of the understanding with itself.\n(s a part of philosophy)\nLogic of the (s common) healthy Logic of the speculative\n}\nunderstanding.\n(s Faculty of rules *in abstracto*.)\n(s To orient oneself when one has become too abstract; otherwise the concept is empty.)\n*Logica naturalis*, *Logica artificialis*.\n(s Doctrine of elements)\n*theoretica*\n*vel*\n*practica*: (g general) doctrine of method.\n*diiudication*\n*exsecution*.\n(g art of combining the manifold in the system.)\n[*Logica sceptica*] *Historia Logices* (history of logic). Aristotle: merely objective laws, form of reason. Epicurus: logic of natural investigation. Locke: origin of concepts. Wolff and Leibniz: demonstrative and distinct. Moderns: not pure, but mixed logic with psychology. Crusius: fanatical logic. Lambert: an organon of mathematics and physics.\n(s dialecticians — *Sceptici*. Verulam. Organon. Descartes: *de Methodo*. Malebranche.)\nUtility of logic. Not as art of invention. Not as preparation for sciences as to content, but for critique according to principles with regard to form.\nIf one has already abstracted certain rules from the exercise, one can easily express them universally; but without acquaintance [of the] *in concreto* they are unintelligible and [not to be proved] unusable.\nUniversal heuristic is impossible. The organon of the sciences behind them.\nUtility of logic. One philosophized before logic, and through logic it has not become much better. Mathematics.\n(g It is a doctrine and not merely critique of cognition or cognitive faculty. But yet not organon of the sciences, but that of the critique of cognition.)\n(s It is doctrine, not critique, but serves more for critique than doctrine in sciences that have their object; for thus it would be organon.)\n(s *Logica sensus communis* — *Logica scientifica*.)\n(s Mysticism, inner magic, theurgy, theosophy. Plato: the father of all mystics. Aristotle: the scholastic. Sceptics. Neoplatonists. Carneades and mysticism. *Eclectici*. Alchemy.)\n(s Pure logic is entirely analytic, i.e., logic of truth. For it contains merely the rules *a priori* of the form of thinking, and these can be cognized only by our actions of the understanding being analyzed and one leaving out that which concerns the object. The agreement with these rules is the merely logical criterion of truth.)\n(s To represent *in abstracto* the rules that the understanding cognizes without consciousness (g faculty of rules) *in concreto*. Metaphysics is in the highest degree abstract and speculative.)\n(s A cognition, insofar as its acquisition is empirical, is subjectively historical; objectively historical, if its acquisition can only be empirical.)\n(s Logic is propaedeutic of all sciences. But part of philosophy.)\n(s Artist of reason (*physicus logicus*) or philosopher.)\n(s The final end of human reason yet also presupposes the means thereto.\nThese consist also in the theoretical use.\nPhilosophy, represented according to the end, not merely a system of rational cognition from concepts, is the science of the final end of reason.)\n(s Philosophical cognition. — 1) rational, not historical. 2) philosophical from concepts, not through construction.)\n(s Philosophy as doctrine of wisdom cannot be learned: for who is wise.\nLearning philosophy gives historical cognition, philosophizing: rational, subjectively.\nNot philosophy, but learning to philosophize.)\n(s Doctrine of wisdom — teacher of wisdom or wise man.\nPhilosophy. 1. As doctrine of art; 2. doctrine of wisdom. (g The ancients wanted that one must not merely speak prudently, but also act so.)\nPhilosopher is the one versed in the laws of reason: either as art or wisdom.)\n(s *Pharus intellectus*. *Medicina mentis et corporis*.)\n(s General logic has no practical part; that would be dialectic.)\n(s Division. Doctrine of elements and doctrine of method. The latter is the doctrine of the form of a science in general. Shows not where it is to be used.)\n(s *Bivium* and *quadrivium*. *septem artes*. Rabanus Maurus.\nJodocus. *octo partes*. *Nomen, Pronomen, Verbum, Participium,* [*Praepos.*] *adverbium, Praepositio, Conjunctio, Interjectio*.)\n(s Strange: that logic can give no universally sufficient criterion of truth.)",[]],"refl:1630":["/\n*in abstracto* from concepts.",[]],"refl:1631":["/\nMathematical ones can to some extent be accepted on belief.",[]],"refl:1632":["§. 5. This definition posits the specific mark of philosophy in this, that it cognizes its truths without belief; we must, however, cognize through belief much historical cognition that lies at the ground in philosophy. e.g., we must believe that it is true, what Roemer and after him other philosophers say of the *phaenomena* of Jupiter's satellites, in order to conclude from this something about the nature of light. One philosopher makes *observationes* and experiments, the other believes them and concludes many sorts of consequences from them.\nWe will thus rather retain the definition in which it is said: Philosophy is the science that is occupied with the grounds of things. One must, however, distinguish philosophical cognition from acquaintance with philosophy. The former is a *habitus rationes rerum cognoscendi* (habit of cognizing the grounds of things) |\nand is acquired *meditando*, *examinando* and *perscrutando* (by meditating, examining and searching through); it consists more in the method of using one's reason than in the filling of the memory with many already worked-out propositions. The *cognitio Philosophiae* (cognition of philosophy), however, is the acquaintance with that which has been presented in the writings of philosophers about the grounds of things. This kind of philosophy has its seat in memory. One reads definitions, learns to understand them through the appended examples and scholia, impresses them on memory, likewise makes oneself acquainted with the interconnection of the propositions in the demonstration of a theorem, in order faithfully to give it out again. When such people have forgotten the words of the definition, they also do not have the capacity to make the same anew. They are faithful parrots of great men and say: we brood, investigate etc etc etc.",[]],"refl:1633":["*Denominatio*. Various definition. The author has without need departed from the customary definition. Belief is not opposed to philosophy. It supports itself just as much as this on incontrovertible grounds, namely on an incontrovertible historical cognition and inner experience.\nDivision of philosophy see p. Ms.: 1.",[]],"refl:1634":["The first chief division of philosophy is 1. that in which the grounds are cognized through a consideration abstracted from the senses and from the distinct images of the |\nimagination, thus through the *intellectum purum* (pure understanding); 2. that which takes the grounds immediately by means of the comparison of sensible representations. The first is philosophy in the proper sense, the second mathematics. These definitions determine the specific mark of both sciences We can, however, compare sensible representations with one another with various aims. But with the aim of grounds we can institute no other comparison that [should lead thereto] should lead immediately through the senses to the cognition of the ground, than insofar as it concerns magnitude. Consequently mathematics is the only science where a distinct insight into the grounds depends immediately on the senses or on their deputy, the imagination. [e.g. When I feel that the fire burns.] For the cognition of magnitude is nothing other than breaks off.\n(g Mathematics is not treated as a particular science for the reason that it is too large.)\nOne could perhaps say: philosophy is not sufficiently distinguished from mathematics by its definition, because the latter also considers the grounds of [grounds] things, namely insofar as one considers their magnitude. Should one thus not say: the *mathematicus* has a philosophical cognition of the relations of magnitudes. He proves, after all? It is to be considered here that in mathematics the ground and [its] the dependence of the relations on this ground is derived and cognized through a series of sensible comparisons or of such representations as can all be made sensible. e.g., What relation all three angles in a triangle have to [those angles] two right ones that can be comprehended in a semicircle. This together with the ground one can have insight into by drawing on the board all concepts leading thereto and making them sensible. But philosophy requires such an insight into the grounds whose combination with what is derived from them is not comprehended through an immediate sensible series of representations, consequently *per intellectum purum*. If I ask, e.g.: whence [according to the] the moon is eclipsed at certain times, I can have insight into this no otherwise than if I have presupposed: a body that does not have its own light but is ex|posed\nto another light can be darkened no otherwise than if something either covers it or another thing prevents the light from falling on it. etc etc. These ideas cannot be made entirely sensible. But if I ask about magnitude: How long the eclipse lasts, I can make everything presentable on the board from the given relations of magnitude, which I express through numbers. Hence mathematical cognition is to be distinguished from philosophical. A philosophical cognition of magnitudes and their relations is entirely different. [e.g. if I ask: whence whence the one figure could not have [more] (g fewer) than 3 sides] I know, e.g., certainly, that the circle is a figure that with its circumference encloses the greatest space that it can enclose with this circumference. (g The proof geometry gives.) But if I ask: whence must then that figure which encloses the greatest space etc etc. be so constituted that it is similar to itself through and through. This would be a philosophical question; this same with perpendicular lines. A philosophical cognition of the geometrical and arithmetical problems would be excellent. it would pave the way to the art of invention. but it is very difficult. e.g., That, if from a point I draw another onto a line such that it makes nothing but equal angles, this is the shortest among all possible ones, geometry proves; but whence must one make precisely this determination in order to draw the shortest? That only a sublime philosophy can show.\nFrom this it is also to be seen why mathematical cognition is more certain than philosophical.\nThe *mathesis pura* needs only the principle of contradiction, the *applicata* sometimes that of sufficient ground.\nDivision of philosophy into theoretical and practical.",[]],"refl:1635":["3. /\nL 3':\nHistory of philosophy in general.\n(s Science begins in the civilized state either from legislation or the priestly estate.)\n(s Beginning from the common understanding; difficult boundary-division between it and science.)\n(s Need gives the occasion for the beginning of [sci] investigation. In the raw state the universal need of the spirit with regard to the higher powers.)\n( Old division into logic, physics and ethics.)\nWe have no remnants of the philosophy of the ancient [Greeks] peoples up to the Greeks. Of the Jews it is believed that they began to philosophize after the Babylonian captivity, although one has no remnants of them, except Maimonides, who learned among the Saracens *seculo XIII* (in the 13th century). Their secret philosophy was the Kabbalah.\nEgyptians: their philosophy is unknown.\nChaldeans. Zoroaster. *incertus*. *inde* to the\nPersians. Zerdust. *Oromazes et Arimanius*. *Magi*.\n(s Astrology brought forth astronomy, demonology theology (g theogony). superstition. Priests.)\nIndians. Brachmans, (g Gymnosophists, *hodie*: Brahmins,) Calanus. *Metempsychosis*. Stoic *principia*.\nChinese. Confucius. 550 years before Christ.\nGreeks*. The time before the sects. The seven wise men. The most eminent: Solon, the most learned:\nThales: author of the Ionic sect.**\nPythagoras: Italic sect. *Acusmatici*. *Acroamatici*. *Metempsychosis*.\nDemocritus. *Atomi*.\nEpicurus: That pleasure which has no annexed discomfort is to be embraced.\nPyrrho: attacked the sophists.\n[Thales] Socrates***:\nPlato: Academic sect.\nAristotle, Peripatetic. Best books: logic. rhetoric. natural history.\nAntisthenes: Cynic.\nZeno: Stoic.\nRomans.\nLucretius.\nCicero. Pliny the Younger. Epictetus. Plotinus. Boethius.\nArabs: Avicenna and Averroes.\nScholasticism, Reformation.\n*Reformatores*: Bacon. Descartes. Leibniz. The present ones one must divide into classes.\nHistory of logic.\nAristotle (s dialectic, canonic).\n*Academici*, the new sect: Arcesilaus, Xenocrates doubted, Sextus Empiricus. Huet. Vayer. Bayle.\n(s *Censores*. *Sceptici*. Misology. Pyrrho.)\n(s *philodoxi vel misologi*.)\n*Scholastici*. Ramus his 2 chapters. *Inventio*. *Judicium*: *secunda Petri*. Malebranche. Locke. Tschirnhaus. [Logic is either critical or technical or heuristic]\n(s The edifice of the common use of reason: real grammar.)\n(s the technical, the dialectical (g *eristica*), the heuristic (*canonica*).)\nL 3:\n*(s Distinction of the common use of reason and the speculative, i.e., of science.\nWhere does rational cognition begin to become philosophy. The universal through concepts *in abstracto*. Mathematics was older among the Greeks.)\nL 3':\n**(s As to content: *Physici* and *theologi*.\n1)\nIonic school. Thales and Anaxagoras. Pythagoras.\nAnaximander. Anaximenes. Pherecydes.\n2)\n*Eleatici*. Xenophanes. Parmenides. Zeno the Eleatic.\nHeraclitus. Empedocles, Archytas.\nFirst in prose; Orpheus in poems.\n*Mathematici*: all Italic ones.\n*Physici*: Democritus.\n*Theologi*: Anaxagoras.\n*Ethici*: Socrates.\n*Politicus*: Pythagoras.)\n***(s Anaxagoras. Socrates.\n*Academia*. Plato.\n*Peripathetici*. *Lyceum*. Aristotle.\n(s *Stoa Poecile*.) *Porticus*. *Stoici* (s *Stoa*.)\n(s *Cynici*. *Cynosarge*.) *Horti*. Epicurus. (g Leucippus. Democritus.) (s Alexandrian academy. *Museum*.)\nAs to form: *Dogmatici*, *Sceptici*: (g Sextus Empiricus,) *Academia secunda*.\n1.\n*Academici*:\nSpeusippus,\nArcesilaus,\nCarneades.\n2.\n*Aristotelici*: Theophrastus,\nDemetrius Phalereus.\n3.\n*Stoici*: Zeno,\nCleanthes,\nChrysippus.)",[]],"refl:1636":["3. /\nL 3':\nDivision of the [merely practical] philosophical schools. (s Crusius, Wolff: dogmatic.) (s Critical: Locke.)\n1.\nmerely practical: Cynics, Socrates, Stoics.\n2.\ntheoretical:\na. dogmatic (s either they treated the matter or dialectical form),\nb. skeptical.\nThe *dogmatici*:\nα. from sensitive principles: Epicurus;\nβ. from rational principles either\n( pure or\nmixed )\nof singular origin or\nfrom a common one with sensitive empirical principles.\n(s Aristotle posited intellectual principles that are discursive, not intuitive.)\n(s One would think: Aristotle, who appeals to the senses, grounds himself on experiments.)\nFrom purely rational principles after the Ionic school under Anaxagoras, the Eleatic under Melissus and Parmenides, the Italic under Pythagoras. Great defender: Plato. The intelligible opposed to the sensible. The former: number, idea, form. The former: the best principle of all things, and the intuition of it: wisdom.\nHe who derived intellectual things together with sensitive ones from a common principle: Aristotle.\n*Sceptici*. *Zetetici*. *Academici*.\nRomans. *Scholastici* nothing but *dogmatici*.\nL 3:\nLack of universities among the ancients. (g A single teacher held a school.) The religion of the ancients gave occasion to fine arts (g to right and eloquence), because it was sensible. The religion of the |\nmoderns gave occasion to subtle, abstract speculations, because it was spiritual, and to erudition in history and languages.\nAt universities alone the theologians and subtleties ruled. *Scholastici*. Aristotle. (g End of all taste and healthy reason. literature.) The philosophers who philosophized free from the yoke of the schools brought up the eclectic way. Until, when finally outside of schools and learned societies the public gives the tone, a universal shallowness gains the upper hand.",[]],"refl:1637":["Philosophy is only in the civilized state [sprung from need] among those who have leisure, among priests. was theology, pneumatology and morals. cosmogony, magic.\nAmong the Egyptians. Among the Jews it was not needful. Among Greeks and Chinese separated; among the latter it was a service of the government, thus morals.",[]],"refl:1638":["Sophists. dialecticians.\n*Sceptici*.\n*Dogmatici*.\n*Practici*. Socrates (g and the three sects of the moralists: Cynics. Epicureans. and Stoics.)\n*Theoretici*: Epicurus, Plato, Aristotle.",[]],"refl:1639":["/\nThe ancient Greeks (g small states) (g Chinese) philosophized for legislation and the state. likewise eloquence, arts, and for the court of law in lawsuits. Morals. Mathematics. The Jews, Egyptians and Persians for religion, either according to their holy books or traditions. Mythology (theogony). Rich in images. figurative.\nGreeks no high schools. The Greeks presented philosophy not as a school instruction but as a mere culture of reason; hence, since they did not have high schools, their philosophy lacked disciplinary exactness, method. For the advantage of advocates and orators dialectic. No observations.\n(g No carefulness of the ancients in observing.)",[]],"refl:1640":["Lack of observations of outer and inner sense; all philosophized from [hypot] ideas of reason or hypotheses of the same, in that they laid things of nature at the ground.",[]],"refl:1641":["Modern physics: right of nature, experiments.\nWolff: order in thinking. Distinctness. definitions.\nLocke: origin of human concepts.\nCrusius: the law of human reason immediately, instinct.\nIn logic, metaphysics, morals not very much further than the ancients. Physics.",[]],"refl:1642":["/\nThe ancients' best philosophy was right.\nMathematical\nempirical.*\nMystical. (g Scholastic.)\nWolff\nLocke\nCrusius\n*(g Critical. analytic.)",[]],"refl:1643":["Epicurus asserted the essential distinction of the sensitive and intellectual, but taught that the sensitive alone can contain truth, distinctness and become a science.\nPlato asserted the essential distinction of the sensitive and intellectual, but taught that only the intellectual gives science.\nAristotle asserted there is no essential distinction between the two, both arose from the senses, and reason was only a particular use of the first, whereby the former could become science.\n*Sceptici*. Romans.\nBarbarians. new times.",[]],"refl:1644":["All philosophy is either speculative or merely practical; of the latter were Antisthenes and Socrates. The former: of sensible, the latter: of wise simplicity. The former sought the human being of nature, the latter the cultivated human being according to the essential determinations of the same. Diogenes erred in the execution, in that he sought [human beings to] nature in the objects to which it has most essentially bound him, only to permit nothing more than this measure; was thus negative. Rousseau seeks out the natural beneath the artificial and seeks out the greatest |\nperfection in the civilized state without contradicting nature, and the means to attain it, but errs in that he holds this to be possible. The speculative can be sought dogmatically or skeptically. The former sensitive or intellectual.",[]],"refl:1645":["Philodoxy distinguished from philosophy.",[]],"refl:1646":["The Greeks had no universities, i.e., societies of learned men who divided the science among themselves. They had no particular theologians, but priests and philosophers. They had influence on the commonwealth, way of thinking of the people and government. As teachers they at the same time gave the example.",[]],"refl:1647":["*Septem artes* (seven arts): grammar, rhetoric, dialectic (s *Trivium*); arithmetic, geometry, astronomy, music (s *Qvadrivium*). (s Rabanus Maurus. Charlemagne.)",[]],"refl:1648":["Pyrrhonists: who accept all sorts of things because it seems so to them. *Academici* reject with certainty; the withholdings are to Arcesilaus |\ngoods, the assertions evils, the equality of two opposed proofs is the chief argument. The wise man may [the ass] not opine, ataraxia. — Carneades made it into the new academy. The *academici* did not dispute against the reality of our cognition, but its universal validity.",[]],"refl:1649":["Historical cognition is opposed to the rational. The historical is either empirical through experience or indeed objectively rational, but subjectively accepted through imitation.",[]],"refl:1650":["All [cognitions] disciplines belong either to art or to science. The former are [where only from individual actions the rules of the skil] Where the individual actions precede the teaching. The second: where the [universal rules] teaching contains the ground of individual actions [of cognition make up].\nAll sciences are (g as to form) either historical or rational. The latter either (g mathematical or philosophical) of magnitude or quality.\nTo arts and sciences of imitation belongs docility, to philosophy genius.",[]],"refl:1651":["“To learn a philosophy” signifies: subjective philosophy, i.e., what has actually been thought; but then one cannot judge it. |\n“to learn to philosophize” is objective: how one ought to think, i.e., the rules of the correct use of reason; thus the philosophical spirit is distinguished from the spirit of a philosophy and consists in the method of reason. The philosophical spirit is thus original and consequently genius (for original is not: what is merely a single one, but: what is no *ectypon*).\nMathematics can be learned. It invents its cognition from distinct ideas. (g devises data for itself.) Philosophy through dissection of the confused ones. (g Must seek them.)\nThe method of the former is synthetic, of the latter analytic.",[]],"refl:1652":["2'.\nL 2:\nOne could distinguish world-science and world-wisdom; the first is erudition, the second acquaintance with the vocation of the human being* according to understanding and will. Metaphysics and morals. thus Hume is the greatest philosopher. (g In other words. philosophical science (g erudition) and philosophical wisdom.) After wisdom no one asks, because it greatly confines science, which is an instrument of vanity.**\n*(g Wisdom is the relation [of a cognition] to the essential ends of humanity. Hence vocation of the human being according to understanding and will. The natural scientist is no philosopher.)\nL 2'.\n**(s One can learn no philosophy, but indeed learn to philosophize. He is not a philosopher who understands some philosophy or other (the |\nother's attempt at philosophizing). He is always original. Just as little as fine sciences.)\n(s The philosopher is either artist of reason or the one versed in the laws of human reason. There is a presentation of philosophy whose effect is immediately felt in the school; but all influence of the same ceases in the manly use of one's reason. The other has in the school no peculiar effect, but all the more in life. To the former belongs mechanism, to the second culture of genius.)",[]],"refl:1653":["Object of philosophical cognition: quality. Form is rational, not historical. Degree is scientific.\n(s rational form (g not historical) [distinguished from the common understanding]. Matter not.\nmatter: all objects.\nDegree: universal concepts *in abstracto*, not common understanding.)\nPhilosophy is the science of reason of the qualities of things.\n1. Science; 2. qualities; 3. reason.\na) Difference from common cognition, whose rules are confirmed in use through experience and go no further. b) breaks off.",[]],"refl:1654":["By original spirit we understand [not only the capacity] the skill that cannot be acquired through imitation. Not every |\noriginal spirit is characteristic. Spirit belongs to the invention of that where an ideal lies at the ground, also in the case of reason.",[]],"refl:1655":["Philosophical cognition is distinguished 1. from historical as cognition of reason. 2. From common cognition of reason as speculative or from universal concepts and rules. 3. From mathematics: from concepts, not construction. The objects are undetermined in all of them.",[]],"refl:1656":["Philosophy is the *organon* for wisdom. It therefore goes to the quality and the measure or the outer relation of cognition, insofar as it is in accord with our true ends. It determines the use of the understanding *in abstracto* with regard to nature etc:, but therein it seeks only the means to its end. One is not a philosopher because one is an investigator of nature (artist of reason), but because one learns the right use of one's reason with regard to our present and future ends.",[]],"refl:1657":["Philosophical is: a universal cognition from concepts.\nmathematical is: a universal cognition from the construction of concepts.\nPhilosophical cognition is empirical or rational, according as the concepts are abstracted from experience and the use of them is a law of experience or not. The latter furnish not truly universal rules, but rules of induction.",[]],"refl:1658":["/\nPhilosophy that is cognized through belief. Citation. Authority.\nSkill. Philosophical cognition.\nPhilosophy: *quid*. Wit.\nMathematics: *Quoties*. imagination.",[]],"refl:1659":["Parts of philosophy: understanding. Feeling. Desire.",[]],"refl:1660":["2'.\nL 2:\nPhilosophy is the science of the maxims of reason, speculative as well as practical; |\nL 2': |\nit goes beyond everything. Cognitions are philosophical insofar as they contain the means to the maxims of reason.",[]],"refl:1661":["Mathematics is a science of the determination of magnitudes *a priori*; it is rightly called *doctrina* or *mathesis*.",[]],"refl:1662":["Of philosophy one part belongs to erudition, the other to wisdom. To the former physics. A philosophical scholar is not for that reason a philosopher.",[]],"refl:1663":["Philosophy is the legislation of reason; the philosopher often throws away the subtlest speculation. Laws and rules of exercise; genius stands under laws, likewise skill. Skill |\nand wisdom. subjective laws (g maxim is a subjective law), insofar as they agree with the objective ones, are maxims. Laws command categorically, rules hypothetically (under problematic condition).",[]],"refl:1664":["Mathematics is the highest art of reason, philosophy science of reason.",[]],"refl:1665":["One can bring into the nation a spirit or mechanism. The latter in the end makes a barbaric kind of erudition.",[]],"refl:1666":["Cognitions belonging to philosophy are distinguished from those belonging to mathematics. The former are cognitions from concepts, the latter from their arbitrary construction. Or: philosophical are cognitions from concepts *in abstracto*, mathematical from construction *in concreto*.",[]],"refl:1667":["Philosophy the way to wisdom.\nOf the probabilism of pure reason. *e.g.* in beings of an entirely different kind than those of the [W] world of sense.\nExplanation of such a plausibility.\nOne cannot prove synthetically impossibilities of the objects of pure reason, hence posit things *probabiliter*. To limit this false probability through retorsion. As long as there is still thinking in the world, reason will at all times overstep the limitations of experiences with its conjectures, [but philosophy must against it well arm] and there will be no possibility of abolishing this presumption, which lies in nature itself. But against it philosophy must be on its guard, to prevent that it not, to the detriment of healthy reason, confuse the highest aims.\nThe art of reason and the legislation of reason are very different in worth. In the first, mathematics is the foremost; in the second there is much that is philosophical, which belongs to the skill of the understanding.",[]],"refl:1668":["Philosophy is the science of the final end of human reason and herewith of all ends, insofar as they make up a system in our faculty of cognition.",[]],"refl:1669":["/\nNamely how one can arrive at a certain cognition and cognize its marks. [Out of caution in exercise one must always |\nrather presuppose that a proposition is merely probable.] A certain one convinces all human beings. The *Logica probabilium* is a still more necessary part of the practical doctrine of reason. e.g. in the estimation of historical credibility. Meanwhile one makes do by proceeding as if one could cognize the matter certainly, thus through approximation.",[]],"refl:1670":["§. 7. In all sciences the theoretical part must precede the practical. One must know beforehand how one should speak before one speaks; one must learn to think correctly before one should occupy oneself with vain thinking. In mathematics alone can exercising logic go before teaching logic. For there one has a secure guiding thread of reflecting: our senses [can] or their *vicaria*, the *imaginatio*, can easily convict us of the errors of the way of thinking. We can afterwards abstract from this a logic, as Tschirnhaus did. [*verte*]",[]],"refl:1671":["At the end of L §. 7: “*logica* — *docens*”:\ncontemplative.",[]],"refl:1672":["/\n§. 8. One always defines a science in its greatest perfection, because this is the point of view toward which all directions of endeavors should run together like the *radii* in a circle. So it is constituted with logic. But one should almost confess that the 1st praise does not hold good. One must already have thought before one should rightly grasp and use with profit the rules for thinking correctly. One must know right away how to apply them to examples. Therefore it is useless in schools. One must be able to walk proficiently before one learns to dance, and he will learn orthography poorly who has never yet written. Mathematics should therefore fairly make the beginning. One cannot err in it, because one is well led. Thereafter one always has a model for the rules of the correct way of thinking.\n2. *intellectum et rationem*. The upper and the lower faculty of the soul. It teaches to ascend from sensible impressions to universal concepts, to distinguish the distinct representations of reason from the obscure concepts of the *imagination*, not to regard the sensible sensation [of the] (g of individual) things as the sensation of their causes. [Not] These are the improvements that it makes with regard to the boundaries of the lower and upper powers. What it achieves with regard to the upper ones themselves will be seen from the exercise.",[]],"refl:1673":["/\nLogic treats either of a science that can be certain, or that can be probable, or that can cognize problematically.\nThe *logica theoretica* shows the conditions under which a perfect cognition is possible, and *practica*: how these should be attained.",[]],"refl:1674":["The lack of logical rules does not make the common errors, but rather the lack of attention.",[]],"refl:1675":["Analytic (g belongs to doctrine; dialectic to critique). Moments of the understanding and reason (undetermined with regard to the objects).\nAs critique logic serves through formulas, terminologies. Grammar. Analysis. (g theoretical.)\nAs an *organon* it must be attained through the *analysis* of the sciences. (g practical.) These go before logic.\nOne can make a cognition practical, but not teach the *praxis*, because this would again require rules whose application would after all be expected from healthy understanding.",[]],"refl:1676":["*repraesentatio est determinatio mentis* (g *interna*), *quatenus ad res quasdam ab ipsa* (g *nempe repraesentatione*) *diversas refertur*. Is that|\ndetermination of the soul which relates itself to other things. But relating I call it when its constitution is in accord with the constitution of the external things, *sive si rebus externis conformis est*.\nThe author [represents to himself] alleges that the representation which is to be met with in the soul of a thing has just such a similarity with the represented thing as a painting with the depicted object. But I assert that this is false, and prove it thus. When I see a house, then according to this [repr] opinion there is a depiction of the house in my soul, which is similar to the represented house. Now because similar things are distinguished only according to their magnitude, there is depicted in my soul an entirely small little house, which, small as it is, must yet occupy a space. which is impossible. Likewise, when I feel the trembling of the air, the sensation of which I call sound: can I indeed say that in the [substance] interior of my soul such a trembling also goes on, what should tremble therein. We can prove just this through experiences. Whoever tastes something sour: can he indeed say that his representation depicts to him pointed and boat-shaped little salt particles which stimulate his nerves of taste? meanwhile one nevertheless sees through a microscope that they are actually so constituted. etc.\nWhat then is there in the representation that agrees with the represented things? The representation, because it borrows its ground from the represented thing, agrees with it in this, that it is composed of its partial concepts in such a way as the whole represented thing from its parts. as e.g. One can say: [a] the notes of a musical piece are a representation of the harmonic combination of the tones. not as if a note were similar to a tone, but because the notes are in such combination among one another as the tones themselves. But when the soul attends to itself, it does seem to notice that a representation in its interior appears to it just as if it sees a painting with its eyes. This also cannot be otherwise. A painting is made according to the similarity of the *object*; just as the *object* affects us, so the painting also affects us, and the same *idea* or determination of the soul which was effected by the painting we can *reproduce* through the *imagination*; therefore it must [us always] just as [well as there] [relate itself toward the object] breaks off.",[]],"refl:1677":["§. 11.\n*cognitio est complexus repraesentationum clararum*. The notion of *repraesentation* is more universal; for it also comprehends under itself the *obscuras*, of which we do not even know that we have them.",[]],"refl:1678":["§. 12. *Objectum repraesentationis est vel externum vel internum*. *Externum* is: when one represents to oneself something distinct from the soul, [*internum* w] under this even the body belongs; or *internum*: when the soul represents itself. (g We can also represent to ourselves our representation itself.) *Repraesentationes distinguuntur vel materialiter vel formaliter*. *differentia materialis* is [whether they whether s] that which is taken from the difference of the represented *object*, as [*repraes*] the *idea* of a *universalis est idea abstracti* or *notio*, the *idea* of an *entis singularis* is *idea singularis*; *formaliter* they are distinguished into *obscuras et claras*, into *confusas et distinctas*, *completas et incompletas*, *adaequatas et inadaequatas*.\nConsciousness is a [*idea*] (g clear) representation of one's own *idea*. This gives occasion for the thinking power of the soul properly to come into exercise according to its whole magnitude. For because the soul knows what it represents to itself and how it represents it to itself, it can deal with its representations in various ways, compare, separate, combine them, and thereby perform that which one calls re|flecting\n. Which the animals cannot do, because they are not conscious of them.",[]],"refl:1679":["To be conscious of a [thing] representation is: to know that one has this representation; that is: to distinguish this representation from the others.",[]],"refl:1680":["Consciousness is *sensus internus*.*Conscientia logica distinguitur a conscientia morali*. The heathens called this religion.\n*animalia habent sensum externum, non internum*.",[]],"refl:1681":["[The author mixes t] The author treats of cognition; we will treat of the *idea* in general. *divisio idearum ratione formalitatis*.\nAn *obscure idea* is one of which we are not conscious, or which we distinguish neither from ourselves nor from other things. We thus do not even know that we have it. We must infer it. Either a *total idea* is *obscure* or the *partial idea* is *obscure*. The *total idea* in sleep, lack of *attention*, when the *organum sensus* is corrupted, *e.g.* in taste. The *partial idea*. When the total *idea* is clear. I hear a mixed clamor; this is clear, but not the *idea* of a particular voice. I taste a mixture of spice, sugar, vinegar, salt, oil.\n*idea* [*vel clara*] *vel confusa vel distincta*. The former: when I have in the represented *object* no *ideam partialem claram*. *e.g.* the Milky Way. When I name sympathy. *distincta*. I come nearer to a fish market.\n*completa, incompleta, adaequata, inadaequata* come up later.\nI point out and see from afar something obscure.\nThe obscure concept that human beings have of duty and virtue.",[]],"refl:1682":["Representing, cognizing, thinking.",[]],"refl:1683":["(s Sensation) (s *relata ad obiectum*) (s *obiectivum*) (g *intuitus*)\n*repraesentatio, perceptio, cognitio, conceptus*,\n(s to represent something) (s to represent something to oneself) (s *conscius*) (s *cogitatio*,)\n(s *conceptus* (g *non*) *empiricus*)\n*notio*,\n(s *idea**)\n(s Model-idea *dessein*\n*cognitio exemplaris* goes to aim.)\n*(s we have no *idea* of plants and of the whole of creatures.)",[]],"refl:1684":["The single representation that contains the ground for rules with regard to the manifold (friendship).\nThe idea of a book can be good, even if the book is full of errors. Hobbes. To search out the idea. Lost books: when no one finds out the *idea*. It cannot be found by patching together. It is a unity and the manifold its consequences. The |\npieces must have their explanation from the *idea*. Our *conceptus* relate to *ideas*. The images that furnish the original, *e.g.* plan of a fortress, are not properly *ideas*.\nThe objective *idea* is distinguished from the subjective; the former is the archetype in God.",[]],"refl:1685":["At the beginning of L §. 11 “Cognition”:\nthrough which we distinguish what it is.\nis an objective representation; the subjective is sensation. Cognition contains concept, judgment, and inference.",[]],"refl:1686":["(g *cuius conscius sum: Cognitio*.[*repraesentatio* refers to *object*])\n*perceptio* is[still] subjective.",[]],"refl:1687":["We obtain the matter of cognition through the senses, the form of appearance through understanding, the form of the concept through reason.",[]],"refl:1688":["At the end of L §. 13:\nClarity.",[]],"refl:1689":["All clarity is either absolute (inner) or relative (outer) clarity. The former is the consciousness of the representation in itself, the latter the consciousness of its relation (of *identity* and *diversity*) in comparison with others. The first as well as the second clarity becomes distinctness through the consciousness of the marks of the absolute or relative representations.",[]],"refl:1690":["(s For distinctness it is required that the manifold, insofar as it belongs to my whole representation, be clearly cognized.\nConfusion is often the cause of indistinctness, but not this itself.)\nClarity is presupposed in logic.\nFrom it distinctness; is opposed to indistinctness (s not confusion; cause of both:)\nOrder and confusion.\nSensible distinctness in intuition (g distinguishing),\ndistinctness of understanding in concepts (g distinctions).\nIndistinct intuition,\nindistinct concept.\nOrder in space and time (g *perspicuitas*),\norder through placing under concepts.\n(s 1. Subject and predicate; 2. ground and consequence; 3. whole and division. These are determinations of concepts.)",[]],"refl:1691":["Logical and [sensible] aesthetic distinctness.",[]],"refl:1692":["Distinctness is the clarity of the manifold in the representation of a thing, either of that which is actually contained in the representation, or can be contained.\nAnalytic distinctness is *perspicuity*: seeing-through cognition.",[]],"refl:1693":["[In] all cognition has first [a relation] as cognition a relation to the object, 2. to the cognition of the object. In the first case it is representation, in the second [consciousness] the universal condition is consciousness. Representation is intuition or concept; cognition through concepts is thinking.",[]],"refl:1694":["Philosophy, insofar as it considers objects, belongs in the series of all other sciences and is distinguished only according to form: namely its cognition is a cognition of reason from concepts.\nPhilosophy, insofar as it considers the cognition of objects in general, is the highest of all cognition and the legislation of the understanding and of reason. Logic.\nAt the beginning. We begin the analytic of the understanding and dissect its actions. First difference between matter (g *object*) and form (our object is merely the form). Cognition according to matter |\nin general is called representation. The [condition] cognition of form presupposes consciousness. (thus in logic we presuppose consciousness.) According to form (g according) it is either intuition or concept. Cognition through concepts is thinking. Logic weighs thinking. Clarity and obscurity. The former distinctness and indistinctness.\n(s Philosophy [considers has to do with] is distinguished from all other cognitions only according to form.)\n(s *Formaliter cognitio est vel intuitus*.(*sensitivi*.)\n— — — — *conceptus: discursiva (intellectualis)*. *Logica forma*. *Cogitatio*.)",[]],"refl:1695":["Representation is either cognition or sensation. The former intuition or concept.",[]],"refl:1696":["1. Simple cognitions are neither distinct nor confused.\n2. All philosophy goes to form, i.e. to distinctness through *analysis*, especially in the moral. Hence it does not properly teach to cognize something that one did not know, but to cognize better that which one knew. Socrates was the midwife of the thoughts of his listeners.",[]],"refl:1697":["Logic begins with concepts and treats of their use. |\nTheir origin from sensible intuitions or the understanding belongs to psychology and transcendental philosophy.",[]],"refl:1698":["*materia*: objects; *forma*: *modus cognoscendi*. Philosophical and common cognition are distinguished not according to matter, but according to form. From this logic abstracts.",[]],"refl:1699":["Division of cognition: 1. with regard to consciousness;\n2. with regard to the power of cognition in general: aesthetic or logical;\n3. With regard to the combination of them among one another.",[]],"refl:1700":["Since logic is a science of the rules of the understanding, it does not begin with intuition: for that belongs to the senses; but with the concept: for this, as discursive representation, is the first element of thinking.",[]],"refl:1701":["*Conceptus*: representation of an object in general.",[]],"refl:1702":["Cognition through concepts is thinking. Logic treats of thinking.",[]],"refl:1703":["1. Logical doctrine of elements.\n2. Logical doctrine of method, of the rules of a science.",[]],"refl:1704":["All relation of representation is either merely to the subject, i.e. (g to [feeling]) belonging to feeling, or also to the object, belonging to cognition. In the latter case it is intuition or concept, empirical intuition: sensation, or pure intuition.",[]],"refl:1705":["4.\nL 4':\nOld division. *Apprehensio simplex, Iudicium et ratiocinatio*. Representations are distinguished into [sensation intuit] subjective or objective. The objective are intuition or concept [the subjective sensation]; or also thus: all representations are sensation or cognition. The former represents merely an alteration in us, the latter an object. Example from taste and from seeing. Cognition is either intuition or concept. Single or universal. To *apprehend* or to think. [Concept] Both obscure, clear, distinct. Of mere sensations concepts cannot be given to one who has not had them. To the contemplative concept intuition must *correspond*, to the practical, sensation. One must not take sensation for cognition.",[]],"refl:1706":["The science of the perfection of intuitions: aesthetic, of concepts: logic.",[]],"refl:1707":["Propaedeutic concept of logic.\nPropaedeutic concept of aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1708":["Indistinctness from confusion or from the weakness of the partial representations. If order were also brought among the latter, the whole would not on that account be distinct. Metaphysics. Logic speaks only of concepts, not intuitions, hence only of marks.",[]],"refl:1709":["1. Distinctness and indistinctness, insofar as they concern thinking (concept) as well as intuition. Distinguishing of the parts in the whole representation. Distinct intuitions, distinct concept.",[]],"refl:1710":["One cannot confuse the marks that one does not yet have; hence cognitions can be indistinct on account of lacking marks or on account of confusion of the marks. The former become distinct through adding, the latter through dissection.",[]],"refl:1711":["Cognition contains intuition, through which objects are given to us, and thinking. Senses, and the faculty of thinking: the understanding. Concepts, judgments, inferences. Understanding, power of judgment, and reason; everywhere a different way of bringing intuitions under rule.",[]],"refl:1712":["With what should logic begin? with cognitions in general, not with sensations as subjective representations.",[]],"refl:1713":["Logic: merely formal part of philosophy. Logic treats of cognition, but not as intuition, rather of thinking, not according to matter, but according to form. *apprehensio simplex, iudicium* — breaks off?",[]],"refl:1714":["We are indeed conscious of the representation, but not of what is contained in it —.",[]],"refl:1715":["Indistinctness from confusion or from the weakness of consciousness.",[]],"refl:1716":["The ground is either the ground of cognition or the [existential] ground of determination.",[]],"refl:1717":["Nothing is *connex* except that wherein the one is *ratio* and the other *rationatum*. If I can cognize everything in a thing without taking another to help, then they are not *connex* at all, or: insofar as in my cognition nothing is determined through another, it is not *connex* with it.",[]],"refl:1718":["Fortune and misfortune are in natural interconnection with the moral properties.",[]],"refl:1719":["For a theft original sin is not the sufficient ground.\nA good wit is not the sufficient ground of erudition.",[]],"refl:1720":["The ground of possibility: the human imperfection of nature with regard to sin.\nThe ground of truth: since we are actually convinced from the knowledge of duties that the actions of human beings are sin.\nThe ground of existence: Is the corruption of the human being through an inherited evil kind.",[]],"refl:1721":["Does not belong to logic; for this treats not of cognitions according to content (*object*), but merely of the form of the understanding, i.e. of thinking in them.",[]],"refl:1722":["The schools bring into us [the order and] the stock (*matter*). the *academy* through logic the order of cognitions.",[]],"refl:1723":["We must here speak not of the ground of things, but of cognition.",[]],"refl:1724":["The cognition of the interconnection of the *rationatum* with the *ratio* is the foremost. *e.g.* If someone says: sulfur and saltpeter are the ground of thunder, or: that a human being cannot perish when he is fastened to bladders, of this the ground is the air.",[]],"refl:1725":["The human being should live virtuously.",[]],"refl:1726":["A rational cognition is distinguished from the philosophical in this, that the former cannot carry the grounds out to the most universal concepts of cognition.\nReason is that capacity of the understanding through which one is in a position to have distinct insight into the interconnection of things with their grounds.",[]],"refl:1727":["is opposed to learned cognition.",[]],"refl:1728":["One knows the constitution of the whole structure of the world *historice*. *Mathesis experimentalis*.\nThis cognition, even if it is merely historical, is not on that account unreasonable. For unreason is not only a lack of the application of reason, but an [lack] application of the powers against reason. *e.g.* If I learn history merely according to pictures. [A]",[]],"refl:1729":["upper margin:\nIn cognition of the understanding we cognize: what things are, in that from reason: what they must be; our cognitions in the former are descriptions or explanations (understanding), in the other insights. The former arise from the particular, the latter from the universal. In the former breaks off.\n(s The form of reason consists in cognition from grounds, not in the cognition of grounds (g causes). The cognition itself must be *a priori*. Having insight.)",[]],"refl:1730":["Cognition of the understanding is opposed to the sensible. 1. According to origin. 2. According to form: the form of the understanding is discursive, the sensible is intuitive.\n(s The perfection of understanding of cognition; 2. perfection of reason (both logical). Thereupon aesthetic perfection.)",[]],"refl:1731":["Cognition of reason is opposed to the historical: 1. according to |\n[origin, since the former] form, in that the latter [*a posteri*] only cognizes what something is, the former from its grounds; but according to matter opposed to the empirical, since the former is drawn from grounds *a priori*, the latter from experiences.",[]],"refl:1732":["Cognition of reason, which has for its object universal constitution of things.\nopposed to historical cognition.\nRational cognition: whose form is rational, let the object be whichever it will.\nopposed to historical cognition.\nHere only the historical and sensible form is opposed to the rational. [*a poster*: *a priori*] *coordinatio, subordinatio*.",[]],"refl:1733":["One can distinguish cognitions in conformity with reason, rational cognitions, and cognitions of reason.\nCognitions of the understanding judge, rational ones infer.",[]],"refl:1734":["Historical cognitions according to matter [conta] are judgments of the understanding [the object t] breaks off.",[]],"refl:1735":["The cognition of the understanding is opposed to the sensible. The former is universal (g objective), the latter is particular and subjective.\nRational cognition is opposed to the irrational,\ncognition of reason to the [sensible] [empirical] historical; the former cognizes the particular in the universal.",[]],"refl:1736":["Historical cognition is that (g which) is given by an object.\nCognition of reason: insofar as the object is also not given.",[]],"refl:1737":["Historical cognition *obiective*: history and description, *formaliter*: narration, insight.",[]],"refl:1738":["*cognitio formaliter rationalis est, quae depromitur ex methodi rationis regulis generalibus sive consistit in methodis vel habitu rationis*.",[]],"refl:1739":["6'.\nL 6:\nCognition of reason: the cognition of the (g particular from the) universal, [*a priori*] necessary.\nNot insofar as the cognition is given through others or the object: *formaliter*, *materialiter* historical.\nL 6':\nWhoever cognizes a particular philosophy not from the universal of the use of reason cognizes it historically. As it is given. The cognition of the universal is the understanding: (if need be from the particular,) empirical.",[]],"refl:1740":["Faculty to think: understanding.\nto think *a priori*: reason.\n(g Thinking —) *a posteriori* — there is historical cognition.",[]],"refl:1741":["Cognition (g through thinking) according to rules *a priori*: cognition of reason.\nCognition according to *data a posteriori* is historical cognition.",[]],"refl:1742":["The first peculiar *requisite* of cognition of the understanding is universality and of cognition of reason the particular from the universal.",[]],"refl:1743":["*subiective* historical or *obiective*.",[]],"refl:1744":["All cognition is subjectively, as it is actually acquired, either historical or rational; objectively the former can very well be rational.",[]],"refl:1745":["Reason is the faculty of cognizing necessity. Understanding is cognition of the universal, power of judgment of the universal in the particular, reason of the particular from the universal.",[]],"refl:1746":["A universal proposition can be empirical. *e.g.* All human beings are mortal.\nA subsumption also, but an inference always contains necessity.",[]],"refl:1747":["This definition is undetermined; something can be perfect according to magnitude or according to exactness.\nBeautiful is what pleases. but it pleases through the agreement with breaks off.",[]],"refl:1748":["A sensible [representation] estimation of perfection is called taste. A cognition that is cognized as perfect by the sensible power of judgment is called aesthetic. Gothic taste.\n[When so] The agreement of the manifold in a thing to a common aim is called perfection. If everything agrees in accordance with the rules of the lower powers of cognition. then it is aesthetically perfect. That is, when the agreement is cognized merely through the sensible powers. And thus the gratification is aroused from the lower powers. *e.g.* the knowledge of epic poems, of the picturesque in description. If it is discovered through the higher powers, then it also makes a gratification, but a distinct one, which sometimes is not so charming.\nWhat taste is. Doctrine of taste.",[]],"refl:1749":["A historical cognition can be combined with the rational without losing anything of beauty (g and the philosophical with the aesthetic, without of thoroughness). *Historia telluris*.\n*Historica praestruatur Philosophicae*. One must read books, experience. All cognition begins from the senses.",[]],"refl:1750":["The definition is undetermined, see the preceding.\nOne can say that someone has a rational cognition of duties without having a philosophical one.",[]],"refl:1751":["which has been carried out to the first principles.",[]],"refl:1752":["Perfection from gratification, beauty also. but different.",[]],"refl:1753":["In every perfection there is to be met with a rule or aim, secondly an agreement with it. In cognition one has chiefly 2 aims: to instruct oneself, or to gratify, or both together. The first is attained merely through distinct insights\n|\n, the second [through] in two ways: either through the beauty of the *object* or the agreeableness of the presentation. This latter, because it cannot be attained through perfectly distinct representations, is the aesthetic perfection of cognition.\nDistinct cognition, without aesthetic aid, merely through the charm of the *object*, through logical perfection, that is the correctness [of the treatise] and order in which it is considered (g is), has in itself such a source of gratification as surpasses everything aesthetic both in magnitude and in duration. Archimedes' gratification at the bath. Kepler's at the discovery of a proposition.\nAesthetics is only a means to accustom people of all too great delicacy to the strictness of proofs and explanations. Just as one smears the rim of the vessel with honey for children.\nExample of a thorough science that arouses gratification, in the natural history of the earth. Who invented the science of aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1754":["Certainty, [compl] exhaustiveness, correct demonstration.\nSkeleton. Algebra.",[]],"refl:1755":["*perfectio concernit vel objectum cognitionis vel modum cognoscendi*.",[]],"refl:1756":["A cognition that is logically perfect can become ugly if it is clothed or presented in such a way that [obscure] concepts which arouse the [obscure] indistinct representation of a displeasure are mixed in. *e.g.* When the considerations in physics of generation, of the *structure* of the human being and his animal *economy* are described filthily.\nBut because one should never blame anyone for the deviation from a rule which he neither set for himself nor was obliged to set for himself: one cannot on that account ascribe an imperfection to a cognition because it does not observe rules that belong to another kind of cognition.\nOne should not say: that cognition which has no aesthetic perfections is [not] ugly. Geometry, algebra: are they ugly? which understanding do they disfigure, neither that of Leibniz, nor Maupertuis, nor d'Alembert's. Ugly is that which [offends against rules the] rightly arouses a displeasure.",[]],"refl:1757":["(g is) or rather should be.\nOn L §. 23 “uglinesses” (l. 16.):\ncrude expressions",[]],"refl:1758":["One can say: all perfection is beauty. Hence all cognition arouses gratification. The gratification that is sensible or through the images of the *imagination* becomes almost sensible is a particular kind of gratification; this latter passes away. Sometimes the rules of both kinds of perfection conflict with one another.",[]],"refl:1759":["barbaric words.",[]],"refl:1760":["On the last two sentences of L §. 24:\n*quaeritur*. If the rules conflict with one another, i.e. one weakens the other, e.g. metaphysics.",[]],"refl:1761":["Polyhistor. Leibniz. Morhof. now: Kästner.\nSuch erudition is like a garden that contains several hides of land.",[]],"refl:1762":["objective magnitude.\nextensive: extensiveness.\nintensive. Importance.",[]],"refl:1763":["subjective magnitude.\n(g Extensive.) Distinct. Correct and certain.",[]],"refl:1764":["Cognition, because it permits a more and a less, has a magnitude.\nThe magnitude of learned cognition is distinguished from importance.\nThe magnitude is measured by the multitude of the cognized *objects*. 2. by the degree of distinctness and completeness of them.\nImportance depends on the worth of the cognized *object*. *e.g.* the knowledge of the world, of God.",[]],"refl:1765":["Every aesthetic cognition can be false, if it only amuses. *e.g.* (g Milton:) One says God also commanded his angels that they should 2 times 10 and — —\nThe truth or correctness of cognition is a logical property of it.\n*Veritas est convenientia ideae vel cognitionis cum objecto*.",[]],"refl:1766":["Distinctness concerns the formal of cognition; in the aesthetic it need only be clear.",[]],"refl:1767":["Certainty is cognized truth; it has degrees, and these rest on the distinctness of cognition.\nThere can be truth in someone's judgments and thoughts, but there is no certainty; *e.g.* in the opinion of Socrates on the immortality of the soul. Seneca on comets.\nSomething is said in company: one who is called as witness knows it certainly; the other does not know it certainly.",[]],"refl:1768":["a kind of importance; the consequences are either in the understanding or the will.",[]],"refl:1769":["[Practical it can be with re]\n*e.g.* the doctrine of the *Scholastici* of the *universalia* is not practical. Practical and useful are different. [practical also that which]",[]],"refl:1770":["One must make the beginning with the logical perfections, which one can also *treat* without aesthetic perfections. The others can follow upon them.\nNo one decorates a house before it has the proper firmness.",[]],"refl:1771":["We do not eat merely to become sated.\nWe do not clothe ourselves merely for warmth.",[]],"refl:1772":["Mathematics has its logical perfections, but also imperfections.\nvery correct, but not distinct.\nNo distinct and correct one is without utility.\nOpposite L § 33 »dass — mehr unvollkommen« (lines 18f.):\nVery distinct, but trifles.\nOpposite L § 33 »als vollkommen — oder« (lines 19f.):\n*e.g.* barbaric.\nOpposite L § 33 »hässlich zu — und zu« (lines 20-22):\nfrosty, *e.g.* common logic.",[]],"refl:1773":["If the rules of beauty and of [aesthetic] logical perfection conflict with one another, then the latter keeps its place, but according to the aim that one has with it.",[]],"refl:1774":["Whoever would apply ornaments everywhere is similar to the Gothic |\ntaste or to the finery of a coquette. In common speeches. Good rational and sensible power of estimation.",[]],"refl:1775":["not to the detriment of truth, although to the detriment of method.",[]],"refl:1776":["Historical cognition can be perfect without beauty, if it is dry.",[]],"refl:1777":["Scholar: who is learned.",[]],"refl:1778":["Erudition is distinguished *materialiter* or *formaliter* from common cognition. In the first sense it is a |\nhistorical, in the second a rational erudition; the latter is either mathematical or philosophical.\n(s Rational cognition arises from rules.)",[]],"refl:1779":["Logical perfection: truth, distinctness, universality.",[]],"refl:1780":["The logical perfections relate to the object, the beauties to the subject.*\n*(s The matter of cognition in relation to the subject is sensation. The form of cognition in relation to the subject is called appearance. All satisfaction is agreement and is either subjective, if it rests on agreement with the subject: feeling or taste, or objective, if it rests on agreement with the object, and is universally valid.)",[]],"refl:1781":["The critique and history of the beautiful belong to erudition, but not the art; in general the skills of genius, e.g. philosophy and beautiful arts, do not belong to erudition.",[]],"refl:1782":["Agreement with the object is logical perfection; consequently this is what is essential in all cognition. The affecting of feeling is what is contingent, hence all beauty requires logical perfection. This must also be in historical cognition.\nIf attention is directed to logical perfection alone, then the cognition is dry; if it is also directed to beauty: then only a determinate measure of logical perfection is possible.",[]],"refl:1783":["To the most perfect subject only cognitions according to their objective perfection would be pleasing. (g The conflict of taste against the form of reason is to be posited in this, because synthesis is opposed to analysis.)\nThat is the right taste, which agrees with the perfection of reason, even in *synthesi*. *e.g.* examples, analogies.\nFeeling conflicts most with the form of reason.\nLittle sensation and much taste.",[]],"refl:1784":["The perfection of reason pertains to subordination, the aesthetic to *coordination*; the former: to consider the *concretum in abstracto*, the latter: the *abstractum in concreto*.",[]],"refl:1785":["[with regard to certainty, not to distinctness]",[]],"refl:1786":["Logical perfection is partly historical, partly rational; the former is speculative.\nThe aesthetic perfection of sensation or of taste (in itself).\nThe practical one of utility or of morality.",[]],"refl:1787":["Taste permits no rules *a priori*, because it is supposed to be a sensible judgment, which cannot be made according to such rules, but only in sensible intuition.\nThe rules serve to explain and to criticize taste, not as *praecepta* [precepts].\nThe norm of taste is models, not of imitation (s prescriptions.), but of estimation.",[]],"refl:1788":["(On the doctrine of taste.) All [work] skill is either that of genius or of profession. A scholar of genius or of profession. A worker etc etc. The latter [are] workers belong to art or to handicraft. With the one of genius one can promise nothing, pledge oneself to nothing. Herein nothing depends on choice except the favoring of natural talent. It is like the pool at Bethesda. |\n(Against this John Streel:) Taste does not need genius. It is a long habituation in the manner.",[]],"refl:1789":["The pleasure that can be attained only through [the feeling about] the sensation of the object (consequently matter) makes charm and emotion and is called feeling.\nThe pleasure that can be attained only [through feeling] about the form of the object is called taste, and because the [form] cognition of the form cannot be attained through the effect of the object on the senses, but arises from the laws of the activity of the subject (principally of the lower cognition, which coordinates): so the |\nground of the satisfaction is subjective as regards the matter and objective as regards the formal ground.",[]],"refl:1790":["(g A shady forest is agreeable in sensation.)\nAppearance is distinguished from sensation in this, that the latter is that in sensibility which makes up a part of the subject's own state, the former however is an actual action of the cognitive power, which pertains to the object as the cause of this sensation.",[]],"refl:1791":["Taste [Satisf] pertains [not so much] to the universality of satisfaction and precisely for that reason pertains to the form of the object that is in conformity with the universal laws of sensibility. But what agrees (g with) the laws of the powers of our mind is agreeable. If [however] this agreeableness, even in private relation, as far as our own state is concerned, is small: then on the other hand it is represented in it that it is universally valid for sociability. In solitude indifference with regard to the beautiful. Young people have much feeling and little taste. The conditions of the beautiful form of objects are representations according to relations of space and time. Those of beautiful cognition: novelty, contrast, manifoldness.",[]],"refl:1792":["7.\nL 7':\nOne and the same thing can appear in various ways (g differently), but the concept of it must be one and the same in this. In the case of the good, therefore, that which makes satisfaction is universally valid.\nAll cognition has either perfection of understanding [or]\nor perfection of taste; if the latter is not there, then it is dry; if the first is not there, shallow.\nL 7:\nThe good pleases constantly, the beautiful everyone, although not always, and taste wants to have change.",[]],"refl:1793":["Beauty consists in the agreement (g of the form — appearance) with the [universal] laws of sensibility. Order. Unity.\nCharm in the agreement of the object, insofar as it alters our state, according to laws of sensibility: novelty.\nTaste is the faculty of cognizing that which universally pleases sensibly.\nBeautiful objects,\nBeautiful representations of [sensation or of] imitation or of the cognition of objects.\nGood is what pleases in the concept, thus[perf] universally, and accords with the object.\nCognition is logically perfect through agreement with the object, |\nthus according to rules of the understanding; aesthetically: what pleases in sensibility, thus subjectively",[]],"refl:1794":["Logical perfection as regards form consists in truth (g in the concept) and its means. Aesthetic, as regards form, in graspability in intuition.\nThe form of sensibility which facilitates the perfection of understanding is the self-sufficient beautiful, which can serve to make universal concepts intuitive, and prepared appearances for distinctness through universal concepts.",[]],"refl:1795":["The estimation of matter takes place through sensation (s relation of the representation to the subject).\nThe feeling of form is taste. But because form can be cognized only *reflectendo* breaks off.",[]],"refl:1796":["The estimation of the object through sensation is not universally valid, through true taste is valid for all human beings, both are subjective; but through reason is valid for all. If the object accords with the feeling of the subject as regards matter, then it is agreeable and charms or moves; if it accords as regards form with the |\nfeeling, then it is beautiful; but if there is an agreement with feeling *in abstracto*, then it is good.",[]],"refl:1797":["The form of beauty consists in 2 pieces. First: of graspability (g clarity) in intuition [*in concreto*] (agreement with rules of the understanding). Second: In the breaks off.",[]],"refl:1798":["The essential form of the beautiful consists in the agreement of [App] intuition with the rules of the understanding. music. proportion. Maxims in the example.",[]],"refl:1799":["8'.\nL 8:\nPerfect sensibility is beauty. (s All beauty consists in presentation.) But sensibility consists in agreement with the subjective laws of execution |\nL 8': |\nand the form is *coordination* in the case of *obiecta seusuum* [*sensuum*, objects of the senses], *subordination* in the case of *obiecta rationis* [objects of reason].\nAbstraction helps toward logical perfection, but association toward aesthetic. The abstract is isolated, dry, difficult; the concrete hidden, lively and easy. From the particular to the universal there holds no inference, and the cognition of the particular [from th] in the universal is without intuition.",[]],"refl:1800":["8.\nL 8':\n1. Perfection of cognition in general (g α, inner): truth or accord with itself. (s Manifoldness and unity. Magnitude.) β. Magnitude and manifoldness (g in one): *non qvot, sed qvantum* [not how many, but how much].\n2.\na. logical perfection. (s Truth.) Distinctness through universal concepts, and certainty. (s Universality, precision.)\nb. aesthetic perfection: distinctness in intuition.\n(g Form\nMatter)\nL 8:\n(s rational. The universal *in concreto*. Shallow or dry. logical, aesthetic loss.\n1. Perfection of cognition in general as regards kind: logical and aesthetic.\n2. logical *vid*: α, β. Among cognitions of one kind as regards *quantity* or *quality*.\nAs concerns logical perfection etc etc.)\nL 8':\n( s (g 1mo *vid*: NB 2. As regards form. Manifoldness and unity.) Perfection as regards *quality* and *quantity*. The former either logical or aesthetic. The latter either *extensiva*: as regards spread, multitude, or *intensiva*: as regards degree. (g NB 2. theoretical or practical perfection. The former alone belongs here.) )",[]],"refl:1801":["On aesthetic perfection, accords with the logical. [Manif] Order (g *Coordinatio*), unity (g *subordinatio*) and sensibility.\n(s Aesthetic perfection of cognition in accord with the understanding consists in this, that through it [of] much of one thing, but in the logical: little of many things is thought. Is art and not science.)",[]],"refl:1802":["Truth, manifoldness and unity are the perfections of every cognition.\nTruth either of cognition with regard to the object in itself or in relation to the intuition of the subject; the former is breaks off.",[]],"refl:1803":["Through reading the ancients one can indeed sharpen sensation, but not taste.\nVirgil has more taste than Homer.",[]],"refl:1804":["In the left margin below no. 1763,\nPerfection concerns either *quantity* or *quality*. (g The latter either material or formal perfection.) The latter is either (s theoretical or practical) according to rules of |\nunderstanding and reason or according to rules of taste (s sensibility) or according to rules of the will [feeling]. (s Cognition, feeling and desire.)\nLogical perfection concerns either the matter: truth, or the form: distinctness. (s Universality.)\n(s logical (g according to concepts), aesthetic (g according to intuitions) and practical perfection.)\nDistinctness is opposed to beauty. *e.g.* sententious and enigmatic expressions.",[]],"refl:1805":["Interconnection with grounds according to the rule of reason or according to the rule of taste. According to the latter the following *canones* hold: What other people say is true, and what they do is good. According to the same, that which has a similarity with something ridiculous is itself ridiculous.",[]],"refl:1806":["8'.\nL 8:\nThe teachers of taste have greatly erred in this, that in aesthetic perfection they have commended sensation (charm and emotion) more than intuition; for the latter is what presents concepts *in concreto*, |\nL 8': |\ngives back to them what the understanding has withdrawn from them through abstraction. Essentially beautiful.\nThere are creative intuitions, which are distinguished from [borrowed] empirical ones. There are deceptive intuitions or [dreaming] enthusiastic ones, because they are actually not painted out, but thought in addition.",[]],"refl:1807":["Whether logical or aesthetic perfection must go first?\n[Whether the aest] The aesthetic must bear in itself the mark of contingency, thus not be sought.\nIt must not be superfluous with regard to the logical, but carry with it (s intuition) unity.\nIt must [only serve] never be without simplicity.",[]],"refl:1808":["What is erudition, erudite cognition?",[]],"refl:1809":["In the upper margin:\nLogical perfection relates to the object (g and pertains to *quality* or *quantity*) and is either truth (perfection of matter or as regards *quality*) or distinctness (perfection of form) or magnitude (perfection as regards *quantity*). Subjective perfection is in relation to feeling (aesthetic) or to the will (practical).\npractical: Either with regard to the cognitions themselves or to the things and their actuality.",[]],"refl:1810":["9. 10'.\nL 9':\n( s There is no principle of taste, (s (doctrine). Thus also no doctrine of taste (critique) for the imagination.) )\nL 9:\nAesthetic perfection in relation to cognition or in relation to the mere feeling of pleasure. The former business, the latter play.\nIt pleases greatly when business looks like play. It displeases when play looks like business. But it is a business: to bring sensibility and understanding into agreement for the furthering of cognition. On the other hand a mere play: to bring sensibility into accord with the feeling of pleasure according to universal laws (of the Verstheilung), because that contributes nothing to cognition, like music and sought-after sound of words or noise of sensation. Thus [dis] there can |\nL 10':\nbe no aesthetic perfection of cognition, but mere perfection of taste. It displeases greatly when in a [cogn] lecture that is designed for cognition we encounter what is sought after for taste.",[]],"refl:1811":["The usefulness of cognition is not beauty.",[]],"refl:1812":["The pleasing manner (not inclination) of choosing sensibly is ta|ste.\nTaste and its authors give culture without teaching, i.e. polish.",[]],"refl:1813":["The object is beautiful whose intuition pleases sensibly.",[]],"refl:1814":["Beauty is self-sufficient, [which] since sensibility harmonizes with perfection according to [the laws] of reason according to universal laws; self-sufficient beauty thus lies in the understanding, insofar as it is suited to the subject. Original beauty also cannot be drawn from experience; it is rather the means of estimating beauty; but from experience the understanding takes as it were the end of nature.",[]],"refl:1815":["To think up beautiful objects belongs a creative spirit; yet reason or experience must have given the thing itself, and the architectonic head only orders and forms according to taste.",[]],"refl:1816":["The formative arts are distinguished from the depicting ones, the former pertain to beautiful objects, the latter to beautiful representations of objects. 1. That which represents the thing. 2. breaks off.",[]],"refl:1817":["It seems after all that everything that is judged from private sensation, i.e. from the part as *principio*, is under the critique of that which is estimated from the universal, i.e. from the whole, and hence the variable through the constant and unchangeable. From this latter comes the ought.",[]],"refl:1818":["There are beautiful objects, (g and) beautiful [arts, and beautiful] presentations of objects, either to make beautiful images of all sorts of objects [or beautiful cogn], thus in the representation of the senses, or also to bring beauty into the representation of reason.",[]],"refl:1819":["The solemnity at the change of hours is beautiful in the viewing, good for the owner even without viewing.",[]],"refl:1820":["Universal validity is either an outer or an inner one: the first pertains to all persons and is subjective and contingent; the second pertains to the object in general, is objective and necessary.",[]],"refl:1821":["5'.\nL 5:\nAll cognition is (g contains) intuition or concept (both can be distinct or indistinct). Cognition through concepts is thinking. The faculty of intuitions is sensibility, the faculty of |\nthinking understanding (of thinking *a priori*: reason). (s For cognizing both together are required.) In the former case it is opposed as an upper (disposing) faculty to the lower one of sensibility, which offers the matter. (s Thus intuition belongs to sensibility and concept to the understanding.)\nThe perfection of cognition according to laws of sensibility is aesthetic, that according to laws of the understanding is logical. The [Pe] former is an agreement with the subject (s pleasure or displeasure), the second with the object, both according to universal laws*, logically or empirically universal.\nL 5':\n*(g [To] In the first: novelty, ease, liveliness, domain. (g how we are affected in intuition or sensation.) In the last: truth, universality, purity. (g To both belongs distinctness, universality, but of different kind.) The faculty of originally producing both kinds of (products) cognition is genius. (It is the origin of the rule; to estimate it is called either power of judgment or taste.) The objective in sensibility is intuition; consequently this accords with the understanding and is by its nature universally valid, because otherwise human beings would not understand one another. Feeling has private validity.)\n( g There is no doctrine (*a priori*), but indeed critique of taste. Rules not rational ones. )",[]],"refl:1822":["There are two ends of [sensibility] sensible pleasure: first for the sake of immediate gratification, 2ndly of the furthering of perfection, thus the relation to intellectual satisfaction. To the latter with regard to logical perfection belongs: to make cognition sensible, i.e. to present it *in concreto* (intuition). 2ndly [to] in the practical to move and stir. It is a play which makes serious occupation liked.",[]],"refl:1823":["The judgment of taste we make not from a rule, but from intuition; for the rules are not *a priori*.",[]],"refl:1824":["The power of judgment tames and orders sensation, taste: spirit, dryness.",[]],"refl:1825":["Sensation, power of judgment, spirit and taste. Beautiful art.\nAgreement either with the (g universal) laws of sensibility or of the understanding.\nAgreement with the subjective laws. Sensation, intuition, ease.\nThat with objective laws logical truth distinctness with the rules of cognition in general.",[]],"refl:1826":["Because what is essential in every representation is the idea of the thing, all aesthetic perfection is a unification of the subjective with the objective.",[]],"refl:1827":["Taste: what pleases in intuition.",[]],"refl:1828":["The constitution of the thing and its worth.",[]],"refl:1829":["Two kinds of distinctions, what the thing is (g relates to the object, the other to the subject), and how it pleases. That which pleases pleases either (s mediately or immediately. What pleases immediately pleases either) in private relation or universally; the former gratifies. What pleases universally pleases either according to universal laws of human sensibility* or according to laws of reason; the former is beautiful, the latter good.\n*(g in appearance; these judgments are universally valid, those of charm have only private validity. In the case of whether something pleases in appearance, one must separate from it all share of the subject and its well-being; the charm in a beautiful object [goes] passes away |\nalso with time, although the judgment about beauty not only remains, but also indicates the very same degree of it.)",[]],"refl:1830":["Beautiful presentation of objects. *hypotyposis*. Beautiful objects. Beautiful cognitions. Beautiful sciences. Critique.",[]],"refl:1831":["Beauty is the harmony of sensations.",[]],"refl:1832":["The understanding is not called the upper faculty because it is preeminent, and sensibility not the lower because it is of lesser worth, but because that which treats stands *a priori* to that which is to be treated. Otherwise sensibility without understanding is something, but understanding without sensibility nothing at all.\nTo be well understood: that the understanding be considered as a human understanding, which is only the faculty of [conc] discursive cognition, and intuition [in] be assumed only as sensible. But because intuition in general can be partly sensible, partly pure: so the understanding [partly of sens] is also considered as a faculty of intuitions, but not in the human being.",[]],"refl:1833":["There must be something that makes a perfection out of agreement with laws of sensibility or of the understanding. The good.",[]],"refl:1834":["Understanding and intuitions make up [the] the objective of cognition.\nThe subjective pertains to sensation, power of judgment, spirit and taste.\nSensation and spirit belong to content, power of judgment and taste to form.\nThe final aim of aesthetic cognition is truth and [mo] good-naturedness. Hence they accord with the understanding and will through subjective means.",[]],"refl:1835":["To lecture on something historically or dogmatically.",[]],"refl:1836":["[Beauty, dryness\nthoroughness]\nBeauties are opposed not to distinctness, but to the depth of cognition. Everything must begin from the raw. Beauties make thoroughness unrecognizable.\n(s Charm, i.e. effect on feeling, is not beauty, but *parergon*.)",[]],"refl:1837":["Cognition through an idea is architectonic. Cognition through aggregates is technical. City.",[]],"refl:1838":["The logical perfection of cognition as regards matter is truth (agreement with the objects), as regards form distinctness as condition of truth according to laws of the understanding.\nAesthetic perfection as regards matter is sensation (feeling), as regards form intuition* (liveliness). For [the] considered as cognition sensation pertains to the object, and intuition is only the form of representing it. Although [as alteration] intuition [sub] properly [only subject] is objective, but sensation subjective.\n*(g accords with the perfection of understanding.)\n(s aesthetic perfection: intuition and feeling.)",[]],"refl:1839":["The end of all logical perfection is: to bring everything to concepts (g to universality) and these to distinctness.\nThe end of all aesthetic perfection is: to bring everything to intuition and this to liveliness. Yet in both order and harmony in one idea is still needed.\n(s Agreement with the object — truth.\nAgreement with the subject. Agreeableness.)",[]],"refl:1840":["The science for the culture of taste, wide reading of the ancient poets and Romans are so called because they further *humanitatem* and further the dryness of the sciences through what pleases universally in sensibility, hence the sociable in cognition, likewise further the concord of human beings for teaching, the connection of the doctrines of the ancients with ours. Through these breaks off.\n(s Aesthetic perfection for the (g furthering) communication of cognition is business.)",[]],"refl:1841":["Aesthetic perfection is (s for the sake of cognition merely) play (s *tanqvam per otium* [as if at leisure] (s — easy)), logical is business (s *tanqvam per negotium* [as if through business]). If business (s is as free as)* seems to be play, yet with retention of its perfection: then it pleases taste, in that it is useful; if play is pursued as business** (s pedant): then it displeases, in that it is not useful and yet is difficult. Sought-after beauties displease.\n(s The foremost logical perfection, insofar as it is supposed to be merely logical, is truth.\nThe foremost aesthetic perfection, insofar as it is supposed to be merely aesthetic, is sensation.)\n*(s is pursued as play: shallow head.)\n**(s Purposive (g lawful) [is] turns out — the first is eloquence, the second poetry.)",[]],"refl:1842":["The power of judgment pertains to application. Understanding to the form of perfection. 1. Sensible cognition among itself, 2. the harmony of sensibility with the understanding.",[]],"refl:1843":["In taste each always has the model or the archetype in his head.",[]],"refl:1844":["Sensation and spirit move. Power of judgment and taste direct and moderate. The former is the wind in flutes, the latter the fingers.",[]],"refl:1845":["Perfection consists in general in agreement with universal laws (what pleases universally necessarily). Either with universally objective laws, whereby cognition acquires an agreement with the object (distinctness. thoroughness), or with subjective ones, whereby it accords with the nature of the subject. The latter is aesthetic. What sets our [cogn] powers of mind into an easy and strong play is agreeable. What sets them into a harmonizing play is beautiful. What at the same time sets the upper powers into a play in accord with sensibility is good.",[]],"refl:1846":["What awakens and enlivens the understanding belongs to spirit (s what enlivens the entire cognitive power); what awakens sensation: to sense.\nWhat accords as regards form with the understanding belongs to the power of judgment; what with sensibility: to taste.",[]],"refl:1847":["Sensation and spirit belong to genius. Power of judgment and taste bring forth nothing, but only administer in relation to the object or among each other.\nWhat agrees with the subjective laws of the understanding has spirit.\nWhat enlivens belongs to sensation and spirit; what preserves and protects, to power of judgment and taste.\ngenius is architectonic. creates. Power of judgment and taste administer.",[]],"refl:1848":["One chooses something from reflection (because it is cheap, because it is healthy) or from appetite (inclination) or from taste. Foods and drinks. Likewise a house betrays the choice according to reflection or inclination, comfort etc etc. Taste makes no choice, but only directs it.",[]],"refl:1849":["To sensation belongs the inner of sense (pleasure).\n(s The greatest perfection of cognition lies in intuition; sensibility is to replace what the understanding takes away.)",[]],"refl:1850":["The faculty of choosing in a universally valid way is taste. The use of universal rules *in concreto* is power of judgment. Feeling distinguishes only for itself; the judgment is not valid for others. Taste social.",[]],"refl:1851":["Whether taste has constant and universal rules. But not to be cognized *a priori* (g *in abstracto*), but *in concreto*. Is a sensible power of judgment that is valid for all. Sensibility gives no other judgment than *in concreto*, otherwise it would be understanding. All rules leave out much of the *concreto*. The rules are not laws, namely which make aesthetic perfection and are universal.",[]],"refl:1852":["Whoever does not choose that which pleases universally does not have taste.",[]],"refl:1853":["The egoism of taste. One's own taste changes. Corrupted taste. Necessity of a model, to form and to preserve taste; the models: not of imitation, but of estimation, not models [Modelle] and objective, but usable on average, but not sufficient in themselves.",[]],"refl:1854":["The faculty of choosing the universally pleasing sensible is taste",[]],"refl:1855":["The means of the beautiful is art, the rule nature. Nature is not the model (s but example) of the beautiful, for the beautiful lies in ideas; yet it is the *substratum* of it. Nature signifies in the beautiful the unforced. Art the purposive and orderly. Artificial, however, the painful. Everything is natural which seems to have arisen according to a universal law of efficient causes.\n(g If art resembles chance and chance art: then this is the unexpected.)\n(s Beauty is the constitution of an object or cognition whereby the cognitive faculties are set into harmonious attunement.)",[]],"refl:1856":["Taste is the polishedness (*politesse*) of the power of judgment.* One becomes polished only through social intercourse. Sensible power of judgment has its ideal, but not taste; the latter has examples, but not models. Archetype not example. Venus of Praxiteles not model.\n(g idea. which are drawn from many examples.)\n*(g Taste shows itself in the moderation both of the strong and of the graceful. Taste costs nothing, is pleasing, as it were polite and playful, compels no one to inconvenience, is virtue [in the] from inclination.)",[]],"refl:1857":["The sketch belongs to the power of judgment (g image) (drawing), the |\nraw elaboration to genius and the refinement to taste.\nThe idea breaks off.",[]],"refl:1858":["On the idea of the beautiful. Beautiful objects and cognitions.",[]],"refl:1859":["[*Regulae*] The *norma gustus* [norm of taste] is not a universal law, but an *exemplar*.",[]],"refl:1860":["*Diiudicatio aesthetica* [aesthetic judging] (g according to *sensus communis* [common sense]) is taste. According to *sensus privatus* [private sense] it is appetite. Taste is a judgment of society or social.\n(s Barbaric beauty oriental.)",[]],"refl:1861":["Power of judgment, *jugement*, healthy understanding are one and the same and the capacity of understanding in application.",[]],"refl:1862":["Simplicity furthers universal validity; for what can be easily represented or procured is suited to everyone's understanding. |\nTaste seeks to prevent roughness, also giving offense against usage. It presupposes beautiful or magnitude, yet like the smooth it makes an *analogon* of beauty.",[]],"refl:1863":["On the models of taste. On genius.",[]],"refl:1864":["Sensation and power of judgment together make sensible intuition. Sensation is distinguished from feeling; the former is the matter of sensible cognition, the latter the subjective of satisfaction and is either emotion or charm. Feelings corrupt the judgment of the understanding through the interest aroused. The truly beautiful must please in intuition; whoever is interested in it is corrupted and cannot judge impartially. Whoever judges according to whether something charms or moves him no longer judges about the object; that is *metabasis eis allo genos*, such a judgment is not advantageous to the understanding and does not concern the essentially beautiful. This is the good in intuition (but all intuition is sensible). To the power of judgment belong the design, the order, the precision, the natural. It is not the gratification which arises from sensation that makes the satisfaction, but the satisfaction, insofar as it arises universally from all 4 pieces, makes the gratification.",[]],"refl:1865":["8'.\nL 8:\n[Philos] Historical cognition is as regards matter opposed [to] to the dogmatic, because the object of the |\nL 8': |\nfirst is an object |\nof the senses, of the second of the understanding. *Formaliter* a cognition is rational if it is grounded on the universal rules of method according to which it can arise *a priori*. But it is called historical if it is grounded on [the universal laws of] a particular example of the use of reason. In the latter case philosophy sits in memory. A pupil in the second case puts to shame the one of the first kind. The latter seems to be ignorant, the former learned; afterward the former rational, the latter a dunce.\nL 8:\n(g In the didactic method historical or rational form.)\nL 8':\nCommon cognition (s aggregate) and science (s system) (s[with regard to combination] not as regards *quality* or degree, but as regards order and unity) are distinguished in this, that the latter holds cognitions as contained in a whole of cognition of a certain kind, the former however only as pieces that belong to no whole. Science thus contains besides the partial cognitions also an outline of the whole and the place of each particular cognition in this whole. Historical is a cognition whereby something is cognized insofar as it is given. Everything historical is either *materialiter* (s what cannot be cognized except insofar as it (object) is given) or *formaliter* historical; the former only insofar as the objects are given, the latter insofar as a cognition of the objects is given. Philosophy is *materialiter* rational, but can be *formaliter* historical, if one merely learns the philosophy of another, just as it is given. That does not yet make a philosopher, who can philosophize, but: one who knows a philosophy.\n(s Science of reason either mathematics or philosophy; the sum total of historical cognition is erudition.)\n(s Logical perfection of historical and rational cognition.)\n(s Historical sciences from sciences of reason.)\nSome rational science can be learned, others only acquired through genius and its culture. The former belongs to erudition. The tools of erudition: philology.\nMathematics can prove its universal rules *a priori*, and the application *in concreto* is intuitive.\nin philosophy — — — — — — — not intuitive.",[]],"refl:1866":["Common cognition and science; [both either] the latter: cognition in a system.\nScience\nof erudition* is the sum total of historical sciences. (g depends on how one knows something.) of insight is the sum total of sciences of reason.\nArt\nart of teaching mechanical (handicraft) depends on how and whereby one can do something. of genius.\nKnowing and** being able.\nIn all of them logical and aesthetic perfection can be looked to. It depends on what the main work is. Aesthetic perfection can have a private relation and accommodation or relation to perfect sensibility in general. In this case it is beauty.\n*(g which is grounded merely on what is taught; thus mathematics does not belong to erudition.)\n**(g just as science to common cognition: so art to handicraft (mechanical), namely that one knows and is able according to rules (teaching *a priori*).",[]],"refl:1867":["Cognitions are practical as prescriptions and as incentives; the former: insofar as they subject, the latter: in that they move. They both do not belong to logical perfection.\nThe aesthetic stands in greater interconnection with it.",[]],"refl:1868":["The perfection of cognition is 1. theoretical (g in relation to cognition), 2. practical (g in relation to the will); the latter does not belong to logic.\nThe former either logical (g objective) or aesthetic (g subjective).\nAesthetic must have a relation to the logical. A certain idea lies at the ground.\n(s Ease, because it is contingent (s play). Sociable manner of the presentation of the objects.)\nUnderstanding in intuition *in concreto*.\nLiveliness, examples, sensible distinctness, not emotions or charms.\nUniversal concepts. Natural.\nExtensive influence.\nStudiedness of the beautiful does not look good. Easy manner. Logical and aesthetic do injury to one another* (g dry or shallow. Also sought-after beauties seem to want to persuade.), but also support each other.\n*(s distinct for the sensible cognitive power and obscure or con|fused\nfor the understanding; the former: in order to know it among the usual appearances, the latter: in order to order it under concepts. Beauties of cognition are *parerga* and must be represented as such.)",[]],"refl:1869":["To the beautiful (g to produce it) belong not prescriptions (rules), but models (g rules need examples (illustration)); and these not of imitation, for this would itself again be the beauty, but of analogical conformity. The models of style are the ancients. Precisely because they are our models, they cannot be surpassed. Boldness is: to want to surpass the first models (of nature) themselves, which is feasible, because nature has made not everything for our sensible satisfaction, but also for utility. No one of our time can become a true model. The archetype is the model which is not borrowed at all. It lies merely in the idea. Morals requires [nothing] rule, permits no model, but indeed example.",[]],"refl:1870":["Taste* is the last culture, preliminary exercise through reading good models.\n*(g Method of teaching and delivery.)",[]],"refl:1871":["The understanding is in itself already communal (g judgment: commonly valid, sense has private validity); hence one also does not say: *intellectus communis*, but: *vulgaris*, i.e., the empirically capable understanding. But one also calls this *sensus communis*, because that which is judged by the understanding most immediately in the case of the senses is counted along with sense.\nTaste can arise only through comparison of many judgments of the senses.",[]],"refl:1872":["The faculty to choose what pleases the sense of everyone. *Facultas diiudicandi per sensum communem*. The faculty to choose sensibly and universally validly is taste. This concerns more the form than matter of sensibility.\nUniversally valid laws are not universal, but it is asked how they are valid. The understanding judges according to universal laws, i.e., according to concepts.",[]],"refl:1873":["Imagination and understanding are the two active sources of representations. [Imagination] Sense is passive.",[]],"refl:1874":["aesthetic perfection is either [according to private laws or universal laws of sensibility. Taste] (s agreement with the necessary or contingent laws of sensibility; the former agreeing with the logical.) Agreeable or beautiful, charms or moves.\n(g not according to rules *a priori* or *praeceptis*, but norms.)",[]],"refl:1875":["in the presentation.\nBelow it as elucidation:\nA/greement with the subject. Beauty concerns presentation and delivery.",[]],"refl:1876":["Aesthetic perfection: the subjective agreement of the cognitive faculties in the representation of a thing.*\n(g Hence not concept, but feeling of pleasure, yet universally valid. Understanding and sensibility.)\nLogical perfection: concept (universal). Distinctness.\nUnderstanding: also empirical; the rule objective — reason: *a priori* — power of judgment: understanding and sensibility in relation. reflecting.",[]],"refl:1877":["Philodoxy, not philosophy.\n(s Historical or rational cognition. Common, learned.)",[]],"refl:1878":["The sum total of all historical cognitions is called learning. Learning is properly the historical cognition of the sciences.",[]],"refl:1879":["Historical cognition as science is learning.",[]],"refl:1880":["Spirit consists in the arousal of the powers of the mind to more extended use, is something other than dry cognition. Art indicates no spirit.",[]],"refl:1881":["Novelty, ease are merely subjective perfections.",[]],"refl:1882":["The sum total of formal historical cognitions is called learning, *erudition*; to it belong [phil] literature and philology. The philosophical and the beautiful spirit are both not learned men. Sciences of insight or learning. Art either of utility or of beauty. Agreement with the object (matter): truth. Distinctness (g in concepts): form.\nThoroughness, dryness. Beauty, ease.\nCogency is lively thoroughness.\nOf the good meaning: »to make something sensible«.\nTruth: in agreement with object — with subject: distinctness in concepts and in intuition.",[]],"refl:1883":["The historical science is learning.",[]],"refl:1884":["Logical perfection goes out to this: to cognize the particular in the universal, the aesthetic: the universal in the particular. The former brings everything to concepts, the latter to intuitions. They promote each other in form. The former abstracts, the latter unites much in one concept.",[]],"refl:1885":["Manifoldness and unity magnitude — quality\n)*\n[Distinctness of order and form] [Truth of the M]\n*(g as to matter is truth, as to form (g unity) order.)",[]],"refl:1886":["Universality, truth (and distinctness), manifoldness and unity in all cognition.\nDistinctness, consequently order and unity, are only means.",[]],"refl:1887":["Subjective truth in appearance, subjective distinctness in intuition (s liveliness); subjective universality is popularity (g *sensus communis*).",[]],"refl:1888":["On the union of the logical and aesthetic (g difficulty) perfections.\n1. Universality, the universal in the particular: popularity, and to make speculative cognitions (s *in abstracto* or *concreto*) comprehensible to the common understanding.\n2. Distinctness through few marks: abstraction, and liveliness through many without abstraction.\n3. Truth with object, subjective: with the way of thinking of the subject, truth in appearance.\n4. Necessity. (s from concepts.) (s Habit.) empirical: through experience, habit (g inner as well as usage), e.g., in morals, feeling.\n(s Classics. Humanists.)\n(s Conflict of the aesthetic with the logical perfection. Hence beauty is a product of art that looks like nature.)",[]],"refl:1889":["Feelings are least of all to be united with taste.",[]],"refl:1890":["The truth of illusion requires only small analogies, e.g., in the fable, and indeed, as the imagination lets things speak through |\nthe inner speech which we, in judging them, let them hold to us: echo.",[]],"refl:1891":["The immediate satisfaction merely in the formal aspect of the manner of representation is taste, in the material (for sense) is the feeling of the agreeable and disagreeable. The mediate satisfaction through reason in the good is the way of thinking.",[]],"refl:1892":["In logic [there is no] practical perfection is not taken into consideration at all.\nOf the beautiful there is no science, but only art.\nArt is a faculty of execution which cannot be [taught] learned according to rules. Knowing and being able are here different. For (g skillful) presentation for cognition is no cognition. The rules of presentation are subjectively universally valid and yet *a priori*.\nTo choose the beautiful presupposes science. Culture of the power of judgment with regard to the beautiful through cognitions are *humaniora*.",[]],"refl:1893":["Arousal of feeling without preceding logical-aesthetic form is contrary to taste.",[]],"refl:1894":["Essential aesthetic perfection is that which contributes to cognition in general, thus [the] not feelings, but what as intuition makes the concepts of the understanding concrete.\n(g Ideas of intuition (g represented.) is the essential and *substratum*.)\nSensation is merely subjective, but intuition objective.\nSensation, power of judgment concerns how the sensation harmonizes with the concept.\nSpirit (*genie*): animation through idea. Taste compares it with the universal sense.\nAnimation through associated sensations is different from through a universal *principium* of the association of many sensations.\nInstrument of animation or *principium*.\nField for fantasy and unity of the same.\nWe treat only of the beauty of cognition, not of the objects.",[]],"refl:1895":["What agrees with the laws of a power of representation pleases. Now what pleases immediately, that pleases either universally or has only private satisfaction. The former either according to laws of the sensible or of the understanding. Beautiful and good. although *a priori* grounds of what pleases sensibly can be given, they are nevertheless not probative, but the *criterium* is experience.",[]],"refl:1896":["What pleases only me is agreeable.\nWhat is to be called beautiful must please universally.",[]],"refl:1897":["The universality of satisfaction is cognized and proved either *a priori* or only empirically.",[]],"refl:1898":["Emotion and charm belong not to the objective, but merely subjective, thus not to the beautiful except as contingent. But intuition which corresponds to the concept —",[]],"refl:1899":["Perfection (aesthetic) in general, or what contributes to it.",[]],"refl:1900":["Everyone who has an inner *principium vitale* of cognition has a *genius* (*genie*). *Sed genio suo indulgere non licet nisi [sub] hac lege, ut sensui communi, h.e. genio aliorum, etiam indulgeamus* (But it is not permitted to indulge one's own genius except under this law, that we also indulge common sense, i.e., the genius of others).",[]],"refl:1901":["Logic is doctrine, aesthetic critique (because the principles are not *a priori*, but empirical and nevertheless contain universal subjective laws (of form).) Taste is pleasing, the understanding commanding.",[]],"refl:1902":["Sensation is properly only subjective and for [the understanding] sense, intuition objective and for the understanding; hence for sensation spirit, for intuition power of judgment. which latter with regard to both together is taste.",[]],"refl:1903":["Of cognition: 1. agreement with the universal laws (g conditions) of intuition (sensible) or of thinking.\n(s Form of sensibility is intuition. Sensation.)\naesthetic and logical perfection. Essential. Unity of intuition and of thinking. Ends.",[]],"refl:1904":["Logical perfection*, formal: distinctness. Material: truth (through concepts).\naesthetic perfection:**\nDistinctness and truth of intuition.\nHarmony with the subjective conditions [of all] of the [entire] collective cognitive power is aesthetic, with objective is logical. (subjective are the conditions of appearance.)\n(g subjective harmony, objective harmony.)\nUniversal validity in sensation: aesthetic, in thinking: logical. (s Sensation weakens logical perfection.)\n*\n(g[Magnitude] Universality as concept. Rules are empirically universal.)\n**(g Singular as intuition and sensation. Sensation constitutes the contingent of aesthetic perfection.)",[]],"refl:1905":["Objective perfection pertains to cognition of the object. Intuition also pertains to the object, but yet in relation to the subject, the whole human nature.",[]],"refl:1906":["The aesthetic perfection which promotes the logical is interest and not affect (with regard to feeling); the preacher must not arouse weeping.",[]],"refl:1907":["That in whose representation sensibility and understanding harmonize into one cognition is beautiful.",[]],"refl:1908":["The ground of a universal satisfaction according to laws of sensibility is beauty, (g according to laws) of a particular one is charm; to beauty belongs understanding. Charm. Sensation.",[]],"refl:1909":["The united interest of the imagination and understanding.",[]],"refl:1910":["The essential aesthetic perfection is agreement of intuition with concepts, the contingent: with sensations.\nSelf-sufficient beauty.",[]],"refl:1911":["Beautiful cognition. Presentation of concepts. Well-spokenness, eloquence and poetry.",[]],"refl:1912":["6'.\nL 5:\nThe subjective agreement of the cognitive faculties* in the presentation of concepts.\nL 6':\n(s The beautiful presentation of what is thought through words is beautiful delivery (not cognition).)\n(s to communicate oneself universally: *humaniora*.)",[]],"refl:1913":["Essential aesthetic perfection, i.e., harmony with understanding, is sensation, and that is a hindrance to concepts.",[]],"refl:1914":["Universality in the case of the beautiful is empirical (understanding). But there is a universality of reason, which is necessary.",[]],"refl:1915":["Agreement of cognition with the subjective conditions of its possibility.",[]],"refl:1916":["Beautiful cognition can be explained through subjective perfection of the same, but not beautiful objects.",[]],"refl:1917":["Above and \n1. Perfection\nof cognition as mere cognition.\n2.\nin relation to the will.",[]],"refl:1918":["Quality: distinctness (g subjective) (lively, singular). (g aesthetic: clarity of intuition.)\nrelation: truth. (g 2. subjective truth in appearance.) (s 2. the relation aesthetically is to the subject. Charm and emotion.)\nquantity: universality. (g subjective.) (g 3. The universal in the particular.) (s subjectively for all.)\nmodality: certainty, necessity (s of cognition in general. The usual, usage. It is *coustume*.) (g 4. empirical necessity. Approval. Universal. The usual. Taste.)",[]],"refl:1919":["Magnitude of the content.\nOn L § 26 »importance«:\nof the consequences.",[]],"refl:1920":["The aesthetic as to matter in sensation, as to form in (g unity in the) intuition and [determinateness] manifoldness.",[]],"refl:1921":["Sensations do nothing but animate; they do not let us cognize the object better.",[]],"refl:1922":["That displeases which our power of mind resists as a hindrance of its free play. What therefore conflicts with sensibility according to universal laws (at most empirical ones) displeases aesthetically.",[]],"refl:1923":["Beauty of cognition is the agreement of the freedom of the imagination with the lawfulness of the understanding in presentation of concepts. Poetry has the first as end, eloquence the latter, well-spokenness both.",[]],"refl:1924":["Cognitive faculty, feeling of pleasure and displeasure and faculty of desire.\nIf cognitive faculty according to principles is a ground of pleasure, then it is at the same time faculty of desire, i.e., the cognition is practical.",[]],"refl:1925":["All logical perfections are objective; if they are made subjective, without conflicting with the former, although with their restriction: then they are aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1926":["Taste permits no prescriptions and rules,* because the imagination gives the law, namely the universal judgment, and this is free.\n*(g For it is subjective power of judgment, which determines the judgment not [through] according to concepts, but according to pleasure and displeasure.)",[]],"refl:1927":["Necessity of the sciences for taste. Logical perfection lies at the ground as at a meal, but only of the common sense.",[]],"refl:1928":["Beauty and virtue agree in this, that they are judged according to the satisfaction of the commonwealth and not according to private gratification. They require a spectator whom the collective gratification interests. Only with the difference that the grounds of satisfaction in the former are merely empirical and *a priori* have no necessity at all, but in the second intellectual, and necessarily must arouse everyone's pleasure, because they pertain to the harmony of the ends of rational beings in general. Taste and sentiment (g character) are both disinterested, both do honor. The former can be confirmed only through the concurrence of the judgment of many human beings over long times, but the latter through reason by each. In nature beauty and utility for senses and reason are enjoyment and ends; in freedom manners and virtue. The one prepares for the other.",[]],"refl:1929":["1) Cognitive faculty; 2) feeling of pleasure and displeasure; 3) faculty of desire.\nlogical, aesthetic, practical.\nUnderstanding, power of judgment, reason.",[]],"refl:1930":["*Sensus communis*: the commonly valid sense. The popularity [of sense] of a cultivated and finer sense is taste. *gustus*. Taste requires cultivated sensible judgment. The lack of skill to choose popularly is tastelessness. The universal pleasingness of sensible choice is taste. The propensity to choose perversely is false, inverted taste.",[]],"refl:1931":["Because the beautiful by itself carries no interest with it, namely so that its existence is indifferent to us, nevertheless a satisfaction in it takes place: so it must consist in the arousal of the feeling of that in which we take an interest, i.e., the harmony of the understanding and sensibility into a cognition in general.\nThus the beautiful does not gratify, but the harmony of the cognitive powers. For the union of both is good in every aim, the promotion of the understanding, e.g., mathematics, only in one. The agreement of representations with [universal] laws of sensibility can only be universal if it pertains to cognition.",[]],"refl:1932":["The agreement of sensibility with the understanding in a cognition is beauty. Beauty does not rest on sensation, charm and emotion, for these contribute nothing to cognition.\n(s Sensation does not belong to cognition at all.)\nThe feeling of this harmony of both cognitive powers constitutes the satisfaction in the beautiful.",[]],"refl:1933":["Aesthetic perfection is either that of taste, i.e., of the form of sensibility, i.e., of taste, or of the matter of sensation, i.e., feeling; the former grounds a universal satisfaction.",[]],"refl:1934":["Pleasing in the concept, good, in sensation, agreeable, in reflection, beautiful.",[]],"refl:1935":["Beauty pertains only to one part of cognition, namely the sensible presentation of the concept, not logically *in abstracto*, but aesthetically *in concreto*.\nBeautiful cognition is an expression without sense; for the more I look to the subjective, the less is the objective of the manner of representation, i.e., cognition, promoted. But the beautiful manner of representation of an object as presentation of a concept consists in the (g subjective) agreement of the two [cognitive] faculties of representation which belong to cognition, [toge] with one another, in that each follows its laws by itself. Thus freedom of the imagination with the lawfulness of the understanding. Taste is the sensible, subjective (aesthetic) power of judgment (g insofar as it agrees of itself with the logical), either of subsumption under a given concept and thus of the presentation of the same, i.e., that of delivery for a cognition, or of the object of the senses as a beautiful object (of the agreement of the imagination with the understanding into a cognition in general [without B] prior to all concept). — Freedom of the imagination |\nwith the lawfulness of the understanding in harmony; aesthetic perfection of the manner of representation has no rule that determines (g would precede), because the imagination is to harmonize with the understanding precisely in its freedom. The aesthetic perfection in (g immediate) relation [to the] of a representation through the imagination to feeling (not by means of cognition to the same) is not beauty, but charm or emotion.\n(g NB. Here it is treated not of beautiful objects, but of the beautiful presentation of concepts through the imagination, the objects may also be ugly.)\n(g Agreement for cognition is logical perfection.)\n(s Beautiful is a manner of representation in which [the fr] the agreement of the free play of the imagination with the lawfulness of the understanding is sensed.)\n(s Taste is aesthetic power of judgment.\nOf the beautiful as such there is no science. One demands a universal satisfaction, which therefore is not merely feeling; for that (agreeable) cannot serve others as rule of satisfaction; but the satisfaction can nevertheless not be grounded on universal rules.)",[]],"refl:1936":["Sin of omission. If one does not arrange the order properly. [Exten]\nIf one does not agree with rules, e.g., of distinctness, of certainty.\nImperfection of lack.\n*privative* (g ignorance). Who\ndoes not give to the poor.\n*contrarie dictum*: who oppresses him.\nOpposite L § 36 »perfection — become« (l. 25f.):\nIf one acts according to opposed ones.\nOn L § 36 »faults« (l. 28):\nErrors.\n[Either against logical or aesthetic rules.]",[]],"refl:1937":["It is superfluous to bring intuitions into cognition through art where common sensibility gives still greater ones, e.g., paintings of natural things. Flowers.\nJust so it is with charm — voluptuousness.\nHarmful, however, is this when the charm is naturally disproportioned and derogates from the remaining charms.*\nOne must give to rational cognition and to rational motives charm [seek] or intuition, and combine with it a charm to despise sensibility.\nFaults of aesthetics are the superfluous, the long-winded. Measuredness and ease are pleasing to sensibility.\n*(g to flatter a despot is no art; but to contradict him in a polite way, to put the rein of reason on him without his noticing it: that is art, for which he thanks us afterwards. Thus one will indeed not eradicate the voluptuous drive; one should also not even weaken it very much, but only let it borrow the right charms from virtue.)",[]],"refl:1938":["The ignorant one does not know how to ask, the one with insight does not know how to answer (s to questions of the ignorant one, who does not even know his ignorance. *Quaerit delirus*). Because the former does not even know the questions, it is no wonder that he does not know his own ignorance with regard to the answers. One must call an ignoramus (g idiot) only him who knows everything without (g this) that he knows nothing, who thus, while he does not even know how to ask (g rightly) at all, nevertheless answers all questions.",[]],"refl:1939":["Nothing is more contemptible than the imitated (but there are imitations according to universally valid laws, customs). Nothing is [more harmful] more repugnant than the studied (the painfulness in that which belongs merely to agreeableness) (g gives itself airs and boasts), [nothing] because beauties\n|\nmust come along of themselves and are accidental grounds of satisfaction. Hence simplicity (what is not sought), *naivetaet*, insofar as it cannot have been learned, natural, but yet not common.\nNegligence, the *air degagèe*, master in trifles, who gives laws and does not prescribe, who is impolite not with peasant ignorance, but according to a new taste.",[]],"refl:1940":["A lack that one takes for a cognition is a formal error. e.g., empty formulas, tautological definitions.",[]],"refl:1941":["Beauty is dryness — the contrary: ugliness.\nLack of practical perfection is unusefulness, the contrary: harmfulness.\nOn L § 36 »faults« (16328):\nTransgressions of the law: *reatus*.\nIgnorance and error are oppositions — the latter *diametraliter*.",[]],"refl:1942":["True beauty must not be studied and coquettish, affected and painfully dressed up.",[]],"refl:1943":["The good could be still better. negatively evil.",[]],"refl:1944":["What is good could yet be still better, namely complete.",[]],"refl:1945":["Not explaining is lack, explaining falsely fault.\nA lack, e.g., of a syllable in verse, can at the same time be fault. The latter is every transgression of a law.",[]],"refl:1946":["Beautiful +; not-beautiful (g dry) 0; ugly –.",[]],"refl:1947":["*Oppositum* is called the opposite. [a×0 = oppositum]\n*Contrarium* the contrary. a–a = contrarium.\n*diametraliter* opposed like gratification and pain; thus [cognition] true cognition opposed to ignorance negatively, to error *contrarie*.",[]],"refl:1948":["If one makes comparisons, one must first have brought things under a common concept and with regard to this investigate their agreements or lack of the same. e.g., is a peasant's house or a dovecote more perfect? is an elephant or a bird of paradise more perfect?\nOpposite L § 37 sentence 2:\nIf one has the aim to please, then the author is right; if the aim is to cognize and to be useful, then it is false.\nOpposite L § 37, beginning of sentence 3:\nThe practical cognition of a general can be greater than of a bookworm.\nPedantry.",[]],"refl:1949":["e.g., geography of a traveler.\nOn L § 37 sentence 2:\n[Homer and] Pope and Locke.",[]],"refl:1950":["A merchant, a statesman, an economist who have studied. Likewise surveyors.\nMother wit, school wit.",[]],"refl:1951":["Experience.\nOn L § 39 »indeed although — many learned«:\nE.g., theoretical economy and practical.",[]],"refl:1952":["[Learned cognition] Philosophy cognizes of much little, the common of little much.",[]],"refl:1953":["Every distinct insight of the ground draws after it an uncommon series of consequences.",[]],"refl:1954":["Learned cognition in general.\nHarms with regard to the moral,\nthe aesthetic.",[]],"refl:1955":["To study [is som]: to learn science. I.e., cognition [of the from] (g learning with) insight. But insight is the judging of an object from [its] grounds. Now positive (territorial) right is not an object of judging, for that is not free. Roman right must be judged. Hence the learning of positive right is just as little a studying as the learning of the catechism. This learning is called memorizing and routine. For [all inst] all cognition studying is required, where it is not to be a craft.\nOne even learns a language without studying it.",[]],"refl:1956":["This poverty arises in two ways. 1. If one has a learned cognition of no thing. 2. If one has a learned cognition of a very small object. e.g., the criticism of the Latin language. 3. If one has it in an incomplete way.",[]],"refl:1957":["Thus the common man knows nothing at all of the course of the stars. A mere calendar-maker knows them without the grounds.\nA historically poor cognition can [sometimes] only rarely be philosophically rich [but only rarely]. A philosophically poor cognition can often be historically rich. Scaliger, Salmasius.\n(s The ignorant one who has the pride [not] to decide is *idiot*. The layman has no cognition of right.)\n(s Learned and ignorant: historical. *Sophista* and *idiotes*. *Clericus* and *Laicus*. (g *initiati* and *profani*.) *Patronus* and client. *Demagogos* (*patricius*) and *plebeii*. some always set themselves up as leaders of others.)",[]],"refl:1958":["One would rather divide into absolutely or unconditionally necessary, conditionally necessary and voluntary ignorance. (s contingent. e.g., ignorance of the circulation of the blood, of the pressure of the air.)\nIn certain matters ignorance is unavoidable for us. e.g., in the mysteries. The nature of the first elements of bodies. Hypothetically necessary is that which we have on account of lack of the aid of cognition. e.g., for the ancient astronomers the moons and ring of Saturn, or the animalcules of pepper water, or for the heathens the knowledge of the right way to serve God. [voluntary] Wanton is either from negligence or from incorrect application of the powers of the mind. The former is the most common source; hence one also decries much as impossible which is not so. e.g., the constitution of the bond between soul and body etc. *Philosophia pigrorum*. The other happens in that one cultivates the historical part more strongly than the philosophical. e.g., *philologi*. Experimental physics. *Theologi* who treat merely revelation. (g With deliberation, voluntary.)",[]],"refl:1959":["Absolutely and conditionally.\nThis condition is external: e.g., microscope, *tubus*. internal: in the will.\nwanton: if the ignorance happens against reason.\nrational: e.g., Socrates.",[]],"refl:1960":["The boundaries of learned cognition, which are determined either through the constitution of the object and its relation to us or through our duties.",[]],"refl:1961":["We cannot cognize everything learnedly. We may not cognize everything learnedly. We should not cognize everything learnedly. 1. The state of our soul before birth. 2. What has no consequences [for] in practical insights. e.g., the slippers of the ancients. Whether there were philosophers before the Flood. 3. We [can] should not. e.g., the [state] kind of happiness of the future life. [The mysteries] The most common practical arts.",[]],"refl:1962":["Incorrectness of the expression »above the horizon«.\n2. Rule: we must posit nothing without ground above the horizon. e.g., it be impossible to have insight into the manner of generation of humans and animals.\n1. Rule: we must believe that much is elevated [outside] above our horizon. Folly of the freethinkers. That human cognition is not interconnected with all possible objects. Comparison with someone who sent *tabulas logarithmorum* or a book on pure *calculo algebraico* among the Hottentots.\n3. Rule: What is above my horizon is not on that account above the horizon of others. For the bending of vanity.\ne.g., to measure the mountains in the moon.\nI do not comprehend the thing: it is impossible.",[]],"refl:1963":["If a matter is probably posited above the horizon |\nand the striving after it cannot be maintained without disadvantage, then one must give it up. Gold-making, quadrature of the circle.",[]],"refl:1964":["Nothing is below the horizon (g of learned cognition) except that of which one knows that learned cognition cannot and may not bring it further than the common. (g 2. where learned cognition is merely historical and has no influence on insights and rational cognition. Heraldry.)\nThe knowledge of insects seems contemptible to some. Likewise of filth, to the physician urine-inspection.",[]],"refl:1965":["e.g., the philosophical cobbler. Mathematical method in the parts of cognition which in turn are not interconnected with any learned cognition. *de gestu oratoris*.",[]],"refl:1966":["Of Socrates: *qvae supra nos, nihil ad nos*.",[]],"refl:1967":["What with regard to the (g chief) aim of human beings is useless and (g yet) too [laborious] extensive for it to be able to be learnedly cognized without disadvantage to the latter, is outside the horizon etc etc. e.g., metaphysics for a mathematician. Syllogistic for a soldier. Womenfolk.\nOne must not extend or restrict the horizon too much. He who wants to know too much knows nothing. He who believes of some things that they do not concern him often deceives himself. e.g., history for the philosopher. But of poetry he can believe it.",[]],"refl:1968":["One must have a horizon of learned cognition. (s Not want to learn everything.)\nOne must not alter the horizon of cognition too much. (s Not fall from one thing onto another.)\nOne must not be audacious in determining the horizon of others: first because one does not know sufficiently their capacities, second their aims.",[]],"refl:1969":["[Because] The extensiveness of cognition depends: 1. on the magnitude of its horizon, i.e., on the multitude of cognized objects, 2. on the importance of the same.",[]],"refl:1970":["To this belongs 1. that he examine for which part of cognition he has the greatest capacity and satisfaction. 2. What in (g regard to) certain duties is more or less necessary to him. [That] 3. What cannot subsist together with the necessary duties. 4. He should rather seek to extend his horizon too much than to narrow it.",[]],"refl:1971":["Imperfection of cognition 1st is either lack or of privation; the former:\nof ignorance, the latter: of error.\n2nd\nignorance either concerns the material and is lack of historical cognition, or the formal and is lack of rational cognition.\nEvery error is a formal imperfection.\n[Ignorance] [Imperfection of cognition concerns either the material (ignorance) or the formal]\nignorance either material: lack of historical cognition;\nor formal: lack of rational cognition.",[]],"refl:1972":["The horizon of the human race (s in general or of one human being in particular.)",[]],"refl:1973":["The horizon is determined logically, practically and aesthetically. (s objective. or subjective. popular. Womenfolk. Courtier.)\nThe logical horizon is either historical or rational. The former is much wider than the other. but it is [lim] limited through the relation to reason or utility or taste. (s Private horizon.)\nOf the horizon of that which human beings can know in all sciences in general.",[]],"refl:1974":["The horizon of cognitions according to age, sex, station or way of life. logically determined (above or within), practically (below or outside).\nTo find the horizon of my cognition. Not to alter it easily. To compare it with that of others and of the entire human race.\n(s 1. Objective: what we cannot or may not know.)",[]],"refl:1975":["(g Appropriateness) Congruence (g of the boundaries) of cognition [and] with the boundaries of human perfection (s of the ends of human beings: the actual or necessary) [is] obtains insofar as cognition, considered objectively, is adequate both to the cognitive faculty and to utility. And therefore the horizon is determined both logically and practically.\nIf the cognition is smaller than the logically determined horizon, [is of less, which is imperfection] it is nevertheless in itself possible for the human being; if it is greater, it is impossible; if it surpasses (g exceeds) use, it is practically impossible (g since it harms); if it is not sufficient for use or if it contributes little, it is practically possible, but is not adequate to practical necessity. The radius of the horizon is determined by the greatest utility according to one's powers.\n(s the horizon is determined practically through choice.)",[]],"refl:1976":["On the heading \nThe quantity of cognition as to degree is universality (reason);\nas to multitude is extensiveness (historical).\n(s The magnitude of cognition can be understood *materialiter* or *formaliter*; in the 1st case it is extensive or intensive. In the 2nd it is logical magnitude of universality *in abstracto* or aesthetic of fullness of thought *in concreto*.)",[]],"refl:1977":["Something goes above my horizon if it requires a greater radius than is in my faculty, a greater height of my point of view than lie in my faculty.\nSomething is, lies outside my horizon if it requires another standpoint than that in which I am placed.\nHorizon is the appropriateness of the magnitude of the collective cognitions to the capacities and ends of the subject.\nThe horizon will be different either as to magnitude or as to position, as to the standpoint.",[]],"refl:1978":["2. Ignorance of the grounds of cognition.\n3. Ignorance of the grounds of things. The common man asks: what something signifies, or what it is useful for; the philosopher: whence it comes, the cause; the rational one: from which grounds one accepts this cognition as true.",[]],"refl:1979":["The universal horizon, the particular with regard to certain kinds of human beings and ends, and the singular horizon for one head.\nMy horizon is not the measure with that of others.",[]],"refl:1980":["The outer extension of cognition: where the public also takes part. The inner: of the scholar.\nThe intensive magnitude loses in the former case. But that which at the same time harmonizes with quality, enlightenment, can thereby increase.\nAppropriateness to the (g necessary) ends of humanity: both for itself and of the citizen.",[]],"refl:1981":["On the heading \nMagnitude of domain. Multiplicity.\nMagnitude of content — many-validity.\nMultiplicity of cognition: extensive magnitude.\nDegree of cognition: intensive magnitude;\n*non multa, sed multum*.\n( s (g multitude) 1. manifoldness. 2. content. )",[]],"refl:1982":["I cannot, I may not, I should not know some things. The latter I can\n|\nnot say for the sake of uselessness. Below the horizon, but in order not to be a hindrance to other ends.",[]],"refl:1983":["Does not belong to logic, but to the architectonic of philosophy.",[]],"refl:1984":["Imperfection with regard to magnitude.\nof quality. form. either of lack or of error, potential or actual.\nDifference in magnitude. Lack or\ncompleteness\nDifference in quality. Error or truth\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n*respective* to the object.\nDifference [in form] in the *modus*: distinct, certain (*respective* to other cognitions).",[]],"refl:1985":["On the heading \nExtent and degree. e.g., in the kettle boiling water more warmth, but not greater.\nMany arguments, each of which proves nothing at all.\n(s Objective) universality is logical magnitude,\n(s Subjective) [manifoldness] aesthetic.\nDistinctness is ground of logical perfection.\nLiveliness is ground of aesthetic perfection.",[]],"refl:1986":["The quantity of cognition is either objective or subjective.\neach is either material or formal.",[]],"refl:1987":["*Amplitudo cognitionis* [breadth of cognition].\n*Intensitas cognitionis* [intensity of cognition].\nThe perfection as to quantity is *materialiter* [materially] extensiveness, *formaliter* [formally] universality; as to quality *materialiter* truth, *formaliter* distinctness and certainty.",[]],"refl:1988":["The mathematician has a large field of cognition (it is for him infinite). The philosopher a larger one of objects, namely also of the supersensible, of which he has no, at least no theoretical, cognition. For the supersensible of morality is capable only of practical cognition.",[]],"refl:1989":["On the heading\nConcerns the matter of cognition, thus does not belong to logic.\nOn L §. 41 beginning:\n(g in poetry) *nugaeque canorae* [and melodious trifles].\n*Splendida miseria* [splendid misery]. in contrast with the wealth, *opulentia* [opulence] of cognition.",[]],"refl:1990":["It is of great importance to determine for the cognition of the human being the boundaries, hence its *territorium* [territory], *sphaeram* [sphere].",[]],"refl:1991":["One must ask first not about usefulness but about perfection (g of cognition).\nHorizon of humanity in general (g whether through utility — division of labor in studying) and private horizon, *e.g.* of an estate, age, sex.",[]],"refl:1992":["Acquaintance with the horizon belongs to this, in order to determine the boundaries of one's cognition, and indeed according to principles. 1. What one cannot know*, in order to know how little it is that human beings can know. 2. What we, given our aim, need not know.** 3. Of which the cognition is dispensable and null for everyone. (g The various chimeras in religion.) (g What we ought not to know. Harmful to us.) (g What we ought not to brood over, e.g. the impure intentions of human beings or divine judgments.) demarcation |\nof our cognition. Ignorance is not disgraceful. Deliberate renunciation of various pieces of knowledge and leaving them to others is praiseworthy; of the distribution of the soil among various cultivators. Not contempt for what one does not need. *ars non habet osorem* [art has no enemy].\n*(g One must not assume this too hastily. And yet proceed according to the method.)\n**(g in religion the human being as human being (g esoteric, exoteric) needs only little; but here the aim is practical.)\n(g III. Whether the horizon of healthy reason limits that of science?\nThe horizon determined by others: men must not know everything.)\n(g 1) What can I know or not? 2. what may I or not? 3. what ought I or not? — The latter is no breaks off.)\n(s In youth one gathers up cognition, in old age one seeks to judge its worth.)\n(s In youth we cannot ourselves determine our horizon.)\n(s The horizon of rational cognition needs to be drawn according to *principia*, above all where experience cannot serve as a guide. *ars non habet osorem nisi ignorantem* [art has no enemy except the ignorant].)",[]],"refl:1993":["The horizon (g determined theoretically or practically) is either the universal one of humanity or the particular one, the latter either of popular cognition in general or of science.\n[Pr] Subjectively determined by sex, age, vocation.",[]],"refl:1994":["Private horizon and universal horizon.",[]],"refl:1995":["speculative self-cognition. It is determined: 1. logically according to the faculty, 2. aesthetically according to taste, 3. practically according to utility, interest of the understanding, of feeling and of the will.\nThe logically determined horizon: with regard to the A) objects 1. of historical cognition, 2. of rational, and of the latter: a. pure, b. physical. B) With regard to the subject: 1. private horizon, 2. universal one of human cognition (g of the species) in general.\n(s (theoretically) logically determined horizon, what we can know, practically: what we ought.)",[]],"refl:1996":["12'.\nL 12:\nAesthetically the horizon is determined (g popularity) through its |\nappropriateness to the good tone of society. |\nL 12': |\nThe good tone is either that of importance or court tone (g or politeness, obligingness) or that of flattery; [the former makes the] the latter makes the gallant man; the same combined with the last: *petit maître*. French omniscience. Is as it were the drawing in bird's-eye perspective: surface.",[]],"refl:1997":["The boundaries of the empirical cognition of the whole human species cannot indeed be determined *a priori*, but naturally it has boundaries. The historians after 1000 years cannot know as much as we now, and on top of that what has happened in the 1000 years. The observer of nature perhaps also. The mathematician will perhaps not be able to grasp all the already existing inventions in one head and thus be able to invent nothing of which he would know for certain it had not already been invented before. But then everything will rest on the invention of methods (in natural history) and *principia*, through which we, without burdening memory, can ourselves find everything at will. Hence he makes himself deserving of history like a genius who grasps it under ideas that can always remain. Otherwise if just as much must be forgotten as is added, cognition will no longer grow, or indeed decrease for lack of encouragement to new cognitions.",[]],"refl:1998":["(s Horizon of the healthy understanding or of speculative reason.)\nOne must determine one's horizon early, not change it lightly (g always expand it). [Skill. practical horizon.] Not measure the horizon of others by one's own (g division of labors) and not hold that to be useless which is of no use to us (g pleases; *ars non habet osorem*). Seek to determine in advance the horizon of the whole human species (according to past and future time). e.g. history. Seek to determine for one's science its place in the horizon of the whole of cognition (g and to unite it with others). *Encyclopaedia universalis*. Universal chart. (g *Mappemonde* of the sciences.)\nThere is no need to fear what d'Alembert fears. With the expansion of mathematics new methods will be invented which shorten the old and make the multitude of books dispensable. Us[burden] it is not the load that oppresses us, but the *volumen* narrows for us the space for our cognitions. Critique of reason, of history and historical writings, a universal spirit which [at the] goes to human cognition *en gros* and not merely in *détail*, will always make the domain smaller without diminishing anything in the content; merely the dross falls away from the metal, or the less noble vehicle or covering, which was so long necessary.",[]],"refl:1999":["On the heading\nQuantity — extensive (cognition spread either internally in one human being or externally among many human beings);\nintensive: the degree, i.e. the magnitude as ground of many and great sequels.\nThe sphere of the cognition of a human being: whether something is outside or within it and in general whether the human being keeps himself within his |\nsphere (of cognition or business) or goes beyond it. That is distinguished from the horizon of the human being, what he (g ought,) can and may know. The latter concerns the faculty. The former the end. — Logically —, aesthetically — and practically determined horizon. — Only the first belongs here.",[]],"refl:2000":["On the heading\nHere the [perfection] quantity of a cognition in general is spoken of, insofar as one abstracts from the [quality] specific difference of it, whether it be empirical or rational.",[]],"refl:2001":["The horizon of opinions must not concern pure truths of reason. Only empirically grounded propositions. The horizon of knowledge is with regard to speculative cognition unnecessary for the practical, but the practical one must know. The horizon of belief concerns only the defense of the practical that is attacked by speculation.",[]],"refl:2002":["Because of the limitations of the understanding there always remains ignorance.\nThis is *formaliter* twofold: a learned and a common ignorance.\n[The latter: when one] The 1st. When one distinctly has insight into the limitations of cognition, thus the field of ignorance, from where it begins (g thus knows the magnitude and causes of the ignorance). e.g. A surveyor knows how to determine by how many feet he can be mistaken in taking a certain distance. A philosopher knows where ignorance begins because of lack of the given pieces [and of the lack]. e.g. A philosopher proves how little we can know with regard to the structure of gold, in accordance with art.\nThe 2nd is unconcernedly ignorant, and without his having insight into [bound] the grounds and boundaries of the ignorance.\n*Materialiter*: ignorance, disdainful, of objects altogether too sublime (s enthusiasts extol ignorance, theological enthusiasts as well as genius-enthusiasts) or of those that are necessary and worthy and capable of cognition.\nA woman who has studied is not ashamed not to know the logical figures.",[]],"refl:2003":["Blameless (s *inculpabilis*, not *meritoria*): those that are above our horizon.",[]],"refl:2004":["misology.\nOn L §. 50 »praiseworthy ignorance«:\nwith regard to the secret malice of human beings. misanthropy.",[]],"refl:2005":["voluntary.\nFurther, at the end of L §. 50, from β1−ε2:\npraiseworthy.",[]],"refl:2006":["Learned ignorance: when one has insight in a learned way why we do not know something. e.g. That it is not certain that bodies exist.",[]],"refl:2007":["Not ignorance, but the acquaintance with one's ignorance, the |\nmodesty of the opinion of one's knowledge. which does not puff up. What Socrates knew is praiseworthy; this acquaintance presupposes science.",[]],"refl:2008":["With regard to the speculative use of our faculties of cognition no ignorance is praiseworthy, but at most permitted, because it is, although not above, yet outside our horizon.",[]],"refl:2009":["Not knowing and ignoring something, i.e. taking no notice of it, is a difference. It is good to ignore much that it is not good for us to know. (s many a hidden evil (g impurity of heart) of human beings or bad gossip.) (s what others say to our disadvantage (Socrates: we will act so that no one believes that man).) From both is further distinguished: abstracting, i.e. when one ignores the (g still suspends acquaintance with something) application, whereby one gets the cognition *in abstracto* and can better consider it in the universal as *principium*.\n(g It is (g for a prince) in conformity with duty to ignore what belongs to the private life of his subjects, private opinions etc.)",[]],"refl:2010":["At the end of L §. 51:\nA shameful ignorance 1. of such things as are very necessary to him and also easy to know.",[]],"refl:2011":["In a theoretical respect much ignorance is *inculpabel* [blameless], but none *laudabel* [praiseworthy]. In the practical, because of the wrong that we might do others through inquiring, or of inner corruption, often *laudabel*.",[]],"refl:2012":["Historically ignorant are commonly [speculatively learned] teachers of reason.\n*idiota* and *sophista*.\n( s ignorant and philosopher. The former can be a sophist; idiot and philologist. The former can be a humanist; *laicus* and *clericus*.) s in both, skill is distinguished from critical science. Craft from art. )\n(s Limitations and boundaries.\nA scholar without science.)",[]],"refl:2013":["One can never represent one's ignorance otherwise than through science, just as a blind person cannot represent darkness until he has become sighted.\n(s Socrates. The ignorant one does not even know that he knows nothing.)",[]],"refl:2014":["What we have said of the domain (g sphere) of human knowledge and within it of the determination of the sphere of each one, belongs properly in the scholia.",[]],"refl:2015":["Such a one is like a nobleman who seeks only to ac|quire\nmany estates but is in debt on all of them, or like a traveler who travels through the whole earth in a few years.",[]],"refl:2016":["The excess in this is called learned windbaggery, charlatanry, excessive addiction to wide reading.\nFurther from µ−ψ:\nphilology: the science of the instruments of erudition.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\nCritique of the sciences diminishes pride.\nFurther L 15' from β1:\n*pansophia*.\nFurther from ρ−ψ:\npolymathy concerns rational cognition.\nFurther from φ (? µ−ξ?):\npolyhistory puffs up.",[]],"refl:2017":["Shallow age: where science becomes universal. and the public judges in Latin.",[]],"refl:2018":["(s *Humaniora* — barbaric erudition.)\n(s What is erudition and a scholar? not philosopher, but yet in the faculty significance, man of letters.)\nAll erudition is either barbaric or rational without the superintendence of philosophy.\nThe barbarism of erudition is grounded on tradition, superstition etc etc., and in our time is still to be met with above all in history, either because it does not offer philosophy a helping hand, or because it is bound by theology.\nbarbaric erudition can contain much industry, but without end, without idea above all for the best of the human species.\n[Another fault is the]\nBarbarism is not abolished through elegance; for this belongs not to erudition but to taste and to culture. Rather it is a decay of all erudition when, instead of knowledge, playing gains ground in fables, poesies. It concerns only the manner of things.\n( s *Humaniora**. Refinement. Politeness. Urbanity. rusticity. *\ng (s Acquaintance with the ancients.) Which promote the union of\nscience with taste, grind off roughness\nand promote the communicability of human beings,\nin which humanity consists.\n)",[]],"refl:2019":["Historical erudition — science of reason.\nPhilologist is the connoisseur of the instruments of erudition: languages and books.\nCritical philologists.",[]],"refl:2020":["A Cyclops is the mathematician, historian, describer of nature, philologist and linguist who is great in all these pieces and holds all philosophy about them to be dispensable.\nPolyhistory: cyclopean erudition.\n(s Belletrist — bel-esprit-ism.)",[]],"refl:2021":["Cyclopean scholar: who lacks one eye, above all philosophy, with great science otherwise, and yet decides about everything.",[]],"refl:2022":["The humanist (belletrist), *bel esprit*.\nThe linguist.\nThe *litterator*.\nThe philologist. (g *pertinet vel ad eruditionem vel ad humanitatem vel ad cognitionem rationalem* [pertains either to erudition or to humanity or to rational cognition].)\nThe polyhistor. Scaliger. Cyclopean erudition.\nThe *pansophus*.\n[The philosopher] The universal spirit. Philosopher. Mathematician. *historicus*. Poet. Orator. Properly the architect of the sciences.\nErudition and taste from works in dead languages.",[]],"refl:2023":["Architectonic of the sciences, which considers their kinship and systematic combination of them in a whole of the cognition that interests humanity. Is philosophical and thus not polyhistory. A system according to ideas. Encyclopedia distinguished from it. Compendium.",[]],"refl:2024":["7. *Artes*. Logic, arithmetic, dialectic. Astronomy, [Orator] poesy, rhetoric and music.\n9 Muses and 3 Graces.\nWork and\nplay.\nThe sciences serve either immediately for agreeableness or for gain. The former: liberal arts, the latter: bread-art.",[]],"refl:2025":["1, All [sciences] instruction belongs either to humanity or to science (s skill); the former contains the liberal arts (s *ars ingenua, liberalis*), which merely civilize (s please for themselves) and [one on taste moral along with a] ground the agreeableness [of the in the recip.] of the reciprocal communication of human beings. They are distinguished from bread-arts (s *ars mercenaria — servilis*), which are only means [to the s] to our self-interested ends.\n2, To science (s *vel habitus vel scientia*) belong historical or rational science. The sum total of the historical is erudition (s polyhistory — polymathy.) [Bread-art and liberal art] [The instr] The sum total of the instruments of erudition is philology. Consequently [this] the philologist contains the linguist [the humanist] (s and) the *litterator*. (g his opposite is the belletrist.) (s and philology belongs to science. Humanistic study to liberal art.) Learned languages and books in all sciences. The [acquaint] historical acquaintance with all sciences is polyhistory.\n3, The sum total of rational cognition is (s not erudition, i.e. science which must be taught to one, but which each can himself invent) philosophy and mathematics. Polymathy.\n(s The formation through the latter is that of the way of thinking.)\nUniversal head, [with] driven by vanity, makes the pansophist.\n(g Ancient models of good style (g and acquaintance with the world) or merely the[acquaintance] skill of becoming acquainted with the ancients. Englishmen. [Refin] Culture of the healthy understanding and of taste.\nPainstakingness (punctiliousness) with regard to order with neglect of the end.\ncyclopean erudition without philosophy.)",[]],"refl:2026":["All instruction is either in art or in science. The first is either bread-art or liberal art. The former is craft. Liberal art is that which no one can himself produce moved by wages, in which therefore the mind is entirely free, i.e. art of genius; of this kind are properly only the arts of the mind and not of bodily use.",[]],"refl:2027":["The polyhistor\nas philologist [is human] cultivates,\nas humanist\ncivilizes.\nAs philologist he is linguist and *literator*,\nas humanist he is classicist and their interpreter.\nBelletrist is an ape of the humanist.\nthe humanist according to (g contemporary) models [to se] in living languages is no scholar. Only dead languages are now learned languages.\n(g To culture belongs that of *habitus* or *scientia*.)\nThe philologist is distinguished from the humanist in that the former seeks among the ancients the instruments of erudition, the second the instruments of the formation of taste.\nThe belletrist* is no scholar (g connoisseur), but a *dilettante* of the cognitions of taste according to fashion. He does not need the ancients. He |\nis a humanist of the contemporary writings of taste (s or rather the ape of the humanist).\n*(g *Institutio* (g methodical culture) *pertinet vel ad humanitatem vel ad* (g *scientiam*) *eruditionem latius dictam* [instruction pertains either to humanity or to (science) erudition in the broader sense].)",[]],"refl:2028":["It comes down to sufficiency with regard to multitude and degree. *Extensionis et intensionis* [of extension and of intension].",[]],"refl:2029":["Order in studying.\nWhat you can do today, do not put off until tomorrow.",[]],"refl:2030":["*Ars longa, vita brevis*. Hippocrates.\nMotive.\n1.\nTo let useless trifles go,\n2.\nto let no time [go] pass by for the acquisition of an important cognition.",[]],"refl:2031":["how to seem learned in discourses. in order to earn bread.",[]],"refl:2032":["Of the people who do not forget much. *Item* of those who forget altogether too much.\nThe extensiveness of cognition can become a cause of forgetfulness if it is had insight into without the order of reason, without reflection and without those marks which are had insight into through a precise attention; then [something] much can be forgotten and yet much remains.",[]],"refl:2033":["On the concluding sentence of L §. 61:\nOne who learns music forgets those pieces which he learned at first, but he retains the facility of playing. Thus after one has lost from memory the particular pieces of cognition, there remains a facility of thinking further according to the manner of the cognition once acquired.\nYet one must guard as much as possible against a negligence in attention and in the faculty of recollection.",[]],"refl:2034":["e.g. The science of philosophy and mathematics in its whole domain.\nOn L §. 62 No. 2:\nThis is either the natural or acquired capacity.",[]],"refl:2035":["If this principal science has a slight influence neither on cognition nor on use, then one must combine it with other sciences, in which one must likewise seek to become strong.\nFurther from µ−φ:\nnot bread-art, but principal business.\nThe principal science in an economic or learned aim.\nFurther from µ−φ:\nCyclopean erudition. The one eye must always be philosophy. Subsidiary science belonging to humanity and to the connection of scholars.\nFurther from φ (ω?):\nTheology (g as *disciplina principalis*) has the most auxiliary sciences (g *subsidiarias*).",[]],"refl:2036":["If the principal science is not great enough to serve as point of reference, then he must apply it as much as possible to the aid of others.",[]],"refl:2037":["(s The misuse of a small cognition: when one holds it [for] in all things to be perfect.)\nExample in Faustus.\nPedantry: when one always brings in that which one knows (s from lack of pieces of knowledge), without regard to where it is fitting or has utility.\nThere are many kinds of pedants. War pedants, state pedants, finery and gallantry pedants. Of the *Rep: Sabinensi*. School pedants. e.g. He spoke: o ornament etc etc. A strict connoisseur etc.: the wine which etc etc.\nIn medicine. When he cures only according to Hippocrates; in jurisprudence: when he judges according to the *placitis clarorum Jurisconsultorum* [opinions of famous jurists]. Theological pedant: when he always cites *patres*.\nCharlatanry: when this use of one's limited knowledge occurs out of arrogance.\nPedantry is moreover also taken *objective*, when it signifies a useless brooding.\nOne must not call something pedantry whose utility one does not know. e.g. many logical *principia*. Example. The drilling of soldiers.\nCorruption of the present time: that one raises no learned, but yet aesthetic pedants.\nOne must apply oneself to sciences of which one can make use in discourse. e.g. Recent history. Geography. *Jus naturae*. Morals.\n(s Learned *petits maîtres*, who pass off a [learned] common cognition as a learned one.)\n(s Popularity in book-writing, as to content as well as to language (lack of the Latin parliament because of codex).)",[]],"refl:2038":["Precision and assiduity with regard to cognition which is only a means, without skill in making it appropriate to its ends.\nWhen someone makes not the use of his skill conform to the occasions, but these to the display of his skill.\nA lack of the healthy [and pr] understanding [and of the] (s with regard to the use of erudition). As it were skill which is not directed by prudence in application.\n(s Expert and amateur.)",[]],"refl:2039":["18'.\nL 18:\nThe punctiliousness of rules [or prescriptions] is pedantry. Practical rules are not always [laws i.e. *a priori* sufficient] prescriptions or norms. The action is not good or right because it is in conformity with them; rather they are universal directions of the [mean] way in which that which is right and good is to be attained. But to practice belongs healthy understanding and thus power of judgment, (*in concreto*) the rules e.g. to make out the braid for the hat. School-knowledge. School-fox. Calligraphy.\nL 18':\nHe who takes an empirical rule or rule of necessity for a law is a pedant.",[]],"refl:2040":["Cognition, how it can be communicated universally (g *dilettante*) or [also t] only to connoisseurs.\nPopularity according to the content or to the manner.\n(s Great world. School or *toilette*.)\nPopularity, when it becomes universal, becomes the cause of shallowness.\n*malim convivis quam placuisse coquis* [I would rather please the guests than the cooks].\nPedantry concerns only the exit of cognition from the school into the world.\nGallantry is the courting of the approval of taste, affectation.\nScholars by profession or by taste.\nGuild scholars\n|\nGuild-free\n(g The French are the great masters of popularity, Germans the least and fail in affected gallantry.)\n(g Popularity consists in this, that participation in a science be not business but play.)",[]],"refl:2041":["Of the rules (g and conditions) of the popularity of a learned cognition, e.g. of the philosophers. When one makes nothing [oth] philosophical from another point of view than because it interests the amateur or even only social intercourse. e.g. Prejudices, acquaintance with human beings, weather, ghosts, birthmarks. *Aer fixus* in champagne and pyromancy. Popularity in materials or in methods. The latter is a work of genius. The scholastic philosopher must have much understanding, but as a popular one much practical power of judgment and acquaintance with human beings through social intercourse.\nPopularity with regard to the understanding or to taste.",[]],"refl:2042":["One must not in society repeat and recite one's school lesson, but make use of the skill that is acquired through this lesson. A certain form belongs merely to learning and falls away in practice, which, if it is retained, is called pe|dantry.\nThe constraint of rules, insofar as it shines through, is pedantry.\n(s The punctiliousness of scholastic perfection in the free use of the understanding.)\n(s Popularity is the common utility of cognition with regard to community.\nThe professional or the amateur (g *dilettante*, connoisseur of art) of science. making universal.)",[]],"refl:2043":["The pedant is the slave of rules.\nThe *galant homme* is the freethinker of rules.\n(s Addiction to genius is the freethinking of rules.)\n(s *Magister pedaneus*.)",[]],"refl:2044":["The way of teaching or learning a science is either (s correct for the school) art-expert or (s subject-expert) popular (g esoteric or exoteric); the first is correct for the school, the second (g of common utility) condescending. The restriction (g narrowings) of cognition through the constraint of the school is pedantry (when one does not make it universally comprehensible or pleasing); the shallowness of cognition through popularity is the gallantry* of style. Acquaintance with the world, insofar as it moderates school constraint without loss of thoroughness, is the courtly.\n*(s or rather from taste and refinement of the world. Out of gallantry to botch cognition. Exactitude of method — thorough and popular. For school and world.)",[]],"refl:2045":["The scholars, not indeed by craft, are the *depositaires* of all cognition and science, with which the great world plumes itself [and guild-free]. They would soon run wild into bears, as indeed they were before, if school industry slackened. Guild-free scholars can only be formed by guild members, and all science receives only in the hands of the latter its precise determination or *praecision*, its order and distinctness, its auxiliary means, the domain and boundaries. Acquaintance with the world promotes only the application of it appropriate to circumstances, which is either courtly or gallant. The condescension of erudition merely to the simplicity of common healthy reason, without flattering either pride or the[comfort] refinement of taste, is the greatest effect of the power of judgment. Utility of polyhistory, in order to know how a science borrows auxiliary means from others. Gesner's *isagoge*.",[]],"refl:2046":["Pedantry is opposed to acquaintance with the world, which [a] is merely an extended cognition of the fitting use of science, thus an expanded power of judgment.",[]],"refl:2047":["18'.\nor 2. Gallantry. (s *petimaetrise*.) Thoroughness and *politie*. (s *ostentatio erudita*, |\nL 18': |\n*fastus*.)",[]],"refl:2048":["The lack of popularity from habituation to what is correct for the school is pedantry. The lack of the natural (*bon sens*) from striving to be artificial is affectation. The surprising manner of the natural, which approaches art, is naïveté.\n(s He who cannot communicate otherwise than correctly for the school is a pedant.)",[]],"refl:2049":["Pedantry: the universal in abstract formulas.\nPopularity: the universal in experience.\nGallantry: for universal taste.\nCharlatanry: empty erudition and pomp of wide reading.",[]],"refl:2050":["There are methods which are necessary only for learning but fall away in application and in life. These are correct for the school.",[]],"refl:2051":["Acquaintance with the world and school wisdom. Healthy understanding.",[]],"refl:2052":["The appropriateness of cognition to the *object* is scholastic perfection (s thoroughness).\nThe appropriateness for all *subjects* is popularity. The striving for scholastic perfection to the disadvantage of a possible popularity is pedantry.",[]],"refl:2053":["Pedantry is erudition without any popularity.",[]],"refl:2054":["The *bel esprit* apes the tone of the scholars and becomes a chatterer, the scholar the tone of gallantry and becomes a pedant.",[]],"refl:2055":["The go-cart of rules or the compass.",[]],"refl:2056":["Besides the objective perfection of cognition it must also have the property of communicating itself as easily (g and universally) as is possible. If science is restricted only to the use of learners and yet steps into the world before amateurs, then it is pedantic.",[]],"refl:2057":["Conscendent to erudition. Condescendent to common concepts.",[]],"refl:2058":["*Lenocinia orationis* [enticements of speech]. Coquettish beauty. *veneres*.",[]],"refl:2059":["Popularity is the skill (also way of thinking) of accommodating oneself to the interest and capacity of others. Popularity in lecture is the talent of accommodating one's knowledge to the interest and capacity of common reason (or of mere amateurs). Such a philosophy presupposes acquaintance with the human being and clear insight, in order, even without its craft-tools of school expressions and methods, to [obtain] communicate thorough insight and as it were to play, so that science costs no effort, and to bring science into the tone of society.\n(s Boasting and playing. Charlatanry and *petit maître*.)",[]],"refl:2060":["The master in formalities is a pedant. Punctiliousness in formalities: pedantry.\nPuffed-up erudition without manners of social intercourse: pedantry.\nThe pedant stands out most in intercourse with women. |\nHe is full of formality and school method. Pedantry in the soldier's estate.",[]],"refl:2061":["17.\nL 17':\nThe human being is a head (*ingenium*) who thinks for himself.\nThe human being is a brush who merely imitates, as it were a mere *instrument* which another makes use of to copy his thoughts.\nIn the latter [exactit] exactitude degenerates into punctiliousness (s micrology).\n(s Pedantry and gallantry in arts and sciences. Manner of representation.)\n(s Micrology in formalities. Pedantism. Affected thoroughness. and affected popularity. (s Gallantry.) Scholastic or popular.)\nTo thoroughness belongs a methodical head; brooding punctiliousness makes a formality-picker. Brooding punctiliousness (s useless precision) in formalities (s school formulas and terminologies) is pedantry ((g as *Criticus*) formality-sniffer).\nPurposive exactitude in formalities is thoroughness.\n(s Freedom of the way of thinking from the constraint of formalities.)\n(s Guild scholars — guild-free.)\n(s *Magister pedaneus*: the formal element of school method outside the school.)\n(s Muses and graces. Scholastic and popular.)\nPedant is a formality-picker (like word-picker), formality-brooder (g -monger), (g formality-carver (*Logodaedalus*)). He is limited with regard to the [spirit] kernel of things, looks [he] only at |\nthe garment and the shell. He is the unsuccessful imitation or caricature of the methodical head.\nThe ceremonial at courts, also in social intercourse: what is it other than hunting and picking formalities? in the military it is not completely so, although it seems so. But in conversation [is], in clothing, in diet, in religion there is often much pedantry; |\nL 17: |\nthe old form and cut cannot easily be changed.",[]],"refl:2062":["The pedant is either as scholar opposed to the man of the world and is the puffed-up scholar without acquaintance with the world, i.e. acquaintance with the way of bringing his science to people. — Or he is [the C] as the man of skill in general, but only in [regar] formalities, not according to essence and end, opposed to the useful. Matter is always useful.",[]],"refl:2063":["Two degenerations of the prevailing taste with regard to the sciences:\n1. when they are pursued merely for the school: *doctor umbraticus* (s *graeculus*),\n2. merely for social intercourse or the world.\nPedantry and gallantry. The first: limitedness with regard to use, the second: with regard to content.\nScholastic thoroughness and at the same time popularity.\nIf the refined world is the supreme judge, then everything becomes shallow and fickle.",[]],"refl:2064":["Purposive precision in formalities is thoroughness (scholastic perfection, correct for the school). (g Un)purposive [P] (contrapurposive) precision, pedantry. This is taken only relatively.",[]],"refl:2065":["Magnitude is distinguished from extensiveness. The latter consists in manifoldness, the former however in the multitude of sequels. e.g. A pension of one thousand @ French is much smaller than of some 100 @ English.",[]],"refl:2066":["The object of the cognition of the acquaintance with antiquities is indeed great with regard to extensiveness, but not with regard to fruitfulness.\nBut mathematics is both.",[]],"refl:2067":["The magnitude of the sequels must not always be measured by bodily utility.",[]],"refl:2068":["If a harvest has yielded much grain and weighty grain then it is great.",[]],"refl:2069":["The art of calculation.",[]],"refl:2070":["Cognition can be great according to that which it comprehends under itself, or also according to that which it contains in itself. The former, the universal, is often (g in)determinate for use in particular cases. The cognition which concerns the great, i.e. the whole in the use of the understanding, is to be distinguished from subtlety in the small, micrology. (s Latin books.)\nThe lack which does [merely] not contradict the end is no fault. Hence one must avoid [perhaps] putting forward a great aim, so that the lack does not afterwards become a fault.\nOne must not without need pretend to a complete concept, a demonstration.",[]],"refl:2071":["Great according to matter or form. *non multa, sed multum* [not many things, but much].\nMany kinds.\nHe who goes from the great to the small.",[]],"refl:2072":["Absolute magnitude or that with regard to the sequels. (Importance.)\nAbsolute magnitude either of content (g mathematical) or of domain (logical magnitude).\nThe technical or architectonic magnitude.",[]],"refl:2073":["18'.\nL 18:\nOf the degree of cognition and of logical importance, not practical.\nL 18':\nLogically important is every cognition which promotes logical perfection as to form. e.g. every mathematical proposition, every new distinctly understood law of nature or philosophical definition of something. Practical importance one cannot foresee, but must wait for.",[]],"refl:2074":["The invention can be great with regard to the power of the mind or to the sequels. Difficulty or importance.\nImportant objects make no important cognitions.",[]],"refl:2075":["What the author calls learned cognition should be scientific, opposed to the common, *dignitas* and *foecunditas* — Mathematics cultivates in itself, but is yet also a good instrument.",[]],"refl:2076":["1) [In the subject] with regard to the *objects* extensively or intensively greater cognition.\n2. with regard to the *subjects*. extensively: to many human beings (through living languages), or intensively: through insight, greater [cognition] en|lightenment,\ncognition of what belongs to one's true and necessary ends.",[]],"refl:2077":["It is a great fault when one wants to know at once the sequels in each part of cognition. One must do it as the miners do.\nA cognition without important sequels is called a brooding, e.g. scholastic philosophy.",[]],"refl:2078":["This magnitude is called the degree; the previous one was called extensiveness.\nFurther L 19':\nThis is the first thing to which one must apply oneself in practice.",[]],"refl:2079":["On the concluding sentence of L §. 70:\nBecause cognition is otherwise kept from what is important.",[]],"refl:2080":["Of the fatherland of Homer.",[]],"refl:2081":["Sometimes it contains much in itself, but our cognition is not proportioned to the same. *e.g.* Of the intermediate state between death and resurrection.",[]],"refl:2082":["Many trifles, when they are combined, one must not take for trifles.",[]],"refl:2083":["The acquaintance with Celtic antiquities. Of the *marmoribus*. Runes.",[]],"refl:2084":["The casuistical morals.\nEdifying thoughts on the broomstick of Swift. dedication of his treatise on making gold out of dung to Woodward.",[]],"refl:2085":["Whoever reflects just as zealously on the place of the human soul as on immortality, he does not think proportionately enough. *Ars longa, vita brevis.*\nGreat in the small. and small in the great. cit: Haller.",[]],"refl:2086":["In which he is capable of the cognition of the greatest scholars.",[]],"refl:2087":["But one can guide and form the faculty of judging.",[]],"refl:2088":["or upon one another.",[]],"refl:2089":["A mind: 1. a great one,\n2. a strong one,\n3. a beautiful one,\n4. a small one.\nJust this gives to cognize that one should take that for small which a great mind takes for small; is deceptive.",[]],"refl:2090":["A small mind: whose cognition is not proportioned to the things.",[]],"refl:2091":["Example in metaphysics.",[]],"refl:2092":["Electricity.Top. Magnet.",[]],"refl:2093":["The perverse application of the proposition [of contradiction] of sufficient ground, of the best world, of the *principii indiscernibilium*.\nO adornment of the best world etc etc.\nFurther L 21' from β1−κ3:\nE.g. Mathematical method.",[]],"refl:2094":["*e.g.* The philosophical cobbler.\nThe venerable and greatly estimable *metaphysica*.\nNext to L §. 82 »werden — verachtungs«:\nFundamental crotchets.",[]],"refl:2095":["One must not be subtle in trifles.",[]],"refl:2096":["The invention of the air pump Algebra Kepler's harmonic relations\n}\n}\n}\nthereto those who collect *observationes*. *e.g.* meteorological ones.",[]],"refl:2097":["That imperfection which arises from a voluntary neglect of duty is called a disgrace. Our duty is to apply our powers of understanding.",[]],"refl:2098":["Important for science, but not for the dilettantes.",[]],"refl:2099":["In the doctrine of the human soul the generation of human beings and animals is an intermediate truth.",[]],"refl:2100":["Toil is commonly proper to the investigations of sublime truths.",[]],"refl:2101":["*e.g.* those verses that can be read forwards and backwards, *anagrammata*.\n*e.g.* the fallacies of the skeptics. Of the ball that is supposed to be forced through a hole etc. The liar. The dreamer.\nFurther L 22 from ψ−θ:\n*nugae difficiles*.",[]],"refl:2102":["Many trifles make something great. *e.g.* in history.\nAll universal truths are not trifles.",[]],"refl:2103":["1. According to difference of aim. It is with regard to the law of nations no great investigation what ceremonials the envoys of each nation have, but in respect of political prudence it is important.\n2. According to difference of sequences.\nOn the concluding sentence of L §. 89:\nIt can have no great sequences, but yet many sequences. E.g. the rules of decorum.\nWhat always occurs, even if in each case it were of only little use, is yet of much use.",[]],"refl:2104":["Something can be not great in respect of bodily utility and yet is so in respect of the understanding.\nLanguage.",[]],"refl:2105":["In respect of the theologians it is an indifferent investigation whether the souls of children take their origin from the souls of the parents, but not in respect of philosophy.",[]],"refl:2106":["If a mind has an exceptional greatness, then it comprehends in the domain of its cognition trifles as well.\n*e.g.* Leibniz.\nThis is not to be advised to everyone.",[]],"refl:2107":["On the beginning of L §. 92:\nit is a cognition *subjective*, but not *objective*.\n*e.g.* Ptolemy's world-system.\nOn the second sentence of L §. 92:\nThere are learned and common falsities and *formaliter* or *materialiter* false. *e.g.* thus [*Harmonia praestabilita*] restoration of all things is a learned falsity, but ghosts a common one.\nFurtherL 23':\n*formaliter* it is false if something seems to be learned and is yet common. *e.g.* the *causae aeqvivocae* of the *Scholasticorum*, the *species visibiles*.\nThe plastic power, entelechy, *lusus naturae*.",[]],"refl:2108":["On the beginning of L §. 93:\n*Convenientia cognitionis cum subjecto.* E.g. The immortality of the soul.\nOnSentence 2 of L §. 93:\nThe knowledge of the simple parts of bodies.\nOf the cause of the winds.",[]],"refl:2109":["The inner cognitions of incorrectness rest on the contradiction of concepts among one another or the lack of inter|connection;\nthe outer: if it contradicts other things or has been determined by outer grounds, which are a mark of incorrectness.",[]],"refl:2110":["Criterion: means of separation.",[]],"refl:2111":["We have of the truth that a God is inner and outer marks. *item*: Of the Christian religion.\nbut of the incorrectness of the proposition: the highest good consists in voluptuousness, we have inner and outer marks.",[]],"refl:2112":["Ariosto fabulates that everything that human beings have lost here on earth is to be found again there in the moon; this lacks inner possibility.\nThe eagle that flew about in the air as a machine lacked inner possibility.\nBut the story of ghosts, likewise of the exorcists, has an inner possibility.\nThe story between swallows and sparrow, but not the story of the stopped-up young.",[]],"refl:2113":["The possibility of the opposite is a mark of the arbitrary in judgment.",[]],"refl:2114":["The possibility or impossibility of cognition is either *objectiva* or *subjectiva*; the first is determined by the impossibility of the object, the second by the impossibility of the representation of the same. *subjective* the certain cognition of the inhabitants in the moon is impossible, *objective*, however, the opinion of purgatory.\n*Absolute*: to know future things beforehand. Hypothetically: to know the figure of the first particles of matter.\nWhat is *subjective* false can be *objective* true, *e.g.* a dream.",[]],"refl:2115":["If *objectum* is possible, then the cognition is also possible *in sensu absoluto*.",[]],"refl:2116":["The practice of the rules of Christianity, says Bayle, are possible *in abstracto*, to be sure, but not in a prince. The proposal of the Abbé Pierre was impossible in interconnection.\n(s E.g. The proposition. There is a God, is a sequence of correct grounds and a ground of correct sequences.)\nIt is possible in interconnection to see the mountains in the moon. If therefore someone cognizes their existence, then the cognition is possible; it was, however, impossible in former times.",[]],"refl:2117":["This is an outer mark *a posteriori*.",[]],"refl:2118":["(g *Canones*)\n[Something] a cognition can, taken in and for itself, be possible, but it is not possible in interconnection. *e.g.* republic of women.",[]],"refl:2119":["If something has no sequences, it is not on that account impossible.",[]],"refl:2120":["Something can be possible in itself, but is not possible in interconnection. l. It is ungrounded. *e.g.* ghosts; that there are inhabitants in the earth. 2. false sequences. *e.g.* If the world is a necessary being, then it cannot be altered.",[]],"refl:2121":["Where we perceive no inner impossibility, there the possibility is probable.\n(s If we discover no inner possibility, then it is improbable.)\nIf we see nothing that outwardly contradicts it, then the outer possibility is probable.\nWhere we assume no ground either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, there the cognition is arbitrary.\n(s Subjective taking-to-be-true.)",[]],"refl:2122":["It is *e.g.* not impossible that the earth perish through a comet.\nOnL §. 98 No. 2:\nWe do not have insight into the possibility of the attracting of the magnet.\nOnL §. 98 No. 3:\nE.g. Because it is possible, it is not on that account at once true.\nFor a long time one did not have insight into the groundlessness of sympathy.\nL 24'. On L §. 98 No. 4:\nE.g. Immortality of the soul.",[]],"refl:2123":["Onthe heading \nThe marks of truth are objective or subjective. [The latter eith] the former rational or empirical.",[]],"refl:2124":["On the beginning of L §. 92:\nTruth and falsity is only in judgments.\nIt agrees with the object if it agrees with itself.",[]],"refl:2125":["If one gives someone a confused or unlearned cognition and he is to make it learned, but he gives it back just the same: then this deception is a falsity *qvoad formale*. as e.g. in *qvalitatibus occultis*. *Circuli* in definitions.",[]],"refl:2126":["What is truth. [Because] This proposition is answerable only through such rules as already presuppose that I can distinguish the true from the false.\nIf I seek the means to prevent incorrectness, then I do not yet demand the means to attain truth. but first of all to ignorance. Just as with the winnowing shovel I do not demand to increase the wheat, but to remove the chess.\nUntruth is a hindrance to a cognition, but not ig|norance;\nthere belongs, to remove it, so that first of all 0 may be, an [even] action of a true cognition.\nShould not in philosophy error and truth reckoned into one another and subtracted from one another give the 0 just as the grossest ignorance?",[]],"refl:2127":["What is universally valid is also valid for me (ground of moral judgments). What is to be valid for my reason must be universally valid (ground of the drive to communicate one's cognition to others [ih], as it were logical experiment, and unfairness of the prohibition against writing in matters of religion what one thinks).\nFalsity arises when the judgment according to subjective laws of reason or other powers of cognition is taken for an objective one.\nA judgment is logical through which I express a constitution of the object.\naesthetic: if it contains a sensation.\nTruth is the agreement of cognition with the object (through the cognition of the same), thus with itself.\nSensation is no judgment and is never false.\nThe true judgment must indicate not my own sensation, i.e. my state, but the constitution of the object and therefore be universally valid.",[]],"refl:2128":["Truth.\nThe first question in respect of the same is: whether the representation be a *sensation* (whether its object be actual).\nThe 2nd question in respect of the same is: whether the representation be a cognition (whether its object be possible).\nThe 3rd question in respect of the same is: whether the representation be a cognition of an already given cognition (truth of judgments).\nThere is thus 1, the question of the object and its relation to the representation, without comparing the representation with itself. For if the cause of the representation is subjective, then it lies either in the imagination of an outer object or in the form of thinking every [outer] object under a certain shape.\nThe objective *criterium* of truth is the agreement of the [cognitions] representations in a judgment among one another according to [the] universal laws of the understanding and of reason. I.e. [immediately or by means of] through intuitions or concepts.\nThe subjective *criterium* of truth is the agreement of one judgment with others both in the same subject and in different ones.",[]],"refl:2129":["Possibility of the [cognition] judgment is not yet truth.",[]],"refl:2130":["Truth is cognized either from grounds *a priori* or from sequences *a posteriori*. The truth of the sequences proves the truth of the cognition thus: From a [true] false cognition nothing true can follow, but this flows from the part of the cognition which is true. But where many sequences are true, one believes that all the remaining may be true (hypotheses) (s *inductio*); for the incorrectness perhaps lying in the proposition would have to be discovered.",[]],"refl:2131":["One can in logic expound not the characteristics of cognition of the understanding in general, but the actions of the understanding in general in judgments [vo]. I.e. it is merely analytic. Logically a cognition can be correct, but not on that account yet true; where therefore there is nothing further for warranty than logical form, there the cognition is only dialectical.",[]],"refl:2132":["The agreement with an object, without something being determined in respect of this object, cannot be specified, i.e. to determine something in respect of the object. Thus logic is merely analytic in respect of cognition in general.",[]],"refl:2133":["(ν−ξ?) L 23.\nCharacteristics of truth in general cannot be specified, because these must always be related to *obiecte*, but only the conditions of a cognition (g of the understanding) in general, i.e. of judgments in general, that they do not contradict themselves. (I.e. the understanding cannot itself universally determine whether judgment has been made in conformity with its laws, but that must *in casu* be the power of judgment.)",[]],"refl:2134":["To truth in judging illusion is opposed *negative*. |\nError *contrarie*. Through the first nothing is determined, through the second the opposite.",[]],"refl:2135":["One cognizes by this that the cognition is false, that it conflicts with itself, but only through reason. If it conflicts with others, then it is only false if it contradicts true cognitions.\nIf the falsity cannot be cognized from an inner contradiction, then from the relation to other cognitions the falsity is only to be cognized if thereby every characteristic of truth ceases.",[]],"refl:2136":["Whence the possibility of errors in discursive cognition, both historical and rational? Against this, freedom from errors in mathematics? Because this gives the concepts in intuition *a priori* and comes down to sheer *congruence*.",[]],"refl:2137":["24'.\nL 24:\n1. Possibility of a cognition in itself. *Categoria subiecti*.\nL 24':\nPrinciples, indemonstrable.\nL 24:\n2, [in relation] its agreement with grounds and sequences.\nL 24':\nproven propositions.\nL 24:\n3. [The correctness of the sequences] With others in composition in a whole, system.\nL 24':\nconfirmed.",[]],"refl:2138":["analytic or synthetic mark of truth.",[]],"refl:2139":["Above and to the right \nWe can more easily infer from the sequences to the grounds apagogically than directly, because one false sequence already makes a cognition false.",[]],"refl:2140":["(g Immediate cognition of truth.)\napagogic [Er] means of truth,\nanalytic from sequences,\nsynthetic from grounds.\nEverything mediate.",[]],"refl:2141":["Subjective truth [is truthfulness this occurs] (s occurs) in sensations (s and their consciousness) alone, [which] (s if they) |\ndo not express the constitution of the object at all. (g Inner and outer *criterium* of truth; objective and subjective.) The limitations of the understanding are not the source of errors. An error yet arises in part from reason. Is therefore at all times in part true.",[]],"refl:2142":["Error and truth is only in judgments (s and indeed when they are thought as propositions. The geometer says: Posit that there be in a ® more than one *angulus rectus*.)\nJudgments are actions of the understanding and of reason.\nTruth is agreement of the understanding and of reason.\nThe understanding alone does not err* (s Because it cannot conflict with its own laws). The senses do not either (s because they do not judge at all). The grounds of error must be lodged in other powers [not either]. Pure reason does not err. The human, however, can have no cognition without intuition, which is sensible. In logic sensibility is merely the subjective in cognition insofar as it does not agree with the objective, as precipitancy, confusion, habit, miscalculation. Logic has to do only with the agreement of cognition with itself both according to the *principio contradictionis* and *rationis*.\n*(g It also does not judge alone, i.e. independently; for there mix in it all the conditions that *subministriren* to it one or another cognition. In the human being it is in part passive. Some things are *suppeditirt* to it, others held back. subjective *conditionen*. One must become conscious of what one thinks in the |\nconcepts; and this comes from the state of inner sense, whether its horizon is clear or befogged. Sensory stimulus.\nThus sensibility is a power that is at all times required for judging; the understanding the other; both make the oblique motion along the diagonal, in which there is something true and also something false.)\n(g As in calculating good rules, but oversights in practice.)",[]],"refl:2143":["My judgment is to agree with the object. Now I can compare the object with my cognition only by cognizing it. *dialele*.",[]],"refl:2144":["Truth of the judgment or truth of the intuition of an object.",[]],"refl:2145":["Logical truth consists in the agreement of the predicates with the assumed representation of the object. real: [with] the [agreem] relation of the representation to an actual object. The latter is the truth of intuitions. In sensations there is subjective truth. they are actual, *e.g.* agreeable.",[]],"refl:2146":["Representations, be they as they will, determine the object. If they do not agree with one, then they represent another. There can thus be no falsity in them. If they represent what is impossible, then they represent something impossible.",[]],"refl:2147":["24'.\nL 24:\nExtra-essential marks of truth consist not in the accord of reason with its own laws, be it according to inner or outer marks, but in the agreement with foreign reason. This gives no characteristic (s proof), but *praesumtion* of truth; thus [the opinion] others have |\nL 24': |\na *votum consultativum*, not *decisivum*. Hence with the desire for knowledge the drive to communicate oneself is combined, because our own judgment must be *rectificirt* through a foreign standpoint. Self-conceited and participating reason. Egoist and pluralist, in the logical sense; on the other hand, timid reason is that which entrusts to others the decision about truth, is often also servile.",[]],"refl:2148":["*Conditio sine qva non.* The understanding must be in accord with itself in judgments, propositions and certain assertions *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2149":["We have so far spoken of the subjective *quantity* of cognition.",[]],"refl:2150":["We have spoken: 1) of the *quality* of cognition: whether clear or obscure. 2) of the *quantity*: whether extensive or intensive. 3. Relation: [Tru] whether true or false — related to the object.",[]],"refl:2151":["What is truth. *Dialele*.",[]],"refl:2152":["Before the question: whether the cognition agree with the object, there must precede: whether it agree with itself (as to form). This is the business of logic.",[]],"refl:2153":["The *criterium* of a cognition that fits all objects, (g must be indifferent to all objects) is one that properly concerns no determinate (distinguished from others) object. It is therefore the *criterium* of cognition which concerns object in general (g or rather abstracts from all (g difference of the) objects), consequently of a judgment agreeing with itself.",[]],"refl:2154":["1. *Quantity*. logical universality. 2. Quality: distinctness. 3. relation: truth.*\nOnly formal, not material *criterium* of truth.\n*(s Truth can be drawn to *relation*, but also to *modality*; according to the latter the relation is possible, actual or necessary.)",[]],"refl:2155":["Matter of cognition is the object. Agreement with the same is truth. Logic abstracts from the matter; consequently it gives no *criterium* of truth other than that without which cognition would be no cognition in general at all, i.e. of the agreement of cognition with itself. This is the formal and propaedeutic *criterium* of truth (*negative*); the material is agreement of judgments with intuitions, thus not formal tautology and identity.\nThe material criterion of truth is to concern the difference of objects, consequently not to abstract from these. There can thus be universal formal, but not a material criterion (g universal either sufficient or necessary) of truth.\nIt is absurd to demand a criterion of truth which would be sufficient for the [distinguishing] determination of the same in all judgments; for, if it is to be universal, it can determine nothing in respect of truth as to content.",[]],"refl:2156":["Truth in the *principien*. Rule.\nTruth in the [App] use. Correctness.",[]],"refl:2157":["The conflict of subjective causes of judgments with the objective ones is the ground of error.",[]],"refl:2158":["That which makes error possible is illusion, i.e. the subjective that is taken for objective.",[]],"refl:2159":["Outer *criteria* of truth: The concurrence of others, hence *allegata* of the jurists, of the philologists. If one wants to be an inventor, then one demands to be the first; if one wants only truth, then one demands predecessors. Citations of the mathematicians.",[]],"refl:2160":["Sensible in our cognition is called everything that holds merely subjectively.",[]],"refl:2161":["The material *criteria* of truth consist in the agreement of cognition with the representations that relate immediately to the object, thus in the agreement with intuitions and perceptions.\nWhether we cannot, even without the experiment with others' reason, distinguish illusion, i.e. the subjective in the judgment, from the objective, e.g. in religious belief?",[]],"refl:2162":["In and \nWe can in logic specify only formal *criteria* of truth, i.e. the conditions of the agreement of cognition as cognition in general without relation to the object (as matter); these criteria are negative: namely that one find no incorrectnesses in the form. Even if one speaks of cognition in general, there can be talk of nothing further than the form. (In transcendental logic the matter is universally determined and distinguished; hence criteria of truth, but no *organon*.)",[]],"refl:2163":["Objective criteria of truth are logical (g agreement of the understanding with itself).\nSubjective criteria: concurrence of others.",[]],"refl:2164":["Next to the end of L §. 96:\nCognition of truth immediate without inferences. indemonstrable.",[]],"refl:2165":["The upper *criterium* of truth (it is at all times only in judgments) is the possibility (problematic) or that it is grounded (categorical) [or] and indeed through the impossibility of the opposite (apodictic).",[]],"refl:2166":["Possibility. Truth. Necessity.",[]],"refl:2167":["*Principium contradictionis*: possibility of the judgment.\n*Rationis*: truth; assertoric.\n*disjunctionis*. Necessity; apodictic, because the opposite is impossible.",[]],"refl:2168":["If all sequences are true, then the assumed ground [are] is true; *hypothesis*.",[]],"refl:2169":["That through which a thoroughgoing interconnection with what is given follows is true.",[]],"refl:2170":["The inner criteria consist in the agreement of the understanding with its own laws.",[]],"refl:2171":["The outer mark of truth is the concurrence of others: *Citata*, the appeal to famous teachers of law. and universal accords of peoples. is not logical, but psychological.",[]],"refl:2172":["The (g logical) *criterium* of truth in logic is firstly that of possibility according to the proposition of contradiction, secondly of logical actuality according to the proposition of sufficient ground. Possible and grounded.\nThe historical *criterium* of truth breaks off?",[]],"refl:2173":["Logic can indeed give us universal criteria of the correct use of the understanding, but not of the power of judgment, because it gives only rules, but not at the same time: how one is to distinguish what belongs under them (error of the legislators, to make laws fitting to particular cases. *Jus certum*, sheer *casus in terminis*). The mark of the correct use of the power of judgment is outer and consists in the concurrence of others, who *verificiren* or *reformiren* ours. all conditions must, as with the *mathematicus*, be drawn into a rule in order to determine the power of judgment. In *iure* this does not work, (g because the smallest circumstances give rights and obligations.) hence the jurists cite the *sententias* of other *juris consultorum*.",[]],"refl:2174":["Logical judgment of truth and falsity (g in itself to the understanding in general). 1. From the cognition in itself; 2. connection with other cognition: a. with the grounds, b. with the sequences. Logic here considers only the formal rules. The outer mark of truth is the understanding of others, thus not inwardly, but relatively true.",[]],"refl:2175":["The [inwardly] objectively valid *criterium*; the [outwardly valid] subjective: concurrence of others* (s for avoidance of illusion). [Citations.]\n*(s In judgments of taste we have no other.)",[]],"refl:2176":["The inner (logical) criteria of truth. 1. Possibility as of problematic judgments; 2. that they are grounded as assertoric; 3. that [they] it must necessarily be judged thus and not otherwise, i.e. the opposite be false, for apodictic judgments. The outer mark of truth (after one has previously judged oneself) is, for avoidance of deception through illusion, the concordant judgment of others.",[]],"refl:2177":["A universal material criterion of truth is impossible, because it would have to contain the agreement of cognition with the object without difference of objects. But in the distinguishing of the object consists precisely objective truth. A universal criterion of truth can therefore only be formal, i.e. consist in the logical marks of the agreement of cognition with the universal laws of the understanding and reason, i.e. of the agreement of cognition with itself. This, however, is not sufficient for objective truth.*Conditio sine qva non.* The mere agreement therewith as sufficient criterion makes dialectic.",[]],"refl:2178":["From the sequence one can indeed infer to a ground, but without determining it; but only from the sum total of all sequences to a determinate ground, that this is the true ground. This totality cannot be cognized apodictically.*\nThrough the proposition of contradiction I can conclude positively the possibility of cognition (not of things), negatively the impossibility of cognition and things, indirectly the necessity of cognition and things. As negative *princip* it is universal, as positive, both direct and indirect, not sufficient for the *criterio* of truth.\n(g The third *princip* is that *exclusi medii*: since through the falsity of the opposite truth is proven.)\n*(s The sequence can be a negative sufficient criterion; namely, if a false sequence flows from a cognition, then it is false; but not: if no false sequence flows, it is true. Hypothesis.)\n*(s Thus there are three logical (g formal) ways to find truth: 1) *principium contradictionis et identitatis*; 2. *principium rationis*; 3. *principium exclusi medii inter 2 contradictoria*.)",[]],"refl:2179":["*Cognitio sit possibilis.*\n*Cognitio sit rationabilis.*",[]],"refl:2180":["Above and \nThe interconnection of cognition with truths given elsewhere as sequences also belongs to inner logical criteria. For there [is] the mark of truth is yet inner to a cognition in general.",[]],"refl:2181":["Logical possibility: agreement of cognition with itself, actuality: interconnection with others given.",[]],"refl:2182":["3) Criteria.\n1. That it not contradict itself (for judgments);\n2. have ground (for propositions);\n3. not have false sequences.",[]],"refl:2183":["To the objective criteria belong the inner (proposition of contradiction) or outer. Grounds and sequences.",[]],"refl:2184":["The logical *criterium* of possibility is the proposition of contradiction: that the thought is possible.",[]],"refl:2185":["The inner, i.e. logical criteria of truth are: 1) inner possibility. 2) sufficiency for given sequences, but not apodictic. 3) The false sequence from the opposite.",[]],"refl:2186":["Nothing is true because it is possible; there is therefore required, besides the lack of contradiction, also the sufficient ground.\nIf this ground is not completely sufficient, then the cognition is merely probable.\nWhat is inferred from the grounds of the best and of the wisdom of God is merely probable. *e.g.* inhabitants of the planets. For for this there are not *sufficientes rationes*.",[]],"refl:2187":["Conviction is an insufficient ground (g *ratio cognoscendi*) of objective certainty.\nTo this belongs not only my, but also others' conviction.\nIt thus makes the truth probable, namely if it arose from consideration of the objective grounds.\nIf, however, it arose merely from trust in others, then it makes such suspect. (g *e.g.* The opinion that the sun stands still.)\nWhere an impartial examination is certain, there the presumption of at least some truth is probable. For:\n1. We must presuppose that every understanding takes something to be true only for the sake of the combination of a proposition with its grounds.\n2. That, if there are no grounds at all or no combination at all, no conviction would arise either.\nThus one must in the dispute of opinions, where on both sides love of truth reigns, seek just as well wherein the other is right as wherein he is wrong.\nE.g. of monads. Example of Maimonides.\nWhere a plurality of opinion from different grounds is encountered, there is a presumption that not everything be true.",[]],"refl:2188":["E.g. There are many gods. The human being still now has the image of God.\nFurther next to L §. 100 »in derselben wahr — derselben« (l. 26f.):\nHe who found another reading of a passage.",[]],"refl:2189":["Correctness is *perfectio formalis*, consequently it does not rest on plurality — —\n(s Correctness is agreement with the norm.)",[]],"refl:2190":["Coarse and subtle are properly those higher degrees either of incorrectness or of correctness *formalis*. Exact is the middle.\n*e.g.* The cognition of the constitution of the earth among the Indians is coarse, because it an incorrectness that could have been avoided through an easy application of distinct concepts. Mahomet's paradise.\nSubtle is a correctness that is obtained through a great abstraction from confused concepts. *e.g.* Newton's science of colors.\nExact is that correctness which [through not] is obtained through concepts which *respondiren* to the constitution of the object. *e.g.* Socrates on prayer.",[]],"refl:2191":["Coarse cognition of the thing in the *Orbis pictus*.\n(s Coarse cognition as to matter in respect of truth, as to form in respect of thoroughness.)",[]],"refl:2192":["101-102:\nExact and rough estimate. [*Cognitio*] *veritas late vel stricte spectata. Veritas approximando exacta. respectu definitae.*\nη−φ. In L §. 102 beginning:\n*Late vel stricte determinata veritas.*\nOnL §. 102 »*cognitio exacta*«:\n*acu tangere*. Further: *praecise — circa*.\nOnL §. 102 »welche aber — crassa« (265 31f.):\n*praeter propter*, *circa* in a building estimate. Further: room for error. *plus minus*. Further: *generaliter*. On the whole it holds. Further: superficially, on average, indeterminate. Further: raw, *rudis*.\nAbove and below »Alle grobe — und« (265 32f.):\nTo work something out from the coarse and afterwards add the fineness.\nThat which suffices for the aim is, notwithstanding that much fineness is lacking, yet not coarse, but simple and natural.",[]],"refl:2193":["Something is exact if one carefully seeks to avoid the small incorrectnesses.\nThe lack of exactness is thus when small incorrectnesses creep in. *e.g.* instruments. Compass. Clock. Telescope.\nFurther on the end of L §. 102 from »je grösser« (266 27) on:\nThe moon be as large as the Peloponnesus.\nOne must know exactly to determine the (g length) of the year; but it is not necessary to determine just as exactly the course of Jupiter.\nFurther L 26 next to the end of L §. 102 (from µ−ψ):\nPartial truth in errors.",[]],"refl:2194":["1. Then the interconnection must be incorrect. *e.g.* Whoever infers from the [wisd] unity of God against the Trinity.\n2. From false grounds true things can correctly flow. Because falsities from one side yet have something correct.[Whoever infers from the goodness of God that he will damn no human being.]\n3. Then the incorrectness is only in respect of the way of cognition, i.e. subjective.\nWhoever infers from the goodness of God that he will damn no human being.",[]],"refl:2195":["If I infer the immortality of the soul from its simple nature.",[]],"refl:2196":["Complete correctness is exactness. *Praecision* is punctiliousness. If one does not want to attend to the parts, it is not on that account coarse.\nCoarse cognition is beneath the common form of the understanding, subtle is above it. With coarse and subtle it comes down to the degree of the form of the understanding and not to correctness.\nSubtle is what requires a greater attention for clarity, either because the object is small or the difference from other things is small. Universal abstract concepts are subtle, distinctions.\nWhy is the subtle taken for unnecessary? because the healthy understanding is enough for the useful. Teachers must be subtle, but not in the exposition, except where they also want to make teachers.",[]],"refl:2197":["If it comes down to the ground of truth, then it is\neither according to logical rules true\n— — —\naesthetic —\n— — —\nmoral: practical assent.\nObjectively every cognition is either true or false.\nSubjectively — — — — — — or ungrounded.",[]],"refl:2198":["*tolerabiliter vera sunt, in qvibus est approximatio ad veritatem.*",[]],"refl:2199":["»Something is [tru] to be assumed« and: »it is true« is different.\nThe former relates to (g universal) grounds of the judgment in the subject (g use of the cognition), these in the object (g cognition in itself). Logical, aesthetic, practical presupposition. The latter shows that without any regard to the use it contains in itself grounds of the judgment.\nTaking to be true from objective grounds (g judgment of truth) is either certainty or probability.\nTaking to be true from grounds of application is either breaks off.",[]],"refl:2200":["*regula late vel stricte determinans.* A room for the arbitrary determinations always remains (in ethical laws), but not in juridical ones. *determinatio practica* is also mostly *lata*. To determine this *latitudinem*, the leeway of [error] truth and error, as to magnitude. *Latitudinarius* in respect of morals.\nThe object can be determined *late* or *stricte*, but the ground of cognition must be stringent.\nFor exactness subtlety is often required. Hence that is called subtle which is superfluously exact, inasmuch as many a closer determination can be left to choice.",[]],"refl:2201":["All rules limit, but the *latae* leave a room of free choice; of the mean [betw] in the leeway of the contradictions, through which truth is determined *stricte* not *a priori*, but *posteriori*.",[]],"refl:2202":["The lack of exactness which produces an error contrary to the end is the coarse of the determination. To the coarse the correct is opposed *contradictorie*, and the fine *contrarie*.",[]],"refl:2203":["What *subtilitaet* is with regard to cognition, that is *delicatesse* with regard to taste and correctness in feeling, likewise the punctiliousness of conscience (s *scrupulositaet*).",[]],"refl:2204":["Cognition is either *vaga** (which has no determinate *obiect*) (g e.g. perfection, happiness) or *determinata*. *determinata* either *stricte* or *late determinata* (g kindness and right); the former (g *obiective*) *exacta*, the latter *rudis*; *subiective*: *subtilis* or *crassa*.\n*(s Not too much, not too little. *aurea mediocritas*. Hair-splitting is mere play of the power of judgment.)",[]],"refl:2205":["Aesthetically true: what is actually so described as it appears, or what all people say.",[]],"refl:2206":["Grounds of taking to be true: aesthetic and practical, or something is true according to logical laws.",[]],"refl:2207":["The degree of truth (g according to the content, not according to the ground) is distinguished from the degree of taking to be true. The former is partial with regard to the object, the latter with regard to the cognition of the object.",[]],"refl:2208":["*tolerabiliter* true. (s whose incorrectness does not hinder the end.) (s Partial truth is distinguished from degree of truth; the latter is [easy] approximation.)",[]],"refl:2209":["Either partial truth. e.g. What is said universally can still hold of much.\nOr partial degree of truth. *Probabilitas* is distinguished from illusion, *verisimilitudo*.\n(s True on the whole (of the aim).)",[]],"refl:2210":["In and \nTruth has no degrees. More than, all too true.\nThe distributive, the collective universality of truth.\n1. there is something false in an otherwise true cognition. 2. or: not all truth, the whole truth.",[]],"refl:2211":["True, or false, or logical *adiaphoron*: *non liquet* (g problematic judgment, no proposition), when from the *data* nothing is determined.",[]],"refl:2212":["In historical (g empirical) assertions someone can be entirely wrong, e.g. he who posits an occurrence before the birth of Christ which nevertheless happened after it. On the other hand in rational [propos] judgments he must always, considered from one side, be right, and it is fair first to grant him this right and only afterwards to add the incorrectness as mere restriction.",[]],"refl:2213":["All our dispute about truth [is] has a common interest as between friends (man and wife), should thus be sympathetic, not exclusive, self-seeking and egoistic. I must begin by noting wherein the other is right.",[]],"refl:2214":["Probability properly concerns only things, but not cognitions. The cognition of the probable must be true.",[]],"refl:2215":["The *totalitaet* is either collective, i.e. everything in the cognition is true; or determining, i.e. it *congruirt* with the object. True according to degree.\nEither the whole of the cognition is true, or one cognizes thereby the whole given determination of the object.\nWhat this one says is wholly true — he says the whole truth.\nIn L §. 101:\nEither truth concerns the whole present cognition or the whole *obiect*. The first is necessary, otherwise cognition is coarse; the last is *circa* breaks off.\n(s *Cognitio vaga* or *exacta*.)\n(s True on the whole, but not *exact*.)",[]],"refl:2216":["On the whole this one is right, i.e. in the main thing, but not wholly right.",[]],"refl:2217":["Everything I say is true; but I do not say the whole truth",[]],"refl:2218":["The (g *praecise*) exact pertains to truth (*obiectiv*).\nThe *subtile* to attention (*subiectiv*).",[]],"refl:2219":["Historical determination to within half a century. Astronomical.",[]],"refl:2220":["Cognition is called exact (g when it is adequate to the end) with regard to the *obiect* and *subtil* with regard to the cognitive powers.\nUnder exact stands as contrary opposite: *rudis*, under *subtil*: *crassa*. Before *rudis* the s-addition: (ω): *exacta* — *vel*, after *rudis* the s-addition (ω): raw, which are not enough — *Crasse*, which are too much determined for the senses, to which they do not belong.Inω *crassa*(line 15) is changed into *crasse* and to the left of it concepts — added, to the right of it: *subtil* — coarse. The mean is *praecision*, not *micrologie*.",[]],"refl:2221":["Cognition either *vaga* or *determinata*. *determinatio stricta* or *lata* for delimitation, but not logical.\nWe *determiniren* first *late*, then *stricte*. e.g. Philosophy is science of reason.\n*repraesentatio vaga plane non est determinatio* (a vague representation is not at all a determination). e.g. *Mediocritas in absumendis opibus* (moderation in consuming one's wealth).\n*perfice te*: *regula vaga*. Is without use.",[]],"refl:2222":["The approximate, the exact and *praecise* determination as against the undetermined. more common, coarser. pardonable error.\n*Crasse* concepts.",[]],"refl:2223":["Dogmatic truths are properly all universal truths; for these are not cognized immediately through experience and senses.\nInferred and observed truths.\n*veritas Historica*: in the mountains it thunders the most.\n*veritas dogmatica*. The weather-matter is located in the clouds.\nHistorical cognitions are here considered according to the matter (the object) and are [from] as things that have happened distinguished from dogmatic ones, whose *obiect* is a property of possible things.\nOnL §. 104 »Lehrgebäude«:\nWhen a manifoldness of cognition flows from a single ground. e.g. The *propagatio per traducem*.\nHistory is no system.\nL 26 on the same word from υ−θ:\nsystematic or aphoristic presentation.",[]],"refl:2224":["Of the harmfulness\nutility\n)\nof systems.",[]],"refl:2225":["The form of the system must first be sketched.\nhistorical form has no ground in reason.",[]],"refl:2226":["All doctrinal edifices are learned *proiecte*. Sketches, however, are necessary for methodical investigation. Without doctrinal edifice all investigation is tumultuary; but one must only always hold it to be a *proiect* until it is proved.",[]],"refl:2227":["All systems are historical or rational. in the former is empirical |\n(g understanding-), in the latter rational unity; in the former is synthetic unity, insofar as much is the ground of one (whole, which is the same as this together); in the latter analytic, insofar as the manifold hangs together through a ground *a posteriori* or *a priori*.\n(s Synthetic unity is the unity of taking-together, analytic the unity of derivation; the former is empirical, the latter rational; in the former [are] is the manifold parts, in the latter sequels.)\n(s E.g. Derivation of history from the principle of the unification of human beings into a common being. Second principle: of the unification of states in a whole — either of fusion through war or of rightful combination through law.\nHuman beings have not gone to work systematically according to their concepts, but nature proceeds with them systematically.)",[]],"refl:2228":["Universal rules from concepts. Cognitions of the understanding from concepts — ideas.\n*apodictische* cognition from concepts is dogmatic.\n(s *apodictische* cognition from the *construction* of concepts is mathematical.)",[]],"refl:2229":["That which is in agreement with reason is thoroughness (g shallowness, *superficialitas*). The systematic, cogency.\nHistorical cognitions can be acquired without system. Mathematical also [gl] to a certain degree, but philosophical (g of pure |\nreason) not without system. [The Wh] The outline of the whole must go before the parts.",[]],"refl:2230":["Many imagine they have written systems, and have only aggregates. These require only a manner, systems however a method.",[]],"refl:2231":["A system is distinguished from rhapsody. The former can be presented in various ways. Aphoristically, dialogically etc etc.",[]],"refl:2232":["Many imagine system belongs only to presentation, but it belongs to the *obiect* of cognition and to thinking.",[]],"refl:2233":["Historical and reason sciences are both systems and distinguished from common cognition; the first constitute erudition. System can be made not through juxtaposition, but only through derivation.",[]],"refl:2234":["Interconnection of cognitions in one *princip*, i.e. the idea of the whole, wherein all parts are members — system. *infelix operis summa — quia totum ponere nescit* (unhappy in the sum of the work — because he does not know how to posit a whole).",[]],"refl:2235":["Truth *formaliter* has only the *singularis* — —\nFurther:\nThere are truths with regard to the matter, but only one truth with regard to the form.",[]],"refl:2236":["The taking to be true of universal cognitions is either dogmatic or problematic.\n[Universal] truths of reason: either *dogmata* or *mathemata*.",[]],"refl:2237":["All certainty is either empirical (certain) — *apodictisch*.\nAll truths: historical or truths of reason.",[]],"refl:2238":["Fables. Plays of wit. Poetry. Examples — —. Buttler.\nWriters of comedies.\n(s Significance of dreams,\ncomets,\nghosts and sorcery.)\nOpposite L §. 106 »eine — sie« (lines 29-31):\nThis is false. e.g. Kepler. Newton.\nOnL §. 106 end:\nAccording to the aim, with Maupertuis it is necessary, with Euler not.",[]],"refl:2239":["If the aesthetic (g or logical) is only in combination with truth, then the truths are only *accidentaliter* distinguished. But if [one] each is a ground of a different degree of truth, then they are *essentialiter* distinguished. Proverbs are aesthetically true.",[]],"refl:2240":["There are unnecessary truths, trifles.\nThere are hair-splitting and very abstract truths.",[]],"refl:2241":["*in sensu absoluto*: yes; but *respectivo*: no.",[]],"refl:2242":["Error arises 1. from the desire for cognition. |\n2. From the lack of the necessary fundamental concepts. 3. From the [negligence] omission of attention.",[]],"refl:2243":["The first endeavor (s NB.) of logic is negative and indeed [the ...]: to prevent errors or to cancel them (to make them undone). *catharcticon*. The second positive: to increase and to further cognitions. *organon*./\nIf it is more presumable that certain cognitions will bring about errors, then ignorance is more advisable.",[]],"refl:2244":["We can become conscious of error only through our understanding, and we can thus err only in that the understanding acts contrary to its own rules. This however is impossible. No force of nature can act contrary to its own law, [and] if it acts alone. But just as bodies in empty space fall according to |\nthe laws of gravity or describe perfect parabolas, but through the resistance of the air [in] deviate from this rule: so with the judgments of the understanding other activities of the soul combine themselves, such as charm, imagination etc., and one errs in that one is conscious of this mixed effect as of a judgment of the understanding. e.g. We have a drive to compare concepts *qva identitatem et diversitatem*, [but s] which is wit, but also a drive to combine them positively or negatively, which is the understanding; the one action mixes itself into the other. The *imagination* combines formerly connected concepts; hence also imitation.\nWith the moral of actions it is just the same. We do what we disapprove. Only [herewith] herewith there is this difference, that the disapproval can here be simultaneous with the action, there however [with] can only follow upon the perverse judgment.",[]],"refl:2245":["We err either sensitively or intellectually. [The] optical deception: whereby the understanding nevertheless does not err, we are deceived and yet not. Fear [without any certain distinct] with greatest certainty of the opposite, shyness in a large assembly, fear of a wretched person before death.\nThe apprehension of erring is not yet a doubt. Wavering state.",[]],"refl:2246":["If we had a pure reason and pure understanding, then we would never err; and if we had a pure will [would] (without inclination), then we would not sin.",[]],"refl:2247":["*apparentia* (*phaenomenon*), appearance, (* which is taken for the thing itself, is)\n*species* (g *veritatis*), illusion. (g Seeming.) *probabilitas*: probability.\n*speciem evolvere et exponere*.\n( s Appearance, an (g empirical) intuition of which I do not know [whether] how much of it is to be counted to the *Subiect*, how much to the *obiect*. )( s The *subiective* grounds of judgments which are taken for *obiectiv* are illusion. )",[]],"refl:2248":["*non ex ignorantia sola, sed ex specie* (not from ignorance alone, but from illusion).",[]],"refl:2249":["We would not be able to see *a priori* that errors are possible, i.e. that an understanding could act contrary to its own laws; rather experience teaches it to us and also had to teach it to us. They are thus *phaenomena*, which NB. must be explained, not only in general, but also each particular error, how it was possible, because otherwise one cannot thoroughly remove or pre|vent\nit. To distinguish the subjective [which on] which (g in that it is held to be objective) has influence on our judgment, i.e. the sensible [and the determination of th] in us which misleads into error, we can also attempt without comparison with others' judgments merely by putting ourselves in thought in the place of others.",[]],"refl:2250":["Understanding deviates from its rules. The grounds of this lie in what is not understanding, thus in the sensible.\nThe limitations of the understanding are not the causes of errors.\nAn error is always *partialiter* true. The *subiective* cause of them is *phaenomenon* and must be explained. Error in rules and in application.\nSensibility consists in *subiective* grounds of cognition,\nerror in confusion of the *subiective* with the *obiective*.\n(s A *Criterium* of truth (negative) that one has not erred is thus when no combination according to sensible (g *subiectiven*) laws has come into the place of that according to logical ones. Error must be explained as *phaenomenon*.)",[]],"refl:2251":["For error there is required besides falsehood also illusion, and indeed here logical illusion.\n(s Considered merely subjectively true can with consciousness be logically, i.e. objectively, false.)",[]],"refl:2252":["In the judgment is truth or falsehood. In the [taking to be true] proposition error or truth subjectively.",[]],"refl:2253":["Error is opposed to taking to be true, not to truth. The middle is the problematic judgment.",[]],"refl:2254":["27.\nL 27':\nThe (g logical) opposite of truth is falsehood. The counterpart or diametrical opposite is error. The taking to be true of the false rests on illusion. |\nL 27: |\nTruth is agreement with the *obiect*, illusion on the other hand with the *subiect*. Every error presupposes an illusion.",[]],"refl:2255":["he who does not judge does not err.",[]],"refl:2256":["We speak only of the formal cause of error: deviation from the logical laws.",[]],"refl:2257":["formal truth *iuridice*.",[]],"refl:2258":["Sensibility in judging influences not the understanding, but the judgment.",[]],"refl:2259":["Merely subjective propositions (of the immediate consciousness of sensation) are always true, if only one is truthful. Truth and falsehood lie not in concepts, but in judgments, and indeed as propositions. — (g Error is a false judgment insofar as it is held to be true.)\nHence easy way to prevent errors — either not to judge at all or to judge problematically. Truth is (according to form) agreement [with laws of the of the judg] of the proposition with laws of the understanding. All effects, however, always agree with the laws of the forces from which they arise. The understanding alone therefore does not err, because it always judges correctly; — the senses also not, because they do not judge at all. Besides these, however, there are no other [cog] sources of judgments. Thus error arises only when both in union influence a judgment, e.g. diagonal motion.",[]],"refl:2260":["There is properly no unavoidable error; there is however an un|avoidable\nignorance (s e.g. where hell may be). Except where one is necessitated to take one of two opposed cognitions to be true. e.g. in *religion*.\nAn error is either [theoretical] practical. e.g. That there are presentiments and significant dreams. [Or practical] (g That the soul dies.) That bodies are infinitely divisible.\n(s That the earth stands still.)",[]],"refl:2261":["One could ask whether there are small errors. *Comparative*.\nOf the *materialitaet* of the human soul is a great error. Of the sleep of the soul a smaller one. Errors are either practically or theoretically great. The error is practically smaller that the [ho] desire for honor must be the end of actions than that it is voluptuousness.\nAn error can be theoretically great, but practically small.\nOpposite L §. 112 »und desto — Folglich muss« (lines 30f.):\nThat not every thing has its ground.",[]],"refl:2262":["One must presume that the [error not] contradiction is a seeming contradiction when one meets it in persons of insight and impartiality or has historical grounds for confirmation.",[]],"refl:2263":["Nothing is false in this way if it is held to be true. But to make the falsehood manifest.",[]],"refl:2264":["The doctrine of the restoration of all things is in a hidden way false.\nThat matter can think.",[]],"refl:2265":["That, when someone hiccups, another is thinking of him.\nFurther L 28':\nThat snow is black.\nHow it nevertheless comes about that clever people have absurd errors.\nPrejudices which hinder investigation.\nFurther L 28 from ρ−φ:\nmocking, bitter, biting censure. For this guilt is necessary and evil or obstinate intention.\nFurtherL 28' from θ (? ψ−χ?):\nhe who denies universal logical *Criterien* of truth is absurd.",[]],"refl:2266":["To him who errs the falsehood is not manifest.\nOne compares error with the *ideal* of the *sensus communis*. Middle magnitude of understanding. An error which can coexist with this is pardonable. The man misuses the permission which men have to be ugly.\nOne can prove to no one that he is absurd, because rationalizing would be in vain. If one proves the absurdity, then one speaks no longer with the one who errs, but with the rational one. But to that one it is not necessary to uncover the absurdity. *deductio ad absurdum*.",[]],"refl:2267":["Unavoidable illusion.\n*ignorantia invincibilis*: from this *error invincibilis*. *juridice*. e.g. a law has not been *publicirt* in a place. Error in the *balance*: when I do not know of others' claims, or he who buys another's goods after public *proclamation*.\nHere the judgment cannot be postponed.\nPersonal censure or censure of opinions.",[]],"refl:2268":["28'.\nL 28:\nThe one who errs is a putative possessor (g *bonae fidei*) of truth; if he is taught and nevertheless persists: then he is an unjust possessor and can be brought *ad absurdum*.\nL 28':\n(g Persistence in a manifest error which has no grounds [for] for itself is absurdity.)",[]],"refl:2269":["From error one does not turn straight back to truth, but first to consciousness of one's ignorance and *suspension* of judgment. One becomes wiser by experience, but not on that account more insightful.\nCoarse error: which proves ignorance in common cognition (g of the *sensus communis* or of science) or offense against common attention. Absurd error: for which nothing serves as |\nexcuse, not even illusion. Dangerous error: which draws many other, especially practical, errors after it. (s *conseqventz* in *ius*.)\nSympathy with the infirmities (g *homo sum* etc etc. Chremes.) of universal human reason. Leniency in the judgment of them.\ntolerable error. Hard to avoid. Way of thinking: to judge problematically, not decisively. (s to assert irrevocably.)\n(s To orient oneself in thinking (for popular writing).)\nTouchstone\nThe external mark of error is* the incompatibility of others' judgments with our own. This is a hint to investigate our procedure [One], not to reject it. (s Even with one's own experience still to consult that of others, but not with mere truths of reason, except with regard to application.) One can be right in the matter and wrong in the manner, i.e. the presentation. Universal illusion is the greatest hindrance to truth, and he who errs with the great crowd appeals to common human understanding. This is also in itself a touchstone to discover the errors of artificial use. Test, counter-test. (s Orienting through common understanding.)\n*(s the internal: that [represent] judgments do not follow from the same principles. Logical danger.)",[]],"refl:2270":["Error in *principien* is greater than in their application. False rules. Offense against grammar worse than vocabularies. (g in logic worst.) *Priscian vapulat* (Priscian is beaten).",[]],"refl:2271":["To distinguish the subjective from the objective determining ground of judgment there serves, besides the judgment of others, also comparison of our judgment with other truths as grounds or sequels.",[]],"refl:2272":["External (g not logical) mark of truth is comparison with others' judgments; because the subjective will not inhere in all others in the same way, thus the illusion can be discovered thereby. *citatio autorum*. Jurists.",[]],"refl:2273":["1. Possibility of error; 2. of partial truth.\nUniversal conditions of the avoidance of error: a) to think for oneself. b) to think oneself in the place of another; c) always to think in agreement with oneself. 1. The enlightened, 2. the extended, 3. *conseqvente* (cogent) (g consistent) way of thinking.",[]],"refl:2274":["What taken for itself is not otherwise than true, or what is conditionally true.\n}\n}\n}\nthe soul is a spirit. the human being shall be resurrected.\n(s That in 3 weeks we will\nhave the shortest day.\nThat it is cold in winter.)",[]],"refl:2275":["*id, ex qvo diversitas rerum cognosci potest, est nota* (that from which the diversity of things can be cognized is a mark).\nWith consciousness there are marks. but where marks [se] are represented, there is not always consciousness. e.g. When I do something absent-mindedly. (s Cut something in two.)\nThe marks must be represented clearly.",[]],"refl:2276":["Grounds of cognition are either internal or external in relation to others.\nThis relation is either of *identitaet* or of *opposition* and diversity.\n[Cognition] External grounds of cognition are marks.\nthus *notae vel identitatis vel diuersitatis*.\nA mark is that of which I am conscious in a thing.",[]],"refl:2277":["What I in a thing which I represent to myself through a concept, is its mark. The mark of comparison is distinguishing sign (s of derivation).",[]],"refl:2278":["Each concept always represents a universal mark of certain things.",[]],"refl:2279":["That in a thing which constitutes a part of the cognition of |it,\n*cognitio partialis*, is the mark. We cognize things only through marks.",[]],"refl:2280":["What is considered as belonging like a part to the whole (g possible) representation of a thing is called its mark.",[]],"refl:2281":["We cognize things only through marks; that is precisely what cognizing means, which comes from knowing. For the understanding is a faculty of thinking, i.e. of cognizing discursively through concepts; concepts, however, are marks of universal use.\nIntuition comes from the senses; through marks the understanding resolves the intuitions and puts them together. Reason goes afterwards from what the marks contain to what the whole concept contains.",[]],"refl:2282":["A mark is not always a concept of the thing, but often only of a part of the thing. e.g. The hand is a mark of the human being; but only having hands is this mark as concept of the human being. Thus the partial concept serves through its universality to bring the thing even without comparison under a ground of distinction.\nA partial representation as ground of cognition of the whole representation is a mark.",[]],"refl:2283":["The partial concept as ground of cognition of the whole representation is the mark. The ground of cognition is of twofold use, either internal: of derivation, or external: of comparison. The latter either of identity or *diversitaet*.",[]],"refl:2284":["Marks as grounds of cognition for internal use: in order thereby to cognize the thing, or for external: in order to compare it.",[]],"refl:2285":["The mark is considered first as representation in itself, second as belonging like a partial concept [or] to another representation and thereby as ground of cognition of the thing.",[]],"refl:2286":["29.\nL 29':\nMark is a partial representation (g which) as such (g is a ground of cognition). It is either intuitive (g synthetic part): a part of the |\nintuition, or discursive: a part of the concept, which is an analytic ground of cognition. |\nL 29: |\n*vel intuitus vel conceptus partialis*.",[]],"refl:2287":["All our concepts are marks and all thinking representation through them.\nWe speak here only of marks as concepts.",[]],"refl:2288":["Human cognition is on the side of the understanding discursive, i.e. it takes place through representations which make what is common to several into the ground of cognition, hence through marks as such.",[]],"refl:2289":["Analytic mark is the partial concept of a concept as of an actual whole. Synthetic mark (g is the *compars* of a concept [as belong], insofar as this with that as part belongs to another |\nwhole): [as] the part of a concept as [actu] possible whole, which is first to come to be through several parts. The former are all concepts of reason, the second can be concepts of experience.",[]],"refl:2290":["The partial concepts of my actual concept (which I think in it) are analytic; those of the merely possible whole concept are synthetic marks.",[]],"refl:2291":["All marks are analytic or synthetic. (Place).",[]],"refl:2292":["The mark of an animal is: that it has no reason.\nThe mark of reason: that one can consider universal truths in abstraction.\nThe mark of a clock: that it indicates the time. The mark of the indication of time: that it divides it into equal parts, in accordance with the course of the sun.\n[The mark of a city is: that a multitude of houses]\nThe mark of godliness is the love of God.\nThe mark of the love of God is a living cognition of his perfections.\nThe mark of this living cognition is the [submission] perpetual occupation with divine things.\n( g The mark of a philosopher is: that he can give the ground of things. The mark that he can give the ground is: when one can understand from it why something is. )",[]],"refl:2293":["(s *Complexus vel series*.)\n(s *divisio* and *subdivisio*.)\n(s Extended distinctness (g through concepts or intuitions) — deep distinctness.)\n(s Clarity of the marks is distinctness.)\n(s Completeness.) The series (s aggregate) of the coordinated marks (s synthetic, of empirical concepts) is a straight line without bounds. (s Definition.)\n(s Thoroughness.) The series (s *Series*) of the subordinated *a parte ante* runs up against unresolvable concepts and is terminated. (s *deduction*.)\n(s [Comprehensibility] (s systematic.) Cogent.) The series of the subordinated *a parte post* with regard to the sequels is infinite.\n(s We have a highest *genus*, but no lowest *species*.)\n(s Combination with grounds *a parte ante* the cognition is thorough, with the last sequels *in concreto* cogent.)\n( s *Notae tanqvam compartes (aggregatum). Notae tanqvam subpartes (seriem).* )\nBehold there three *dimensionen*.\nin the practical: feeling, desire and force.\nTime has three *dimensionen*: the simultaneous, the before, the after.\nThe *complexus* of the (s immediate) marks:\nas aggregate\nor\nas series\nof the immediate\nof the mediate\nmarks.",[]],"refl:2294":["(g With regard to all cognition) Extensive, intensive and protensive (g completeness in connection of the grounds of cognition) perfection |\nof cognition (s distinctness) in the connection of its grounds. 1. [in] of the composition of the whole; 2. of the [derivation] derivation from *Principien*; 3. of the application to (g sequels) other things. (g Complete, deep and fruitful distinctness.)\nThe *totalitaet* of an aggregate has its boundary in the *adaeqvat* (*definition*).\nThe *totalitaet* of a series of marks has its boundary in the simple.",[]],"refl:2295":["29.\nL 29':\nThe magnitude of a ditch*, which is determined with regard to the *qualitaet* of the aim, rests on the breadth and depth. The length can afterwards be continued with the magnitude of the aim of it and of the capacity as far as one will. Just so cognition with regard to the whole of the marks as aggregate or as series, extensively and intensively, the former for completeness, the latter for thoroughness of the system, can further be continued protensively with regard to the sequels to infinity.\nL 29:\n*(g There belongs, in order to make a piece of land (undetermined in magnitude) dry, only breadth and depth, and indeed the relation according to the constitution of the soil, according as one — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — —)",[]],"refl:2296":["Simple beings are those which are not composite.\nFurther from γ−φ:\nAlgebra has no negating *aeqvationen*.\nFurther from φ−θ:\nAll marks [Ma] can become negating.\nFurther from φ−θ:\nAffirming marks are necessary, because negating ones are possible only through them.",[]],"refl:2297":["*Negantia non declarant praeterqvam in contradictorie sive immediate oppositis* (negatives do not clarify except in contradictorily or immediately opposed things) (s not composite, not imperfect). (s [When I say: it is no triangular, also no polygonal figure.])\n*sunt steriles. Sterilitas earum minuitur per positiones, sub qvibus continentur et qvae negationibus aeqvivalent* (they are sterile. Their sterility is diminished through positions under which they are contained and which are equivalent to the negations).",[]],"refl:2298":["(g restricting and extending marks.)\nNegating marks serve only to keep off errors; hence they are unnecessary where it is impossible that one err. Of a being such as God the negations are very necessary.\nNot-knowing, abstracting, negating.",[]],"refl:2299":["Negating concepts (g with regard to the *obiect*) are freedom, simplicity etc etc. But negating marks (g of cognition) for concepts serve only to keep off errors, such as: the soul is not corporeal. Through the former I want to understand something, through the latter only not to misunderstand or err, even should I be acquainted with nothing of it.",[]],"refl:2300":["Negative utility of all cognition is that of the restriction of our judgment to truth.",[]],"refl:2301":["Because negation is to be understood only through affirmation.",[]],"refl:2302":["It is a more important mark which contains the lesser under itself. e.g. That the human being has reason or that he laughs.\nIt is a [import] fruitful mark that[he] God contains in himself the ground of all possible and actual things, than if I say: he is [the most perfect among all beings] the holiest being.\nFurther L 30 on L §. 118 »leviores« etc. (line 24):\nThe circle is a figure which rolls most easily.\nFurther L 30' on L §. 118 »diese aber nicht« (line 27.):\nThe mark of an [animal] fish, that it is mute, is a small mark.",[]],"refl:2303":["fruitful marks for much use.\n[Great] Important ones for great use.\nEmpty ones for no use. tautological.\n(s Logical importance, practical importance.)",[]],"refl:2304":["for distinction.\nOnL §. 118 »fruchtbare«:\nfor derivation.",[]],"refl:2305":["Beside the first line of L §. 118:\nFor use.\nFurther \nor numerous, complete in number.\nFurther L 30' from φ−θ:\nwith regard to internal use (g derivation) (s for derivation from them as ground) or external: for identity or distinction (g comparison). The first are more important. (g Distinguishing marks.)",[]],"refl:2306":["Logical (s or grammatical) importance is distinguished from practical (g usefulness). Logical fruitfulness from practical usability.",[]],"refl:2307":["relative importance. (g e.g. of the starry heavens.)\nAbsolute importance can never be imparted to others (g who himself has no standard), e.g. of the cognition of God, of the future and of morality and of the consideration of them, but only relative importance.",[]],"refl:2308":["All marks are grounds of cognition.\nOnL §. 119 sentence 2:\nSufficient mark is: to distinguish the thing at all times from all things. (s e.g. Of the animal the lack of reason.)\nOf the dog barking is insufficient. (s Bird that barks.)",[]],"refl:2309":["relative.",[]],"refl:2310":["Necessary:\nwhich must at all times be met with in the represented thing.\nOf a triangle: that it has 3 angles.\nOnL §. 120 »zufällige« etc.:\nOf a triangle: that it has an obtuse angle.\nIn a human being erudition.",[]],"refl:2311":["31.\nL 31'. β1. On »äusserliche Merkmale« (line 24):\nGenealogy.\nThe place.\nFurther L 31 from β1−ρ:\nThe external ones are never internally necessary.\nL 31'. β1. On »Diese — Menschen« (lines 27f.):\nhis capacity. Virtue.\nL 31'. β1. On »Gründe aller übrigen Bestimmungen« (line 28):\nWith the triangle the 3 sides.\nL 31'. β1. On »Eigenschaften« (line 29):\nWith the human being soul and body.\nFurther L 31 from µ−ψ:\nThinking. From this mortality cannot be understood.\nFurther L 30 from µ−θ:\nunsuitable word.\nL 31'. β1? ε2−κ3?? On »essentia«:\nThe logical essence it can find of all concepts, but not the real essence. The first, however, is not necessary to find where the concept is empirical, e.g. water, because after all not everything in the thing can be derived from it.",[]],"refl:2312":["On»Die letztern« etc. (31126ff.):\n(g What belongs necessarily to the concept is for logic.)\nThe first fundamental concept of all necessary marks of a thing is the essence. Belonging to the essence* are either *constitutiva*[but] or *rationata*. *Extraessentialia* are either *interna* or *externa*.\n*(s *ad essentiam pertinentia vel extraessentialia*; (s *priora*) *ut constitutiva vel ut rationata (originarie vel derivative)*.)",[]],"refl:2313":["Necessary or contingent marks.\n*ad essentiam pertinentia vel extraessentialia*.\n*ad essentiam pert inentia ut rationes vel ut rationata. essentialia et attributa*.\n*extraessentialia vel modi vel relationes*.",[]],"refl:2314":["*essentia est conceptus rei primitivus, completus, determinatus* (essence is the primitive, complete, determinate concept of a thing).\n*ad completudinem reqviritur: coordinatio; ut sit primitiva notio: defectus subordinationis* (for completeness there is required: coordination; that it be a primitive notion: lack of subordination).",[]],"refl:2315":["[The necessary fu]\nThe [nec] fundamental concept: everything that belongs necessarily to a thing.",[]],"refl:2316":["Essential pieces belong to one another as coordinated marks in an aggregate, i.e. the essence. *Attributa* to the [ess] *essentialibus* in a series.",[]],"refl:2317":["We are here occupied merely with the completeness of the aggregate of coordinated marks.",[]],"refl:2318":["Natural essence — logical. (g The sufficiency of the marks, according to coordination or according to subordination.)\nNature and essence of a thing.",[]],"refl:2319":["*complexus omnium notarum conceptum aliqvem primitive constituentium* (the complex of all marks primitively constituting some concept).\nThe sum total of all primitive marks which together constitute the whole concept of a thing.",[]],"refl:2320":["The essence is *complexus notarum (g primitivarum) interne (g conceptui [qvodam] dato) sufficientium* (the complex of (g primitive) marks internally sufficient (g to a given concept)).",[]],"refl:2321":["All predicates with regard to which an *obiect* is determined through its concept belong to its logical essence. Those [through] on which everything that belongs to its existence depends as determining grounds, to the real essence.",[]],"refl:2322":["We are not dealing here with the essence of things (g *essentia* or *habitus*), but rather: Everything belonging to a concept belongs to it either (g necessarily) essentially (i.e., the concept cannot subsist without it) or extra-essentially. The concept contains *constitutiva* as parts, from which it is first possible at all, and *rationata*, consequences, of which the former together are called the essence, but each of the rest *attributum*. The extra-essential pieces are attached to the object internally or externally. E.g., the former distinctness, the latter popularity or influence in sciences.",[]],"refl:2323":["The *esse rei* or *conceptus*. *ad essentiam (esse conceptus) pertinentia* is the inseparable, necessary mark.",[]],"refl:2324":["The logical or real essence. Logic abstracts from all content, hence also from the thing itself.",[]],"refl:2325":["To the logical essence of a triangle the measure of the 3 angles does not belong; for this is contained not in the concept, but in the construction.",[]],"refl:2326":["On the exchange of the attributes with the essentials. *e.g.* Definition of the square. Only in mathematics.",[]],"refl:2327":["A representation conjoined with consciousness is thought (*Repraesentatio cum conscientia conjuncta est cogitatio*).",[]],"refl:2328":["Clear cognition (*clara cognitio*): by which we distinguish things from one another.\nThe representation that everyone has of an animal is clear.",[]],"refl:2329":["The cognition of the peasant of the gemstone.\nIn every [Erke] clear cognition there must be marks.\nOn the concluding sentence of L §. 124:\nWhoever believes that he is right, but can cite no ground, has an obscure cognition of this ground.",[]],"refl:2330":["32.\nL 32'. On “cognitio absolute obscura”:\nThe word of the Scholastics, entelechy.\nOn “cognitio relative obscura”:\nThe concepts of the enthusiasts: el gibbor, little worm.\nOpposite L §. 125 “Dunkelheit der Sachen”:\nThe relative obscurity prevails in the consideration of the origin of the human soul.\nOpposite L §. 125 “Dunkelheit in dem Kopfe” etc. (31738f.):\n*e.g.* the one who could not read through the spectacles.\nFurther L 32 beside the same words:\nWhoever calls a profound writing obscure.\nL 32'. On the penultimate sentence of L §. 125:\nThe concept of how the body causes the ideas in the human soul is wholly obscure; but the concept of the way in which the mechanism of the human body takes place is *partialiter* obscure.\nOn the last sentence of L §. 125:\nAt the least we must stop having marks of some marks. *e.g.* The colors. Indeed, also in the sciences.",[]],"refl:2331":["Beside the three last sentences of L §. 125 and the first of L §. 126:\nExample is no mark and belongs not as part to the concept, but as intuition to the use of the concept.\nUnderstandability is clarity through examples.\nThe objective distinctness often causes subjective obscurity.",[]],"refl:2332":["(g Distinctness according to degree, which rests on the subjective clarity of the marks, i.e., the liveliness, is aesthetic.)\nLogical distinctness [ge] is often possible only to the detriment of the aesthetic, and conversely distinctness through examples (g similes), which do not fit exactly, but must be taken (g only) according to an analogy, harmful to the logical.",[]],"refl:2333":["(g Clarity through concepts or intuition.)\nThe subjective clarity and even distinctness (g of the cognition*, which one has) can with the objective: namely of the cognition which one seeks, very often (s not) be combined. *e.g.* [The] what duty is: there one can speak of laws of happiness, which are very understandable, but do not in the least explain what one demands to know, namely duty. To extricate oneself from this latter obscurity, one must first be able to see and at the beginning support the still unpracticed judgment of the eyes only through groping around. Whoever lacks this second sense, namely to assess cognition from principles, hence *a priori* and *in abstracto*, not merely *in concreto* [in what is given], as far as the examples lead palpably.** Graspability and distinctness are distinguished as are ease and thoroughness, making easy and depicting easily.\n*(s [empirical] aesthetic distinctness through examples is not logical through concepts.)\n**(g Understandable is that which I cognize through examples *in concreto* (without marks). Distinct: what I cog|nize through clear marks.\nMuch can thus be understandable which is nevertheless not distinct, and distinct what is difficult to understand, because it goes out to remote marks, whose connection with the intuition is possible only through a long series.)",[]],"refl:2334":["I seek not to bring distinctness [zu brin durch] into the cognitions which one has, but to effect distinct cognitions[in das] of that of which one as yet had no cognitions, but merely representations.",[]],"refl:2335":["We perceive obscurity only in a distinct cognition, when we thereby perceive distinction of whose grounds we are not conscious.",[]],"refl:2336":["Errors in instruction.\nOn L §. 126 No. 2:\nSaying of Socrates.\nOn L §. 126 No. 3:\nI can forget.\nOn L §. 126 No. 4:\nThe capacity and the fundamental concepts accumulate.",[]],"refl:2337":["There are degrees of obscurity. One night is more obscure than the other. *e.g.* he whose concept of vanilla is this, that it is a fruit, is not so obscure as his whose concept cognizes it only as a creature.\nFurther L 33':\nWhen one reads a book, it is more obscure the first time than the second.",[]],"refl:2338":["Obscure cognition is a cognition, just as a picture remains a picture that has been hung up in an obscure chamber.\nWithout obscure cognition there would also be no clear one.\nFurther L 33:\nWe are often in bad spirits without knowing: why?\nThe concept of the sour, sweet is obscure.",[]],"refl:2339":["A necessary obscurity of the combination of the souls with the brain.\nThe human being has a lack of powers from neglected cultivation.\nFurther L 33 from β1−ε2, beside “wenn — Dieser” (Z. 26f.):\nObscure cognition of the essence.\nOf the nature of the mind.",[]],"refl:2340":["Whoever listens to a sermon without attention.\nFurther L 33 from β1−ε2, beside “aus — gewisse” (Z 29f.):\nAn obscure cognition of the origin of dreams.\nWhen we hear that bodies have a force of attraction.",[]],"refl:2341":["From the lack of concepts.\nNo ignorance is properly praiseworthy, except only insofar as it can do evil effects. *e.g.* That one does not know all the vices of the emperors of Suetonius.\nI make it an honor for myself not to know it.",[]],"refl:2342":["All cognition is either true or false.\nThe obscure true cognition is the material for clear true concepts, *e.g.* of the Christian in religion. The sense. Contrarily false. [ente] Sympathy.",[]],"refl:2343":["The making clear of a cognition. but the production of a clear cognition is not *evolutio*. synthetic origin of clarity.\nFurther L 34' (from ε2−κ3? ρ2?):\nWhy it is called unwinding.",[]],"refl:2344":["2. Whether one should and may make it clear?\nOn L §. 131a No. 3:\n3. The doctrine of monads presupposes a presupposition of metaphysics.\nOn L §. 131b:\nRules of attention.\n1. To direct one's eye at the thing alone. Abstracting.\n2. Long — — —. Ghosts in the night.\n3. With as many senses or cognitive powers as is possible. *e.g.* in botany seeing, tasting, smelling. Water.\n4. Comparison with others. *e.g.* Green color. is of middling strength. one can make it from yellow and blue.\nTo recognize old runic writings.\nFurther β1−ε2:\nI hear a tone, I have obscure cognition of its nature.\n1. Attention to the thing in all sorts of circumstances in the bell.\n2. I take the tone of a string, of a glass, of a pipe.\n3. I abstract from the matter.\nVibration of the air.",[]],"refl:2345":["Which are to be met with in no other than this thing,\nand without which the thing cannot be.\n*essentialia reciproca*.\nOn L §. 132 “ausführlich klar” (Z. 23):\ncompletely clear.\nFurther from γ−φ:\nSufficient for comparison or derivation.\nFurther L 35' from ψ−θ:\nThe author distinguishes here *complet* clear from *complet* distinct.",[]],"refl:2346":["Everyone has a clear cognition of a star; but if at night he takes a light which burns in a distant village, or a shooting star, for one, then it is non-exhaustively clear.\nThis is the [extensive] objective magnitude of clarity.\nTo the complete clarity of cognition belongs [a cognition] a representation of such marks as are *essentialia reciproca* and thus both pertain to the thing necessarily and unalterably, and are common to it with no other. *e.g.* The triangle I distinguish from all other figures through the 3 angles; but the human being I cannot distinguish from other beings through thinking, for God and all other spirits also think.",[]],"refl:2347":["The cognition of colors is exhaustively clear, but not wholly clear, for in the colors I distinguish nothing from one another, this belongs to distinctness.\nThe intensive magnitude of clarity belongs to distinctness.\nIf I distinguish a human being from all others at all times and all circumstances, then I have the greatest (g objective) clarity that I can have. But if I distinguish in him himself the distinguishing marks, then I have a distinct cognition.",[]],"refl:2348":["not when I distinguish it from all things.",[]],"refl:2349":["*e.g.* wisdom from cunning, devotion and piety.\nOn “leichter” (Z. 23):\nEase can be obtained through more frequent comparison. *e.g.* in tasting.",[]],"refl:2350":["The *completudo externa* cannot be attained without *interna*. The latter can be attained only of pure concepts of reason and arbitrary ones, but not of empirical ones; hence the last are inexplicable.",[]],"refl:2351":["The *completudo* [*profunditas*] and *praecisio* make up the *soliditas*, thoroughness.",[]],"refl:2352":["this is not always necessary, indeed also seldom possible, because I will not or cannot compare a thing with all others.",[]],"refl:2353":["Aesthetically complete is no cognition at all. Completeness must not be called *adaeqvatio*.",[]],"refl:2354":["The totality of (g whole) clear marks either as aggregate or as series. Exhaustiveness or thorough distinctness.",[]],"refl:2355":["One can give oneself two sorts of efforts: 1. to cognize that (s something) clearly (s through that) which one does not (s previously) know, or 2. to cognize that clearly (s through that) which one already knows. The first can perhaps become wholly clear, the other not. Merchant: who wants not to gain, but to know how he stands. The first action is synthetic, the other analytic.",[]],"refl:2356":["The intensive magnitude of distinctness: depth.",[]],"refl:2357":["Degrees of distinctness through coordinated or subordinated marks*. The sufficient distinctness in coordination is *completudo* (g and together with precision *adaeqvatio*); that in subordination: completeness.**\nOf coordinated distinctness there are degrees, just as of subordinated.\n*(g The whole is equal to all its *compartibus* taken together, consequently also the [whole] distinctness of the whole representation *completudo* (internal or external). On the other hand the distinctness of all *partium subordinatarum* is profundity.)\n** (g Distinctness through coordination occurs *per synthesin*. through subordination *per analysin*. The former in mathematics and nature, the latter in the philosophy of pure reason.)",[]],"refl:2358":["“To make a distinct cognition”: synthetic [marks] distinctness.\nOr “to make cognition distinct”: analytic.",[]],"refl:2359":["Total distinctness through coordinated marks with regard to the concept, not to cognize the thing exhaustively.",[]],"refl:2360":["A cognition as ground of distinction [and] is relatively or absolutely sufficient.",[]],"refl:2361":["Synthetic as well as analytic distinctness can be complete. 1. Exhaustive, 2. thorough distinctness.",[]],"refl:2362":["Cognition is either extensively or profoundly distinct (*Cognitio est vel extensive vel profunde distincta*). Here only the first is still dealt with.",[]],"refl:2363":["Distinctness through analytic marks, hence *a priori* [through concepts] of that which lies in the concept.\nDistinctness through synthetic ones through that which comes in addition beyond the concept, hence is given in intuition as mark (the pure or empirical).\nNot all distinctness rests on dissection of a given concept. That [through] of mathematical and [physical] cognition of experience on the amplification and hence synthesis of the marks.",[]],"refl:2364":["When one distinguishes the thing from others on many sides.\nA liveliness of cognition is chiefly when one makes it similar to the impression of the senses and expresses all different marks of it through sensible representations.\nOn “der Stärke nach klärer” (Z. 27):\nThus the representation of the red color is *intensive* clearer than that of celadon.\nA picture in which the colors are very bright.\nFurther L 36:\nOf the soul is *intensive* clearer.\nOf the body *extensive*.",[]],"refl:2365":["The subjectively greater clarity (s through sensation: for that is not objective) is liveliness, the objectively greater the [Deut] under|standability.\n(s evident. cogent. logical: distinctness of dissection.)\nClarity through externally sufficient marks.\nOn L §. 135 sentence 2:\nClarity through internal ones.",[]],"refl:2366":["Aesthetic clarity\nLogical clarity.\n(g rests on the magnitude of the\n(g determination of the object.)\nalteration in the subject.)",[]],"refl:2367":["On “lebhafte Erkenntniss” (33324):\nHence liveliness is from sensibility, from interest, from extensive magnitude.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\ncoordinated marks.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\n*vivacitas* (s brightness) *et perspicuitas*.",[]],"refl:2368":["extensive clarity through external marks. intensive through internal. the former: through coordinated, the latter: through subordinated. The former: extended, the latter: deep clarity. Lack of the first: dryness, of the last: shallow|ness.\nThe former merit: aesthetic, the latter: logical. It is impossible to make cognition perfect on one side without losing on the other.",[]],"refl:2369":["The extended (distinctness of understanding —) distinctness hinders the deep (g distinctness of reason) and the latter the former. The deep brings dryness and liveliness shallowness. deep and high are the same. Shallow, deep, thorough are altogether analogies from divers.",[]],"refl:2370":["Liveliness concerns intuition, strength, sensation.",[]],"refl:2371":["Clarity that is perfect in logical relation is that which makes cognition suited for distinctness through concept; that in aesthetic: through intuitions. Consequently the first rests on representation *in abstracto*, the second on that *in concreto*.\nClear concepts can be very useful for distinguishing.",[]],"refl:2372":["Liveliness is the greater relative clarity, strength the greater absolute clarity. The former in comparison of it with others. *per notas proprias*: for distinguishing, *per communes*: for identity.\nThe liveliness of the merely clear, 2. of the distinct cognition. The |\nlatter *per notas immediatas plures*. Internal distinctness is either extended: and this is also lively, or deep: and this is dry. One must begin from the former. When I cognize something immediately in examples (*concreto*), then I cognize it lively; the more mediately: the duller and drier. Extensive distinctness does not have its boundaries in such a way as the intensive. —",[]],"refl:2373":["(κ3?) L 36.\nLiveliness often hinders and furthers distinctness.",[]],"refl:2374":["Liveliness for distinguishing, distinctness for seeing through, i.e., internal cognition.",[]],"refl:2375":["Sensible distinctness and distinctness of understanding (g clarity); the former through (g in) intuition, the latter through concepts.\nDistinctness of reason through [vern] cognitions *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2376":["On “der Stärke nach klärer” (33327):\nExternal clarity. Distinctness is internal clarity.\nFurther:\non sensation, having force, not interest.\nFurther:\nby which others are obscured.",[]],"refl:2377":["Distinctness is a logically greater clarity, liveliness an aesthetically greater clarity.\nLiveliness of the impression (interest) (g strength) or of the intuition and idea (extensive magnitude).",[]],"refl:2378":["Evident, cogent, thorough.\nAesthetic distinctness of cognition through examples, logical distinctness through concepts.",[]],"refl:2379":["Magnitude of clarity in intuition or in the concept.",[]],"refl:2380":["Logical sharpness in separation, aesthetic strength in the amplification of the imagination.",[]],"refl:2381":["Luminous — bright concepts.",[]],"refl:2382":["Liveliness is representation that excites the feeling of life.",[]],"refl:2383":["Sensible representation has liveliness in intuition and strength in sensation. The first alone is advantageous to the understanding, the second makes partisan. The aesthetic art is: to make the former preponderant over the latter through the harmony of intuition through imagination with ideas of reason.",[]],"refl:2384":["Whoever has not often compared a thing with others, his cognition becomes obscure.\nWhoever has not represented it in separation must cognize obscurely.\nWhoever has not applied any attention to it.",[]],"refl:2385":["Second degree of clarity.\nWhen the marks are represented clearly. [*e.g.* I have a clear] i.e., when one can distinguish them from others. Thus when not only the total representation but also the *partialis* is clear. *e.g.* I know that gunpowder has an expansive force when it is ignited. I know that it consists of sulfur, saltpeter and charcoal. — —\nWhen I distinguish the marks from one another.\nWith distinctness I can say the marks.\nFurther L 36. On “In jener — klar” (33824, 33918):\nI can say the marks.",[]],"refl:2386":["On the beginning of L §. 138:\nA cognition that is indistinct can nevertheless be clear; but if now the marks are not also cognized clearly, then I cannot say them, hence I cannot make my clear cognition comprehensible to others.",[]],"refl:2387":["On the 2nd sentence of L §. 138:\nThe beautiful sciences are those which provide the rules for guiding the |\nlower cognitive powers, i.e., confused cognition. *Aesthetica*.\nFurther L 36 on the beginning of this sentence from β1−κ3:\nTo seek to increase extensive clarity.",[]],"refl:2388":["Opposite the end of the second and the beginning of the third sentence of L §. 138:\nIf someone treats the metaphysical question of the origin of evil as Leibniz does, then it is beautiful and thorough at the same time.",[]],"refl:2389":["If I pay attention to the parts of cognition, then I dissect them. This is called *reflexio*. Now because I can have no total distinctness without partial clarity and this not without separate consideration, |\nI must dissect and consider each part separately. *e.g.* The body, the space. The motions, the figure.",[]],"refl:2390":["*e.g.* *notio irresolubilis*: being and not being.\nBeside L §. 139 “oder — werden” (34026-34117):\nTo such [concept] things only a clear concept corresponds.\n(s First come analytically to partial concepts, then synthetically from these back to the whole.)",[]],"refl:2391":["We must distinguish understanding and comprehending; if I cognize something clearly or distinctly, then I understand.\nBut completely comprehending breaks off.",[]],"refl:2392":["Not all distinctness arises analytically, namely through marks that already lay in the concept of a thing, but many synthe|tically\nthrough marks that one does not [in de] include in the concept, but connects with it as belonging to it. Composite concepts can be made distinct analytically, simple ones synthetically; in the first action there arises merely a new form of the concept, in the second a new matter. The subject is distinct from the concept through which it is thought. This contains marks of it; hence the concept that in one case serves to designate the (g logical) subject is used in the other case instead of the predicate. E.g., A body. But although this does not work with the *conceptus singularis*, e.g., the earth, Julius Caesar etc.: we nevertheless also represent to ourselves through this concept many marks through which we think an individual subject.",[]],"refl:2393":["Distinct cognitions are made *per synthesin* in mathematics.\nObjects are made distinct *per synthesin* in physiology,\nand cognitions are made distinct *per analysin* in the *scientiis rationalibus*.\n(s *per synthesin vel arbitrariam vel ab obiecto datam vel a cognitione rationali datam*.)",[]],"refl:2394":["The following degrees are to be distinguished:\n1. to represent something to oneself.\n2. to know something. to represent with consciousness. (s to represent to oneself with consciousness.) (s[perceive] *percipere*.) (s *apprehendere* [grasp]: the beginning of *percipere*.)\n3. to be acquainted with something. Thereby to distinguish from others in comparison.\n4. to understand something. (s what I am acquainted with and understand, that I cognize.) (s to be able to present and communicate to others.) to cognize something through the understanding. (s *concipere*: to cognize through a concept. *intelligere*: through a judgment.)\n(s know — opine.)\n5. to have insight into something. (s *perspicere*.) to cognize something through reason. (s to understand something *a priori*.) (s through grounds: either possible or also actual.)\n6.* to comprehend something. to have insight sufficient for an aim. (s to be able to make it oneself.) (s to have insight wholly through reason.)\n*(s I comprehend that which I can determine (relatively) *a priori*, hence would also have cognized from *datis* (g or have been able to cognize) if it were not given, *e.g.* eclipse of the moon, but not warmth from rubbing. The rest is called having insight in explanations.\nOne can explain something that experience gives, i.e., make for oneself a concept of its possibility, without however having insight into it, i.e., also cognizing *a priori* its necessity, consequently that the object will be so. E.g. One explains the dissolution of salts in water through the same attraction by which water makes wet; but one would not, if experience had not shown that a piece of salt can be wholly distributed in such a way in a fluid, have been able to predict it. We have insight into nothing but what we can make.\nFor explanation hypothesis is sufficient (principle of possibility),\n—\nInsight\n—\nCertainty of the ground.)",[]],"refl:2395":["Either neither the object nor its concept is given. Arbitrary. mathematics.\nor the object is indeed given, but not its concept. Observing. physics.\nor the concept is indeed given, but not the object. analytic. Metaphysics. Morals.",[]],"refl:2396":["Synthesis is everywhere where the concept is not given, but made or extracted.\nAnalysis is everywhere where the concept given. but confused.\nThere where the thing is not given to me (through senses) (g and also no concept): there I think it *per synthesin arbitrariam*.\nThere where the thing is given to me (through senses) (g but no concept): there I think it *per synthesin empiricam*.\nThere where the concept of the thing is given to me: *per analysin*.\nNow the thing itself can also be given through the concept, as *in moralibus*.",[]],"refl:2397":["1. All distinctness is intuitive or discursive (through concepts); the former aesthetic, the second logical.\n2. a. Analytically elucidating; b. synthetically amplifying. Both through the clarity of the marks that make up either the actual concept that we have of the object or its possible complete concept. empirical distinctness. [All] Clarity of the partial representations in a whole given one or of the whole representation as a part of another given whole representation.\n(g Elucidating marks. *nota explicans vel augens*.)",[]],"refl:2398":["We comprehend only what we ourselves can make.",[]],"refl:2399":["L 37\nDistinct cognitions arise through the *analysin vel empiricam vel rationalem* or through the *synthesin*.",[]],"refl:2400":["In the analytic origin of distinctness I go from the |\nwhole to the partial representation, in the synthetic from the parts to the whole.",[]],"refl:2401":["On the first “inconceptibile” \nUnthinkable; inexplicable (what it is); e.g. genius or self-help of nature in illness.",[]],"refl:2402":["The method of inversion is that in which I can conversely produce again *a priori* the concept of what is explained from the grounds of explanation. *e.g.* Chemistry, which dissolves mineral waters and produces them again, but not wine.\nThe *astronomia physica*, optics can do that.\nWhether I can synthetically produce again a dissected concept must be shown from the properties that flow from it.",[]],"refl:2403":["I do not comprehend the thing, it is impossible. la Motte.\nFolly of the freethinkers; likewise of the common man with regard to astronomy.\nOpposite “eine Erkenntniss — schliessen” (34625-34733):\nBut we must hold the comprehensibility of a thing suspect when we are assured that we have the fundamental concepts necessary for it. *e.g.* The speeches of the enthusiasts.\nOn “was wir begreifen, das begreifen auch andere”:\nHence we must declare ourselves distinctly.\nOn “was ich jetzo begreife, werde ich auch künftig begreifen”:\nHence we should impress the marks on ourselves through attention and often repeat them.\nOn “was ich jetzo nicht begreife” etc.:\nHence we should not become faint-hearted.",[]],"refl:2404":["[*abstrahere*] *evolvere*.\nOn L §. 142 No. 2:\n*reflectere*.\nOn L §. 142 No. 3:\n*comparare*. E.G. indivisibility, understanding, will. Governing of the body by the soul.\nOn L §. 142 No. 4:\n*coordinare*.\nOn L §. 142 No. 5:\n*abstrahere*.",[]],"refl:2405":["The distinct cognition of the opinions of some enthusiasts.",[]],"refl:2406":["To a complete concept nothing more can be added, for if it contains all marks, then there are no more; but the very same thing |\nthat was already thought in it can be added tautologically. The [abs] prevention of this tautology is precision. *Conceptus completus et praecisus rem adaeqvat*.",[]],"refl:2407":["(s extensive distinctness through coordinated marks, intensive through subordinated. Congruence.)\n*Completudo*: extended. *profunditas*: thoroughness. *praecisio*: measuredness. (g *sume* —) *adaeqvat*. Adequate.\n(s Perfection of understanding — of reason, and both together.\nPrecision is the negative perfection of the extensive magnitude of distinctness.)",[]],"refl:2408":["First of extensive and intensive distinctness; secondly of the former as abundant or defective or adequate.",[]],"refl:2409":["The distinctness of understanding (g Through coordinated marks) (g through immediate marks) that is complete is called *completudo* — extensive.\nThe distinctness of reason (g Through subordinated marks) (g through mediate.) is depth. intensive, *totalitas*.\nThe distinctness of the power of judgment* (g through subordination under the marks) is that in which the mark is the intuition that corresponds to the concept.\n(g Through examples; the intuition is here also as mark, i.e., partial concept, not indeed of the given concept, but yet of a cognition through it.)",[]],"refl:2410":["The dissection is driven farther or less far according to the diversity of the aims.\n1. If one wants to comprehend the thing from the ground and practice oneself.\n2. If one wants to make it graspable to others. — — Until the marks are clear.\n3. In order to prove it. — — — — — —\n4. So that I distinguish things that agree in many pieces.",[]],"refl:2411":["[Thoroughly sufficient] *sufficientia extensiva* and *intensiva*. The former: exhaustiveness, the latter: thoroughness.\n[Great degree of cognition *a priori*, very universal. is required for thoroughness.]",[]],"refl:2412":["This rests more on the kind than on the plurality of the marks. *e.g.* What flame is.",[]],"refl:2413":["The *completudo* (s extensive (g complete) (sufficient) distinctness) is the [suff] total distinctness through coordinated marks (s exhaustiveness).\nThe [ad] [completeness] depth is the total distinctness through subordinated marks.\n(s Intensively complete distinctness is *profunditas*.\nBoth together thoroughness.\nThe extensive magnitude of distinctness, insofar as it is not abundant, is called *praecisio*.\nPrecision together with *completudo* makes *cognitionem, quae rem adaeqvat*.)",[]],"refl:2414":["The intensively: adequate cognition in profundity [is] makes, together with the extensively-adequate in *completudo* and precision, the *consummatam cognitionis perfectionem*: exhaustiveness and thoroughness together.\nExhaustiveness and measuredness together make adequacy.",[]],"refl:2415":["One must seek out the *essentialia reciproca* or compare things with all possible ones. how the classes help with this.\nFurther L 40 from β1−ψ:\nconstant and proper marks.",[]],"refl:2416":["Measuredness.\nA measured exhaustiveness. Adequacy. (s *adaeqvat*.)",[]],"refl:2417":["When one mark is determined through another.\nBeside “Erkenntniss — ist” (Z 20f.):\nNot in the beautiful.\nOn L §. 151 end:\nSo that not too many marks confuse him.",[]],"refl:2418":["On the first sentence of L §. 152:\ne.g. in mathematical problems.",[]],"refl:2419":["On the first sentence of L §. 153:\nWhoever is exhaustive with the mathematical point and brief with other materials.",[]],"refl:2420":["Logical, i.e., scholastic, combined with the aesthetic or popular. The universal *in concreto* for the common understanding.",[]],"refl:2421":["A gloomy head is one who is clear in obscure idols and obscure in those that can be cognized most clearly.",[]],"refl:2422":["Truth is *convenientia cognitionis cum objecto*. To certainty there belongs, therefore, not only *clara repraesentatio objecti*, but also *clara repraesentatio cognitionis de objecto et convenientiae ipsius cum illo*. *e.g.* I represent to myself something that shimmers behind the clouds as the moon. This cognition is clear and true. But it becomes certain when I clearly represent to myself this agreement of my representation with the object. When I distinguish that representation through which I see the moon from the representation through which I see an air-sign or the sun.\nWe are not certain of the universal conversion of the Jews; we do not clearly have insight into the [Merkmal d] truth of our cognition.\nSocrates was not certain of the immortality of the soul. He represented immortality to himself, but he did not clearly have insight into the agreement of his cognition with the object.",[]],"refl:2423":["On the 2nd sentence of L §. 156:\nNo thing is uncertain in and for itself.\nOn “gewiss falsch” (Z. 34):\nIf someone travels on a wrong road and represents it to himself |\nalso as wrong, but in such a way that he does not have insight into the agreement of his representation with the object.",[]],"refl:2424":["It is sensibly certain that the light of the sun warms.\nBut learnedly certain would be: if I have insight into the marks through which the representation of warmth agrees with the representation of light.\nIf I have insight into the way and manner in which the marks determine certainty, then it is logically certain.",[]],"refl:2425":["*e.g.* that there is a God.",[]],"refl:2426":["We have an exhaustive certainty that the sun is the cause of summer.\nIf I am thereby in a position to distinguish it from all marks of the opposite.\nOpposite the end of L §. 159:\nNon-exhaustive certainty: whether the moon is inhabited. We lack marks, or those that we have are not the right ones.",[]],"refl:2427":["Beside the concluding sentence of L §. 159:\nThe human being has no exhaustive certainty of the temptation who in misfortune becomes faint-hearted.",[]],"refl:2428":["Certainty removes *formidinem oppositi*; but this is no secure mark of objective certainty.\nOn L §. 160 “die unausführliche — vertreiben” (36427f.):\n*e.g.* Two who travel a road.",[]],"refl:2429":["Certainty of a believer,\nof a freethinker.\nOn the end of L §. 160:\n*e.g.* Freethinker. because he has no insight into those opposed truths that conflict with his marks.",[]],"refl:2430":["It resolves itself finally into merely sensibly clear concepts, has nothing superfluous.\nOn L §. 161 end:\nOne must seek to cognize everything that can be cognized mathematically certainly in that way.\n(s We have a mathematical certainty of the immaterial nature of the human soul.)",[]],"refl:2431":["On the expression: It is as certain as 2×2=4.",[]],"refl:2432":["The ultimate concepts are either wholly simple or concepts of experience.",[]],"refl:2433":["*e.g.* That is certain, that Troy was destroyed, but not: whether Achilles was in the world.\nIt is not wholly certain that human beings after death shall view remote heavenly bodies. In part, namely that they shall be acquainted more closely with the world-edifice, it is certain.\n[It is certain that the blessed shall come into heaven. But whether the same]",[]],"refl:2434":["Of which my sense can notice the truth in a lively way (g and [sie gen] [alle] distinguish enough marks of it), of that one is convinced. Many have indeed a certainty of the truth of religion, but no conviction. Exhaustive certainty is conviction.\nTo produce conviction there belong enough (g and right) marks. and a clear representation of them. *e.g.* that there is a God.",[]],"refl:2435":["Living cognition of truth.\n(s In relation to others.)",[]],"refl:2436":["2. is taken by the absurdity of the opposite. Cognition that is not undeniable is merely probable.",[]],"refl:2437":["Assumed,\nÜ The opposite:\nSettled,\nÜ Uncertain,\nIndisputable.\nÜ Groundless,\nManifest,\nÜ indemonstrable,\nIntuitive, thorough,\nÜ disputed.\ncogent, consistent.\nÜ",[]],"refl:2438":["When a cognition is certain through an interconnection with the grounds into which one clearly has insight.",[]],"refl:2439":["In sciences.\n[It is credible\nplausible\nAssumed\nFrom]\nFurther from ψ−θ:\nFrom secured principles; shallow without them.",[]],"refl:2440":["4. cogent in interconnection and system, in proofs.",[]],"refl:2441":["Whoever has insight into something in an interconnection with many grounds, to him something is very certain.",[]],"refl:2442":["according to all its determinations.",[]],"refl:2443":["45.\nL 45'. \nIt is certain to us that the blood circulates. Was it on that account certain to our forebears?\nOpposite L §. 165 No. 2-4:\nThere is a difference between the 2 propositions. “This I do not cognize certainly” and: “this I cognize as uncertain”. I cognize the theory of inhabited heavenly bodies as uncertain, and I cognize the Un breaks off.\n(g Because one has no certainty of the immortality of the soul, one must not on that account be held to be as if one denied it.)\nDo not read books of the doubters.\nL 45. On L §. 165 No. 2:\nIt is only a defect of cognitions.\nOn L §. 165 No. 3:\n*e.g.* The prediction of comets.\nOn L §. 165 No. 5:\n*posterior dies docet priorem*.\nOn L §. 165 No. 6:\n*e.g.* the *objectiones* of the scoffers at religion.",[]],"refl:2444":["A traveler takes his time when he packs.\nFurther L 45' on L §. 166 end from γ−ρ1:\nAssent in provisional judgment, but not giving in determining.",[]],"refl:2445":["45'.\nL 45:\nA *theologus* has a shallow cognition if he proves more from the *patribus* than from Holy Scripture.\nL 45':\nNot everything that cannot be demonstrated in the highest degree is shallow.",[]],"refl:2446":["(g On the unalterability of rational belief.)\nIt is called moral certainty, to be sure; but it is no knowledge, but only a believing, as with the jury. If one absolutely, and indeed from moral grounds, must pass a judgment and may not remain *in suspenso*: then this judgment is necessary.\nThe principle of the self-preservation of reason is the foundation of rational belief, in which the taking to be true has just the same degree as in knowledge, but is of another kind, in that it is taken not from the cognition of the grounds in the object, but from the true need of the subject with regard to the theoretical as well as practical use |\nof reason. It always remains believing, never will it become knowing, and is also, as the former, the most purposive for creatures. — Knowledge puffs up (if it is delusion), but knowledge up to its boundaries (Socrates) makes humble.",[]],"refl:2447":["What is grounded on rational belief is morally certain.",[]],"refl:2448":["Historical belief cannot properly be opposed to knowledge; for it can itself be a knowledge. It is then called merely a belief if it is practically sufficient, although logically insufficient. — Morally sufficient, much belongs to that: to take something upon one's conscience.\nRational belief alone can be distinguished from knowledge. It rests, however, on a practical ground, but is also a hypothesis, against which nothing can be said and which is nevertheless very fruitful as a presupposition.",[]],"refl:2449":["In opinion one is still free (g problematic), in belief assertoric (s one declares oneself). (s In knowledge apodictic, |\nirrevocable.) With the former one needs only to bring a better opinion against it; with belief, however, I am already bound as regards the subject.\n(s *a priori* believing.)\nObjective is the taking to be true which is valid for everyone without distinction of the subject; thereby an object is determined for it.\n(s What I merely believe, of that I am content not to give it out for a judgment valid also for others (objective), *e.g.* probability of narratives. They are subjective grounds, which cannot all be made logical, i.e., objective.)",[]],"refl:2450":["43.\nL 43':\n(s presume)\n(s expect)\nOpining,\nBelieving,\nKnowing\nTaking to be true\nBetting\nSwearing\n(s to have more grounds on one side than on the other)\n(s presume)\n(s assume)\n(s assert)\nKnowing and believing are decided, opining undecided.\n(g Certainty is the subjective *completudo* of taking to be true.)\n(s Believing: subjective necessity of taking to be true; (s logically) objectively necessary (s adequate) is the taking to be true |\nwhich, from grounds (which are communicable) that are independent of the constitution of the subject, determines the truth.)\n(s To believing belongs not only subjective *sufficientia*, but also subjective necessity.)\n(s He who opines reserves for himself the retraction of his judgment. (g God knows that better.) *Salvis melioribus*, he who believes: not, but unalterability, yet with freedom of everyone else.)\n(s A taking to be true that is with consciousness insufficient is opining. A sufficient taking to be true conviction. If it is (g practically) subjectively necessary: believing (g betting), if it is (g logically) objectively necessary: knowing (g swearing).)\nL 43:\n(s In speculative (g philosophical) cognition one can indeed opine, but not believe. In mathematical one can neither opine nor believe, but only know. In empirical (g or historical) all three. In practical believing alone.)",[]],"refl:2451":["Practical belief is decided and completely certain, so that its taking to be true is complete *in sensu practico* and even [according to the] through the greatest grounds of speculation can receive no addition.\nOpining is not contradistinguished from believing, but from knowing.\nBelieving is more properly opposed to knowing than merely, as in the historical, to rational cognition; for* we say also of historical cognitions, e.g. where Madrid lies, that we know it, although we have it from the narratives of others.\nIn actions according to belief everything proceeds honestly, but not |\nin presumptuous asseverations and swearings, except when one swears that one sincerely believes this.\nThe difference of taking to be true [seems] seems actually merely practical (g to) be, namely how much one could wager on it.\n*(s The subjectively sufficient taking to be true which [not] is at the same time objective is conviction; if it is not: persuasion. (s Lottery winnings.))",[]],"refl:2452":["If the grounds of taking to be true are only a part of the sufficient ground, then it is called probability.\nIf the taking to be true is considered only *subiective* and is thus not universally valid, then it is called plausibility.\nIf the cognition is plausibly certain, then it is called persuasion.\nIf the objective certainty is intuitive, then it is called evidence.",[]],"refl:2453":["The cognitions of reason contain not merely what is there, but, because they are *a priori*, what they must be or necessarily are (apodictic).",[]],"refl:2454":["(s All conviction is either logical (the object is certain) or (g practical: belief as against knowledge) moral (I am certain).)\nCertainty is either empirical* or rational (s of the common understanding or of speculative reason).\nThe rational is either apodictic (s knowledge) (speculative) or (s rational belief) moral (practical).**\nThe apodictic is mathematical or philosophical; the former intuitive, the latter discursive.\n*(s one can also know historically, not merely believe; *e.g.* geography; yet a good deal of it goes as far as belief.)\n**(s consequently that is morally certain the denial of which abolishes morality.* Something can be morally certain only for the jury.)\n*(g Moral certainty is subjectively the greatest of all, and if (g something according to the prescription of the laws) a judge can be juridically certain that someone is the debtor: then he is not on that account yet morally so, i.e., he cannot assert it with the most punctilious conscientiousness. Moral certainty, however, holds only for him who is to do something; he can and must presuppose it according to all conscientiousness. But he cannot on that account assert it objectively for others with all conscientiousness. The *argumentum a tuto* is not conscientious.\nMoral certainty is not objective, i.e., a thing is certain, but subjective: I am etc etc.)",[]],"refl:2455":["Certainty empirical or rational. The first intuitive or discursive. The second also.\n(s The empirical either through our own experience or the report of others.)",[]],"refl:2456":["Certainty is either demonstrative or *probationis*.",[]],"refl:2457":["Certainty is either (s practical or) empirical or apodictic (g with the consciousness of necessity); the latter either discursive or demonstrative.",[]],"refl:2458":["Believing rests on a thoroughgoing agreement of what cannot be proved. What then is the *criterium* of truth therein?\nThe subjectively sufficient taking to be true is no illusion, but rather when this is taken for truth. I say that I believe this wholly certainly, but not that it is wholly certain. Both lie in the understanding: the one in concepts [*a priori*] *in abstracto*, the other *in concreto*.\nAssume (s possible, problematic), concede (s negatively agree, let it hold), presuppose (s objectively).",[]],"refl:2459":["Opinion is a taking to be true that is with consciousness insufficient, is a provisional (s or determining judgment) taking to be true. (g *sub conditione suspensiva ad interim*. Interim assent.)\nTaking to be true: the subjective validity of the judgment (s *valor subiectivus coram intellectu*).\n*Modus* [of judgments] of taking to be true:\nOpining (s it can be), believing (s is contingent), knowing (s is necessary).\n(s Not so much degrees as kinds of taking to be true.)\nOpining*: a [incomplete] subjectively (s a with consciousness) incomplete taking to be true.**\nBelieving is a conviction (g[sufficient] complete taking to be true), which is subjectively sufficient, but not from objective logical grounds, which therefore cannot be universally communicated. (g *e.g.* A pious human being believes.)\nCertainty is the [subjective] necessity [in the quality of judgments] of taking to be true, namely that it is impossible for us to posit the opposite in the judgment.\nEverything true is objectively certain. The complete taking to be true is that which refers to sufficient grounds.\n*(s a taking to be true that is with consciousness both objectively and subjectively insufficient (s opinion).)\n(** s Persuasion: if it is subjectively sufficient*; conviction: if it is objectively. Certainty (g empirical or) not apodictic; the apodictic is not always demonstrative. *(s Persuasion is the subjective taking to be true (s through illusion), insofar as its ground is not contained in the objective at all.) )",[]],"refl:2460":["43.\nOn the concluding sentence of L §. 157:\nL 43':\nAll certainty is ultimately resolved into a sensible one.\nThe [pragmatic] practical certainty* is (g firm) belief.\n(s 1.) The apodictic certainty (s logically sufficient). (s Belief: practical.) (s 2. empirical.)\n*(g either from grounds without which no *praxis* would take place, or: the abolition of which would conflict with the practical universal laws.)\n(s Moral certainty does not go to cognition objectively, but to right and wrong; |\nL 43: |\nit is negative.)",[]],"refl:2461":["43.\nL 43':\nBelief is a taking to be true from inwardly sufficient, although not outwardly sufficient grounds.\nL 43:\n(s I believe another world, although I do not know.)\n(s By what do I know that my taking to be true is subjectively sufficient, i.e., can never cease.)",[]],"refl:2462":["43.\nL 43':\nBelieving is an assuming of a cognition from resolve.\nOne must assume some things, even though one does not certainly know them, because of the usefulness of presuppositions in finally confirming themselves through their consequences. What one has first believed, one afterwards knows. Worthy of assumption.\nThe complete taking to be true according to practical laws (not from practical grounds) is belief. I believe that there is an America (not: I know it according to logical grounds); for if with such testimonies I still did not want to assume it: what then would I want to assume [in] with regard to my actions or resolutions. Here the grounds are indeed merely theoretical, but [an anal the laws] if they are considered according to the analogy of the practical, they are sufficient according to practical laws. This assuming is then a complete decidedness if it must be assumed according to practical necessitating laws. E.g. I believe that there is another world. This belief, if it were grounded merely on reports, would not be rightful. For then more grounds of credibility would be required. Moral belief is that which is necessary according to moral laws [Where if I] as consequence or as ground of morality. Here |\nis a ground which decides for me, according to morals, what I ought to assume.\nAccording to practical laws in general it goes thus: if I did not want to assume that, then with such a way of judging I could do nothing prudent. My opinion I need not answer for (except when someone disputes the correctness of my grounds), for I have assumed it. But what I believe I must answer for.\nL 43:\nOne can well set up an investigation of a *factum* on a mere opinion, but to accusation belongs a belief that the other is guilty. In chemistry propositions that are believed.\n(s *qvod dubitas, ne feceris*. moral certainty.)",[]],"refl:2463":["In believing one assumes something, one is decided.\nIn opining *in suspenso*. One is still free. In the former under constraint.",[]],"refl:2464":["One must first opine before one [decides or] assumes or asserts. Must one not, in the decision, opine for the sake of trial?",[]],"refl:2465":["Persuasion: objectively insufficient taking to be true. Certainty: objectively necessary taking to be true, which, if it is at the same time subjective, is conviction. The subjectively sufficient, which it is not objectively, is called [belief] persuasion; if it is at the same time objective, conviction. Certainty* *a priori* is apodictic, either intuitive, evidence, or discursive.\n*(g Certainty *a posteriori* is empirical. The adequacy of taking to be true without necessity (g belief) takes place when its grounds are only greater than all grounds of the opposite, although they do not make the opposite impossible. Now they can be subjective without being objectively sufficient: then it is called persuasion; or both at once: [conviction] belief (without necessity).",[]],"refl:2466":["Objectively sufficient is the taking to be true if it holds for everyone.",[]],"refl:2467":["All knowledge is logical and merely objective; we can also know from testimonies. Believing, however, is always something aesthetic.",[]],"refl:2468":["In certainty it is in general not possible to assume the opposite.",[]],"refl:2469":["What I believe, of that I am morally certain; but the [cogn] object is not on that account certain (logically). Morally certain is not [logically] juridically.",[]],"refl:2470":["Historical [taking to be true] believing is only opining; for otherwise we can also know historically. Morally certain is what I can assert with all conscientiousness. One can also believe mathematical propositions, but not philosophical ones. Practical belief rests on subjective grounds of willing; it can have practical adequacy for all human beings, but the taking to be true is nevertheless logically, namely when I abstract from all subjective grounds, inadequate. If I consider the human being merely according to his understanding and as practically indifferent, then the proof of the existence of God and of the other world is not speculatively sufficient. He is, however, interested in it, and with regard to that he sees himself *in absurdo practico* if he does not assume it. The conviction is not smaller, but rather more secure in the practical sense, but of another kind.\nNarratives are not the proper objects of belief, for they are a knowledge. Nor truths of reason (g One cannot opine with regard to them); but the presupposition* of the theoretical conditions for moral use is.\n*(g what can be made only subjectively sufficient, but not objectively distinct. *e.g.* the perpetrator of a crime.)",[]],"refl:2471":["Mathematical truths of reason one can indeed believe on testimonies, but not know, historical ones however know. Philosophical ones not even believe. Philosophy in itself tolerates no mere persuasion.\nWhat are objects of opining, believing and knowing?",[]],"refl:2472":["A practically indifferent reason would not always be able to be convinced. A practically legislative reason must necessarily presuppose it, [although it] but in practical relation, although logically it can only be persuaded.",[]],"refl:2473":["Truth [as] is an objective property of cognition.\nThe judgment through which something is represented as true (g the re|lation\nto my understanding and thus a particular subject) is subjective: the taking to be true.\nThe taking to be true is either combined with the consciousness of subjective necessity and is called certainty, or of contingency and possibility of the opposite. The latter is [s] either both subjectively and objectively insufficient and is opinion, or objectively insufficient, to be sure, but subjectively sufficient: believing.",[]],"refl:2474":["42.\nL 42':\nWhat I opine, (g there) I judge (g my taking to be true) with consciousness (g only for) problematic. (s The consciousness of the alterability of my taking to be true.)\n*what I believe: assertoric, but not as necessary (holds for me). (s For believing argument enough, need not be proof.)\nWhat I know: as apodictic according to laws of the understanding; even if the truth is merely empirical**, the taking to be true (g relation to the ground of cognition) is nevertheless apodictic, i.e., universally necessary (holds for all).\n*(g This distinction concerns only the power of judgment with regard to the subjective criteria of the subsumption of a judgment under objective rules. I ask only whether it is with me an opining [is], i.e., is contingent, not whether it is objectively contingent; just as, if I do not trust myself to say it is true (objectively), I express only my taking to be true. As e.g. to swear *de credulitate*.)\nL 42:\n**(g it is impossible that I could be deceived in my judgment of experience; there the taking to be true is apodictic, although the proposition is objectively contingent.)",[]],"refl:2475":["(s My taking to be true is solely either:)\nOpining (problematic),*\nKnowing (assertoric),**\n*a priori* (apodictic).***\n*(s [believing] action as if I were immortal (g not acting against it).)\n**(s because I believe that I am immortal (also direct actions toward it. Sacrifices).)\n***(s because we all know that there is another world (not to regard the present own well-being of this life); the latter would be harmful.)",[]],"refl:2476":["Understanding and reason are free: subjective causes indeed affect, but do not determine the understanding. Hence there is here a kind of *culpa* (although no *dolus*) in error (except in the inwardly con|trived\nbelief resting on the voluntary giving up of all investigation).\nOutwardly there can even be *dolus* in the affectation of inner conviction.",[]],"refl:2477":["The taking to be true from a ground of cognition which, however, is neither subjectively nor objectively sufficient is opinion.*\nThe taking to be true from a ground of cognition which is subjectively, to be sure, but not objectively sufficient is belief. (s is not communicable and capable of proof.) (s private judgment.)\nThe taking to be true from a ground of cognition which is both subjectively and objectively sufficient is knowledge.\n*(s A taking to be true that is with consciousness both (g neither) objectively and subjectively insufficient is opining. One that is not objectively, to be sure, yet subjectively sufficient: [opining] believing. One both subjectively and objectively: knowing.)",[]],"refl:2478":["On the heading »on the certainty of learned cognition« (35918):\nModality of [cognition] taking to be true.",[]],"refl:2479":["Taking to be true can be apodictic without the cognition being objectively apodictic. The former is only the consciousness that one cannot possibly have erred in the application of undoubtedly-certain rules, e.g. in experience. It is certain that it is experience.",[]],"refl:2480":["Of matters of opinion*, of belief** and of knowledge. Matters of belief are those in which the taking to be true is necessarily free.\n*(s preponderance of insufficient grounds (g which I am acquainted with) over those of the opposite — *verosimile*, opinion.)\n**(s preponderance of subjectively sufficient grounds over those of the opposite.)",[]],"refl:2481":["Objectively there is no difference between truth and certainty.",[]],"refl:2482":["The *creditor* does not know, but is practically certain of the payment. Narrative requires belief. Yet there is a knowledge, i.e., necessarily believing.",[]],"refl:2483":["Without taking an interest in something, there is no practical belief.",[]],"refl:2484":["Certainty in belief can only be practical under the presupposition of unalterable maxims. I am morally certain, not: it is morally certain.",[]],"refl:2485":["Certainty of the senses is a metaphysical question. Empirical certainty goes to the mere relation of the sensible. Rational certainty either of mere objects of reason or of the senses.",[]],"refl:2486":["A taking to be true from merely subjective grounds is persuasion. From objective and subjective grounds, of which the former, however, are not sufficient without the latter, is believing; from merely objective [but] insufficient: opinion; from objective and sufficient: knowledge.",[]],"refl:2487":["44'.\nL 44:\n(s Here the subjective adequacy rests on this (g logically), that one cannot count one's grounds, but only weigh them.)\nAll belief is either theoretical (s in God as hypothesis, the ends in nature) or practical; in the first all grounds of conviction are objective (logical), in the latter partly subjective (practical or interest). The theoretical is [either] historical [grounded on testimony or rational on]: either of empirical propositions or of rational ones (this takes place only in mathematics). The practical is a rational belief, [both] partly with regard to the cognitions, partly to the interest of every rational being.\nL 44':\nTheoretical (g empirical) belief (g with regard to that which is merely for reason.) (g which cannot be called an opining with regard to that which is beyond all theoretical faculty of cognition) is never (conviction) combined with the consciousness of the* unalterability of it; for it is only, as far as my present knowledge goes, sufficiently or preponderantly grounded. Only the belief which rests on practical subjective conditions that are at the same time objective can be combined with the consciousness of unalterability. I am morally certain.\nIn kinds of cognition which concern no objects of possible experience one cannot opine and allege probability, but one must know or believe.\nBelieving is the idea of a free taking to be true.\nOpinion is a taking to be true which can become complete.\n*(s That which I assume from moral grounds and in such a way that I am certain the opposite could never be proved.\nOf opinion I have insight that the opposite could perhaps be proved.)",[]],"refl:2488":["The [merely] taking to be true from merely objective grounds is either opining or knowing (insufficient or sufficient).\nThe taking to be true from merely subjective [but], which [certainly] are always insufficient: persuasion; from objective and subjective, of which the former are not sufficient in themselves, but together with the latter are sufficient: believing.\nWhat one knows: of that one hears no counter-grounds.\nWhat one believes: also not.",[]],"refl:2489":["Believing gives a conviction that is not communicable. (because of the subjective grounds.)\nKnowing must let itself be communicated and commands assent. postulated.\nIn that [which] in which each needs only to be convinced for himself, believing is sufficient.\nLogically sufficient taking to be true — without interest.\nPractically sufficient taking to be true — [if th] with an aim to the interest, and indeed of everyone.\n(s A taking to be true held to be subjectively sufficient, because I know that no one will refute me. *e.g.* moon has inhabitants. theoretical rational belief.)\n(s Of being morally certain. It is to be understood merely subjectively. I can be certain of the unalterability of my practical belief. Belief in a practical respect (of what I have to do as means or absolutely) is not on that account always a practical belief.)",[]],"refl:2490":["If we weigh our cognition merely objectively, then it is either conviction or persuasion.",[]],"refl:2491":["Practical belief is grounded on a knowledge with regard to the laws. I know that he who conducts himself well is worthy of happiness, and believe he will become partaker of it. rational belief. God and other world.",[]],"refl:2492":["A taking to be true that is with consciousness objectively as well as subjectively insufficient is opinion. One subjectively sufficient, but objectively insufficient — — believing. One sufficient on both sides — — knowing. The subjectively sufficient taking to be true is so either absolutely or for now (sufficient according to what I know). The latter concerns historical belief, the second moral. Whence can I know |\nthat it will always suffice? because it is the condition of an objectively necessary resolve. But whence can I not communicate this conviction? Because it presupposes moral disposition. The cause is: whoever regards morality as in itself *a priori* certain must assume a divine being. Whoever believes it itself presupposes, even in the estimation, a divine existence, cannot be convinced of it through moral belief.",[]],"refl:2493":["The adequacy of taking to be true (in belief) can be put to the test through betting or through swearing. For the first [belongs] is comparative, for the second absolute adeq] adequacy of objective grounds necessary, which, if they are not there, a taking to be true that is absolutely subjectively sufficient nevertheless holds.\nOf moral certainty of the existence of God as *hypothesis*.",[]],"refl:2494":["If doubt about the existence of God and of another world should do damage to morality, then the existence of both is morally certain.",[]],"refl:2495":["Believing is a taking to be true sufficient for practical reason (thus subjectively), in which therefore an interest (subjective ground), which nevertheless holds for everyone, is laid at the basis, whereby |\nnevertheless the objective (merely theoretical judgments) are not sufficient (logically).",[]],"refl:2496":["*Sensus veri et falsi*.\nThe problematic judgment, what I assume.\n—\nassertoric and apodict. taking to be true\na proposition",[]],"refl:2497":["To persuade oneself (g not valid for everyone) (*mihi persuasum est*); to confess something to oneself as true (*sic sentio*); to be convinced (*convictio*).",[]],"refl:2498":["Because opining has a merely subjective sufficiency, even rational human beings can, from the same causes, be very different in belief, from habit, inclination, hastiness in judgment. One cannot communicate one's belief to another.",[]],"refl:2499":["Matters of belief are propositions which (g belong to rational cognition, but) belong not to possible knowledge, yet [to] through interest to\n|\ncognition. Believing is thus a merely subjectively grounded taking to be true.\n(g Faithful: *fidelis*.)",[]],"refl:2500":["A scholar can opine with regard to the advantages of trade, a merchant must believe, a judge know.",[]],"refl:2501":["*Modus* of taking to be true. Problematic or assertoric judgment (taking to be true); provisional assertion is the cause of opinion.",[]],"refl:2502":["The frivolity of opinions. The human being is an animal that opines etc etc.",[]],"refl:2503":["Belief — What I wish I gladly believe, if only I had ground for it. (s but not on that account easily, but rather I seek to persuade myself that I could also hope for it, which is good in a practically commanded aim.) — But if it is duty to wish it (for there is no duty to believe), then I have the right to believe it if I can. — But if I cannot believe it (e.g. the future life), I still have ground enough to act as if such a one lay ahead. — Thus |\nthere is a ground sufficient in a practical aim for believing, even if the theoretical one is insufficient for me, and, as regards the latter, I may always doubt.",[]],"refl:2504":["Moral certainty: *cautissimi cuiusqve praeceptum*: — *Qvod dubitas, ne feceris*. Pliny. One must be completely certain that something is permitted. On the uncertain one can venture nothing in moral things, i.e., resolve at the risk of violating the law.",[]],"refl:2505":["45.\nL 45':\nThis is in agreement with the *certitudine subjectiva*.\n*e.g.* to take the inhabited planets to be true.\nFurther L 45' from ψ:\nTo assume something (s *posito, sed non concesso*) is not yet as much as to agree.\nFurther L 45 from γ−ξ:\nconcur\nconcede grant\n)\njudge provisionally",[]],"refl:2506":["*aeqvilibrium judicii*.\n*suspendere* (s not *differre*) *iudicium** *ad iudicandum*. (s *iudicium praeceps, anceps*.)\n*(s not *renunciare iudicio*)\nto leave something *in suspenso*.\nnot because we doubt, but do not know enough.\nTo (g hold back) postpone or give up one's judgment.\nproblematic (g assertoric).\nyouthful or mature reason.\nUndecided or dogmatic cast of mind. εποχη, *suspensio iudicii*.",[]],"refl:2507":["We cannot withhold our assent except insofar as we cognize the possibility of error. (s Constraint to assent through the understanding: mathematics.)\nWe can withhold our assent even with the most evident proofs if we know that with similar ones one has otherwise already been deceived.\n(s Fear of the opposite from fear of the object. *argumentum ad crumenam* (s betting), (s *verecundiam*.) *ab utili*. Swearing.)\nGrounds not of conviction but of inquiry, so that one has something by which one steers one's investigation to the side where truth is presumed, are provisional judgments; sagacity.",[]],"refl:2508":["The will has no immediate influence on taking to be true, that would also be very absurd. When it is said: we gladly believe what we wish, that signifies only: our good-natured wishes, *e.g.* of the father about his children.\n*suspensio iudicii est vel indagatoria vel sceptica*. There is yet a withholding of judgment which belongs to the principles of lazy philosophy. only in order not to need to decide anything, and to seem prudent in laziness. or disbelief in rules. Consciousness of the limitedness of human insight. Particular direction.",[]],"refl:2509":["He gives assent easily who (s does not think for himself) has a peaceable and sociable mind. The propensity to resistance often comes from |\nthe fear of being the dupe of others. The cleverest can often be deceived more easily than the stupid.",[]],"refl:2510":["To judge problematically through provisional, not determining judgments.",[]],"refl:2511":["We assume, give assent, (concur.) reject. withhold assent in mere problems of cognition (problematic). we suspend the *iudicium* when we are in *aeqvilibrio*. Needle-point. therein it is hard to maintain oneself therein. The mind must be unprejudiced, not preoccupied. In all these points the will has a faculty to steer the understanding. It would be good only for investigation, but it happens also for concurrence. We must assume something for trial, and because it merely has grounds for it, but we do not yet assert it.",[]],"refl:2512":["The *Suspensio Iudicii* is either critical or skeptical (s *renunciatio*); the former for investigation, the latter in order to judge nothing. (s The *non liquet* of the judge.)\nThe demand to remain firm with a proposition once proved, however difficulties may be found, is unfair and impossible.",[]],"refl:2513":["Philosophical propositions (pure, synthetic) cannot so without hesitation, on their own credit and separately, presented with proofs, be presented as decided, but one must look at the consequences: whether they hold up there, whether they do not betray a lack of more exact determination or even error, and in this they are different from mathematical ones. Hence the philosopher must survey the whole of his science in order to assess each proposition in relation to all and [only] then to determine its true worth for it.",[]],"refl:2514":["On becoming-uncertain from confusion.\nOn being-uncertain from distrust of oneself.",[]],"refl:2515":["When from an insufficient ground something is taken to be true without investigation.\nBeside »*praecaria cognitio*« (l. 40025):\n*in sensu deteriori*.",[]],"refl:2516":["Prudent prejudice. When from an insufficient ground one takes something to be true so long until (g the examination has been undertaken or) the opposite has been proved.\n*in sensu saniori* it is the [assent] ground for keeping our assent open which is taken not from the inner constitution of the object but from other connected things. *e.g.* Prudent people take this to be true. [I judge in no way into the blue.] Whoever is acute in many points will not be absurd in others.\nWe must have prejudices so that we are enticed to pursue the proofs carefully. *e.g.* prejudice regarding the Christian religion. The sources of prejudices make them harmful.",[]],"refl:2517":["Taking to be true from insufficient grounds is not prejudice, but conjecture.\nBeforehand to be prepossessed for or against something, preoccupied.\nPrejudices resist teaching, in that they 1. do not want to be taught, 2. in that they judge the teaching through the same prejudice which was supposed to be abolished. Hence they are more harmful than ignorance. It is not advisable to ground a prejudice in others even (g of) true propositions. (s and blameworthy to imprint them deliberately. Prejudice of experience.) Formal falsity. *Praestigiae sensus interni*.\nNatural and introduced prejudices. (s The former e.g. of age, of parental love, of family, of laziness.)\nProverbs, canons, sentences, aphorisms. (s *brocardicon*.)",[]],"refl:2518":["On L. §. 169 »assume through a prejudice« (l. 27):\n*e.g.* most human beings religion.\nBut if I do not concede so much to the prejudice, but only that it makes something probable to me. *e.g.* that this or that human being is not the author of a book.",[]],"refl:2519":["All investigation requires a provisional judgment as to on which side we presume the truth. All immediate truths need no investigation except a subjective one, whether they spring from understanding or habit, i.e., reflection. The provisional judgment does not make the investigation dispensable.\n(s Prejudice of experience.)\nImitation, habit, inclination (s and fear), predilection.\nProverbs*. Canons. *sententiae*. ** Refutation succeeds only through deviation.\nTo reflect means: to compare something with the laws of the understanding and of reason. A universal rule for judging something without reflection is called a prejudice.\n*(s are nevertheless valid for the common public, but habit has only private validity.)\n(** g s *Brocardicon*\n(s sayings) mottoes aphorisms didactic sayings\n(s *formula*)\nproverb:\n*sententia*\nCanon: *loci communes*: where one finds the precept without reflection. Counting-frame. Multiplication table. )",[]],"refl:2520":["Overhastiness is a lack of the suspension of the *iudicii*.\n(s The subjective ground [of a rule] for judging [which precedes all reflection], insofar as it becomes an objective rule, is prejudice.)",[]],"refl:2521":["We judge only through understanding. In a prejudice, therefore, before the judgment, i.e., the comparison with the understanding, something must pre|cede\nwhich contains the ground for steering the action of the understanding, just as partisan judges, even before they have heard the grounds, know what they are to pronounce.",[]],"refl:2522":["Maxims of provisional judgments.\nMaxims of reason in general.",[]],"refl:2523":["Provisional judgment is not prejudice, but is a caution in order to avoid such.\nPrejudice of high esteem and low esteem.",[]],"refl:2524":["[Taking to be true from (g merely) subjective grounds.]\n(g A universal ground for judging before reflection.)\nA subjective [law] ground* of taking to be true before all reflection.\n*(g which is taken from the disposition of the mind to judgment. The disposition is a sensible *habitus*. The *habitus* of sensibility make accustomedness, which is a necessity to act, just as habit is a facility.)",[]],"refl:2525":["There are good maxims of provisional judgments, which, however, must not become principles of determining judgments (s That one does not understand a rational man), *e.g.* country people always complain. Women's tales are not reliable. Rules that have many exceptions.\nThe martyrdom of philosophy, which writes in vain for the present time and usefully only for the future.",[]],"refl:2526":["On passive and active reason.\n(s The *principium* of a passive use of reason is prejudice.)",[]],"refl:2527":["(s Formula: a rule whose expression facilitates the application and serves it as (g model) example.)\nSaying: a proposition that is bound to certain, customary expressions. (s whose expression facilitates remembering for the common understanding. Mechanism for the understanding.)\nProverbs (s *proverbia*, vulgar formulas) are current (g received) formulas of prudence. *vehicula*. What is peculiar to them in different nations.\n( s aesthetic, classical formula. Sententiae of wisdom. *Canones*, *brocardica* of science. (s Pronouncement that is law.) *Dicta* (s holy formulas) of faith. in the wisdom of others. Propensity to the mechanism of reason instead of its spontaneity under laws. let themselves be led. )",[]],"refl:2528":["Is a principle from subjective causes (s of judgments), which are falsely taken for objective grounds. Causes [are here not cogn] signify here grounds of the origin of a judgment [and], which are distinguished from the understanding. Prejudices must be principles.",[]],"refl:2529":["Prejudice is properly no judgment, but rather: what in sensibility is a preceding guidance toward judgment. No one will want to confess his prejudice under the form of a principle. I am not superstitious.",[]],"refl:2530":["A subjective *principium* of judging, which is falsely taken for an objective one: prejudice.\nAbove the beginning of the Rfl. is subsequently added:\nPrejudice considered subjectively is, |\nto which lines 406 19-20 (apart from the final word) are to be drawn as predicate.\nBelow the beginning of l. 406 19 is subsequently added:\nobjectively it is a principle (proposition).\nLater (θ? ψ−χ??) lines 406 19-20 are altered so that they read:\nA *principium* (propensity) to judge from subjective causes, which are falsely taken for objective [grounds] principles: prejudice.\nTo the right of the words written above, aus — Ursachen (l. 7), is then finally (in ω) further added:\nto make objective principles.",[]],"refl:2531":["The logic of provisional judgments as distinguished from the determining ones. The illusion. The sun moves. Before the investigation.",[]],"refl:2532":["45'.\nL 46:\nPrejudices are judgments that anticipate the understanding, and since this comes afterwards too late. The understanding is at first passive.\nL 45':\n(g The judgment of a passive reason, insofar as it is used as principle, is prejudice.\nWhence the name pre-judice?)\n(g An [judgment] objective principle of judging from subjective causes — illusion.)\nL 46:\nThe principle of a passive reason. The mechanism of reason in principles is prejudice. Mechanism is what is possible according to laws through the impression of external causes. Outside of reason is inclination.",[]],"refl:2533":["A principle from subjective causes of sensibility, which are falsely taken for objective grounds of the understanding.\nA principle of passive reason. Without objective reflection.",[]],"refl:2534":["Prejudice is the *propension* (propensity) of the understanding to judgment from subjective causes. These bring it about that one judges without reflection.",[]],"refl:2535":["A subjective *praesuppositum*, which is falsely taken for a *principium*.",[]],"refl:2536":["A judgment that precedes reflection. Reflection is comparison with rules of the understanding.",[]],"refl:2537":["The propensity (g to a certain way of judging) to judgment before reflection, not merely *iudicium praeceps*.",[]],"refl:2538":["Provisional judgments as principles are prejudices. Most provisional judgments arise from subjective causes. If they are valid as principles, then this always arises from subjective causes. This too is a propensity of a passive reason from inner *illusion*, from which one can become free through comparison with the judgments of others.",[]],"refl:2539":["Prejudice is the confusion of a universally operative subjective cause of judgment with the objective ground.",[]],"refl:2540":["Prejudice (g objective) is a principle of correct provisional judgments, which are there falsely regarded as determining judgments. e.g. the apple does not fall far from the trunk.\nsubjective. Inclination, habit.",[]],"refl:2541":["The illusion that is valid in place of a principle.\nSubjectively: the propensity to (g one's own) persuasion.",[]],"refl:2542":["Prejudice is illusion insofar as it is made into a principle.",[]],"refl:2543":["Prejudices hinder the proper use of our talents and reason.",[]],"refl:2544":["To accept something as true through a prejudice is always wrong.",[]],"refl:2545":["Subjectively, in prejudice there is the propensity to pass universal judgments from particular grounds.",[]],"refl:2546":["Obstinacy of prejudices.",[]],"refl:2547":["Prejudice is the propensity to regard the subjective necessity of a judgment as a [ground] proof of the objective.",[]],"refl:2548":["*Praejudicium* is a principle of judging that precedes reflection. Principle of a passive reason.",[]],"refl:2549":["*propensio causam iudicii in subiecto pro ratione iudicandi in obiecto habendi* [propensity to take the cause of the judgment in the subject for the ground of judging in the object].",[]],"refl:2550":["Prejudice. The subjective ground (g A maxim) of (g universal) judgments, which [as principle] (g falsely) is taken for an objective principle (of reason). Hence it is a maxim of passive reason. — Illusion is to be considered either as illusion of the understanding, i.e., ground of possible errors, or as illusion of the power of judgment or of reason. In the first case it is the illusion of sense in the single judgment, in the second that of the reflecting power of judgment (g and prejudice), which ascribes to the object that which is required merely by the action of the imagina|tion\nfor the [universal] common judgment, in the third an illusion of wit and of the faculty of discrimination in universal judgments, which regards that which belongs to some as a universal objective condition.\nPrejudice is a maxim [z] of judging objectively from subjective grounds. (g e.g. from examples of the judgment of others. or habit.) All mottoes (g as principles) are [Prej For] prejudices *in terminis*. (g Imitation,) inclination, habit.",[]],"refl:2551":["There are metaphysical, physical, moral ones.\nFurther L 46:\nCommon prejudice about unlucky days. dreams.\nFurther L 46 γ−ρ:\ndistinguished from the aesthetic.",[]],"refl:2552":["*Si omnes patres sic, at ego non sic. Nullius in verba.*\nPrejudice, moral, in experiences. Should move one to examine, not to give assent.\nWith this writings, also opinions, are received. Should move one not immediately to take something to be absurd.\nFurther L 46' from ψ−χ:\nThe authority of testimonies over one's own reason | of the empirical over the rational.\nFurther L 46 from θ (? ψ−χ?):\nFrom imitation. (g as contemporaries) In order to despise others.",[]],"refl:2553":["Mark of self-love: when one takes something to be false because one cannot have insight into it.\nFurther L 46 from θ (? ψ−χ?):\nThe self-loving.",[]],"refl:2554":["Since ancient times dreams have been believed.\nOne nevertheless makes use of them to confirm new things. *Praejudicium* [*consensus*]. (g *testimonium*. *Citationes*.) All peoples believe that there is a God, that the soul is immortal.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ), below the end of L §. 170a no. 3, above »praeiudicium — wenn« (l. 28f.):\nThe prejudice of habit.",[]],"refl:2555":["The newest philosophers assert that the soul has a body after death.\nThe new ones do not believe in the influence of the moon.\nFurther L 47 to the end of L §. 170a no. 4:\nor taken to be false because it is old.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) to L §. 170 »Neuigkeit« (l. 28):\nfrom the advantage that the descendants have over the older ones in aids. Innovations are partly unpleasant, partly pleasant.\nProvisional judgment.",[]],"refl:2556":["The agreements of things with one another support each other's probability.\nFurther L 47 from γ−ξ:\nOf the (g lost) effort of having learned it. Of the unity of explanation. to prefer subjective grounds.\nFurther L 47 (from σ−φ? ω??) to »Lehrgebäudes« (l. 27):\nof the great man.\nFurther L 47' (from γ−η) opposite the end of L §. 170a no. 5:\nThe prejudice for learnedness.\nThe prejudice for the common understanding.\nFurther L 47 left margin next to the end of L §. 170a no. 5 and the beginning of no. 6 (from σ−φ):\nto accept something through a prejudice.",[]],"refl:2557":["opposed is: when one does not take a truth to be true on account of the ease with which it is understood.\nFurther L 47':\nVerse.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) above »theil des faulen« (l. 29):\nOf ease.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) above »Vertrauens« etc. (l. 29-31):\nOf healthy understanding: when one has learned nothing. likewise of learnedness and in general of that which one has learned with effort.",[]],"refl:2558":["With reading just as with meditating.\nFurther L 47' from γ−κ:\nThe prejudice of unity.\nFurther L 47 (from µ−φ) above »der« etc. (l. 17):\nof common cognition *in concreto*.",[]],"refl:2559":["e.g. All opinions of the ancients are useless. *In historicis*.",[]],"refl:2560":["Our dear ancients did not judge so.",[]],"refl:2561":["The French with regard to the Germans. With regard to individual people.",[]],"refl:2562":["When, in opposition to great people, one imagines that one will surely not have better insight into the matter.\nFurther L 47' from µ−φ (? ω?):\nFavorable judgment to the advantage of the taste of the ancients.\nFurther L 47 from ψ−θ:\nNot everything accepted through prejudice is to be rejected, but certainly the prejudice αυτοσ εφα.\nFurther L 47' from θ:\nJurists: *Citata*. *Praeiudicium est vitium cognitionis formale* (not merely *defectus*).",[]],"refl:2563":["Of the prejudice of taste, indeed even of feeling.\nTo L §. 170a no. 1:\nOf the multitude in general or of individual men.\nThe former: either from logical grounds or from sensation.\nThe latter: because one [well to] is comfortable or likes to make others small (s and exalts only one. Envy has brought up *despectum*).\nTo L §. 170a no. 2:\nEgoism consists in this, that with regard to the cognition of reason one finds the judgment of others unnecessary and their contradiction as no sign of a hidden untruth.\nThe egoism of indifference or of self-conceit. the former only isolates itself.",[]],"refl:2564":["Because the universal validity of our judgments for every reason indicates objective truth, there follows (s that the judgment of others is an external criterion of truth) the [part] necessity of a participating reason, which is opposed to egoism; likewise the right to make one's judgments known, and love of honor as the incentive of the sciences. A participating understanding and participating will are always good; a correct and healthy understanding is always combined with uprightness, at least conversely.\nThe provisional judgment to the advantage of others consists in this, that, when others (g evidently) seem to have erred, one would rather believe that one does not understand them.\n(s The prejudice of the person: of one's own or of others*. (s Only in the historical is authority valid.) Truth must be valid anonymously. One must not ask whether it is begotten of noble parents.)\n*(s egoism: the prejudice of indifference to the judgment of others as a *criterium* of the truth of our own.)\negoism was later (ω) changed into egoismi and above it added: obsequii vel, to which »praeiudicium« is to be supplied from L §. 170. Finally in ω the words were further added:\n(s First, thinking for oneself. To think in the place of every other. thus to test one's judgment on others.)",[]],"refl:2565":["Because we find it necessary to teach others our judgments, we must not merely be communicating, but participating; and the drive to communication, which guides our understanding, is also in us only so that we should verify our own judgments through others.\nWhat has been able to survive antiquity must be good, just as the Russians who can survive their hard upbringing. (s But what is faulty has also disappeared.)\n(s The ancients invented everything beforehand.)\n(s The harm that great men have done through their greatness. It is not good to make oneself an idol and to think of a man as great.)\n(s Envy toward others makes a man excessively great. Spirit of imitation lulls to sleep. Reform becomes difficult.)",[]],"refl:2566":["The communicating inclination of reason is fair only under the *condition* that it be at the same time combined with the participating one. Others are not apprentices, nor judges, but colleagues in the great council |\nof human reason and have a *votum consultativum*, and *vnanimitas votorum est pupilla libertatis*. *liberum veto*.",[]],"refl:2567":["The authority of the multitude (s healthy understanding, *sensus communis*). The authority of the person. (s or of the multitude. In the historical — in judgments of reason — in taste.) Arises often from envy toward the rest. Is grounded through high praising. The prejudice of the kind of cognition: feeling, inspiration. The authority of learnedness. (s The unlearned. This the learned have toward the common understanding.) Of healthy reason. Of antiquity. Of distance. Common prejudice and learned.\n(g The multitude is either of the common crowd or of the learned (s citations).)",[]],"refl:2568":["People who grow old in a doctrine are very much for the old. Flighty, lively, fashionable ones for the new.",[]],"refl:2569":["47.\nL 47':\n(s A. 1,) The prejudice of* antiquity seems to come from this, |\nthat one believes the world is growing old. (s The prejudice of tradition (s *absqve autore*).)\n*(s authority arises, just as the supreme power of a single one, from envy toward the rest, so that these are equal.)\n(s Propensity to prejudice in religion, because we do not ourselves have to answer for what is handed down to us.)\n(s The subjective ground of esteem or rejection.)\nThe old also pleases us through an *illusion* of fantasy. (s just as the distant, rare. Acquaintance with the old or distant is itself learnedness.)\nThe prejudice [again] of novelty is either this: that one esteems the new highly because it contradicts the old, or: because it adds something. (s Gratitude.)\n3. (s To no. 1.) There is a [prejudice] grounded provisional judgment in matters of taste in favor of antiquity, which arises from this, that time as it were sifts writings and only the good ones have remained, just as one judges from some old and strong ones that all were so.\n(s Aesthetic advantage.)\n(s Because acquaintance with them and their language is learnedness and they, in a dead language, are the only enduring models.)\nFurther the judgment about linguistic correctness: [about] whether the expression is flat and common or too affected and forced, the characteristic of expressions, even the beauty of phrases turn out to their advantage. The ancients on that account do not stand under the law, but give the law themselves.\nThe need, given the changeable shape of taste in the progression of the ages, to have an enduring archetype, which must necessarily be composed in an unchangeable language, at the same time demands that it be a dead one, which is regarded as the means of learnedness. This makes the inconvenience of studying languages and not learning them through intercourse. Will one of our living languages ever in the future become a dead and learned language. Which will it be and will it displace the ancient ones. The present time is the most inconvenient and will become still more so if more nations cultivate themselves.\nIf one looks merely at the works of the understanding and not of taste, then a dead and learned language is not necessary.\n––––––––––\nIn order that a language be preserved as a dead and learned one, it is required [either] [that] not that works of the understanding and of reason shine forth in it, for this (g their) content can be preserved in any other language, but works of fine wit, especially poetry, whose beauty is peculiarly bound to the language. But because this is not yet a sufficiently important (g and universal) impulse to learn a dead language, there is no other motive than *religion*. Its documents and the first [defens] state of the church make necessary not only its language, but also related languages.\n(s 2.) It is the question whether the prejudice of antiquity does not come more from the [self-loving prejudices of novelty] difficulty that they had. One (s is first astonished, afterwards — —) admires very much that which surpasses the expectation according to a universal rule: thus we admire the reason of a woman or the science of a mediocre burgher, and it seems that the merit of such kind of people is greater (s because they have fewer aids than we), consequently |\nit is also in accordance with magnanimity to exalt it. Moreover there is something ugly in speaking ill of others after their death*, especially of one's teachers.\nL 47:\n*(s one can judge them and not condemn them.)\nL 47':\n(s 6. We would not have believed them capable of it.)\n(s 6. Astonishment is taken for admiration.)\n(s We admire the ancients because we have a low concept of antiquity according to the circumstances of their time.)\n( s (s To 2.) We admire a woman, but to the disadvantage of her sex, because we take the circumstances in which she is to be disadvantageous and hindering, and the ancients, because we regard the circumstances in which they lived as little conducive to their capacities. From this admiration of the overcome difficulty there arises through an *illusion* the immediate high esteem of their propositions. It also seems fair to judge the ancients with respect, because we are their pupils, and the coarse censure of them is uncivil. )\n(s To 3)) When one knows the ancients, one despises the moderns just as he who has traveled far praises distant lands at home and his fatherland abroad.\n(s The learnedness of knowing the ancients is admired.)\n( s (s 3.) Acquaintance with what is in distant time or space is itself learnedness, and thereby the old and foreign is admired. )( s (s 4)) We ourselves will one day become ancients, and we promise ourselves the honor that we show our forebears. )\n(s 4. Gratitude. 5. Envy toward contemporaries (s *pascitur in vivis livor*).)\n(s 6. From laziness: old people like to leave things as of old; *querulus*.)\n(s 7. Gratitude.)",[]],"refl:2570":["The mistrust of the whole of human reason.\nHeart.\nThe former besets the most rational, the latter the most honest.\n(s When one expects more of reason and of the heart than both can accomplish, there arises a hatred of reason and hatred of humans. *misology* and *misanthropy*. This befalls only the experienced.)\n(s Misanthropy. Misology.)",[]],"refl:2571":["The prejudices of approval have their ground either in high esteem for the person (s authority, antiquity, multitude) or in inclination (g imitation) (fellow-countrymanship, fellow pupils). (s *Praeiudicium rationalismi, empirismi*. Superstition. Trusts in *Facta*.)\nOr lack of importance of the judgments of others. One isolates oneself: egoism, through indifference or self-conceit.\nOr from our relations to cognition. Habit. Novelty. systematic unity.",[]],"refl:2572":["Prejudices are either of pride or of humility (s timidity); the former concern oneself and one's party, the latter |\nothers. To the latter belongs the humble respect of the common man for the learned, especially in matters of religion.",[]],"refl:2573":["Against sciences that have so often erred: *scepticismus diffidentiae*. Weakness is mistrustful. Also the fear of uttering something foolish and what is laughed at is the cause of an assumed mistrust.",[]],"refl:2574":["The judgment of the multitude is a *criterium* of truth. The servile.",[]],"refl:2575":["The prejudice of trust rests on inexperience and boldness. That of mistrust on experience and doubtfulness: hesitant.",[]],"refl:2576":["*Praejudicium est vel hominis vel* (s *facultatis cognoscitivae*) *temporis et loci. Prius vel Personae vel multitudinis. Hoc vel eruditorum vel intellectus communis.* [Prejudice is either of the human being or (s of the cognitive faculty) of time and place. The former either of the person or of the multitude. This either of the learned or of the common understanding.]",[]],"refl:2577":["*Praeiudicium hominis est vel personae vel multitudinis* [The prejudice of the human being is either of the person or of the multitude] (s *vel temporis vel loci* [or of time or of place], of antiquity, the new). (s *egoismus*, *pluralismus*. plu|ralism:\nwhere one believes one can do without the inner *criteria* of truth when there are many voices; what all the world says is true.)",[]],"refl:2578":["Whether it is also permitted to recommend prejudices or at least to leave them untouched. That means: to make everything immature.",[]],"refl:2579":["The fear that, if old prejudices fell away, the long-standing edifice would then collapse, is null. A grown human being has good feet even if one takes the go-cart away from him. (s Rich peasants are easier to govern than poor ones.)",[]],"refl:2580":["The prejudice of leaving human beings in immaturity under the guardianship of the ancients.\nThe way of being well-read instead of that of reflecting.",[]],"refl:2581":["The prejudice against an age, against an estate.",[]],"refl:2582":["*Praeiudicium vel egoismi vel pluralismi. Hoc vel personae* (of yet another besides me) *vel multitudinis. Posterius vel eruditorum vel rationis communis.* [Prejudice either of egoism or of pluralism. This either of the person (…) or of the multitude. The latter either of the learned or of common reason.]",[]],"refl:2583":["Probability is a cognition of truth through insufficient grounds. which however are nevertheless more sufficient than the opposite has. e.g. That the moon is inhabited.\nIf someone who is known in a house was present at the time when something disappeared, then there is a slight ground breaks off.\nFurther L 47 from µ−φ (? ω??) above L §. 171 »verwerfen — unsere« l. 27f.):\nDistinction: persuasion — conviction.",[]],"refl:2584":["The cognition of falsehood through etc etc.\nFurther L 47:\nthat a rich man steals.",[]],"refl:2585":["Every merely probable cognition is uncertain, but not on that account doubtful.\nDoubt is the equilibrium of judgment. e.g. whether there are [over] still more planets than we know. Whether a comet will ever touch the earth.",[]],"refl:2586":["The immortality of the soul is to Christians certainly true, to [freethinkers] Epicureans certainly false, to philosophers probable, to freethinkers doubtful.\nOpposite L §. 172 »unwahrscheinlich — können« (l. 19-21):\nOne accepts through a prejudice that there are ghosts.\nIt is probable that there is a [earth moves, not the sun] subterranean fire. It is also improbable.\nThe Christian religion is improbable in its mysteries, but probable in its historical credibility.",[]],"refl:2587":["A very great degree of probability is certainty.\nTo L §. 173 end:\nIt is probable that air (g which) is full of very coarse vapors is heavier than a pure one; but it is false.",[]],"refl:2588":["One should not without need [postpone] pass one's judgment, thus postpone it: unless the rules of prudence require it.\nTo L §. 174 sentence 2 beginning:\nin the degree that it deserves.\nTo the end of L §. 174:\nThis is no obligation, but sequel.\nCapacity for trust and for doubt.",[]],"refl:2589":["A.\nHistorical probability (g Mostly diminishes with time):\na.\nOne presumes that untruths betray themselves.\nb.\nOne takes a narrative to be without ground if the way in which the narrator could have understood it is hard to see. But one does not take it to be ungrounded in this if one knows no causes.\nc.\nOne infers from moral marks to the actions.\nd.\nThe inner possibility, likewise the interconnection, confirm it.\nThus from the constitution of the thing and the constitution of the witnesses (g multiplicity, of which one does not have it from the others). On the accompanying, preceding and following circumstances. Whether the witness has been able to tell the truth. willing. and confirmation.\nRare things have an improbability. e.g. Great armies.\n    ( g Historical: 1. Constitution of the thing. Inner possibility. Example: Rare ones not. 2. Constitution of the witnesses: Whether he has been able, willing the truth etc etc. Multiplicity of witnesses. 3. From the circumstances (g accompanying circumstances): At what time he said this. Preceding, following; in confirmation, not against agreement. )\nB. physical (g Does not diminish with time):\nIf [two things] the opposite of a thing is possible in many ways.\nIf two things are constantly alongside one another.\nIf a combination of many circumstances belongs to a thing, is not — —.\n( g If two things are always alongside one another. If the opposite of a thing is possible in many kinds of ways. If for a thing a combination of very many contingent circumstances is required. )",[]],"refl:2590":["e.g. a human being who is suspect is found at a murder.\nFurther L 49':\nThe probability of actions from their motives.\nFurther L 49' from ω (? φ?):\nShould properly read: I am in this judgment [certain] conscious of my conscientiousness.",[]],"refl:2591":["In probability one must compare the grounds that one knows for the thing not with the grounds of the opposite, but with the sufficient ground of certainty. The former constitutes only plausibility, *verisimilitudinem*. Probability in philosophical things is not to be measured, but to be sensed.",[]],"refl:2592":["When an inclination represents something as interesting, then one [falls] acts no longer according to probability alone, but multiplies into the importance. Smallpox in children. Thunderstorm.",[]],"refl:2593":["Objective probability (g *probability*.) is merely the possibility of the object, and its ground is certain.\nIllusion of truth from subjective grounds.\n( s practical. something can be personally acceptable without its declaring itself about probability. *Procax*. )",[]],"refl:2594":["With regard to the supersensible, if the cognition is not certain, then we can not at all therein breaks off.",[]],"refl:2595":["*verisimilitudo* gives the ground of a provisional judgment;\n*probabilitas* (s the insufficient ground) of a (g [practically]) determining judgment, to determine the understanding;\n*certitudo* (s the sufficient one) of a (s [logically] determining) necessary judgment.\n(s If the talk is not of the probability of things, but of judgments, then the only concern is that these approach truth in accordance with the rule.)",[]],"refl:2596":["The necessity of taking to be true is, if it is subjective, conviction, objective: certainty.\nThe possibility of taking to be true subjectively: plausibility, objectively: probability.\nProbability of things or of cognition alone; mathematics considers the first through wholly certain grounds.",[]],"refl:2597":["One can give no (g universal) rules of probability in general, except that the error of different people will not fall on one and the same side, but there must be a ground of agreement in the object. (g For error comes from subjective causes that are taken for objective grounds. But the former cannot be one and the same given the difference of subjects and circumstances.) Likewise: that, if from |\ntwo opposed sides there is erring in equal amount and degree, the truth is in the middle.",[]],"refl:2598":["If the moments of probability are homogeneous, then they are numbered (in mathematics); if they are heterogeneous, as in philosophy, then they are weighed, i.e., estimated according to the effects, these however according to the overpowering of the hindrances in the mind; but the latter give no relation to certainty, but only of one plausibility to another.\n(s The mathematician can determine the relation to the sufficient ground; the philosopher has only *verisimilitudo*. subjectively, practically sufficient.)",[]],"refl:2599":["The object is probable; but our cognition, as much as we know of the object, is true.",[]],"refl:2600":["Probability is truth from insufficient grounds, which however have a greater relation to the sufficient ground than those of the opposite. practically sufficient is: where the motive is greater.",[]],"refl:2601":["*Verisimilitudo*: the relation of the grounds for the assertion to |\nthose of the opposite, without having the measure of certainty. e.g. That planets have inhabitants is related to certainty as this end to the sum of all possible ends.",[]],"refl:2602":["Probability is the relation of the insufficient ground of taking to be true to certainty (more than half). Probable representation of things before investigation; not: probable cognition is to be accepted, but rather: examined; but the cognition of probability can be wholly grounded.",[]],"refl:2603":["The subjective ground of taking to be true, which is greater than that of the opposite, is plausibility (*verisimilitudo*), the objective etc etc. is *probabilitas*. This one can only know if one cognizes the relation of the grounds to the sufficient one.\nThe ground of taking to be true is either objectively greater [than t] or subjectively greater than that of the opposite.\nThis one can only find out if one compares the grounds of taking to be true with the sufficient ground; for then |\nthe grounds of taking to be true are greater than the grounds of the opposite can be.",[]],"refl:2604":["*verisimilitudo* is magnitude of persuasion, *probabilitas* approximation to conviction.",[]],"refl:2605":["If there are still 10 lots in the lottery urn and among them 2 prizes: how much can I stake against the lottery urn. [Not] 2 against 8 (grounds for against grounds against it); [but] my probability is 2/10, that of the lottery urn 8/10, thus 1/5:4/5=1:4.\n(s With two dice 36 cases are possible, among these 5 by which 8 is thrown, thus *probabilitas* =5/36.)",[]],"refl:2606":["Everything future is true (or the actual), but our judgment about it is not always probable.",[]],"refl:2607":["In cognitions *a priori* only mathematics can judge probably, metaphysics not; it must know, because cognition *a priori* is necessary; to opine therein is only a relation of subjective grounds on both sides and can suffice for belief.",[]],"refl:2608":["The probable judgment is uncertain; the judgment about probability must be certain.",[]],"refl:2609":["It is not probable that someone will hit 3 times in a row (if the dice have a point on half and nothing on the others). [but it not im] From this one should conclude: it is also not probable that, if he has already hit twice, he will also hit the third time. But it is not probable to get this series of hits [to have] in the whole, but certainly each part separately. He who has had much luck has not on that account greater apprehension of misfortune, e.g. of shipwreck or thunderstorm or of dying in old age. For in this case it is as if I draw a lot every year and the blanks remain, which always diminish in *proportion* to the previous prizes. Yet one can say: he who comes often must after all once run aground.",[]],"refl:2610":["In the whole of the infinite series of causes every action is determined, although, when it is to happen, it is free. For from the grounds that the human being can cognize it is nevertheless not determined.",[]],"refl:2611":["That what is contingent in each single case should nevertheless be necessary in the whole; thus it is in each single case naturally contingent what a human being does; but in the whole he nevertheless does what God wills; thus providence is mindful of the preservation of the species and has |\nabandoned each *individuum*. If human beings lived long, then every rogue would come to the gallows.",[]],"refl:2612":["The past free throw has no influence on the following one, but each throw has an influence *a priori* on the whole in the idea of the possibility of all throws together.",[]],"refl:2613":["48.\nL 48':\nIf the throws have already happened, then one does not ask about the probability of the |\nL 48: |\npast ones; for they are actual, it must thus not be brought into the calculation, but certainly when this improbable case is not yet actual. One represents the matter as if [the drawn] all possible cases (lots and blanks) were in an urn; now as many of the former as I have drawn, so many fewer would be remaining* and a greater relation of the blanks to the prizes. [Just as] Whether we do not have a certain *quantum* of |\nL 48': |\nsleep, of digestive power, since, the less we allot to ourselves now, the longer we can live on it, is the question.\nL 48':\n*(g but I must put back the drawn ones.)\n(g The Turks' means for long life.)",[]],"refl:2614":["Whether one can call a lucky general prudent?",[]],"refl:2615":["To draw correct rules from incorrect *observations*, if only the former are many.\nThe degree of possibility that the mathematician gives must be certain, i.e., in an infinite series of cases necessarily suffice.",[]],"refl:2616":["Distinction of probable cognition from cognition of probability. Widows' funds. Whether comet will hit the earth. Thunderstorm. Insurance.",[]],"refl:2617":["To draw the true from the multiplicity of *observations*. How the inclination of both sexes to a vice is related. Confessional. How many blanks are in a lottery urn.",[]],"refl:2618":["Between the first two lines of L §. 174:\nArt of deciphering.",[]],"refl:2619":["From the number of actual cases (white and black balls) to guess the number that there is of both.",[]],"refl:2620":["Properly one should say: the cognition is plausible, the object |\nprobable. For the cognition of the probability of the object must be true.",[]],"refl:2621":["If the grounds of taking to be true are not at all objective, then thereby no step at all nearer to the object takes place; hence one can there also not posit probability, e.g. whether the soul thinks after death; but rather cognition that rests on belief accepts that which is not at all probable.",[]],"refl:2622":["»A thing is probable«, one says only of occurrences. Otherwise: »our judgment is probable«.",[]],"refl:2623":["With regard to the supersensible there is no probability; we are not at all on the path of knowledge.\nWhether many insufficient grounds of different kinds (many proofs) constitute one proof?",[]],"refl:2625":["Practical certainty is distinguished from the pragmatic and moral.",[]],"refl:2626":["Morally certain is that whose cancellation would cancel all morality; the following two theoretical propositions are morally certain:\nThere is a God;\nThere is another world.",[]],"refl:2627":["What is certain under presupposition of good moral dispositions is morally certain; hence only subjectively and not sufficient for every understanding being. Thus I should say: I am morally certain.",[]],"refl:2628":["Conscientiousness is the way of thinking always [only according to] to [judge and] speak and to act with moral certainty.",[]],"refl:2629":["Above and \nWhat is certain according to laws of the ends of the will is morally certain. Logical *principium* — and practical aim.\nMoral certainty is only subjective, namely: to declare something to be certain with conscientiousness.",[]],"refl:2630":["Moral certainty (s is the probability that is sufficient for obligation.) is the necessity of the presupposition of a logically uncertain proposition as condition of an apodictically certain practical proposition (and indeed, under which the latter as law can first of all become maxim). Pragmatically I can be certain [that] through miracles that there is a God who commands us to do this or that which is in itself permitted (thus I am certain with regard to the contingent maxims of prudence). But morally I am certain that there is a God, because that is the presupposition under which alone apodictically certain practical propositions, *e.g.* you shall not deceive, can become maxims.",[]],"refl:2631":["One can only say: I am morally certain; for moral certainty merely expresses the conscientiousness of the judgment. E.g. There is no such thing in the court of heresy.",[]],"refl:2632":["What is certain according to the principle (*plinii*): *quod dubitas, ne feceris* [what you doubt, do not do], is morally certain (*probabilism* of the Jesuits).",[]],"refl:2633":["A doubt of the existence of God from the evils.\nFernerL 49':\nNot all doubts must make a human being doubtful or uncertain.",[]],"refl:2634":["Difficulties: what one cannot have insight into.",[]],"refl:2635":["Scruple about eternity, Trinity.\nFernerL 49':\nThe scruples remain when one cannot have insight into how a thing is possible, supposing that one also does not see that it is impossible.\nFerner L 49 aus µ−υ:\nsubjective doubts.\nFerner L 49 aus ψ−θ:\nTo resolve the doubt.\nFerner L 49 aus ω:\nThe [taking-to-be-true] opposite of being certain is subjective doubt, *e.g.* fear; the opposite [of the objec] from grounds of cognition is the objective. This is either objection or scruple.",[]],"refl:2636":["A difficulty is no objection.\nsubjective.\nobjective.",[]],"refl:2637":["Doubt is either objective or subjective. The first are the grounds for untruth, the second the uncertainty itself.\n(s Reciprocal objections are no proofs.)",[]],"refl:2638":["Doubt is merely a (g subjective) hindrance to taking to be true.\nObjection a (g objective ground) ground for taking a cognition taken to be true to be false.\nThrough the former [merely] our cognition becomes merely ungrounded. He who doubts asserts nothing.\n(g Doubts arise about the grounds; objections bear on the proposition.)",[]],"refl:2639":["It is modesty to put forward one's objections only as doubts.",[]],"refl:2640":["Doubt is sometimes taken subjectively as a state of an undecided mind, but also objectively as the cognition [a] of the insufficiency of the grounds for taking to be true. Objection is the cognition of the grounds for the opposite, although also with the consciousness of their absolute insufficiency, but yet of the relative *sufficientz* to hold the counterweight to the grounds for the truth.",[]],"refl:2641":["If someone has a project for something, *e.g.* to make a *perpetuum mobile*, and I put forward the doubt from friction.",[]],"refl:2642":["Ground taken against the infinity of the punishments of hell from the goodness of God is a doubt that has great illusion.\nFerner L 49 aus ξ−φ:\nThe ground of a provisional judgment; objective illusion for the understanding. subjective for inclination.",[]],"refl:2643":["In two ways: 1. [that the doubt does not overturn the thing] by showing, if it [at the sa] infers falsely. 2. That one explains the illusory [das Scheinbare] of the doubt and shows that it does not conflict with the truth.\nFernerL 49':\nfirst by uncovering the true, 2. the false of the doubt.",[]],"refl:2644":["One can raise doubts against the Trinity; but they are all answerable. But certain doubts are unanswerable without a purified logic.",[]],"refl:2645":["*Ad resolutionem dubiorum pertinet* [To the resolution of doubts belongs]:\n*Nodum resecare, non solvere* [To cut the knot, not untie it]. If one shows it is not necessary to answer it. *e.g.* Against the objections that are drawn against God's goodness from the evils. One cannot engage in all difficulties. *Difficultates non auferre, sed differe vitium* [Not to remove difficulties, but to defer them, a fault]. If one represents bodies to oneself as composed of little bodies. If one resolves a difficulty through another in which the same occurs. *e.g.* If one explains the origin of human beings from other human beings. (s Whence came the chaos?)\n*Ad resolutionem difficultatis pertinet* [To the resolution of a difficulty belongs]. *Status quaestionis* [state of the question];\n*quid pertineat, quid non* [what belongs, what not].\n(s *Respondere obiectionibus, non refutare* [To answer objections, not refute].)\n*Quaestionem obnubilare. non diluere* [To cloud the question, not wash it out]. Blackfish.",[]],"refl:2646":["To cut off the doubt is: if one [sho] opposes to it the truth of a clearer proposition.\nTo resolve the doubt is: if one shows its falsity.\nWith an objection the illusion must be explained.",[]],"refl:2647":["To cut the knot: to prove that a cognition is nonetheless true, or that its opposite has harmful consequences, let one object what one will.\nTo dispatch objections is: to show that one oneself understands nothing of what one objects, *e.g.* the evil in the world.",[]],"refl:2648":["dispatch.\nÜber »schlechterdings« etc. (44724f.):\nOne can have objective doubt that cannot be removed, and yet subjectively take to be true without doubt. morality.",[]],"refl:2649":["If the illusion is not resolved and explained (g its title examined and deduced), then the error is not cured from the ground. For the illusion can still deceive in other cases, and one also does not know whether the refutation does not itself rest on illusion. The subjective causes must be developed which are easily taken for objective, notwithstanding an otherwise clear-sighted reason.\nRefuting must always be undertaken with a *satisfaction* toward the rights of common human reason. Not *argumentum ab odio* [argument from hatred], so that minds are not divided with regard to the truth.",[]],"refl:2650":["The skeptical method is that of making the matter ready for verdict through the mustering of all grounds for and against.",[]],"refl:2651":["Not against mathematical ones, but scruples.\nZuL §. 177 »und — werden« (Z. 25f.):\n2. but if the doubts find entry with many great people and the answer is seen to be without effect, then it should at least make one cautious.",[]],"refl:2652":["1. That truth which only shows that something is, without showing how it is possible, is more exposed to doubts than others.\n*item*. That which has no known example.\n2. to the freethinkers the doubts of a Bayle, Toland, Marquis d'Argens are insoluble.\n3. This proposition is directed against Bayle. He asserts: truths of faith and truths of reason were so directly opposed to each other that |\nsomething must seem manifestly false according to reason, and indeed could not be seen otherwise than as false, that yet is in fact true.",[]],"refl:2653":["One can count the incorporeal nature of the human soul among the settled truths.\nFerner aus ξ−φ:\nwhich were long doubtful (g disputed) and afterwards become certain: lunar air. Southern continent.\nFerner aus ψ−θ:\n*exploratae* [ascertained].",[]],"refl:2654":["It is a (g un)settled truth that the soul continues its thinking uninterruptedly after death.",[]],"refl:2655":["Am Rand unten, wohl \nIt increases conviction if one oneself seeks out and resolves the doubts that can be raised against a truth.",[]],"refl:2656":["If someone [not] has something in mind which hinders him from the [insight] assent to the truth and cannot express it, then this need not be [explained] answered, it also cannot be answered.\nZuL §. 178 Nr. 3:\nAgainst the Christian religion many doubts can be raised which one need not answer.\nZuL §. 178 Nr. 4:\nThe doubts which [1.] attack the ground from which one is certain of a truth. E.g. the credibility of the witnesses of a historical occurrence, the inner possibility. [2. Removes the doubt from the ground, by showing what makes up the illusion of truth.]\nZuL §. 178 Nr. 5:\nTo this answer belongs that one puts forward the doubts in their whole strength and abstracts from the truth of the opposite so long, and afterwards refutes. Removes the doubt from the ground by uncovering the false illusion.\nIn return doubts contribute indirectly something to the certainty of cognition. He who in a matter against which doubts can be raised has never from the start had any, has not reflected on it enough. In a matter where the rules of prudence require acting according to probability, one should least of all let oneself be held up by the doubts.",[]],"refl:2657":["The uncertainty regarding the order of the weather.\nZuL §. 179 Nr. 2:\nThe ancients were uncertain [whether the blood circulates or not] whether the soul lives after death.\nZuL §. 179 Nr. 3:\nOne is uncertain whether the moon has inhabitants, because one cannot consider it from close by.\nFerner L 50':\nFor a *theologus* it is not blameworthy to be uncertain whether a *perpetuum mobile* is possible, but for the *mechanicus* it is.\nL 50'. Zu L §. 179 Nr. 4:\nIf one very often occupies oneself with doubts and turns more attention to them than to the truth, then uncertainty is sustained.",[]],"refl:2658":["Which then are the marks of certainty? 1. objective: *convenientia cum principiis catholicis* [agreement with universal principles]; 2. subjective: The light of conviction. Malebranche has only the latter cognition; 3. external: *consensus hominum et antiquitas* [consensus of human beings and antiquity].",[]],"refl:2659":["It is uncertain whether the world is finite or infinite.\n[*e.g.* that we do not know where the place of the blessed or damned is.]\nZuL §. 180 Nr. 2:\nIt is no disgrace for a jurist to be uncertain whether in the dispute about monads Leibniz or his opponents are right.\nFernerL 50':\nIt is necessary sometimes to confess uncertainty. Harmfulness of the mathematical method.\nL 50' zu L §. 180 Nr. 3:\nA theologian must not be stuck in any uncertainty with regard to the origin of evils.",[]],"refl:2660":["On the addiction to doubt.\n(s The Pyrrhonian and academic doubt.)\nOne ascribes the remote origin to Socrates and the *academicis* because they fought against the Sophists.\n*Dogmatici, Sceptici*. Pyrrho. *non liquet*. Denied the certainty of the *dogmatum*, not of experience.\n*Scepticismus. Logicus*. Liar. *Physicus*. Achilles. *Moralis*. That the rules of virtue were arbitrary. *Historicus*. Bayle.\nSome *Sceptici* denied the certainty of sensible sensations. Pherecydes had his pantry full of food and drink. He noticed that every day something was missing. He sealed the room. His servant took the seal off for him and sealed it up again. He complained of it to his friend. This one laughed at him. The servant made it too crude — — —\n(s Pyrrhonian and academic doubts. Arcesilas *et* Carneades.)\nHarmfulness of skepticism in morals. Theology.\nRefutations from their propositions. Arcesilaus said: even this is not certain, that everything is uncertain.\nGrounds that give occasion to skepticism. Discord of opinions. On rational Pyrrhonism. Basic rule according to it: where the rules of prudence do not require acting according to certain rules, one must suspend one's deciding judgment so long as distinct counter-grounds are still present.",[]],"refl:2661":["Rand unten:\nOne must have insight not merely into the uncertainty but into the insufficiency, consequently inadmissibility of the decision. To call something uncertain as to cognition in general is itself to pass an uncertain judgment. And would say as much as that something is uncertain in itself.",[]],"refl:2662":["Absolute skepticism passes off everything as illusion. It thus distinguishes illusion from truth, must thus after all have a mark of the difference and thus presuppose a cognition of truth. [For]\n(s Objective uncertainty can subsist together with subjective conviction, i.e. belief. Taking to be true can be objectively alterable but subjectively unalterable.)",[]],"refl:2663":["Critique is the art of judging the relation of taking to be true to certainty.\nDogmatism: way of thinking of [no other] affecting apodictic certainty. Skepticism: of admitting none.",[]],"refl:2664":["Dogmatists (g universal rational cognition from concepts) and skeptics. Asserting and doubting philosophers.\nMaxim is a subjectively practical principle. The maxim of doubting is skepticism.\nComplaints about uncertainty of human cognition and about the limitations.\nSkeptical method is: to put grounds together with counter-grounds into equal strength.",[]],"refl:2665":["The critical method suspends the judgment in hope of attaining it.",[]],"refl:2666":["The skeptical, dogmatic and critical way of thinking. Dogmatism is the doctrinal way of thinking* without critique (s of reason itself).\n*(s in philosophical [cognition] cognition extending itself *a priori*. The illusion arises here through success (g either) in [cas] mathematics or in such cases where experience confirms the propositions or the concepts are clarified through mere analysis.)",[]],"refl:2667":["Dogmatism is the [principle] prejudice*, of being able, in [reas judg] regard to cognitions of reason, to do without the critique of this faculty of reason itself (g for the sake of success), *e.g.* mathematics and physics — but not in metaphysics or pure rational cognition from concepts. — Skepticism: the prejudice of trusting no cognitions of reason for the sake of failure.\n*(g of the sufficiency of reason with regard to all its use, [also] without needing a critique of its own faculty. — This has brought forth skepticism. — Criticism is the [tru] trust in itself restricted to the condition of the self-cognition of reason.)",[]],"refl:2668":["Unterer Rand, unter Nr. 2655. Wohl zum Anfang von L §. 178:\nTo fear something it is not necessary that it be probable, but merely: that it be possible; but in order to hope, it must be probable. [because one not] Hence critique is more necessary than dogmatics.",[]],"refl:2669":["Dogmatism is the blind trust in the faculty of reason to extend itself *a priori* (g without critique) through mere concepts, [without] merely for the sake of the [succ] illusory success of the same.",[]],"refl:2670":["Dogmatism is the way of thinking (g maxim) of being exempt from all critique of the faculty of reason in judging even of those [which not through intuition] whose concept is capable of no intuition, merely for the sake of the illusory success.",[]],"refl:2671":["The supposed success in investigations of reason (g indeed in mathematics, but not on that account in rational cognition according to concepts) has brought forth dogmatism, i.e. the maxim of being able to judge, without preceding investigation of our faculties of cognition, about objects *a priori*, whether they be those of the senses or supersensible. But with regard to the latter the supposed success consisted only in this, that [one neither on a], if one only came upon no contradiction, no intuition (pure or empirical) refuted us.",[]],"refl:2672":["There is no probability in judgments of pure reason about ideas and their reality.\nSkepticism as principle renounces all assertoric judgment, belief the apodictic.",[]],"refl:2673":["something.\nAm Rand rechts zu L §. 181»gemeine Meinung«:\nE.g. that there were preadamites; that paradise was in the moon.\nIn L §. 181 über »Meinung « (Z. 46127):\nPresupposition.\nAm Rand rechts zu L §. 181 »gelehrte Meinung«:\nThat a comet made the Flood.",[]],"refl:2674":["Either at the same time probable or arbitrary.\nZuL §. 182 Nr. 2:\nBecause they are without probability and without use. *e.g.* That hell is in the sun.\nL 51'. Zu L §. 182 Nr. 3:\n3. to some it is an opinion that the earth turns, to others a certainty.\nL 51. Zu L §. 182 Nr. 5:\nBoundaries of opinions.",[]],"refl:2675":["ZuL §. 182 Nr. 5?\nHypotheses are indispensable. 1. (g They are attempts of the understanding.) One must approach many a truth by the path of probability. 2. They are encountered everywhere. *e.g.* dividing. 3. They show either the truth through the manifold agreement, *e.g.* The hypotheses of the motion of the earth. or by showing which grounds one has not to assume (g *e.g.* gravity). 4. They encourage the understanding through the prize of discovery.\n*Hypothesis subsidiaria*. If in (g the application) of a hypothesis (g or to show the agreements with the *phaenomenis*.) yet another occurs. Weaken the probability.",[]],"refl:2676":["Hypothesis: existence of bodies.\nFerner L 51:\nOne must examine it according to the rules of probability. it can attain to certainty.\nL 51. Zu L §. 183 Nr. 2:\nThey are the scaffolds that afterwards fall away. *e.g.* of Tycho.\nZuL §. 183 Nr. 3:\n*e.g.* That the earth is an animal. Kepler.\nZuL §. 183 Nr. 4:\nLove for this child of the understanding. *e.g.* Subterranean fire.\nL 52. Zu L §. 183 Nr. 5:\nIf that which I assume for explanation of something else is not known from other marks, but only from the agreement with the *phaenomenis*, *e.g.* magnetic matter.\nThe hypothesis must [be more probable than th] be more distinctly cognized than what is explained. thus not have the same obscurity as it. *e.g.* freezing from a cold-making matter.\nL 52'. Zu L §. 183 Nr. 6:\nFrom showing that no other can take place, and that it satisfies all *phaenomenis*.\nZuL §. 183 Nr. 7:\nDescartes.",[]],"refl:2677":["L 51:\nAn uncertain cognition, if it is taken to be uncertain, is called opinion; if it seems certain, then it is persuasion. This is at all times *formaliter* false. An opinion can be *formaliter* true and [for] *materialiter* false and conversely.",[]],"refl:2678":["The opinion of the truth of a ground from the sufficiency of the same for the consequence is hypothesis; if the impossibility of all other grounds is proved (g this happens if all consequences of the assumed ground agree with the appearance), then it becomes certainty; if just the same that is demonstrated through its consequences can also be proved *a priori* independently, then the hypothesis is confirmed. (s i.e. if the matter of the ground is independently certain or also the universal form. [only not the comb])\n(s The unity from the unity of the ground)\nThe fruitfulness of a hypothesis: if still more true consequences flow from it. Simplicity. That it not be arbitrary *a priori*.\n(s An opinion as ground of a system is hypothesis.)\nSome hypothesis is necessary, as: there is another world; some are contingent.\nThe absolutely necessary hypothesis is that of the necessary being. One cannot prove it *a priori*.\nFreedom is a necessary hypothesis without which practical propositions would not be possible. There is a God, another world: a moral hypothesis.\nThere are subjectively necessary *hypotheses* of the possibility of cognition through reason, e.g. that everything has a beginning.\nThe possibility of a hypothesis must be certain. rational *hypotheses* are not permitted.",[]],"refl:2679":["(s From the consequences I cannot safely infer to a determinate ground, except if the ground fits all consequences.)\nThere are three kinds of hypotheses: 1. Through the assumption of which I can explain a manifoldness of consequences agreeing with one another. (s Belief is no *hypothesis*, for it is not opining.)\n2. Without which I can have through reason no concept of the possibility of certain *datorum*.\n3. Without which the thing is impossible.\nThe supreme hypothesis would be that which [I] is in itself hypothesis and cannot be further comprehended through reason.\n*hypothesis originaria** is capable of no proof, [li] but is a *sumtion* that lies at the ground of the use of reason.\n*(g without which [another] a universal use of the understanding and of reason would be impossible.)",[]],"refl:2680":["51.\nL 51':\n1. The assumed ground must be certain as to possibility*.\n2. The *consequentz*. 3. Unity of the ground.** No *hypothesis subsidiaria*.\n(s 4.*** Sufficiency for all consequences. (g This is at all times uncertain.) This is absolutely not possible. Hence it always remains hypothesis.)\n*(s Possibility cannot be assumed through hypothesis.)\n**(s because otherwise the contingent combination of several assumed grounds is a new hypothesis.)\n***(g In metaphysics there are no hypotheses (s theoretical). Because the (g consequences are not given in experience) actuality of the consequences themselves is not certain in it *a posteriori*, namely it cannot be confirmed through experience. We cannot assume an ethereal body, because we do not even know certainly that we will come into combination with the corporeal world. But *a priori* these consequences cannot be cognized; otherwise the ground would be no hypothesis. Metaphysics bears on cognitions that, being so universal, cannot be proved through experience. It goes beyond the boundaries of experience. The consequences therefore are not given through experience.)\nDie bisherigen s-Zusätze stammen bis auf Z. 6f. (ω) aus ρ−χ. In θ kamen dann die beiden folgenden s-Zusätze hinzu, von denen der erste mit der 3 in 4671, die wahrscheinlich gleichzeitig in eine 4 verwandelt wurde, durch einen Strich verbunden ist:\n(s 3. It must contradict no known truths (appearances).)\n(s 5. Were it sufficient for all consequences, it would be certain.)\nSchliesslich in ω wurde rechts von 1. Der — seyn (46627) eine neue Nr. 2. eingeschoben; demgemäss wurde die 2 in 4671 in 3 verwandelt und vielleicht (wenn auch nicht wahrscheinlich) erst jetzt auch die darauf |\nfolgende3 in4 (vgl. 46723f.). Ferner wurden in weiteren drei s-Zusätzen (ω) die Nrn. 5-7 hinzugefügt:\n(s 2. The consequent truth must be certain. *e.g.* — — ghosts.)\n(s 5. The sufficiency for given consequences must also be certain.)\n(s 6. The sufficiency for all consequences remains uncertain, thus hypothesis always only hypothesis.)\n(s 7. Completely *a priori* no hypotheses can be forged, because the relation of cause to effect cannot be cognized completely *a priori*.)\n(s NB.[H] as *hypothesis* a proposition can be necessary in order to defend a cognition certain in itself; for to that end it is only necessary to show that it is not impossible. It is a right that one has against him who allows himself hypotheses.)\nAuf Rfl. 2680 (in der aus ω stammenden Form) scheinen sich auch noch die beiden folgenden Bemerkungen aus ω zu beziehen:\n(s E.G. The Copernican system. 1. That the earth turns is possible. 2. That the stars seem to move from morning toward evening is certain. 3. That this can follow from that is bricht ab.)\nL 51:\n(s 1) Not sorcery — Not *metempsychosis*. 2.) To want to explain something false through hypothesis, e.g. that it has rained stones. 3) if the consequence is also truth, but [the Cons] In|consequence\nof the connection with the ground. E.g. cold with the north wind.)",[]],"refl:2681":["All hypotheses are relative [presupposi] warrants to assume something arbitrarily, namely in order (g for) something that must have a cause, to find this. They are warrants to invent heuristically. Absolutely one may invent nothing at all. The unity of the ground is the proper mark of truth, especially the greater the manifold in the effects is. *Hypotheses subsidiariae* weaken assent. Natural occurrences do not permit that hypotheses can receive apodictic certainty, because we never cognize the effect entirely. Interpretation of scripture. Ancient history.",[]],"refl:2682":["The hypothesis is an opinion that something is because the consequences which are can be derived from it. The possibility of the presupposition must be certain.",[]],"refl:2683":["There are *canones* of reason *a priori*, i.e. rules without which no use of reason is possible. *e.g.* That certain laws, according to which alone we can bring appearances under rules, are true. Of these no opinion holds. *e.g.* Hydroscope. *Ventriloqui*. |\nMontgeron's Abbé Paris. Sympathy. This certainty is that of the rational use of cognitions.",[]],"refl:2684":["Necessary *hypothesis* of the theoretical (g moral) and practical use of our reason in general.",[]],"refl:2685":["What cannot be proved for itself, e.g. human freedom, must necessarily be presupposed with regard to another practically certain cognition, e.g. of morals, is practically necessary *hypothesis* of a theoretical cognition.",[]],"refl:2686":["If a *hypothesis* is necessary in order to prove something, then I cannot assume it without having made its possibility certain. |\nBut if the proposition is certain even without it and only the objection, which is also grounded on hypothesis, is to be warded off, then I can assume it without showing its possibility. So it is with the immortality of the soul, [as a] insofar as it is necessary for the energy of morality.",[]],"refl:2687":["That hypotheses as such can never receive complete certainty lies in the kind of inference, of inferring determinately from the consequences to grounds.",[]],"refl:2688":["The effects in experience are contingent and can have another cause; but morality is absolutely necessary and, since it is determined through concepts *a priori*, can have only an *a priori* determined condition.",[]],"refl:2689":["No opinion in mathematics, metaphysics, morals.\nThe *hypothesis*, subjectively necessary for reason, of the theoretical or practical use of every reason, *e.g.* freedom: *hypothesis originaria*.",[]],"refl:2690":["Hypothesis is [the truth the] the taking to be true which rests solely on the sufficiency of an opinion for known consequences.",[]],"refl:2691":["If it is in itself necessary that something should happen which however is not possible without a presupposition, then this is necessary. Now it is not in itself necessary to explain the existence of the world, but it is: to promote the highest good morally.",[]],"refl:2692":["*Hypothesis* is necessary if without presupposition nothing at all can happen with regard to a problem. (s or an objection against a certain truth cannot be refuted, e.g. future life for refutation of the objection on account of the evil of this life.) theoretical hypotheses are optional. A practical one is necessary.",[]],"refl:2693":["Hypothesis is a way of opining through reason (thus a necessity of concluding from subjective principles of reason).",[]],"refl:2694":["Hypothesis is a judgment of inferring from the sufficiency of a ground for given consequences to the actuality of the same.",[]],"refl:2695":["Question: whether a conviction or subjective certainty always presupposes the objective. whether thus 2 persons of equal powers of insight cannot be convinced of opposed propositions.\nThe rules of the guidance of the understanding which arises from the comparison of subjective cognition is *logica experimentalis*.\nBoth are right.\nCaution about the suspicion of prejudice.",[]],"refl:2696":["Zum zweiten Satz von L §. 184:\nsince certainty in the subjective sense does not agree with certainty in the objective.\nFerner über »Der Irrthum, durch« (Z. 25) aus ρ3−ψ (? κ3? µ?):\nnot merely error.\nFerner sind in γ−ξ die Worte »Der — sind« (Z. 25f.) am Rande durch folgende Bemerkung ersetzt:\nTaking to be true from grounds of which one does not know whether they are [subjec] merely subjective or also objective",[]],"refl:2697":["Of the greatest extensive clarity and small intensive.\nFernerL 52':\n*e.g.* to unmask dazzling inferences.\nL 52'. Zu L §. 184 Nr. 2:\nHence those who know no mathematics are easily persuaded.\nZuL §. 184 Nr. 3:\nFrom obscure insight into the grounds.\nZuL §. 184 Nr. 4:\nOne wants to reach the end. The examination is laborious. Occasioning motives are necessary. Rational prejudices. One must remember one's false persuasion and be cautious.\n5. If one takes the grounds of the opposite into no consideration at all.",[]],"refl:2698":["[A subjective ground of the] Taking to be true from subjective (g[sufficient grounds]) ground*, which is taken for objective (g [sufficient]).**\nIf we know that we are free of all subjective grounds and yet the taking to be true is complete, then we are convinced. The influence of subjective grounds is very imperceptible.\n*(s without being able [to investigate] to distinguish whether they are [not] objective or merely subjective, [whether] or taking the preponderance of the grounds that we know for the preponderance of the grounds in themselves.)\n**(s If we have grounds on one side and know none from the opposite side, nor seek any. *verisimilitas*.)",[]],"refl:2699":["Persuasion (g of honest people.) can be morally good although logically bad.",[]],"refl:2700":["Complete taking to be true from subjective grounds which in [certain] practical relation hold as much as objective ones is also conviction, but not logical but practical.",[]],"refl:2701":["objective. An extensive learned cognition about a principal *object*.\nFerner:\nAstrology is no science.",[]],"refl:2702":["The sum total of a cognition as*\n*aggregat* is common cognition, as system is science.\nThere are historical and rational science. In a science we often know only the cognitions, but not the things represented through them; **thus there can be a science of that of which our cognition is no knowledge.\n*(g in the *aggregat* is contingent, in the system necessary unity.)\n**(s Critique.)",[]],"refl:2703":["The manifold [is] of cognition is either an *aggregat* or a science. In the former it is (g fragmentarily) rhapsodistically*, in the latter systematically combined; in the former according to one and the same concept, in the latter according to an idea. The former cognition is that of the amateur, the latter of the master. The condescension of the latter is popularity, the singularity is pedantry.\n*(g technically (s according to similarities); in science architectonically (s according to affinities under one descent).)",[]],"refl:2704":["Art. from being able [Können].\nThe faculty of the exercise of a practical idea which does not follow immediately from the acquaintance with the rules. (s is skill. Systematic skill is art.)",[]],"refl:2705":["The unity of manifold skills, insofar as they hang together through an idea, is art. [The interconnection The] Being able [Das Können] from imitation and not according to an idea is craft.\nColumbus egg.",[]],"refl:2706":["Cognition without science, skill without art.",[]],"refl:2707":["Art is [systematic] skill in the execution of an idea which is not yet determined through [the] the common acquaintance with the [object itself] rules.",[]],"refl:2708":["System is a whole insofar as [it] (s the consciousness of its totality) is possible solely according to principles.",[]],"refl:2709":["Surveying is only mechanical art, for if one knows the theory, then one can do the latter. Properly, objectively art is [what] a skill which is not at all possible through instruction [and], to which imitation can also be counted, [thus] and can be called art of genius; but also subjectively much is called art (g which) in comparison with others is merely mechanical skill, which is opposed to the free skill, which one cannot learn.\nThe sum total of skills, considered as a system, can also be called art, i.e. (g as) a whole which is possible only according to principles.\nArt objectively is",[]],"refl:2710":["Morals. Logic.\nL 53'. Zu L §. 186 Schluss:\nOn the *gestus* of an orator.",[]],"refl:2711":["Chiromancy.",[]],"refl:2712":["either of the unconditioned from the concepts of the *subject* and *praedicat*. or of the conditioned from the combination.",[]],"refl:2713":["Through inferences grounded on experience.\nZuL §. 189 Satz 2:\nThe equilibrium of fluid matters.\nZuL §. 189 Satz 3:\nThat nothing can grow without air.",[]],"refl:2714":["(s all certainty is either theoretical or practical*; the former either [mathematically certain] empirical or rational certainty [(the latter apodictic)].)\nempirical or apodictic, and the latter intuitive or discursive.**\n(s I am morally certain of what it is necessary according to my conscience to assume as true. *e.g.* The deed of a criminal who confesses it.)\nan apodictic,\nMathematical proof, which has evidence.\nHypotheses cannot be proved mathematically.\n*(g The practical is also grounded either on empirical laws of the will or rational ones; the former: of prudence, the latter: of morality and of duty. I am morally certain if I know that to assume something as true and to act accordingly agrees with my entire duty.)\n**(s The practical certainty either pragmatic according to rules of prudence (there merely the rule [and its Hyp certain] is hypothetically necessary as with games of chance) or moral according to principles of morality (there the rules are absolutely necessary, and the *hypothesis* of their observance *in concreto* is thereby practically certain, an article of faith of reason). On the supposed moral certainty of the existence of bodies.)",[]],"refl:2715":["intuitive or discursive certainty. Both historical or rational.",[]],"refl:2716":["Morally certain is that whose taking to be true (g rests on principles which) are inseparable from the maxims [us] of duty.\nOne can say of no cognition in relation from the object that it is morally certain, but only of one's belief in relation to us, that it has moral certainty. I find myself bound to think so.\nMoral certainty is the greatest.",[]],"refl:2717":["theoretical, practical.\nFerner L 53 zu §. 190 Satz 1:\nI am not in a position at all times to cognize the truth. I cannot know whether there are not nonetheless creatures in the moon, although it has no air.",[]],"refl:2718":["Decimal reckoning.\nFerner:\nTo divide a circle into 360 degrees.\nFerner aus γ−ρ (? ψ−χ??):\nare arbitrary signs of thoughts.\nFerner aus γ−χ:\nNo truth is arbitrary, but it is *necessitated* by the *object*; they are *positiones*.\nFerner aus ψ−χ:\n*Positiones voluntariae*.",[]],"refl:2719":["Complete certainty from the distinct cognition of the interconnection of a judgment with its grounds.",[]],"refl:2720":["The ground [of a] of the [complete] [exhaustively distinct] certainty.\nL 54'. Opposite L §. 191 sentence 2:\nEither *demonstratio genetica* or *per rationes cognoscendi*.\nWhere the ground precedes the truth, i.e. without which the predicate does not belong to the subject. *e.g.* All human beings err. or *per rationes cognoscendi*: where from the sequel an inference is drawn to [the ground] the truth. *e.g.* Because one perceives errors.\nThe former *demonstrationes* are better.",[]],"refl:2721":["*Baculus stat in angulo.*\nTo L §. 191 »*probatio insufficiens*«:\n*e.g.* of the existence of God from the agreement of the peoples.\nFurther L 54 beside the last two sentences of L §. 191, from µ−ψ:\napodictic, intuitive certainty. demonstrative.\nrepresent *a priori* in intuition.\nImmediately certain.",[]],"refl:2722":["Of the subject and predicate: 1. The analysis of both; 2. the consideration of the connection.\nFurther L 54' opposite §. 192 beginning:\nEither not necessary or not possible.\nL 54'. To §. 192 »indemonstrable cognition«:\nWhere nothing distinct from the proposition itself (g neither *materialiter* nor *formaliter*, neither other things nor (g a resolution into) more distinct concepts) can be given as ground. *e.g. Qvicqvid est est.*\nOne must not out of laziness pass off something as certain and indemonstrable, nor out of prejudice.\nFurther L 54 from µ−ψ:\n1. indemonstrable as groundless assertion,\n2. [or] an immediate certainty.\nFurther L 54 from ψ−θ:\nnot to be proved.",[]],"refl:2723":["There is no *demonstration* except where the universal can be proved *coram intuitu a priori*, hence *a priori* and yet *in concreto*.",[]],"refl:2724":["One can prove something to oneself or to others. The latter works only where the grounds of proof and their interconnection can be communicated. That something is experience, one must often first prove to oneself, not, say, *vitium subreptionis* of cause and effect or imagination instead of perception (enthusiasts should do this, then they would not indulge in enthusiasm). Or in order to prove to others that something is [Ex] actual experience, which they, however, have not had, i.e. the proof of historical credibility. Proofs of reason *a priori* can be communicated.",[]],"refl:2725":["One is accustomed to say: that is as certain as that 2 times 2 = 4. But in philosophy no proposition is ever so certain (as to form). For it is to be apodictically and not empirically certain, hence [a pr] from grounds *a priori*; philosophical cognition, however, can have this certainty only [from] through concepts, mathematics only through the construction of them, the first merely discursively, the second intuitively. There an error can hide, here not.",[]],"refl:2726":["*e.g. theorema pythagoricum.*\nTo L §. 193 no. 1:\n[*Baculus stat in angulo, ergo pluit.*]",[]],"refl:2727":["No errors are small *in sensu absoluto*.",[]],"refl:2728":["Many proofs are good.",[]],"refl:2729":["The world is the best.",[]],"refl:2730":["refute someone.\nrefute the cognition.",[]],"refl:2731":["(s takes place only where the propositions actually are *contradictorie opposita*. (s A and non A, but not A and B, which /is not A. For there there can be a middle: namely that both are impossible.))\nIf of two (g contradicting) each refutes the proposition of the other, but neither can prove his own directly: then both have done their business well; they have both convicted each other of error, but neither has thereby proved his proposition. For a false idea lies at the ground, which, if it is accepted, necessarily |\ngenerates contradiction. *e.g.* The world of sense (as absolute whole) is either infinite or finite; both are false, because there is no absolute whole as object of the senses and no appearance that would take place absolutely and in itself.",[]],"refl:2732":["(s The given multitude of all parts (taken together) is either finite or infinite — neither of the two — the multitude of all parts is not given (together).)\nA body consists either of infinitely many parts or of a (g in itself) determinate finite number. Neither of the two; rather it is divisible to infinity, i.e. we determine the parts only through our *progressus*, and the concept of the parts lies only in this *progressus*; in itself [are] is the *phaenomenon* together with its parts nothing.\nThat everything has natural necessity (according to laws of appearance), consequently also the inner way of thinking of the human being, [which] of which the appearances perhaps give a very incomplete indication, is just as false as that actions as appearances are free.\n*E.g.* All flowers must have either one of these seven colors or a mixture of them; neither of the two: they can quite well be represented without any colors.\nThe world either has a beginning or it is from eternity; neither of the two in itself: it has neither in itself a beginning nor in itself an infinite duration, but is to be encountered only in our possible experience, which is [never] nowhere bounded, also is not infinite, but goes to infinity.\nWhether God is in motion or at rest in space?\n(g To assert: God is at rest because he is not in motion. Neither of the two takes place. He is in no place at all.)",[]],"refl:2733":["If the condition (g unnoticed) attached to a concept contradicts it, then [two] both opposed judgments can be refuted.",[]],"refl:2734":["In everything where a mere idea lies at the ground which we cannot give in intuition, *e.g.* absolute world-whole as to space, one cannot prove apagogically, because we do not immediately see that the ideas are empty or even contradictory, since for that [the A] other investigations are first required.",[]],"refl:2735":["empirical proof,\nanalogical,\napodictic.",[]],"refl:2736":["One can have brought errors into the proof. Example of the existence of God from many ends of things. *e.g.* Mystery. One can arrive at truth through nothing but error.\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\nIf experience contradicts the proof, then the proof is false.",[]],"refl:2737":["Harm that false and weak proofs effect.",[]],"refl:2738":["Most are mediate. The human being has much experience.\nFurther L 57':\nMuch is a mediate experience which we take for an immediate one. Hence the alleged error of the senses. Inferences from experiences.",[]],"refl:2739":["All cognition comes from the senses first; the senses give |\nindividual sensations; the understanding notices those which commonly are together and in what order they are together.",[]],"refl:2740":["Appearance, (g intuition,) perception (sensation with consciousness) and experience (perception according to rules).",[]],"refl:2741":["Experience is the agreement of perceptions (empirical representations) for the cognition of an object.",[]],"refl:2742":["That something is experience, one must often first prove to oneself: *illusio phantasiae vel vitium subreptionis*.",[]],"refl:2743":["I can well be immediately certain of my [experience] perception, but not of experience, i.e. of the objective validity of judgments from perception; for that, frequent comparison is required, in order to distinguish that |\nwhich the understanding does from the sensible. And often also the agreement of the judgment of others.",[]],"refl:2744":["Inferences drawn from experience must not be confused with experiences.\nFurther L 57':\nOne believed one experienced that the pistons in the pump drew the water.\nOne believes one senses that the sourness lies in the vinegar itself, instead of one's having to believe it lies only in the tongue, and the cause of this stimulation in the vinegar.\nL 57. To L §. 203 no. 3:\nfor the sake of the philosophical.\nL 58'. To L §. 203 conclusion:\nWhich certainty is greater: the [of the first] mathematical certainty of the first rank or others.",[]],"refl:2745":["It is to be proved from reason that the world is not eternal: then I must not appeal to the testimony of Scripture.",[]],"refl:2746":["If one is to prove that, if the air has been driven out of a pot by a fire, it will suck water into itself, then one must not appeal to experience.",[]],"refl:2747":["Experiences are either ours or those of other people.\nTo L §. 206 sentence 2:\n*e.g.* I want to prove that it does not rain in the lower part of Peru.\nTo L §. 206 sentence 3:\nHistorical belief. Living for the sake of the inner testimony.\nTo the concluding sentence of L §. 206:\nOne must believe only *res facti* * but not *veritates universales*, except from the testimony of supernaturally enlightened witnesses.\n* (s Historically we can also know.)",[]],"refl:2748":["To L §. 204:\nCognition *a priori* is that through reason.",[]],"refl:2749":["To believe something or to believe someone, morally or historically.",[]],"refl:2750":["Reason, experience, testimony.",[]],"refl:2751":["The inference from the sequels to the grounds should be called *a posteriori*, from experiences to universal judgments: *ab inferiori ad superius*.",[]],"refl:2752":["Moral belief: trust in the uprightness of another. Unbelieving in virtue and providence.\nTo do good on belief is without certainty of reward, since one is in any case content to have none at all for it.",[]],"refl:2753":["From above down and from below up. The former alone prove universal propositions, the second individual ones or also the possibility of things. A *demonstration* can well acquire elucidation through experience, but not confirmation. or it lacks evidence. Reason and experience must be subordinated to each other if they are to give one another the *complementum* for proof; if they are coordinated, then [is] they do not increase each other's certainty, as a *cavente* who has nothing is not strengthened by another who also has nothing.",[]],"refl:2754":["Historical belief is directed at that which is; practical at that which will happen. This latter, if it is a belief in |\nvirtue and uprightness, is moral. without this no society is possible. Faithful in promising and belief, in expecting. Unbelief in acting: *graeca fide*. One also believes that God will do something that he has not promised, because it [to] harmonizes with the dignity of virtue.\nIn historical belief it is first the relation to the object (s the possibility of the thing) (which [something] must be an experience of the other), then to the subject (its logical property, [experience] empirical: memory and capacity for speech, moral, and state of advantage or harm) that is to be known.",[]],"refl:2755":["One must never believe evil of others, but, if one is to act accordingly, one must know it. The good, if one has only a logical ground to opine it, one must believe.",[]],"refl:2756":["One can also call a theoretical ground of taking to be true belief, because one would well wager on it. *e.g.* That there are inhabitants on the moon.",[]],"refl:2757":["Testimony of objective cognitions of reason, testimony of one's own conviction.",[]],"refl:2758":["Belief is twofold: [as] either as to matter (g historical)\nas [princ] the ground for taking something to be true, or\nas to form (g intellectual)\nas the way of taking something to be true.",[]],"refl:2759":["Division into cognition of reason and cognition of experience. The latter into one's own experience and into cognition from reports.",[]],"refl:2760":["Experiments in regard to what one has judged *a priori*. Confirmation. test.",[]],"refl:2761":["only of what one has experienced.",[]],"refl:2762":["historical. There is also a belief of reason in regard to the practical.\nFurther ω? (φ?):\nTo believe something or to believe someone.",[]],"refl:2763":["Historical belief is rational if it is critical.",[]],"refl:2764":["He who is incredulous cannot supplement his deficient experience through reports. Takes everything for lies.",[]],"refl:2765":["Believing on a testimony is not at all distinguished from taking to be true through one's own experience in degree, nor in kind. But indeed believing on (g logically) insufficient grounds of reason. The former we can know, the latter is not a knowledge.\n(g Belief is either historical or belief of reason. The latter is practical.)\n(s Matters of opinion, matters of belief, matters of fact.)",[]],"refl:2766":["Matters of belief (*a priori*) are (s true propositions that nevertheless lie in no possible experience.) those in regard to which one can not only |\nknow nothing, but also opine nothing, and in regard to which one cannot even plead probability, but only that it is not contradictory to think an object thus. The rest is a free (g logical) taking to be true, which is necessary only in a practical aim given *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2767":["Unbelief can be imputed, but not the lack of historical belief.",[]],"refl:2768":["The greater the moral disposition, the more it happens from mere belief in all that which strengthens it; and in order that the actions too happen from the former, it is necessary that these incentives only be believed.",[]],"refl:2769":["Only 2 sources of cognition: reason and experience; for believing is mediate experience through experience attested by others. *formaliter* believing is opposed to knowing. *materialiter* to the communication of another's experience.",[]],"refl:2770":["Morally unbelieving is he who does not accept that which is indeed impossible to know but morally necessary to presuppose.\nBelief of reason is the cognition of the necessity of a hypothesis [of the theoret] of reason without which the (g absolutely) necessary practical laws would be entirely null. It is therefore a necessary hypothesis of practical reason.",[]],"refl:2771":["There is a belief of reason and a historical one. The first rests on subjective grounds of morality (g the objective ground is the possibility of the thing: absolute and conditioned), i.e. on the moral disposition, and on morality's being an incentive for him. The unbeliever disavows this incentive, and his lack of conviction rests on his fault and can be imputed to him. Historical unbelief as way of thinking also, but as lack of the [con] requisite grounds in particular cases not.",[]],"refl:2772":["(s *Citatio autorum* is not *testimonium*.)\nauthentic is the witness who can himself know something (g unimpeachable: if he not only can say the truth but also wants to), *testis exceptione maior*. A witness who needs another witness (s *testis subsidiarius*) is unreliable; insofar as there are objections against his sincerity, suspect. The series of witnesses after one another [di] does not increase credibility but diminishes it. *testes coordinati sibi dant complementum ad sufficientiam*. The testimony that something is from an eyewitness is often difficult. Frivolity, fearful readiness to believe.",[]],"refl:2773":["Believing *: he who acts on belief *a priori* **; is opposed to the slave of the senses.\n* (g theoretically: he who is capable of a moral belief of reason, where no knowledge is possible.)\n** (g Morality is of such a kind that he who is not capable of acting merely on belief that virtue and good conduct will one day obtain their good sequels, but to whom knowledge of it is necessary, does not act from moral principles. For a moral belief mere possibility is sufficient as objective ground. For theoretical, more is required, and there one cannot determine the measure.)",[]],"refl:2774":["Is the credibility of the testimony insofar as it can be cognized from the moral (g and physical) constitution of the witness.\nL 59'. To L §. 207 no. 1:\nIf someone claims to have seen something [that almost no] and has bad eyes.\nL 59'. To L §. 207 no. 2:\nHe must expect no advantage from what he testifies.\nHe must have an abhorrence of lies.",[]],"refl:2775":["He who would have seen everything is not credible.\nFurther L 59 from ρ3−θ:\n*subordinatio testium*.",[]],"refl:2776":["If someone narrates something from former times and cannot cite any eyewitness, he is not to be believed.\nTo L §. 209 no. 2:\nMuch is required for an experience. That one 1. not mix one's judgment with experience. 2. That he have the gift of attention. *E.g.* Ghosts.",[]],"refl:2777":["of a particular occurrence.\nFurther from ρ3−θ:\nwhether something is such a one or tested testimony.\nL 60. β1. Beside L §. 210 »lacks — enough« (l. 27f.):\nExample in Bouguer.",[]],"refl:2778":["He who is entirely disinterested speaks the truth; he who promises himself nothing good but from the truth, *e.g.* youth, also speaks the truth.\nIt does not follow: because I cannot see the good he promises himself, he has no aim.\nOpposite L §. 211 »And — good« (50635, 50721):\nHe can speak sincerely in his opinion; but he mixes his judgment with the senses.",[]],"refl:2779":["*E.g.* On a needle point a squadron of horsemen.\nAfter L §. 212 no. 2 Kant wants inserted:\n3. If all other presumable eyewitnesses are silent.",[]],"refl:2780":["Is maintained through mania for doubt. The aptitude not to believe a (g historically probable) testimony for the sake of rational probab|ility\nis called circumspection. The aptitude to devote [with] to every [slight] historical probability an assent suited to it is also thinking.\nTo L §. 213 »*credulitas*«:\nIs maintained through prejudices, through laziness.\nBoth are equally harmful.",[]],"refl:2781":["Unbelief is practical (g in regard to the sequels) insofar as one is not inclined to do good in hope of the other world which one hopes for. In regard to the grounds it is moral if one does not recognize the agreement with moral grounds.",[]],"refl:2782":["Practical, especially moral, unbelief is that [in which the] which has a lack of moral interest lying at its ground. For otherwise he would have to accept something. Morally certain. A believer is one who gladly believes what contributes to moral interest. *e.g.* Belief in virtue.",[]],"refl:2783":["Incredulous is he who will accept nothing on testimonies [except so] that is not sufficiently confirmed up to knowledge. (One must |\nhere supply much through [Hypo] arbitrary assumption, in order to be able to make the attempt to arrive at certain cognitions.) Commonly he is mistrustful and suspicious.\nUnbelieving is he who, from morally (s for the practical following of duty) sufficient grounds, will not concede something merely because [it] he cannot become theoretically certain of it. Shows lack of moral interest. The unbeliever does nothing on belief (in another world); he demands seal and letters. and cash.",[]],"refl:2784":["Unbelieving is he who will accept nothing as true except from theoretically sufficient grounds. For there are also practically-necessary hypotheses, and indeed ones extending practical use.",[]],"refl:2785":["Blind belief.",[]],"refl:2786":["Spiritual experience.\nTo L §. 215 no. 2:\nTheoretical belief.\nTo L §. 215 no. 3 from ψ−θ:\nOf the belief of reason, which always is directed at the moral. is distinguished from empirical belief.",[]],"refl:2787":["Many insufficient proofs never make up a sufficient one.\nBut many grounds of a single proof as *compartes* can only make up the sufficient ground of taking to be true.",[]],"refl:2788":["*Fides est vel asserti,*\n*vel promissi,*\n*vel speculativa; posterior si sit [moralis est] absqve promisso, est moralis.*\nBelief is properly a practical hypothesis [for the moving |\nof our will in regard to that], (s which is necessarily combined with that) which is cognized in and for itself as certainly good.\nOne does not say: I know that the human race has a beginning, but: I believe it. Hence it is a hypothesis under which alone I can understand its existence through reason, although I cannot myself understand it through reason. The beginning of the world and its infinite duration are equally unintelligible; the former, however, does make my cognition of reason complete, the latter not. (s That hypothesis is a necessary hypothesis of reason.)\nThus I believe every necessary *hypothesin*. This means: to accept something as if it were an arbitrary *position*; mathematical propositions one does not accept.\nOf propositions of reason that are believed. Are more than opinions. One cannot rid oneself of them.",[]],"refl:2789":["(s In knowing and believing, the subjectively unalterable taking to be true is belief, not opining.)\nProperly not 3, but only 2 sources of cognition*: experience and reason. 1. the former either our own or another's communicated experience [historical belief]. 2. Reason: either a knowing or belief, i.e. either from [the] speculative or practical reason. Belief of reason.\n(s NB. Taking to be true is distinguished either according to the sources or according to the kind and the degree. In the latter case it is knowing or belief. Historical belief is not a particular principle of cognition, but belongs to experience. Knowing belongs to theoretical, belief to practical cognition of reason.)\n* (s Both can be a knowing. If one calls them belief, then one recognizes them as uncertain, and in that case it is not permitted to risk on such a belief something that would be wrong if this cognition erred. *e.g.* Forcible conversion.)",[]],"refl:2790":["(s Reason-)Belief (as [a] not source of cognition) is a hypothesis insofar as it is held to be necessary in a practical aim. There is thus a pragmatic and moral [Rea] belief. The latter is the pure belief of reason — contributes nothing to cognition.",[]],"refl:2791":["Belief [in] of the truth of a proposition or of the actuality of a thing is what takes the place of a cognition, is not itself cognition. — Reason is also capable of a belief.",[]],"refl:2792":["*Fides* is properly fidelity in the *pacto* or (g subjectively trust) in one another, that one will keep his promise to the other — fidelity and faith. The 1st: when the *pactum* is made; the 2nd: when one is to conclude it. According to the analogy, practical reason is as it were the *promittens*, the human being the *promissarius*, the good expected from the deed the *promissum*.",[]],"refl:2793":["Believing is no particular source of cognition. It is a kind of taking to be true that is incomplete with consciousness and is distinguished [from opining], if it is considered (g as to) particular (g kind of) objects [restri] (which belong only to believing) restricted, from opining not by degree but by the relation that it has as cognition to acting. Thus, e.g., the merchant, in order to enter into a deal, needs not merely to opine that there will be something to gain in it, but to believe it, i.e. that his opinion is sufficient for the undertaking into the uncertain. — Now we have theoretical cognitions (of the sensible) in which we can bring it to certainty; and in regard to everything that we can call human cognition, the latter must be possible. Just such certain cognition, and indeed (g entirely) *a priori*, we have in practical laws; all these ground themselves on a supersensible principle (of freedom), and indeed in ourselves, as a principle of practical reason. But this practical reason is a causality in regard to [the] an equally supersensible object: the highest good, which is not possible in the world of sense (g through our faculty); nevertheless nature as object of our theoretical reason must harmonize with it, for in |\nthe world of sense the sequel (g effect) of this idea is to be encountered. We are thus to act in order to make this end actual. We also find in the world of sense traces of an artistic wisdom, and now we believe the world-cause also acts with moral wisdom toward the highest good, and this is a taking to be true which is enough for acting, i.e. a belief. — Now we do not need this for acting according to moral laws, for these are given through practical reason alone; but we need the assumption of a highest wisdom for the object of our moral will, toward which, besides the mere lawfulness of our actions, we cannot avoid directing our ends. Although this would objectively be no necessary relation of our power of choice, the highest good is nevertheless [unavoidably] subjectively necessarily the object of a good (even human) will, and belief in its attainability is necessarily presupposed for it.\nBetween the acquisition of a cognition through experience (*a posteriori*) and through reason (*a priori*) there is no middle. But between the cognition of an object and the mere presupposition of its possibility there is a middle, namely an empirical ground or ground of reason to accept the latter, namely in relation to a necessary extension [of our] of the field of possible objects beyond those whose cognition is possible for us. This necessity takes place only in regard to that where the object is cognized (g as) practical and (g practically) necessary through reason; for to accept something for the sake of the mere extension of theoretical cognition is always contingent. This practically necessary presupposition of an object is that of the possibility of the highest good as object of the power of choice, hence also of the condition of this possibility (God, [and] freedom and immortality). This is a subjective [possi] necessity to accept the reality of the object for the sake of the necessary determination of the will. This is the *casus extraordinarius* without which practical reason cannot maintain itself in regard to its necessary end, and here *fauor necessitatis* comes to its aid in its own judgment. It can acquire no object logically, but can only oppose everything that hinders it in the use of this idea, which belongs to it practically.\nThis [necess] belief is the necessity of accepting the objective reality of a concept (g of the highest good), i.e. the possibility of its object as (g *a priori* necessary) object of the power of choice. If we look merely to actions, we do not need this belief. But if we want [ourselves] through actions [the] to extend ourselves to the possession of the end possible thereby [acquire], then we must accept [it] that this [possi] is possible throughout. — I can thus only say: I see myself necessitated through my end according to laws of freedom to accept a highest good in the world as possible; but I can necessitate no one else through grounds (belief is free).",[]],"refl:2794":["Belief of reason can never be directed at theoretical cognition, for there the objectively insufficient taking to be true is merely opinion. It is merely a presupposition of reason in a subjective but absolutely necessary practical aim. The disposition [of] according to moral laws leads to an object of the power of choice determinable through pure reason. The accepting of the feasibility of this object and thus also of the actuality of the cause of it is a moral belief, which is a free taking to be true, necessary in a moral aim of the completion of its ends.",[]],"refl:2795":["One could oppose speculative cognition to the pragmatic and the theoretical to the practical.\nPragmatic is what moves the will (g thus is directly incentive) in conformity with a rule and thus produces action.\n(s The speculative is objectively practical.\nThe one opposed to it is subjectively practical.)\n(s Admonitions teach only what the other already knows, namely that vice is evil. But now the incentive is lacking that actually drives him to leave it. Mere eulogies and sarcasms do not make it up.)",[]],"refl:2796":["theoretical is a cognition insofar as it relates [immediately] to the object through the understanding (represents it as it is).\npractical is a cognition insofar as it relates to the object through the use of freedom (contains the action through which an object becomes actual).\nidle speculation is that which has no ground of conduct in itself. its opposite is *potentialiter* practical. *e.g.* God is just: is theoretical, but *potentialiter* practical.\n(s Practical is a cognition 1. in regard to the means,\n2. in regard to the ends.\nand the incentives.\nDistinction of tautological solutions from real ones.)",[]],"refl:2797":["theoretical cognitions are those which determine the understanding to judgment,\npractical cognitions are those which determine the will to action.\nNature and freedom.",[]],"refl:2798":["Practical propositions (s commands) are either 1) imperatives, and then they are opposed to the theoretical; or they contain 2) — the grounds for possible imperatives, and then they are opposed to the speculative.\nEverything ultimately comes down to the practical. This and not utility is the final aim.",[]],"refl:2799":["We can indeed speak in logic of practical cognition insofar as it is distinguished from theoretical, but not insofar as it is distinguished from merely speculative cognition. But it is tricky to determine what is merely speculation.",[]],"refl:2800":["Logical, aesthetic or practical perfection of cognition; the two latter do not belong to logic.",[]],"refl:2801":["theoretical: what is;\npractical: what ought to happen;\npragmatic: what contains the incentives to actions.\ntheoretical propositions are opposed to practical,\nspeculative propositions are opposed to pragmatic.\nmany theoretical propositions (*e.g.* the world is supremely perfect) are [objectively] pragmatic;\nmany practical propositions are nevertheless not pragmatic, but belong to speculation.",[]],"refl:2802":["Cognition is (g propositions are) either practical or theoretical.\nThe use of cognition either [theoretical] practical or speculative.",[]],"refl:2803":["*Philosophia* (g namely the part which teaches the material use of reason) also has[in] a merely speculative part (s *discursiva*). This is metaphysics of nature and of morals. One could call the second, namely physics, the presenting part: *Philosophia exhibitiua*.",[]],"refl:2804":["merely practical sermons.",[]],"refl:2805":["Utility is either in the understanding or governance of the will.",[]],"refl:2806":["He who practices religion must know that a God is.\nFurther L 63' to »in — lies« (l. 25f.):\nThe doctrine of the monads not.\nFurther L 6.3 beside »obtain — human beings« (l. 26f.):\npractical geometry.",[]],"refl:2807":["Nature of freedom in regard to duties.",[]],"refl:2808":["Of the human being.\nBeside L §. 226 »*cognitio — practica*«:\nComparative.",[]],"refl:2809":["how a mathematical line arises.\nL 64'. To L §. 227 no. 2:\nWhether *virgilius* or *vergilius* should be read.",[]],"refl:2810":["To a philosopher the acquaintance with monads (g human souls) is practical, but not to a *medico*.\nTo L §. 228 sentence 2:\nA theologian can out of ignorance take metaphysics for speculative.",[]],"refl:2811":["At least in regard to the understanding.\nTo L §. 229 no. 2:\nTo whom it does not belong within the domain of his science. *e.g.* to many a preacher philosophy.\nTo L §. 229 no. 3:\nHence it is good if all human beings pursue everything. If something is not useful to the state, it can nevertheless benefit human beings.",[]],"refl:2812":["Of the science of duties, of logic, of religion this is to be granted.",[]],"refl:2813":["In his main science; *e.g.* a philosopher mathematics, a theologian philosophy and history.\nTo L §. 231 no. 2 »that part of erudition«:\nWhich according to his capacities is more practical, in which he can achieve more.",[]],"refl:2814":["An object is sometimes practical, but *modus cognoscendi* speculative.\nthe former can produce good sequels, the latter produces them.\nThe former is an abundant water at a mill or a treasure with a miser.\nLiving cognition carries motives for acting with it,\nthe rational: rational ones, the aesthetic: sensible ones.\nIf someone [has cognized the nullity of the world and] abhors avarice from the ugly idea which the sight of other misers has caused him.\nThe tumult of the desires is opposed to the motives of reason.",[]],"refl:2815":["The Pharisees talked of holiness and lived unholily.\nThe judge knows what is right and yet does wrong.\nIf someone has weighed the end of his existence and the indications of true happiness and acts accordingly, then his cognition has a rational life.",[]],"refl:2816":["The doctrine of the Jesuits.\nTo L §. 234 no. 3:\nWith many scholars.",[]],"refl:2817":["otherwise only in one possible way.",[]],"refl:2818":["Effect on the faculty of cognition.\nL 66'. To L §. 236 »*cognitio symbolica*«:\nIf someone hears of equity (g Indecent.) and inequity and is on that account to avoid vices, then his cognition is merely symbolic.\nFurther:\nWe have a merely [intuitive] symbolic cognition of the motives of piety that are taken from happiness.",[]],"refl:2819":["Everything that is an object of our faculty of desire produces gratification.\nTo §. 237 »*taedium*« (? or to »*indifferentia*«?):\n*e.g.* When one [ge] hears a frosty sermon.\nL 66. To »move rationally — arouse displeasure« (l. 26):\nOn the faculty of desire.\nTo L §. 237 no. 2a:\nMere gratification in the subjective perfection of cognition is the cause of its not becoming living.\nFurther L 66':\nThe (g formal) perfection of cognition always produces gratification.\n*e.g.* of the doctrine of virtue.\nIf in the kind of cognition no motive is contained to know it (g in the most perfect way): then it is dead.",[]],"refl:2820":["The material perfection of the object must gratify.\nOpposite L §. 238 »both — either« (l. 18):\nIf one says: God commands to do this, and one does not see the gratification that arises from following these commands, then it is dead.\nTo the concluding sentence of L §. 238:\nSometimes the object is indifferent, but the cognition (g form) produces gratification. [*e.g.* To see the cause, of the *naturphaenomenorum*] So it is with the moralists.",[]],"refl:2821":["As actual and as ours.\nThe perfection of the world does not move enough.",[]],"refl:2822":["This produces a trust in oneself.\nTaming of the affects.\nHappiness in a freethinker.",[]],"refl:2823":["Otherwise it does not drive to effecting.\nL 67'. To L §. 241 no. 2:\nIf the passions still conflict, then the cognition is not living enough.\nPassions gain.",[]],"refl:2824":["So it is in the doctrine of morals, in the cognition of the utility of the sciences.\nFurther L 67' from β1−ε2:\nWhence the cognition of our duties toward God is not (g living? because it is not) intuitive enough.",[]],"refl:2825":["Both the formal and the material perfection of it.\nL 68'. On L §. 243 “*vita cognitionis eruditae spuria*”:\ne.g. A teacher of morals who defends vices merely through sensible grounds.",[]],"refl:2826":["Many a one judges thus with regard to erudition. Small moving grounds in equilibrium.\nOn L §. 245 No. 2:\nAccording to the constitution of the capacity, of the insights and of the affects. An ambitious one. Voluptuous one. Avaricious one.",[]],"refl:2827":["Only that one must be living, and also then, when it is to move.",[]],"refl:2828":["e.g. Loss of the Romans at *cannis*.",[]],"refl:2829":["*repraesentatio cum conscientia*.\nOn L §. 249 conclusion:\n*tres operationes mentis*.\nFurther L 69 from β1−ψ:\nnot all.\nβ1. L 69 in the margin to the right of L §. 249:\nPrincipal concept. Subsidiary concept.\n*complexus et incomplexus*.",[]],"refl:2830":["distinct, complete, exhaustive, determinate.\nOn L §. 250 “beautiful concept” (l. 25):\nof eternity.\nL 69. On L §. 250 “*conceptus pulcher*” (l. 26):\n*Complexus*.",[]],"refl:2831":["God omnipotent.\nL 69. On L §. 251 “magnitude”:\nImportant, fruitful.\nL 69'. Opposite L §. 251 “of certainty — be”, without doubt referring to the underlined word “practical”:\ngenetic.",[]],"refl:2832":["*abstrahendo*.",[]],"refl:2833":["Seeks useless methods.",[]],"refl:2834":["A concept is a reflected representation.\nThe matter of all concepts is the object,\nthe form of all concepts is universality.\nIn the latter there is abstraction from that whereby the manifold is distinguished.\nThe particular logical form is clarity, distinctness.\nSensation Intuition\nConcept\n}\nsubjective.\nobjective.\nForm is either intuition\nor reflection.",[]],"refl:2835":["*repraesentationes*\n{ s\n{\n{\n{\n{\n{\n(g with consciousness) *perceptiones* of that which is given, [*perceptio*] *repraesentatio a posteriori*\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n}\n(s *intuitus*) *conceptus** (s *forma logica*)\n{s\n{\n{\n*repraesentatio a priori: vel notio vel idea*\n}\n}\n}\n*notiones ideae* (s *communis*) (s *singularis*)\n〈\n(s *vel* [*per*] *intuitivus*)\n(s *vel* [*discursivus*] *cogitativus*)\n*(s *cognitio per conceptus est cogitatio, praesertim per conceptus a priori*.)\n*idea est conceptus archetypus*, contains the ground of the possibility of the object. It is the [Deriv] representation of the whole, through whose limitation others come to be. It is a single one (of the object), everything different is merely the limitation of it; e.g. *ens realissimum* is the transcendental idea. It can never be thought *in concreto*, but rather precedes all judgment *in concreto*. e.g. The idea of justice is the model-idea of the judgment of an aristides. Order, unity and perfection [can] are possible only in consequence of an idea, hence also the world, which is to contain such things. Whether contingent things do not always presuppose an idea as their *principium*, which determines them among everything possible. God is the inhering subject of all ideas as *ens realissimum* and also the first cause of everything contingent.\nMany sciences are presented without first establishing the objective idea. Morals, metaphysics.\nTo draw the parts from the whole.\nThe division of species is with us only nominal and arises from comparison; but it is real, if the idea were known to us. e.g. Of a horse and then what is altered in it according to the difference of circumstances. There must indeed be unity in the idea, namely in that which contains everything in a certain kind of relations or things.\nIn all sciences, chiefly those of reason, the idea of the science, the general sketch, the outline of it in the domain of all cognitions, consequently the whole of it, is the first thing one has to seek. This is architectonic. The idea of humanity, of the |\nrepublic. The idea of the science of right. That of a happy life is lacking in most.\nThe idea is opposed to empirical concepts. A perfect republic is a mere idea. Many human beings have no idea of what they want; hence they proceed according to instincts and semblance.\nAn idea cannot be obtained through composition. The whole is here earlier than the part. So the idea of the best world, in which things are determined. This idea is a whole which through limitation yields the parts; how that is possible is not to be comprehended.\nThe genius has the idea earlier than the partial concepts. An imitative head never arrives at ideas. In philosophy everything depends on the idea.",[]],"refl:2836":["69.\nL 69':\n1. *repraesentatio*.\n2. *perceptio* (with consciousness).\n3. *cognitio** (relation with consciousness to object) (s *perceptio obiective spectata*).\n(* s a perception which is related merely to the subject as a state of it is called sensation; that which is related to the object: cognition. )\n4. *cognitio est vel intuitus vel conceptus* (g *repraesentatio discursiva*). In the former I am passive (receptivity), in the second active (spontaneity). *intuitus* is single, *conceptus* is *repraesentatio per notam communem*. The understanding is here the formal cause of concepts.\n5. *Notio* (*conceptus*** *intellectualis*): when the concept arises from the understanding also as to content.\n6. *Idea*: concept of reason, (g *cui nullus respondet intuitus*,) (s which can have no object in experience,) when the concept of the understanding can have no object of experience *in concreto* and contains the archetype of the use of the understanding, e.g. world-whole. The idea (s can thus serve only for regulative (theoretical or practical), not constitutive use) must (g subjectively- for reason) be necessary, not for the empirical use of the understanding, but for the *principio* of the thoroughgoing interconnection of our empirical use of the understanding.\nIdea is a necessary fundamental concept, in order either to complete objectively the action of the understanding of subordination or to regard it as unbounded.\n**(g *conceptus est vel empiricus vel intellectualis*.)\n( s *Conceptus* (s *intuitus etiam*) *est vel empiricus* (s empirical cognition is called experience) *vel purus, hic vel intellectualis* (g *notio*) *vel rationalis* (g *idea*). L 69: (s is a concept of reason which is subjectively necessary to reason for its completion, but only in the intellectual. For in the sensible there is no completion.) L 69': a. *Conceptus est cogitatio*. b. *Exhibitio cogitati est relatio conceptus ad intuitum*. c. *Exhibitio a priori: constructio* (s objective reality of the concept through presentation). )",[]],"refl:2837":["Notion is a concept which is not drawn from experience; idea\na notion whose object cannot be encountered in experience at all; they can thus also not lead at all to actual objects, because these must all be contained in a possible experience; but they serve to [the] lead through reason with regard to experience and the use of its rules in the greatest perfection. or also even to show that [experience] not all possible things are objects of experience and that the principles of the possibility of the latter do not hold of things in themselves, nor of the objects of experience as things in themselves.",[]],"refl:2838":["Before, cognition in general was treated as propaedeutic of logic; now logic itself follows.",[]],"refl:2839":["The concept is here considered only subjectively*, not as it determines an object through a mark, but can be related to several objects.\n*(g Not how they arise as representations, but how given representations become concepts in thinking. Only the form of a concept.)",[]],"refl:2840":["There are ideas to which an approximation takes place, i.e. of the mathematical generation of a whole, and ideas which are wholly heterogeneous [from] to all concrete concepts, because the whole is distinguished from these not according to |\nmagnitude, but according to kind. As dynamical ideas. Nevertheless they are boundary concepts of the series of concrete concepts, e.g. freedom, to begin a series absolutely.",[]],"refl:2841":["Thinking is *repraesentare per conceptus: cognitio discursiva*.",[]],"refl:2842":["One can procure or prove for no theoretical idea [immediately] objective reality, except only for that of freedom, and indeed because it is the condition of the moral law, whose reality is an axiom. The reality of the idea of God can be proved only through this and thus only with a practical aim, i.e. to act as if there were a God, thus for this aim.",[]],"refl:2843":["Concept of present things is called sensation. The faculty is: the senses.\nouter or inner. Outer: through different senses.\nOn L §. 255 concluding sentence:\nImmediate: thunderclap. Mediate: that it occurs in the clouds. Of the faculty of thinking.",[]],"refl:2844":["We do not experience an occurrence that is narrated to us.\nBefore “only affirmative” (l. 36) Kant places a 2 and remarks on this on L 70':\nWe sense only what is present. e.g. that the wine is not sweet is no immediate experience.\nAll finite things exist changeably, e.g. thinking. Hence the faculty of thinking is not immediately experienced, all necessary properties in general not.\nThe negative ones not. e.g. the wine is bitter, therefore it is not sweet.",[]],"refl:2845":["*analysis*: through this it becomes distinct.\nL 70'. On L §. 257 No. 1 “weapons of the senses”:\nThese have discovered for us a new world in the small and the large.\nOn L §. 257 No. 2:\nWhen one dissects the human being through anatomy, plants through chemistry.\nOn L §. 257 No. 3:\nWhen in a thing coming into being the inner and outer combinations are observed.\nWhen one imitates an earthquake.\nL 71. On L §. 257 No. 4 “best marks”:\nconstant marks. e.g. When I see a human being in the motives of ambition, of voluptuousness.",[]],"refl:2846":["The objects are causes of the representations, thus these in conformity with those.",[]],"refl:2847":["All concepts are universal either of separation or of fiction.\n[The] The former have arisen either *a priori* or *posteriori*.",[]],"refl:2848":["A [abstract] universal concept *in concreto* with the object of the senses.\nThat *in abstracto*: concept of reason.",[]],"refl:2849":["All given cognitions *a posteriori* are given as sensations and appearances, or as concepts (g *conceptus*), i.e. universal representations, arisen through abstraction, and are called *conceptus*, are after-images, *ectypae*. The given cognitions *a priori*: *notiones*.",[]],"refl:2850":["All concepts are either empirical or self-standing concepts of reason or inferred concepts. The latter are the moral ones, which are neither abstracted from experience nor contain the mere laws of pure reason, but something which is thought in relation to the universal law of the will.",[]],"refl:2851":["The origin of concepts is considered in metaphysics and is either empirical or arbitrary or intellectual. But the form of it is logical and consists in the reflection whereby a *conceptus communis* comes to be, as that form which is required for the power of judgment.\nThus in concepts merely the difference of reflection is considered.",[]],"refl:2852":["*Conceptus vel dati vel factitii*;\n*dati vel a priori vel a posteriori*\nintellectual\nempirical concepts.",[]],"refl:2853":["Concepts are either given or made; the former *vel a posteriori vel a priori*. The latter either [arbitrary or natural] (s concepts inferred through reason: ideas. or arbitrarily invented).",[]],"refl:2854":["Logical *actus* in the concept:\nfirst the representation [of] of a *nota* as *communis*: *comparatio*,\nsecondly. This as ground of cognition of a thing: *reflexio*,\nthirdly the abstraction from that which it has different from other things.",[]],"refl:2855":["All concepts as to matter *dati* or *factitii* (g e.g. ®). The first either *a priori* or empirically given.\nThe form of a concept as discursive representation is always made.\n*Dati, a priori*, are *notiones*.",[]],"refl:2856":["The logical question is not: how we arrive at concepts, but: which actions of the understanding make up a concept, whether it may now contain something which is taken from experience, or also something invented or borrowed from the nature of the understanding.",[]],"refl:2857":["The (g transcendental) notions represent not things, but the *actus* of the understanding of making for itself synthetic concepts of things.",[]],"refl:2858":["Empirical concepts are therefore not concepts drawn from experience, but concepts that in part first make it possible. A concept of experience already presupposes empirical concepts, and [it] “to abstract it” means: to separate what one has previously thought.",[]],"refl:2859":["The origin of concepts according to mere form is logical and rests on abstraction from the difference of the things which are designated through a certain representation; as to matter they are *dati* or *factitii*.",[]],"refl:2860":["*conceptus qvoad formam orti reflexione, comparatione, abstractione*.",[]],"refl:2861":["Empirical concept: 1. of sensation, 2. of cognition; the latter is called concept of experience, for experience already presupposes concepts of the laws (of nature).",[]],"refl:2862":["Common separation. Dog.\nOn “make” (54923) from γ−ρ:\n*est conceptus datus, non factitius*.\nFurther from ρ2−ψ:\nThrough comparison and abstraction.\nOn L §. 259 beginning from λ−ρ:\n*cognitio discursiva per conceptus*.\n*in Mathesi simul est intuitiva*.",[]],"refl:2863":["nothing but wholly and utterly different things cannot be cognized through separation.\nlikewise human being and human being.\nOn L §. 259 Nos. 2-4:\n2. So that one arrives at distinct concepts. 3. through attention. 4. so separated they exist only in thoughts.",[]],"refl:2864":["Where many things agree in a common concept.\nOn L §. 260 “singular concepts”:\nGod is first an abstracted concept, finally a singular one.\nNot through experience.\nVirtue.\nFurther from ω? (φ?):\nare already concepts which are considered not in themselves but as in someone.\nβ1. On L §. 260 “contained in it” (55130):\nVirtue in chastity.\nL 72'. β1. At the beginning of the last sentence of L §. 260:\nBecause many agree in it, as: machine. Ocean.",[]],"refl:2865":["Through abstraction no concepts come to be, but through reflection: either, if the concept is given, only the form [or through reflex] and is called reflected, or the concept itself: reflecting.\nComparison and abstraction produce no concepts, but only the logical form of them.",[]],"refl:2866":["*Conceptus communis* (*tautologia*).",[]],"refl:2867":["Through a concept (g universal representation) to think something *in abstracto* or *concreto*. Through abstraction no concept is produced. All concepts are either given or made, invented (constructed). |\nThe given ones are either given *a priori* (pure concepts of the understanding) or *a posteriori* (empirical).",[]],"refl:2868":["*Perceptio communis est reflexa*.\n*Conceptus communis est abstractus, si non spectatur in suo inferiori; est concretus, si spectatur*.",[]],"refl:2869":["not: *aliqvid abstrahere*, but: *ab aliqvo abstrahere*; [I] it is negative.",[]],"refl:2870":["The most abstract concept is that which has something in common with nothing different from it, thus the concept of Something; for what is different from it is Nothing and has nothing in common with the Something.",[]],"refl:2871":["The (g logical) form of the understanding consists in the logical subordination of the *conceptuum communium*; abstraction is the condition under which *conceptus communes* can come to be. *abstrahe ab aliis*.",[]],"refl:2872":["All concepts have a domain of application and limitations of content: the first with regard to that which many have in agreement; the second with regard to that which is different in them.",[]],"refl:2873":["*Vsus conceptuum est vel in abstracto vel concreto, et hic vel in pluribus communi vel singulari*.\n(s I think through the concept an object *in abstracto* or *concreto*.)",[]],"refl:2874":["To represent something through a concept either *in abstracto* or *concreto*.",[]],"refl:2875":["With every *conceptus communis* comparisons must indeed be made, otherwise it would not be *conceptus communis*; but it |\nmay yet not first be formed from these compared representations.",[]],"refl:2876":["71.\nL 71':\n(g 1. attention: relation to consciousness.)\nLogical origin of concepts: 1. through comparison: [how in one consciousness] how they relate to one another in one consciousness.\nL 71:\n(g Comparison among one another.)\nL 71':\n2. through reflection (g with the same consciousness): [how they relate toward one another in one consciousness as identical or not] how different ones can be comprehended in one consciousness.\nL 71:\n(g whether one can have a concept without comparison with others and even before it yet as *repraesentationem communem*?)\nL 71':\n3. through abstraction: [through] since one leaves out that in which they differ.\n    ( s 1. *apprehensio variorum* (g apprehension). (g *comparatio* with the object for cognition.) 2. *reflexio*: reflection on the interconnection for the unity of the concept. 3. *abstractio* from the rest. — It is not always necessary to have comparison with others in order to get a universal concept, but rather consciousness of the possibility of the representation in manifold ways. )",[]],"refl:2877":["(g *repraesentatio communis*)\n*Conceptus* is opposed to *intuitus*: representation of that which is common to several objects, thus insofar as it can be contained in [many] different ones.",[]],"refl:2878":["to reflect means: to become conscious of representations little by little, i.e. to hold them together with one consciousness. To compare: to compare them among one another, i.e. to hold them together with the unity of consciousness. question: whether we can indeed separate something from a single intuition without comparison, in order to subordinate under it more things, if such should be found*.\n*(g We can become conscious quite solely of the action of the imagination, i.e. of the combination either of representations among one another or with our |\nsense, without looking at what is combined and its peculiarity, e.g. house. But the concept becomes clear only through application in comparison.)",[]],"refl:2879":["We do not abstract the mark of agreement, but from the difference.",[]],"refl:2880":["We compare only the universal of the rule of our apprehension. e.g. One sees a shrub, with it one can represent a tree; an oblong quadrilateral gives occasion for the square. The unicorn is a horse, for which the horn is taken from other animals.",[]],"refl:2881":["In a concept there is:\n1. the universal validity of a representation as mark;\n2. the abstraction from the difference of the objects in which this mark is.\nThe universality does not rest on the concept's being a partial concept, but a ground of cognition — by that shall I know you.",[]],"refl:2882":["*Abstractio*. We do indeed begin to abstract through comparison, but when we are practiced, we become conscious that [when] we can think a representation indeterminately with regard to several.",[]],"refl:2883":["71.\nL 71':\n1. *abstractio* (g *in*) *data nota ab aliis*.\n2. *Communio plurium in hac nota sive communicatio obiectiva*. This common validity presupposes, to be sure, a comparison, but not of perceptions, but of our apprehension, |\nL 71: |\ninsofar as it already contains the presentation of a still undetermined concept and is universal in itself.",[]],"refl:2884":["Hence what we prove of a [triangle] right-angled triangle, and indeed from the property of being right-angled, we prove of all, although in the singular. The partial concept (g can) be represented as ground of cognition *a priori* before all comparison on account of the faculty of the productive imagination.",[]],"refl:2885":["Not for the sake of importance, but of the domain which it comprehends under itself.\nOn L §. 261 “lower concept”:\nA higher concept can *diverso respectu* also be called lower.\nOn L §. 261 sentence 2:\ne.g. The organic body of the human being with regard to spirits.\nChristian virtue.\nOn L §. 261 “An abstracted concept” (l. 33):\nSeparation is subtraction.\nOn L §. 261 concluding sentence:\nHow I come to the concept of virtue.",[]],"refl:2886":["When they comprehend *heterogenea* under themselves.\nOn L §. 262 “*conceptus latior*” (56126):\nVirtue. Liberality.\nFurther φ? ω?:\nOne concept is not wider than the other because it contains more under itself, e.g. that of the human being and of metal, for that one cannot know; but when it contains the other concept and besides that still more under itself.\nβ1. On L §. 262 “reciprocal concepts”:\ncontingent, finite. Extension, body.\nOn L §. 262 “*conceptus universalis*”.\nRational beings are all human beings.\nOn L §. 262 “*conceptus particularis*”:\nNot all human beings are learned.\nL 73'. On L §. 262 third-to-last sentence:\nHuman being.\nOn L §. 262 “universal with a certain aim” (l. 34f.):\nwith regard to those to whom it belongs. e.g. All Moors are human beings.",[]],"refl:2887":["*Qvicqvid repugnat Generi, illud et speciei et individuo*.\nOn L §. 263 No. 2:\nWhat does not belong to human beings, that also does not belong to angels.\nI can indeed say: human beings are not omniscient and angels also not.\nFurther L 73:\nbecause it does not contradict that which they have in common with one another.\nL 73. On L §. 263 No. 3:\nThrough all *inferiora* one comes to *superius*. Through this the *vniversalia* arise.",[]],"refl:2888":["Abstraction is an auxiliary means of attention\nSymbolic.\nOn L §. 264 No. 2:\nextensive magnitude.\nL 73'. On L §. 264 No. 3 (? No. 4??):\nBecause that in which things agree flows from the universal properties, and that in which they are distinguished from the particular ones.\nL 73. On L §. 264 No. 4:\nIt can be applied to all particular cases. but not the particular.",[]],"refl:2889":["A universal concept, if it is abstracted from nothing but true concepts, is true.",[]],"refl:2890":["The [universality] subordination of concepts arises either analytically through abstraction or synthetically through fiction.",[]],"refl:2891":["*Conceptus communis*.",[]],"refl:2892":["(g *repraesentatio*) *Intuitiva et discursiva*.\n*Prior singularis. posterior communis*.\n(*abstracta et sphaera*.)\n*tanqvam nota continetur in aliis et haec sub se continet*.",[]],"refl:2893":["*Genus* and *species* are distinguished not according to their nature, but with regard to the *termini a qvo* or *ad qvem* in the logical subordination: *conceptuum communium superior respectu sui inferioris* (s *conceptus*) *est genus, respectu superioris species. hinc species infima sub se continet individua* (g [*nihil*] *non* [*est*] *concipi potest ut genus*); [*sum*] *genus summum non est species. Terminus a priori* (g *conceptus summus*) *est conceptus, in qvo ab omnibus entis discriminibus abstraxi, ideoqve in qvo qvam minimum continetur. terminus a posteriori: sub qvo qvam minimum continetur, nempe conceptus unicus. Primus paucissimum in se et plurimum sub se continet. Quantum cognitio ab una parte lucri facit, tantum ab altera mulctatur*.\n(s *species qvae non est genus, est infima non datur species infima propter legem continui*, [*nec*] *nec datur species proxima sive immediate subordinata*.)",[]],"refl:2894":["*Sphaera notionum* [*max*] *infinita et evanescens*.\n*Conceptus superior continetur in inferiori et hic sub superiori*; [*ergo*] *hinc dictum de omni et nullo*.\n*Conceptus summus paucissima in se continet, infimus plurima, nempe omnimodam determinationem. Qvo superior conceptus: ab eo pluribus abstraxi; qvo inferior: eo magis determinavi. igitur summus est maxime abstractus, infimus sive singularis maxime concretus*. (g *notio* from νουσ: *mens*.)",[]],"refl:2895":["On the fourth-to-last sentence of L §. 262 (56129-32):\nEvery *conceptus* can be used universally and particularly. Universally the *inferior* is used with regard to its *superioris*, particularly the *superior* with regard to its *inferioris*.",[]],"refl:2896":["The lower concept is not contained in the higher, for it contains more in itself; but it is yet contained under that one, because the higher contains the ground of cognition of the lower.\n*nota* contains the things under itself, and the things contain *notam* in themselves.",[]],"refl:2897":["Every *repraesentation* is either *superior* or *inferior*. *Con|ceptus*\nis always *repraesentatio superior*. But *conceptus* are called *superiores* only insofar as they have other *conceptus* under themselves.",[]],"refl:2898":["A mark of a mark — a remote mark — is a higher concept; the concept in relation to a remote mark is a lower one.",[]],"refl:2899":["Parallel between *ens summum*, which contains all things under itself as real ground. and *conceptus summus*, which contains them all logically under itself.",[]],"refl:2900":["To strike the proportion between the representation *in abstracto* and *concreto* in the same cognition, thus of [thought] concepts and their presentation, whereby the maximum of cognition [both] both as to domain and as to content is attained, is popularity.",[]],"refl:2901":["*differentia numerica* (s *Caius, Titius*) and *specifica* (s — *generica* |\n(differing *toto genere*, a *materia peccans sui generis*, μεταβασισ εἰσ αλλο γενοσ).)\n*numero diversa possunt esse specie eadem*.",[]],"refl:2902":["Domain and content.\nEvery concept as partial concept (but not every mark may be such a one) is contained in the representation of the things.\nAs ground of cognition, i.e. marks, these things are contained under it.",[]],"refl:2903":["Carthage's thongs.",[]],"refl:2904":["The *conceptus inferior* is always the *conceptus communis* considered *in concreto*; the *conceptus infimus in individuo* has the smallest *sphaeram*.",[]],"refl:2905":["1. A thing, a composite thing that can move itself. 2. This motion is either according to universal laws or according to particular ones. Here according to particular ones.\nL 74. On L §. 266 “This — not” (l. 29f.):\nOpaque body.\nAir.\nFurther L 74' on the same words:\nIf I abstract the concept of virtue from the Christian, civil, natural one and afterwards add these, then I have no new concept. but another is new, e.g. unchangeable virtue.",[]],"refl:2906":["Universal medicine. is an arbitrarily combined concept, i.e. the powder of immortality.\nOn L §. 267 No. 1:\nInvincible fortress. Model.\nL 74. Beside L §. 267 “perience — either” (l. 26):\na wind-wagon.",[]],"refl:2907":["All arbitrary concepts are synthetic and conversely.\nTo this *synthesi* belongs also severing, which is distinguished from separation; the former is a negative *synthesis*; in the former I deny a part of the whole, in the second I only do not think a part in the whole.\nAll concepts are either rational or empirical or feigned.\nThe latter are either *arbitrariae* or *subrepticiae*.",[]],"refl:2908":["(g arise from reason)\nAll concepts are either given or made.\nThe first are given either through experience (g *empirice*) or pure reason (g *rationales*, self-standing.); in the first, what reason does is only the universality.\nThe invented concepts are (g so) never as to matter, but as to form.\nThe form is 1. the sensible, 2. the rational form. The latter is impossible to invent, but indeed to apply the given form otherwise.\nThe sensible form consists in relations of space and time, of which some are given and cannot be invented.\nAll fiction is either arbitrary or non-arbitrary. The latter either sensible or rational. To the first belong hypochondriacal ones. The second are either aesthetic or heuristic. The aesthetic as to quality or quantity. The ideal concerns quality, that is the proportion of the determinations in the thing that is to please above all. Fiction as to quantity can please only in description, but not in sensation, i.e. magnitude pleases in appearance, but not in the concept.\nFiction occurs either *combinando* or *separando*.\nSeparation is removal, not abstraction. Whoever abstracts invents nothing. One abstracts in a teacher from his language, in socrates from his figure; happy the one in whom, when one abstracts from his clothing, rank, wealth, there still remain honor and respect!",[]],"refl:2909":["Sensations. Intuitions and concepts.\nIn the first there is merely matter, in the second form of sensibility, in the third form of the understanding through abstraction (universality).\n*repraesentatio. intuitus. conceptus. notio. Idea*.\nThe pure concept of reason is that which represents what cannot be contained in sensibility at all, consequently no matter, thus nothing but relations.",[]],"refl:2910":["Concepts of experience are also made, because they determine and compose the object through perceptions which I arbitrarily pick up. But because the object itself is given, one can analyze it.",[]],"refl:2911":["One must begin from definitions. even of the most common things.\nOn L §. 268 “determinate concept”:\nIt must be distinct, exhaustive, determinate. e.g. “The human being is a being that has reason” is not exhaustive, “is a being endowed with reason and an organic body (g that has affects and sin)” is not determinate.\na triangle has 3 sides and 3 angles.\nFurther L 74 from ψ−χ:\n*Conceptus rei complete determinatus*.\nFurther from ψ−χ:\nA sufficiently distinct, measured concept.\nFurther from φ−χ (? ρ−σ?):\n*conceptus rei adaeqvatus in minimis terminis*.\nFurther from φ−χ:\n*Cognitio distincta conceptum adaeqvans*.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\n[*Cognitio*] (g *Conceptus complete*) *distinctus* [*com*] [*plane*] [*complete*] (g *et praecise*) *determinatus*.",[]],"refl:2912":["or is overcomplete. e.g. God is an infinite, omnipotent etc:\nOn L §. 269 “unexhaustive”:\nunexhaustive. God is that being which governs the world.",[]],"refl:2913":["The inner sufficiency of the marks is the ground of the inwardly sufficient distinctness and is attained by the concept of the |\nthing being adequate. Through this alone is the outer sufficiency possible, consequently the *completudo*.\n(s Because the pure concept of reason and the arbitrary one gives the thing and is not given by the thing, it can only be defined analytically; it is capable of logical *completudinis* as well as real.\nDiscoveries *per analysin* can be made just as well with regard to logical as to real *completudinis*.)",[]],"refl:2914":["All empirical concepts, e.g. water, tree, etc: are representations of the understanding and can only be treated synthetically: I am not to dissect what [them] is in them, but to come to know through experience what belongs to them. The rational concepts, e.g. virtue, fate etc., can be treated only analytically.",[]],"refl:2915":["For distinctness it is required that the marks of the definition be coordinated with one another and not subordinated to one another, which latter, if it occurs, is contrary to precision.\nThere is namely a *tautologia implicita* of the marks among one another, just as in the *circulo definiendi* a tautology of the marks with the *definito*.",[]],"refl:2916":["Before all definition the materials for the definition go beforehand, and the judgments of them are passed in empirical concepts *per synthesin* and in rational ones *per analysin*. These judgments are *principia materialia definitionis*, and herein complete certainty can prevail; only |\none must look to the form of distinctness, i.e. without tautology. likewise to the true *synthesin*, namely that one mark is not subordinated to the other and yet is considered as coordinated (precision). As regards the form of definition, it consists in exhaustiveness. This can never be proved, but only presumed through more frequent use, from which it is also already evident that definition is not the first thing.\nReal definition is that which contains the [in itself] ground of that (g *rationem essendi*) which belongs to the thing; the logical: which contains the ground (g *rationem cognoscendi*) of that which breaks off.\nLl. 8-10 were in phase ξ−φ (hardly only in ω) for the most part struck through, and through longer s-additions the following text was produced:\n(s Nominal definition is that which suffices for cognition in comparison; real definition: which for derivation. e.g. The circle is a curved line in which all equal parts cover one another: is nominal, for few properties can be derived from it. It is not sufficient to give the object according to it. The merely negative definitions (e.g. concepts of the understanding are those which are not sensible) contain. The definition: “God is the absolutely necessary being” is nominal insofar, because I can cognize no properties from it except negative ones.)",[]],"refl:2917":["*in conceptibus empiricis non valent nisi descriptiones, quoniam semper augescunt notae*.\n*definitio realis et genetica differunt*.",[]],"refl:2918":["(g Logical) nominal definitions of given concepts of the understanding are taken from an attribute (g consequence from the essence); real definitions from the essence, the first ground of possibility.\n(g Logical) nominal definitions of given empirical concepts contain that which one always thinks in the concept with a word.\nReal definitions: what always belongs to the thing; the former: the logical, the latter: the real essence.",[]],"refl:2919":["2917 and 2918:\nIn empirical cognitions one derives nothing from the concept by which one designates a thing, but from experiences; in rational ones from concepts. In the former the [the] exposition of the name-concept serves only in order to mark out the [sign] object with a word.\nName-definitions are arbitrary.\nTo expound the appearance or the concept that we have; the former occurs *per* [*analysin*] *synthesin*. etc etc.",[]],"refl:2920":["declaration.\nexposition (analytic*: either of the concept *a priori* or of use).\ndefinition.**\nDefinition is either a precise declaration or adequate exposition; the former occurs in *conceptibus factitiis*, the latter *datis*. Exposition is either analytic: of that which (g I) already think in a universal concept (in the concept of the regular hexagon |\nI do not think the equality of the sides with the radii of the circle); it is synthetic when I add to it what belongs to it; the latter is empirical.\n*(g the sense, made distinct, of a given representation)\n**(g The determinate [interpretation of a] laying-out of a — — —)",[]],"refl:2921":["The *expositio* is either *completa* or not. The first *vel praecisa* or not. The latter is the description — — —",[]],"refl:2922":["*designatio* [a name-definition. e.g. Of natural things]: The determination of an object for a word (g According to linguistic usage), e.g. water.\n(s *Explicatio* (s *conceptus incogniti sive termini*): interpretation, not translating: *translatio*.)",[]],"refl:2923":["The distinct word-determination (g of a given expression) is explication. This *explicatio est gratuita*, if the word to the properties the breaks off.",[]],"refl:2924":["declaration* (s announcement) is a significance, made clear, of a sign.\nor the sense, made clear, of an arbitrary representation.\n*(g the distinctly [made] presented arbitrary concept)",[]],"refl:2925":["Through declaration a distinct concept is made.\nThrough exposition a given concept is made distinct.\nThrough definition a distinct concept is made complete and precise.",[]],"refl:2926":["Definition of the understanding or of reason. The former is nominal [the latter] and presupposes the given concept, the second is real, and through it the concept is given *a priori*. The latter is either breaks off.",[]],"refl:2927":["The definition is to be regarded like an equation or perhaps like ad/bc =1. Since I resolve a confused concept into the clear marks which together are equal to it, let the *definitum* be adbc: then the definition is a×d×b×c, and it is seen to that there be neither too much nor too little in the numerator, so that the definition not become *angustior* and *latior*. Precision is reduction *ad minimos terminos*.",[]],"refl:2928":["A definition is either of a given or of a made (*factitii*) concept. The former is either of a given thing or of a given notion. In that case it is the definition of an empirical concept through zetetic synthesis (*synthesin zeteticam*), in this through analysis of the given concept.",[]],"refl:2929":["The definition of empirical concepts must arise through empirical [concepts] synthesis.\nThat of the given concepts of reason through analysis.\nOf the arbitrary through synthesis *a priori*.\nTo make a distinct concept and to make a concept distinct.",[]],"refl:2930":["The mathematician in his definition says: *sic volo, sic iubeo*.",[]],"refl:2931":["I either give to a word its concept (g determination of the word) according to use or to the arbitrary concept a word and establish a use; the first is *declaratio nominalis*, the second *declaratio realis*.\nI determine for a given expression the meaning (*per explicationem*), or I give to a determinate concept a word *per declarationem*.\nThe explication of an expression.\nThe exposition of a concept.\nThe exposition becomes definition through the [*prae*] *completudo* and precision.",[]],"refl:2932":["Exposition is in the case of concepts of reason analytic (of that which is already actually (g *implicite*.) thought in it, not merely can be thought), of empirical concepts only synthetic, otherwise it is explication.",[]],"refl:2933":["In explication the meaning [or the S], i.e., the concept that attaches to a word, or the sense of a concept is determined.",[]],"refl:2934":["To the determination of concepts belongs 1. Arbitrary: denotation, where a universal concept precedes, under which something together with other things is ordered for division into classes, e.g. earths or blossoms of plants. 2. Given. explication. e.g. water. Both are nominal: the former for identity, the latter of diversity, both in order to attach a signification to the expression.\nreal are declaration and exposition.\nDescription is the [distinct representation] empirical distinctness.",[]],"refl:2935":["There are 1. empirically given concepts (body), 2. empirically made concepts (water).",[]],"refl:2936":["The definition of the empirical concept (s what I think through the concept of experience) arises *per analysin* and is at all times nominal; the definition of the object [can], which therefore is to be real, must at all times arise *per synthesin*.\nThe definition of the things of art is also arbitrary.\n(s Instead of the definition of empirical concepts their exposition, and indeed the empirical one, is necessary.)\nThe use of the definitions of empirical concepts is never in order to draw the consequences from the marks enumerated in them, but synthetic. The use, however, of rational or arbitrary definitions is at all times analytic. — definitions of empirical concepts are only for the understanding, and for understanding the word that one uses; and because the word contains much or little of the object, it is more an arbitrary determination. On the other hand, if the concept is given through reason [Be] in company with a word, then its meaning is not alterable, because no synthesis produces it or can alter it. I will not explain water, but describe it for the sake of the word. and then adduce the experiences of it.",[]],"refl:2937":["74'.\nL 74:\ndefinition of a given or made concept. The former either given *a priori* or *a posteriori*. *a priori* from the nature of the understanding, *a posteriori* empirically. |\nL 74': |\ngiven concepts through exposition, |\nmade ones through construction of the concepts; the former either through logical or empirical exposition.\n(s *conceptus vel dati vel factitii*. These *vel a priori factitii* or through *synthesin empiricam*.\nSynthetic, analytic definitions.)",[]],"refl:2938":["Nominal explanation is the one through ..., real explanation ....",[]],"refl:2939":["*definitio empirica* is often also lack of precision.",[]],"refl:2940":["*descriptio* is synthetic distinctness through foreign marks.",[]],"refl:2941":["nominal definition contains sufficient marks of the use of the word. Through it the concept does not become more distinct, but only the concept is determined for the word.",[]],"refl:2942":["All definitions are analytic (of given concepts) or synthetic: of made ones. the former *a priori* — or of empirical concepts; the latter also *a priori*: of mathematical ones, or of the objects of experience: since we do not define the concept of experience, but make ourselves a concept out of experiences.",[]],"refl:2943":["Every adequate concept of a thing is definition, not mere exposition. The definition is either synthetic or analytic. The former either *a priori* through construction or *a posteriori* through exposition of the appearances.",[]],"refl:2944":["The greatest logical perfection of a concept is the definition of it.",[]],"refl:2945":["Right margin \ndefinition of an object of experience is possible only synthetically, although the concept is given.\nThe definition of the [concept] given concept of this object, however, can be found analytically, but is of no use. It is a logical (nominal), not real definition.",[]],"refl:2946":["Nominal definition for distinguishing, real for deriving.",[]],"refl:2947":["All definitions arise either from the analysis of a given or the synthesis of a made concept. This synthesis is either of exposition or of construction. The made concept is either made arbitrarily or from given appearances. The concept is then not given, but the matter for it.\nAll concepts are either given or made. The former either given *a priori* or *a posteriori* (g empirically), the latter either made *a priori* or empirically. The first *per analysin*, the second *per synthesin*. In the former the concept is only made distinct, in the latter itself brought forth.\nWith given concepts everything happens through exposition, which is not always complete. With made ones through [decla] aggregation. If the concept is made from that which in the [object] object given *a posteriori*, then it is called a synthetic concept of experience.",[]],"refl:2948":["All definitions are either analytic or synthetic. — The latter are either empirical or produced *a priori*.",[]],"refl:2949":["Definitions of empirical as well as of mathematical concepts are |\nsynthetic, the former through empirical exposition, the latter through construction. The former give no definition at all.",[]],"refl:2950":["All making distinct of a concept is *explicatio*. All distinct representation of made* concepts is *declaratio*, of given ones: *expositio*, either of empirically or *a priori* given ones. The former synthetic, the latter analytic.\n*(g synthetic. With these the declaration must [precede] precede the concept.)",[]],"refl:2951":["*Arbitrary concepts must (s become distinct through declaration, given ones through exposition), concepts *a priori* may not always, empirical concepts can [not] never be defined (g only described) *Definitio*. *Expositio*. *Descriptio*.\n*(g The *completudo* of the analysis is never certain (s that of an empirical concept impossible). (s thus only in mathematics and in hypotheses can one begin from definitions.) Elementary propositions toward definitions.)",[]],"refl:2952":["description: Is the recension of the marks of a thing which can be used for definition.",[]],"refl:2953":["exposition is in the case of those given *a priori* analytic, in the case of those given empirically synthetic.",[]],"refl:2954":["Concept of experience is only a sign of experience.",[]],"refl:2955":["The exposition of the appearance is not the analysis of my concept into the marks that I think in it (or also think along with it), but the extension of my concept beyond what I think through it, consequently synthesis of that which belongs to it.",[]],"refl:2956":["Description is the exposition of a concept which is not precise; can take place only with empirically given concepts.",[]],"refl:2957":["There remain no concepts [to] as capable of explanation except those *a priori*, namely given through the understanding (analytic), and [synth] made, arbitrary ones (synthetic). the former philosophical, the latter mathematical definitions.\nEverything else is, with arbitrary concepts, declarations, with empirically given ones descriptions. Before the definition (analytic) exposition goes ahead. The empirically [given] made concepts are technical: *per fabricationem*, those made *a priori* are mathematical: *per constructionem*. Hence the fabrication of projects and hypotheses.",[]],"refl:2958":["All definitions must be made *a priori*, otherwise they do not define from concepts or construction.\nThe empirical ones are not definitions.",[]],"refl:2959":["The empirical concepts, except those which indicate nothing but object of experience in general (outer or inner), must all be regarded as made concepts, whose synthesis however is not arbitrary but empirical and therefore can never be complete. They thus cannot be defined.",[]],"refl:2960":["I may not always define: when a part of my concept suffices for the truth of my proposition.",[]],"refl:2961":["The definition of an empirical concept is the (g complete) exposition of the appearances, which is impossible.",[]],"refl:2962":["Only then do I need a definition, when I want to predicate the *definitum* of a certain other concept. e.g. When the question is: what is virtue?, then I need only exposition; but if the question is: whether holiness be virtue?, then I must know this concept wholly. I want |\nnot to know: what virtue be, but: which properties belong to the concept of virtue, and there I must know everything.",[]],"refl:2963":["Definitions of arbitrary concepts which others have devised can be found from the use of them, hence as of given concepts.",[]],"refl:2964":["One can after all define (g explicate) empirical concepts, if one can make the object. e.g. gypsum. cinnabar. sulfur. complete exposition of the appearance.",[]],"refl:2965":["Elementary propositions before the definitions. Synthetic definitions come first, analytic ones not.",[]],"refl:2966":["Approximation to definition is either exposition or description.",[]],"refl:2967":["There is a difference: to make something understandable to someone, and: to explain something to him. E.g. red is understandable to everyone, and no one can explain it. We understand something, however, in a twofold way: 1. when we can think the same thought, 2. when we can think its application. If the object was never given in experience or who cannot give it in experience, does not understand *in concreto*. |\nE.g. whoever has seen no shoe does not know what a shoemaker be. If one understands the mere thinking, of which one can at will give an object of application, then one can also explain it, so long as it is composite. Space and time, however, although in them composite objects enough can be thought, are nevertheless simple concepts which make composition possible, and cannot be explained; likewise in discursive thinking. There must after all in the end be simple concepts which can well be understood but not explained; but these are only logical actions of explaining, i.e., of a rule of representations in general, insofar as they are applied to objects in general. These logical actions of determining the representation of the object in general, i.e., of distinguishing it from others [without exp] prior to all experience, are called categories.",[]],"refl:2968":["definitions of mathematical concepts and concepts of experience must be made synthetically, those of the given *a priori* analytically — because the former have intuition as their ground. The latter require exposition beforehand.",[]],"refl:2969":["The circle is a figure that has no corners.\nFurther L 75 from ψ−θ:\nThe punctiliousness of the definition.\nβ1. L 75'. On L §. 270b:\nThe circle is a figure that has nothing but equal distances from the center, and whose [all] enclosed space is the greatest.\nOn L §. 270c:\nHas not marks and determinations enough.\nA human being is a rational being.\nA spirit is what is not flesh and bone.\nFurther L 75:\nRests on the first. For that through which I cannot distinguish things belongs to them all.\nOn L §. 270d:\n[has not enough marks] Has too many marks.\nFurther L 75':\nIf I say: the triangle is a figure whose angles taken together amount to 180°, then this does not hold of the curvilinear.",[]],"refl:2970":["The lower consists of the *genus* and *differentia specifica*.\nL 75'. Opposite L §. 271 sentence 1:\nIf I want to explain the distinct concept, then I must say: it is that through which I distinguish from one another that through which I can distinguish a thing from others.",[]],"refl:2971":["Because they extend equally far.\nOn L §. 272 No. 1:\nWhoever is a human being must have reason.\nOn L §. 272 No. 2:\nWhoever has reason and an earthly body is a human being.",[]],"refl:2972":["I cannot say: the human being is what thinks.\nFurther L 76 from ψ−θ:\n*nec plus, nec minus*.\nβ1. L 76'. On L §. 273b:\nEither king or subject.\nOn L §. 273c:\nWhen the negative occurs in opposition to the affirmative which have been explained.\nFurther L 76 from γ−ξ:\nNegative marks can indeed be just as good as affirmative ones for the distinguishing of a thing from others, but not for inner cognition of the thing.",[]],"refl:2973":["objective or material truth. e.g. chance, accident.\nIt must be a concept that corresponds to a possible thing.\nto that thing\ncorresponds which one at all times thinks when one names the word.\nsubjective or formal truth.\nOpposite L §. 274 “bei — der” (l. 31-33):\nIf I call a spirit what is not a body: then the concept is objectively true, but *formaliter*, examined according to logical rules, false.\nOne can also define concepts of which one can afterwards demonstrate a contradiction.\nβ1−ε2. L 76. On L §. 274 sentence 3:\nAn explanation can be a true proposition, but no explanation.",[]],"refl:2974":["This is the foremost rule.\nThe rule of the determinate must yield to it.\nFurther L 76':\nWhat is capable of no distinct concepts is also capable of no explanation. Colors. Taste.\nOn §. 275 sentence 2:\nLight is what makes the external objects visible to us.\nOn L §. 275 “aus — voraus setzen” (l. 35):\nOne can thus presuppose logical concepts in other sciences.\nOn L §. 275 “erst erklären” (l. 35f.):\nHence one explains in advance all concepts that are to occur in other definitions.\nFurther L 75 from ψ−θ. Below the end of L §. 275:\n*obscurum per aeque obscurum*.",[]],"refl:2975":["A body is the shriveling of the ethereal essentiality.\nOn L §. 276 sentence 2:\nThe soul is the monad ruling in an animal.\nOriginal sin is the preponderance of the lower powers over the upper.",[]],"refl:2976":["The explanation of ground;\nof magnitude: what can be increased or diminished.\nTherefore these concepts must be explained beforehand.\n(g A horse is an animal that neighs.)\nWhat do you believe? etc.\nL 77. γ−φ. Beside “Wenn — diesen” (l. 35f.):\n*imperspicuitas*.",[]],"refl:2977":["Opinions of some, that they be absolutely indemonstrable.\nOn L §. 278 sentence 2:\na. That it agree with the common concept.\nb. That it contain the first in the thing.",[]],"refl:2978":["A human being is an animal that laughs.\nGod is the almighty being.\nTriangle Is that one among all rectilinear figures which has the fewest sides.",[]],"refl:2979":["The mathematician can make no false definition, therefore he must be precise: *reductio ad minimos terminos*.\nThat one not possess (keep) more than one needs is a practical rule; that one not think more than one needs (*praecisio*), a logical one.\nOne can use incorrect definitions correctly as propositions and, if they are *latiores suo definito*, supplement them; if they are *angustiores*, prescind.\nThe mathematician expresses [in the subject d] in the definition the synthesis of the marks, in the *definitum* the whole idea according to the successive addition.",[]],"refl:2980":["There are 4 actions which I have to perform insofar as I examine definitions.\n1. Whether as a proposition it be true.\n2. As a concept be distinct, i.e., whether the predicate is also a mark and not the same as the subject.\n3. Whether as a distinct concept it is exhaustive, i.e., adequate to the thing itself.\n4. Whether as an exhaustive concept it is determinate.\nJust these actions are to be performed in the preparing of them:\n1. Seek true propositions.\n2. Such [which not already] whose predicate does not already presuppose the concept of the thing.\n3. Collect several of them and compare them with the [thing] concept of the thing itself, whether it be adequate to the consequences.\n4. See to it whether one mark does not lie in another (s be subordinated to one another).\nVerbal definitions are not distinctnesses of concepts, but exchanges of words, so that he who does not understand one may learn its meaning through the rest or, if it has been lost, retain it.",[]],"refl:2981":["Let a definition not be tautological (*obscurum per aeqve obscurum*), not wider nor narrower than its *definitum*.",[]],"refl:2982":["Let a definition determine the *definitum* not loosely but strictly [define]. *Conceptus vagus*: that which has no determinate meaning. Whether *notae* too must be defined, otherwise it would be called explaining *obscurum per aeqve obscurum*.",[]],"refl:2983":["Whether definitions can be proved. Yes, according to the elementary propositions, but not according to the *completudo*.",[]],"refl:2984":["(s Whether definitions should be proved.)\nThe sciences are full of tautological answers to my questions, especially insofar as they are to show how something could be done.",[]],"refl:2985":["(s 1. What concerns the *sphaera*.) (s *quantitas*.) *Definitio et definitum conceptus reciproci* (s *nec latior nec angustior*): *cui competit definitio etc etc.*\n( s 2. quality. *obscurum per aeqve obscurum* [*contra circulum*]. 3. relation to marks that are not identical or tautological (g but as grounds of cognition are different from the *definitum*). *Contra circulum*. 4. Modality [not without need negative marks or nominal definition] against the definition of concepts of experience, because these are not capable of the necessity of the marks and [no] do not sufficiently contain *ad essentiam pertinentia*. (g 4. Concepts cannot be defined through predicates that are added through experience.) )",[]],"refl:2986":["God as the infinite being, distinct from the world.\nThe real explanation of the circle — —.\nFurther L 77 from γ−ρ on “Sacherklärung”:\nfor insight;\nOn “Worterklärung”:\nFor understanding.\nFurther from κ−λ (? ν?? π−ρ??) on “Sacherklärung”:\nobjectively sufficient;\nOn “Worterklärung”:\nsubjectively sufficient;\nin arbitrary concepts there is no difference,\nin intellectual ones also not.\nFurther from ρ−φ (? ν??) on Sacherklärung”.\nin the generation (inwardly sufficient, and to derive the whole concept from it);\nOn “Worterklärung”:\nin the comparison.\nFurther lower margin from ζ (? η? κ??):\n*descriptiones* are comparative definitions.\nFurther lower margin from η−ρ:\nExplanation of the word;\nExplanation of the concept.\nFurther lower margin from κ−σ:\nThe one of these explanations cognizes in the object no more than the other. [But]",[]],"refl:2987":["if I God as the almighty being,\nOn “definitio accidentalis”:\n[if I regard him as the supreme ruler of the world]\nthat being to which everything owes its existence.",[]],"refl:2988":["One cannot always find the same. but in brass it can be found.\nThe circle.\nL 78. β1−ε2. On L §. 281 “mittelbare Erfahrung”.\nWhat makes the objects visible.\nL 78'. β1. On L §. 281 “Absonderung”:\nIn that I always abstract the most universal from all particular properties.\nOn L §. 281 “willkürliche Verbindung”:\nWhen the concept which I make is new. e.g. Of the medicament.\nOn L §. 281 No. 2:\nReal explanations must [then] not be given which are still in need of a proof. e.g. Of the circle from the *angulus ad peripheriam*; of God: that he be an immaterial being; *item*: of the soul.",[]],"refl:2989":["*essentialia reciproca*.\nE.g. Of the circle. Mirror.\nFurther L 78 from β1−ε2 beside “schaften — entweder” (60335f.):\nOf fire.\nL 78'. β1. On “durch einen Beweis aus der Vernunft” (60424f.):\nExplanation of a clear concept.\nβ1−ε2. On L §. 282No. 2:\nAnimal, what has a body which displays in itself desires and aversions.",[]],"refl:2990":["They are not wholly arbitrary. e.g. Sin is an action displeasing to God.\nFurther L 78 beside L §. 283 sentence 1 from β1−ε2:\nVice is an action which occurs against the will of God,\nor: whereby unhappiness is promoted.\nL 78'. β1. On L §. 283 “nach Wohlgefallen — ausmachen”:\nE.g. A triangle is a rectilinear figure of the fewest sides.\n*item*. A triangle is a figure whose angles taken together are equal to 2 right ones.",[]],"refl:2991":["Some we cannot. e.g. Representation, reality, (g position).\nβ1−ε2. On L §. 284 “Horizont unserer gelehrten Erkenntniss”:\nE.g. Thus in morals one must explain what duty is.\nOne may not, however, explain what eating, drinking, clothing is.\nβ1. On L §. 284 end:\n1. When one then explains logically, when one wants to have no learned cognition.\n2. When one makes far too many explanations.",[]],"refl:2992":["The *descriptiones* too are either nominal or real, either sufficient for distinguishing a thing (but not from all) or in order to derive the other properties from it. All definitions which are made only through comparison with others be descriptions.\nA nominal explanation has subjectively sufficient marks, a real explanation objective ones; empirical concepts cannot even be explained nominally, because one does not always think just the same thing with the name of the *definitum*. Since they also always grow, they are *descriptiones*.",[]],"refl:2993":["Logical definitions are all synthetic; real explanations of abstract concepts are all analytic. In philosophy all real explanations are impossible, but their grounds are empirical. We seek [with] *analytice* only the subjective sufficiency, but [objective d] *synthetice* the objective, in that we seek out not what lies in the concept, but what must be joined to it.",[]],"refl:2994":["All definitions are either of concepts (logical) or of things (real). (g Things are defined if they are given only through experience and the distinct complete concept is sought out from experience through observation.)\nThe definitions of concepts are either [of made arbitrary or of given concepts] of concepts (g which) are (g given) *a priori* or *a posteriori*. Of the former a complete explanation can be given. Of the latter [only one] the logical definition is only nominal. The real definition cannot be given at all. Concepts *a priori* can be synthetically |\ndefined, if they [are] have been given arbitrarily, or analytically, if they have been given *a priori* but not arbitrarily. e.g. Virtue, substance. *definitiones* are either diagnostic or genetic.\n(s All definitions are either synthetic or analytic propositions. The first either of empirical or of pure intuitions. The latter arbitrary.)",[]],"refl:2995":["The concepts that arise from the understanding can all be defined, whether they arise through choice or the nature of it. Name definitions are possible of all objects (often something of *positio arbitraria* runs in with it). real definitions, which contain the possibility of the thing itself, are only to be [given] found of concepts which are given through the understanding. [They are per anal they are arb] And here nominal and real definitions fall into one; yet with arbitrary concepts they are produced synthetically, with natural concepts of the understanding analytically; empirical concepts can only be explained nominally. Basic concepts of the senses not at all. (It is lexicographical if I make a word of one language distinct in another not through one synonymous word, but through many.). Various concepts seem to be empirical, but have arisen through the understanding. e.g. Earth: of which one nevertheless says that none pure is encountered. or should it be only the separation of a concept. There are real definitions of that which is given or also already thought, but not under the proper name.\n(g e.g. metaphysics)\nPedantry in explaining: when one [compels someone] determines the meaning of a word where it would not otherwise be confused, or also compels the other to grasp all marks where, in accordance with the end, some suffice.",[]],"refl:2996":["We expound either a concept or experience. The first happens *per analysin*, the second *per synthesin*. Expounding is the [clear repr] connected representation of the marks that are found *per analysin*. Even if we cannot define, we can nevertheless expound. In the case that the exposition is incomplete, we can avail ourselves of the concept *directe* (from the concept to the properties), but not avail ourselves of it to infer *inverse*, i.e., from the properties to the concept.",[]],"refl:2997":["A complete concept for complete distinguishing or for the complete (g derivation of the properties) comprehension of the thing itself. Both are in their objective completeness the same.",[]],"refl:2998":["The nominal definition is sufficient in the comparison (g of the *definitum*) with something else.\nThe real definition is sufficient inwardly.",[]],"refl:2999":["Nominal definition: for distinguishing.\nReal definition: for cognition of the object according to its inner determinations.",[]],"refl:3000":["All mathematical definitions are real definitions, only not those where it is said how something is made.",[]],"refl:3001":["Genetic definition is that which gives a concept through which the same can be presented *a priori* *in concreto*. all others are dianoetic, which suffice merely for comparison with [s] the known.",[]],"refl:3002":["real definitions are not always genetic, they do not concern the way the object appears through its consequences, but what it is according to its sources. They are not always delineating. e.g. The [feeling is] agreeable is [what] the feeling of the promotion of life. Here one must already know the agreeable.\nAll mathematical [concepts] definitions are genetic, the empirical dianoetic.",[]],"refl:3003":["Name explanations are those which contain the meaning which one has wanted arbitrarily to give to a certain name.\nThere are further certain real explanations which are either diagnostic or genetic. The former have arisen from the similarities or dissimilarities of things in comparison, thus through outer marks, the latter through the inner possibility. The former bring about a scholastic, the latter a canonical division. The former is a [compendium] method of bringing an order of coordination, i.e., of the places, into our concepts.\nThere are definitions for concepts which we already have and are not yet properly designated by the name. e.g. Metaphysics. And there it is not so much the meaning of the word that is analyzed as a concept that dwells in us, and there it must particularly be shown which name and how far it expresses it.\n(g Space is defined through pointing out, because it is no universal concept.)",[]],"refl:3004":["Given things of nature, when they are brought under a common mark, [give] stand through this mark under a determinate concept, which is their nominal definition. For the names are to be distinctions, serve for classes, indicate sameness in certain respects. But not properties and the inner, hence not grounds of explanation, but grounds of division.",[]],"refl:3005":["Nominal explanation: that which designates the object (also if need be through description).\nReal explanation: that which sets forth its possibility from inner marks. Objects of experience permit merely nominal explanations.\nDefinition of right as coercive law is nominal.",[]],"refl:3006":["A distinct concept which is inwardly sufficient is called real definition. That which is merely outwardly sufficient in a certain relation is nominal definition (s conventional meaning).\nThe unlimited outer sufficiency is impossible without the inner. (s For only through all necessary marks together can a thing be distinguished from all others, through merely some not.)",[]],"refl:3007":["All verbal explanation must be regarded as explanation of an arbitrary concept and is synthetic *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3008":["Whether *genus* and *differentia specifica* make up the definition. Yes, as nominal definition in comparison, not as real definition in derivation.",[]],"refl:3009":["Lower concepts have something agreeing and something distinct from one another. E.g. human beings and angels, insofar as they have something in common, make a higher concept, e.g. rational (g finite) beings, and what they have different, therein they contradict each other: the human being has an earthly body, the angel not; thus say: rational finite beings, either which have an earthly body, or not.\nRepresenting beings: either distinct or confused or obscure; distinct: either which are endowed with earthly or other bodies; confused: breaks off.",[]],"refl:3010":["far from its being the dividing of the concept, the members of the division rather contain more in themselves than the divided concept.",[]],"refl:3011":["separated concept together with the difference.",[]],"refl:3012":["E.g. human beings in the state in which they are merely through birth: *qvoad connatos habitus*, or *qvoad acqvisitos*: in the natural state or in the civil state.\nOn L §. 286 “codivisiones”:\nLearned or unlearned, governed or obeyed.\nL 79. β1−ε2. On L §. 286 “subdivisum”:\na member of the division.\nL 79'. β1−ε2. Opposite L §. 286 sentence 1, 2; probably on “codivisiones”:\nConcepts are divided either *ratione* of origin into *connatas aut acquisitas* or *ratione* of clarity into *claras* and *obscuras* or *ratione* of the [practical] consequences into practical and theoretical.",[]],"refl:3013":["E.g. I cannot say: all human beings are either Jews or Christians.\nThe ancients divided into bodies and spirits.\nFurther L 79 from φ (? ω?):\nToo few members of the division. E.g. All human beings are either virtuous or vicious.",[]],"refl:3014":["If I divide the learned into such as know much, as know little, and as know nothing at all.\nFurther L 79 from φ (? ω?):\nToo many members of the division. E.g. surface of the earth: land, sea and air.\nFurther from γ−τ:\nVirtue or vice (indifferent), holiness.",[]],"refl:3015":["I cannot say: all human beings are either virtuous or vicious or philosophers. (s For one does not belong under the concept of the other.)\n*figurae rectilineae. curvilineae. mixtilineae.* (s Not: ideas are clear or distinct.)",[]],"refl:3016":["they must be contained under it, hence belong to it.\nFurther L 80' from β1:\nSpirits which have understanding and which have none.",[]],"refl:3017":["Because then the members of the subdivision belong to the upper and are not opposed to it. e.g. Concepts into clear, obscure and distinct. For distinct is clear. or triangles into isosceles, equilateral, scalene. For equilateral belong under isosceles. One must divide into triangles which have either [nothing but] some or all equal sides or none at all.",[]],"refl:3018":["E. ex. of the species of creatures. Posit two: simple and composite: divide the simple into such as obscure, (g confused,) clear or distinct breaks off.",[]],"refl:3019":["All figures are either rectilinear or curvilinear or mixtilinear, thus a circle.\nAll human beings are either virtuous or vicious, thus also Socrates.\nFurther L 80 from β1−ε2:\nto whomever the title of a virtuous human being belongs, to him belongs the title of human being.\nβ1. L 80'. On L §. 289 “wem der — Eintheilung zu” (l. 26f.):\nAll goods are either chiefly of the soul or of the body or both at once. Sin is no good.",[]],"refl:3020":["When one has divided into clear and obscure, confused and distinct representations, then one can thereby form learned concepts. (g What is an animal — — — —.)\nOne knows to what higher concepts one is to put them, and what their difference is.\n[All properties of bodies are either the figure or the magnitude. All si]",[]],"refl:3021":["*divisio in opposita vel disparata**.\n*prior est infinita* [*s. in*], *posterior* [*determi*] *definita*.\n**We divide the concept or we divide it up. In the former case we seek what is contained in it: *divisio metaphysica*; in the second: what is contained under it. (*divisio logica sphaerae*). In this case |\n[contain the] arise *membra*, not *partes*. and contain more in themselves than the *conceptus divisus*.\nHence there is a difference between the *judicium logicum* and *metaphysicum*.\n*(s The latter, if it happens *a priori* through concepts, is at all times trichotomy.)\n**(s The division of a tree (s properly partition of the consideration of it) into the root, trunk and crown, of the earth into zones etc: (s butchers, nightcap etc etc.): These are divisions of an object (g of intuition), but not of a concept, as to how many it belongs to; is called partition. Separation concerns the [unde] separation of the heterogeneous and is the resolution of a mixture. Division concerns a composite merely as a magnitude, and division is never complete except as far as the simple. In partition attention is paid to the distinguishing of the heterogeneous pieces, in division to the distinguishing of the parts of one kind. Composition in the transcendental sense signifies the conjugation of the homogeneous and aims at the cancelling of all conjugation, which is also possible when the parts are substances in intellectual signification.)\n(g Division according to diversity of the concepts of the same object (points of view).\nDivision of the points of view themselves.)",[]],"refl:3022":["/\nThe subdivision is a decomposition; this must properly begin from dichotomy.",[]],"refl:3023":["We go up from lower to higher, and afterwards we can from these go down again to the lower through division.",[]],"refl:3024":["The determination of a concept in regard to everything possible that is contained under it, insofar as it [is] opposed to one another, i.e., is distinguished from one another.",[]],"refl:3025":["Division is the representation of the manifold which through opposition is in community of a single sphere.\nAll dividing happens first into two parts.",[]],"refl:3026":["/\nAbove No. 3025:\nAll polytomy is empirical.\nDichotomy is the only one from principles *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3027":["The first dividing is dichotomy.",[]],"refl:3028":["The division of concepts is twofold, either (g 1. *logica* or *Formalis*) of them in themselves: dichotomy. 2. with regard to the possible (g or actual objects of appearance.) objects in intuition (g intuition *a priori*): *divisio realis*.\n*partitio*. (s e.g. land and sea.) (s Partition as distinct from division.)",[]],"refl:3029":["*Divisio sphaerae, non conceptus* (g — is distinct from *analysis*).",[]],"refl:3030":["All division is either according to [a] the principle of analytic judgments or of synthetic. The former according to the *principio exclusi medii*, the latter according to the principle of ground in the combination of the different. The first is dichotomy, the second polytomy. [In synthetic propositions *a priori*] In the division of concepts [of a] which *a priori* contain or prescribe a syn|thesis\n, either the synthesis is divided or a synthetic concept *a priori* is divided; both belong to transcendental division. The first is tetrachotomy, the second trichotomy. Logical division is *partitio*, because all members of the division are in pairs. The second is *decompositio logica*: where first something is posited, second something else is besides posited or cancelled, third the composition of both is represented. The synthetic unity of consciousness is the transcendental ground of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*. I namely combine A with consciousness. Then B (either merely represented as non A or also as something that is added to it). Third the [combination be] unity of both kinds of distributive consciousness into a collective one, i.e., into the concept of a thing. Thus first the analytic unity of the consciousness of A and non A (=B) and then the synthetic unity of both. But there is also this copula breaks off.",[]],"refl:3031":["Polytomy can [not] not be taught in logic; for to it belongs cognition (g of the object) of the understanding as to content; dichotomy, however, requires only the proposition of contradiction, without being acquainted with the concept that one wants to divide as to content. Polytomy requires intuition, either *a priori*, as in mathematics: conic sections, or empirical, as in the description of nature. Yet [a synthetic] the division [of the] from the principle of synthesis *a priori* has a trichotomy. 1. The concept [as] of the condition, 2. of the conditioned, 3. of the derivation of the latter from the former.",[]],"refl:3032":["/\nWhen something can be regarded as a mark of a thing.\nthus through *abstraction* and *comparation*.\nTwo mutually conflicting concepts make up one single impossible concept.\nFurther L 81. Beside L §. 292 »der — logischen« (62421f.):\nA body is divisible.\nThe sun is spherical.",[]],"refl:3033":["always not.\nFurther L 81 beside L §. 293 »im — er dem« (l. 35):\nThe soul is of simple nature.\nOn L §. 293 »copula«:\n*est*.\nOn L §. 293 »Verneinung des Verbindungsbegriffs«:\nThe sign *non*.",[]],"refl:3034":["all propositions are either of absolute *positio* or *remotio* (There is a God, there is not) or relative: of the predicate to the subject (otherwise logical).\njudgments are either *noetica* or *dianoetica* (*ratiocinia*) *discursus*.\ndemonstrations either *ostensivae* or *apogogicae*.",[]],"refl:3035":["Logic looks not to the content, but to the form (relation) as to quality. Hence [are] *negationes* in the predicate yield no negative judgment.\n*Quaeitas* is the relation [pred] of the predicate to the subject.",[]],"refl:3036":["/\nOf particular judgments it is to be noted: that, if they are to be capable of being discerned through reason and thus have a rational, not merely intellectual (abstracted) form, then the subject must be *conceptus latior* than the [subject] predicate. Let the predicate always be =  the subject  , then  is a particular judgment; for some of what belongs under a is b, some not b. That follows from reason. but let it be , then [is] at the least all a can be contained under b, if it is smaller, but not if it is larger; thus it is only contingently particular.",[]],"refl:3037":["A judgment is the relation (g cognition) of the connection or of the conflict of concepts.",[]],"refl:3038":["/\nThe concept is the relation of a representation to others as a mark (g *communis*) of them or breaks off.",[]],"refl:3039":["The matter of the judgment: *termini*.\nThe form or the relation exposits copula.\nIf\neither or —\nin the *iudiciis [limitatis] extraordinariis*.",[]],"refl:3040":["The form of the judgment is considered in *logicis* (it does not attend to the matter).\nIn it there is *relatio, qvalitas, qvantitas, modalitas*.\n*categorica*\n*hypothetica*\n*disiunctiva*\n*relatio* is either *subiecti* or *principii* or *oppositi*.\nThe relation makes up the content.\nThe agreement of the judgment with the rules of relation breaks off?",[]],"refl:3041":["/\nJudgments are the means of distinct cognitions, and the (g real) *functiones* for judgments as distinct cognition of appearances breaks off.",[]],"refl:3042":["Judgment is a cognition of the unity of given concepts: That namely [that] B, with various other things x, y, z, belongs under the same concept A, or also: that the manifold that is under B also belongs under A, likewise that the concepts A and B can be represented through a concept B.",[]],"refl:3043":["All judgments are analytic or synthetic. The affirmative former rest on identity, where the predicate is contained in the subject; the negative: where it contradicts the former.\nThe synthetic: where the subject is contained under the predicate or is not.",[]],"refl:3044":["Judgment is the relation of the subordination of concepts under one another.\nJudgment is the representation of unity in the relation of cognitions.*\nIf [what according to] several cognitions are considered as one, through one of them the other is also posited.\n*\n(s The [subordination] connection of a cognition [under] with its universal condition according to concepts. The connection of different cognition [according to] through concepts.)",[]],"refl:3045":["A concept has, by virtue of its common validity, the function of a judgment. It refers to other concepts *potentialiter*. The actual reference of a concept to others as a means of their cognition is the judgment.\nThrough this our cognition becomes distinct.\nMatter of judgments.\nForm of judgments.\nQuantity.\nAll relation (g of concepts) is either of comparison,\nor of association,\nor of inclusion or connection.\nA judgment is the unity of a concept from the relation (connection) of different concepts.\nIn ω Kant replaced l. 17, retaining Ein — Einheit, with lines 63021-6312, but forgot to strike out l. 18.\nA judgment is the representation of the unity of the relation of the ground of cognition to the [cog] possible cognition of an object. Thus |\nit is the (g [distinct] clear representation of the) unity of the consciousness of different representations.\nFrom phase ω also stems the following remark (beginning under 6266f. and 63314f.), which is introduced by an NB to which no second one corresponds:\nTo judge is: to represent to oneself a concept as contained under another (s or excluded from it): 1. subject under predicate. 2. consequence under the ground. 3. part of the sphere under the whole.",[]],"refl:3046":["The categorical judgments make up the matter of the rest.\nMatter of all judgments: either concepts or other judgments.\nAll distinct cognition is a cognition clear through a judgment. Understanding.\nWhat is distinct through a syllogism is an intensively distinct cognition (*adaeqvat*).",[]],"refl:3047":["Judgment is the mediate cognition of a representation through other representations. The relation of the mediate [cognition] representation to the immediate is (the relation in the judgment or) the form. subject is the immediate representation, predicate the mediate.",[]],"refl:3048":["81.\nL 81':\nIf universal propositions cannot be discerned in their universality, without [the par|ticular\ncases of the appl use] being acquainted with them *in concreto*, then they cannot hold as a guideline and thus heuristically in application, but are only problems for investigation of the universal grounds of that which first became known in particular cases. *e.g.* Whoever has no interest in lying and knows the truth speaks truth. This proposition is not discerned in its universality, because we are acquainted with the restriction to the condition of the disinterested only through experience: namely that human beings can lie out of interest, which comes from this, that they do not cling firmly to morality, which is an observation of the weakness of human nature. *Mathematici* err here |\nL 81: |\nprincipally. They give the illusion of cognition *a priori*, which is yet actually only *a posteriori*. Thereby the presentation becomes abstract without use.",[]],"refl:3049":["Judgment is the representation of the relation of concepts among one another, which is required for the distinctness of cognition.\n[Judgment is the (g composition) [distinct representation of the c combination] of the manifold of representations [in one] into one]",[]],"refl:3050":["Judgment is* the representation of the unity of different representations, insofar as one belongs to the concept of the other.\n*(s the consciousness of the relation of representations, insofar as they make up a concept.)",[]],"refl:3051":["/\nThe representation of the way in which different concepts (g as such) *belong to one consciousness** (g in general (not merely mine)) is the judgment. They belong to one consciousness partly according to laws of the imagination, thus [conti] subjectively, or of the understanding, i.e., objectively valid for every being that has understanding. The subjective connection depends on the particular situation of the subject in experience.\n*(g universally necessary (empirically or *a priori*))\n**(s Concepts belong to one consciousness only through this, that they are thought under, not beside one another (like sensations).)",[]],"refl:3052":["Judgment is the representation of objective unity (s in the cognition of an object) [of several consc] in the consciousness of various concepts. The objective unity (g of consciousness) is universally valid and necessary.",[]],"refl:3053":["Judgment is the consciousness that a concept is contained under another. Either as its predicate or its ground or as a member of its division. This is the matter of judgments in general. The form is that of quantity, quality, relation, modality.",[]],"refl:3054":["The categories represent that objective unity of consciousness as |\nconcepts of things in general, because actually through this alone things are thought as objects corresponding to our representations.",[]],"refl:3055":["Judgment: The representation of the way in which different concepts [representations] objectively* (for everyone) belong to one consciousness.\n*(g i.e., in order to make up a cognition of the object.)",[]],"refl:3056":["The objective unity of representations is, which are not subjectively, i.e., according to the laws of association, not represented as combined.",[]],"refl:3057":["A representation that through consciousness as of a mark becomes universal is called (g clear) concept.",[]],"refl:3058":["The logical relation is the relation of concepts* to the unity of consciousness in general (of agreement and of conflict).\n*(g Under the condition of the unity of consciousness of the manifold in general.)",[]],"refl:3059":["/\nA concept that is clear through a judgment is consequence of it.",[]],"refl:3060":["The judgment is the representation of the unity [of the con] of given concepts, insofar as one is subordinated to the other: 1. As under the sphere of the other; 2. as consequence to the ground; 3. as member of the division to the divided concept.",[]],"refl:3061":["Division into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive judgments.",[]],"refl:3062":["/\ne.g. The soul is immortal.\nThe human being is imperfect.\nOn L §. 294 »qualitas judicii«:\nto this is opposed *qvantitas*.",[]],"refl:3063":["(g Quality of judgments: affirmative or negative.)\nThe relation of concepts. (exponent):\nThe [pred]\nGround —\nsubject to predicate the\nconsequence — whole — part\n} form of judgments.\n}\ncategorical,\nhypothetical,\ndisjunctive.\n[The moment] Quantity. Universal, particular, singular.\nThe *modus*. Problematic, assertoric, apodictic.\nIt is of great importance to know which propositions, expressed problematically, I can use sufficiently. *e.g.* There can be a future life. For problematic propositions are often indisputable, although assertoric ones are exposed to objections.\nLikewise assertoric ones, even though they are not apodictic. e.g. empirical propositions.\nProblematic propositions as grounds of other truths are called hypotheses.\nThe proposition: *qvidam homines non sunt eruditi* can be expressed through fig. 1. (*non omnes*).\nThe infinite proposition: *qvidam sunt non-eruditi* through fig. 2 (*qvidam non*). The concept of the learned is restricted with regard to human beings, i.e., narrower than the[same] latter.\nThat the learned and non-learned [all] together make up all human beings, hence the human beings are thought through the [non] learned, but with a restriction.\nThe learned are either considered as if they all stand under the concept of human beings, but only as a part of its *sphaere* (g the other part is not learned). [The c] Or [the h] a part only of human beings is considered as if it [under] made up one *sphaeram* with the concept of the learned. The latter restricts the concept of human beings. [the former]\n(g Through negative predicates I posit my understanding outside a determinate sphere into an infinite space.)\nThe negative proposition indicates that something is not contained under the sphere of a given concept; the infinite: that something is contained under the sphere that lies outside the given concept; consequently [that someth] it presupposes that outside its sphere there is another, in which it is contained, hence that it belongs to a sphere that restricts the previous one. Fig. 2. The first occurs according to the *principio exclusi medii* (g between a and non a there is no third) etc etc. The second according to that of thoroughgoing *determination*, which is infinite. The first is the principle of determination: of two opposed judgments one is true. It says only that the proposition: “*anima non est mortalis*” is opposed to the proposition: “*anima est mortalis*”. The second occurs according to the principle of thoroughgoing determination, which is to occur with regard to a thing in general, determines only with regard to thinghood in general, i.e., reality, and outside the sphere of a concept draws in an infinite sphere of the determination of all things, namely of thinghood, i.e., reality. Outside the *sphaera* of a concept there is room for an infinity of *sphaeris*.\nThe proposition: “*anima [non] est non-mortalis*” is a judgment of determination, which says that of two opposed predicates a and non a the latter belongs to the soul. Judgments of determination are all infinite [and are of], in order to determine a thing thoroughly, not merely to indicate the relation of connection or of conflict. Logic looks not to the content [through which], i.e., determination of the concept, but only to the form of the relation: agreement or conflict.",[]],"refl:3064":["“The soul is something other than the mortal”: is infinite, but affirmative.",[]],"refl:3065":["/\nThe infinite judgment indicates not merely that a subject is not contained under the sphere of a predicate, but that it is outside its sphere somewhere in the infinite; consequently it represents the sphere of the predicate as limited.",[]],"refl:3066":["In every negative proposition a division of concepts has gone before. *Eruditi* (g *homines*) — *non eruditi*. Now the proposition: “*qvidam homines* (g *non*) *sunt eruditi*” says that the one [divided concept] member of the division does not belong to a subject. But in the *judicio infinito* the subject is contained under the infinity of things outside the *eruditis*. Thus the determination of the concept of human beings is through the limitations of the concept of the learned.",[]],"refl:3067":["All true division is dichotomy. Why then divide judgments into three kinds? e.g. with regard to quantity. Since much, which is all, makes up one, or conversely one with regard to its predicate is all?",[]],"refl:3068":["82'.\nL 82:\nIn the *iudicio affirmativo* the subject is thought under the *sphaera* |\nof a predicate, in the *negativo* posited outside the *sphaera* of the latter. In the *infinito* into the *sphaeram* of a concept that lies outside the sphere of another.\nL 82':\nIn the universal judgment [a concept] the *sphaera* of a concept is enclosed wholly within the *sphaera* of another; in the particular a part of the former under the sphere of the other; in the singular a concept that has no *sphaeram* at all, hence merely as part, enclosed under the *sphaeram* of another. Thus the *iudicia singularia* are to be estimated equal to the *vniversalibus*, and conversely a *iudicium vniversale* is to be regarded as a singular judgment with regard to the *sphaera*. Much, insofar as it is in itself only one.",[]],"refl:3069":["Although the [restriction] exclusion is a negative one, the limitation of a concept is nevertheless a positive act. Hence boundaries are positive concepts [of the] of limited objects.\nEverything possible is a or non A. If I say then: something is non A, then it is a *judicium indefinitum*. For beyond the *Sphaera definita* A it is not determined under which concept the object belongs, but merely that it belongs to the sphere outside A, which is properly no sphere at all, but only the bordering of a sphere on the infinite or the bounding itself. The remainder is infinite if one takes away a determinate part from the infinite.",[]],"refl:3070":["/\nIf (g merely) the logical form is to be altered, then the matter of the concepts, i.e., their content, must not be altered (from *mortalis* not made *nonmortalis*). Now since logic has to do merely with the form of the judgment, not with concepts as to their content, the distinction of infinite from negative judgments does not belong to logic. This term also aims only at preventing the illusion of a negation of the minor premise in a syllogism, if its predicate is merely negating. e.g. Every substance that is not divisible is not perishable. *Atqui* the soul is [a] not a divisible substance etc etc. Here *minor* is affirmative, i.e., the predicate is: *anima est nondiuisibilis*.",[]],"refl:3071":["Above and in the concluding sentence of L §. 294:\na negative judgment is distinguished from the judgment that affirms a negative concept of the subject. In the relation consists the difference.",[]],"refl:3072":["According to the *principio exclusi medii* the sphere of a concept relative to another is either excluding or including.",[]],"refl:3073":["The assertion is either only conditioned (hypothetical judgment) or the subject: then it is *iudicium restrictum*. All the learned as human beings are to be esteemed equal to one another. is the limitation in the copula, then it is modal.",[]],"refl:3074":["1. The former give a demonstration, e.g. of the fallibility of human beings. Finite nature.\ninsufficient is from learnedness the capacity\n2. The first are inner marks of the thing, the others e.g. testimony.",[]],"refl:3075":["If God wants to annihilate the world (g wants always to preserve the world), then it can pass away (the world endures eternally).\nL 83'. On L §. 298 No. 3:\nBodies are divisible.\nBodies all have warmth.",[]],"refl:3076":["The human being can make inferences.\nThe sun illuminates the [northern] poles half a year.\nWhat is taken from the logical definition is a necessary one.",[]],"refl:3077":["It is not necessary to say: the human being, because he has reason, can make inferences.\nIf the human being is virtuous, then he loves his enemy.",[]],"refl:3078":["/\nA triangle that has three sides has three angles.\nA human being, if he is finite, can err.\nOn L §. 299 No. 2:\nA wise man, if he is of finite nature, deceives himself.\nThe sun is eclipsed when the moon steps before it.\nL 83'. On L §. 299 »iudicium determinatum«:\nIs altered from a higher into a lower.\nFurther L 83 from β1−ε2:\nAll human beings must naturally die.",[]],"refl:3079":["The constitution of the (g subject) predicate. Of the condition.\nOn L §. 300 beginning of sentence 2:\nA body is divisible.\nOn L §. 300 end:\nThe world is not God.",[]],"refl:3080":["Adam was fallible.\nChrist is the remover of sins.\nOn L §. 301 sentence 3:\nAll human beings are mortal;\nsome are learned.\nψ−θ. L 84. On »iudicium particulare«:\n*plurale*.\nβ1. L 84'. Opposite L §. 301 sentence 5 (ll. 29-31):\nsome human beings [are] err.\nall err.\nOn the *terminis* A, E, I, O in L §. 301 (ll. 33f.):\nAll human beings are mortal. No human being is blameless.\nSome are prepossessed by prejudices. some are not prepossessed by prejudices.\nOn L §. 301 »Das Prädicat — werden« (64734, 64820f.):\nAll human beings are mortal. No human being is blameless. All are blameworthy.\nOn L §. 301 penultimate sentence:\nGod is without faults. Everything that is God is without faults.\nOn L §. 301 »so — rechnen« (ll. 23f.):\nOnly not the merely particular.",[]],"refl:3081":["85.\nKant adds in L §. 302 before »wenn« (l. 29) a 1, before »oder einigen« (l. 30) a 2 and makes on L 85 in the right margin the following remark, which probably refers to »oder einigen«, but possibly also to »oder einigen — zukommt« (l. 30):\noften the latter can be made universal through the condition.",[]],"refl:3082":["Such a judgment becomes universal. *e.g.* All spirits are immortal.\nOn L §. 303 No. 2:\nAll works of God, if God wills, are indestructible.\nNo human being can naturally be immortal.\nEvery particular judgment can be altered into a universal one through the added condition.",[]],"refl:3083":["All analytic judgments are universal,\nsynthetic are empirical and particular.\nIn the former the predicate is contained both objectively and subjectively in the *conceptu* of the subject, in the latter however only objectively.",[]],"refl:3084":["[As to quality: affirmative negative and p]\nAs to position.\nproblematic and dogmatic or assertoric.\nQuantity concerns not the concepts, but their relation.\nAs to quantity there are only [a and affirmative and negative] universal and particular; for the subject is either wholly in- or excluded from the notion of the predicate,\nor partly in-, partly excluded.\n(s whether the proposition is categorical (or conditioned or disjunctive).)\n*qvaeitas. qvalitas. qvantitas.*\nQuaeity concerns the *copulam* as the relation of a ground of cognition to the subject and the energy, i.e., the degree of this ground and of the relation. *e.g.* God is necessarily just.",[]],"refl:3085":["The *generale* is that which takes into consideration only something, and indeed that which is common to most.\n*Generale* [judgments] propositions (s are those which do not contain sufficient conditions of subsumption) are those which merely [to the] contain something of the universal of certain objects. *e.g.* One must make proofs thoroughly. *Universale*: those which assert something universally of an object.\n(s *Generalia* of the art of governing are only that which concerns the *genus*. e.g. of the education of children as children in general; *specialia*: according to their different age, health, circumstances.)",[]],"refl:3086":["Universal rules [(or also] are either analytically universal: in that they abstract from the differences [and the r], or synthetically universal: and these attend to the differences, yet also determine with regard to them. The more simply an object is thought, the sooner is analytic universality in consequence of a concept possible. Trigonometry already requires many rules.",[]],"refl:3087":["Quantity, quality, relation and modality.",[]],"refl:3088":["God and all finite spirits are immortal.\nOn L §. 304 »subiectum et praedicatum principale«:\n*e.g.* The almighty God has created the best world.\n[God and the neighbor s]\nL 86'. On L §. 304 »iudicium copulativum«:\nGod and the neighbor are to be loved.\nGod has created all things and governs them.",[]],"refl:3089":["[Extraord] Composite judgments:\n1. of copulation: through one subject two predicates or conversely;\n2. of consequence: conditional;\n3. of opposition [disjunction] and conjunction together: disjunctive.",[]],"refl:3090":["If the things of the world are absolutely necessary, then the world is God.\nIf the souls of fathers have no influence on the souls of children, then there is no original sin.\n“This world, which is created according to the highest wisdom, is the best”: is no conditioned judgment.",[]],"refl:3091":["The condition can be false and the conditioned judgment nevertheless be true. If there is a fate, then no actions are free.",[]],"refl:3092":["/\nThe soul is either a composite or simple being.\nBird, eat or die.",[]],"refl:3093":["One must become either a philosopher or a pious man.\nOn L §. 308 No. 2:\nOne must either conquer or die.\nThe souls of animals are either spirits or bodies.\nOn L §. 308 No. 3:\nAll things are either bodies or spirits.\nAll machines are moved either by pressure or impact or by themselves.",[]],"refl:3094":["[The relation] Relational judgments consist of mere problematic ones. *Iudicia extraordinaria*.\nevery judgment is either problematic or assertoric; the latter are those in which the predicate is considered [as] in actual connection with the subject. example of the former is: God is corporeal.\nThe relation between two problematic judgments is either of connection or of conflict. From the latter arise disjunctive judgments, which can contain only two contradictorily opposed ones. [They exp] The logical division which in the subdivisions leaves out the negative *membra* is, like that which in the [comb] chain of subordinated concepts leaves out the subject (in the *sorites*), only abbreviated [*iu*] *series iudiciorum disiunctiuorum*. For the [either] or, once posited, yields an *iudicium disiunctivum*.",[]],"refl:3095":["/\nAll [true] pure disjunction takes place only between *contradictorie oppositis*.\nThe disjunction between *disparatis* is a logical division whose [univ] correctness can be cognized only *per inductionem*, that one has left out no member of the division.\nIn a categorical judgment the thing [which as a] whose representation is considered as a part of the *sphaera* of another [representation the] subordinated representation is considered as contained under this one's higher concept; thus here in the subordination of the *sphaerarum* the part of the part is compared with the whole. But in |\n[cat] disjunctive judgments I go from the whole to all parts taken together: what is contained under the sphere of a concept is also contained under one of the parts of this [sphea] *sphaera*; accordingly the sphere must first be divided. *e.g.* A learned man is either historical or a learned man of reason; by which I mean that these concepts as to *sphaera* are parts of the *sphaera* of the learned, but not parts of one another, and all together complete.\nIf we regard a universal concept as a surface, in which the contained *conceptus communes* are again surfaces, the *singulares* points: then the question is whether the surface consists of surfaces or points, i.e., whether under a *notione communi* a series of the possible *subordinatio notionum communium* to infinity is contained or not; in the first case the *lex continui* holds of the forms, i.e., the *speciebus sub aliqvo genere contentis*. If a thing passes over naturally from one *specie* into the other, then it runs through all these *species intermedias*. Since also, if the world has a common cause, each *species* is possible only through the ground of the other *specierum* taken together, [insofar as] so the transitions of the understanding from one actual *specie* to the other are also in a flow, i.e., there is a *continuum formarum* among actual things.",[]],"refl:3096":["In the disjunctive judgment it is not the *sphaera* of the divided |\nconcept that is considered as contained in the *sphaera* of the divisions, but that which is contained under the divided concept is considered as contained under one of the members of the divisions. *e.g.* categorical judgment is: [all] x, which is contained under b, is also under a:  in the disjunctive: (g x,) which is contained under a is contained either under b or c, etc etc.:\nThus the division indicates the coordination not of the parts of the whole concept, but of all parts of its *sphaere*. Here I think many things through one concept, but there [many] one thing through many concepts. *e.g.* the *definitum* through all marks of coordination etc etc. the question is: through how many higher concepts can a thing be thought, so that [their *sphaerae*] it is no longer contained under any *sphaera*?",[]],"refl:3097":["/\nEvery disjunction is a division of the *sphaera* [of a concept] of cognition:\neither of a determinate concept\nor of truth in general.",[]],"refl:3098":["Everything that is contained under a part of a concept is also contained under the whole. Universal affirmative.\nEverything that is contained under a whole of a concept is also contained under one of the parts. disjunction of the division of a concept, otherwise there is disjunction of mere opposition.",[]],"refl:3099":["Problematic judgments are those whose matter is given with the possible relation between predicate and subject. In these the subject must always have a smaller *sphaeram* than the predicate.",[]],"refl:3100":["Judgment and proposition are distinct from one another. The judgment is the sense; the proposition: how the concepts are placed, whether in categorical |\nor hypothetical or disjunctive relation. One and the same judgment can be expressed under different propositions.\nLogical division of a concept or of cognition in general.",[]],"refl:3101":["Disjunction of the *oppositorum* or *disparatorum*.",[]],"refl:3102":["The members of the disjunction are problematic judgments, of which nothing else is thought than that, like parts of the sphere of a cognition (each the *complementum ad totum* of the other), taken together they are equal to the *sphaere* of the first, i.e., that the truth must be contained in one of them, i.e., one of them must hold assertorically, because outside them the *sphaere* of cognition under the given conditions comprises nothing more and one is opposed to the other, consequently neither outside them can something else, nor under them more than one be true.",[]],"refl:3103":["Thus both kinds of judgments are an assertion [from nothing but pr] of the relation of problematic judgments.",[]],"refl:3104":["In every disjunctive judgment one member determines every other, but |\nonly insofar as they all stand in community as parts of a whole sphere of cognition, outside which in a certain reference nothing can be thought.",[]],"refl:3105":["A judgment is disjunctive if the parts of the sphere of a given concept determine one another in the whole or to a whole as *complementa*. Just so in the real whole of substances through *commercium* in simultaneity.",[]],"refl:3106":["All disjunctive judgments represent different judgments as in the community of a sphere and produce each judgment only through the limitation of the others with regard to the whole sphere, thus determine each judgment's relation to the whole sphere and thereby the relation of these among one another.",[]],"refl:3107":["86'.\nL 86:\nAll division through mere concepts can only be dichotomy: Either, or. The members determine one another reciprocally as *complementa* to a whole. if this determination is not logical and the whole not logical, but real, then they are substances *in commercio*.\nL 86'.\nThe reciprocal exclusion of the members of the disjunction is, like that of the parts in a space, no contradiction, except that if one were in the other.",[]],"refl:3108":["All dark heavenly bodies are either planets or comets or satellites. All matters of the earth are animals or plants or minerals; the others are *vehicula*.",[]],"refl:3109":["polytomy is a subordinated dichotomy.",[]],"refl:3110":["All human beings are naturally mortal.\nFurther L 87 on the concluding sentence of L §. 309 from β1−ε2:\nThe opposite of all occurrences in the world is possible in and for itself.",[]],"refl:3111":["Without modality no judgment at all is possible; thus the [judgment] modal judgment is not impure.\nAssertoric judgments alone are propositions.",[]],"refl:3112":["Affirmation occurs distinctly, negation hidden.\nOnly human beings are redeemed through Christ.\n1. Human beings are;\n2. Those who are no human beings: not.\nTo the left of the preceding three lines:\nFew human beings are pious:\nsome are;\nMany — — not.",[]],"refl:3113":["Is the vanity of the world indeed worth much effort.",[]],"refl:3114":["/\nOpposite the beginning of L §. 312:\nMathematical division aims at exhaustive certainty.",[]],"refl:3115":["Practical judgments are either thought problematically (i.e., practical in the logical sense), or categorically (without condition): moral ones, or hypothetically: *imperativi* of skill.\nThe practical *imperativi* of mathematics are properly possible necessitations to actions: *imperativi possibiles*.\nThe rules of prudence are *imperativi actuales*, and indeed: in which [Pure] inclination commands.\nThe rules of morality are *imperativi actuales*, and indeed: in which reason commands.",[]],"refl:3116":["/\nTheoretical propositions are: those that refer to the object,\nPractical propositions are: those that* refer to the action through which an object is brought forth.\n*(s which determine (g state) the action through which an object becomes possible, which is thus the necessary condition of the latter.)\nObjectively practical propositions contain the idea of the action through which the object becomes possible;\nsubjectively practical propositions the conditions in the subject through which it becomes possible. If in the subject there are conditions of the opposite or the idea is not sufficient for execution, then the *studium* of the subject, hence of the hindrance, must come first.\nSubjectively practical ones are not necessary where the idea at the same time contains the construction.",[]],"refl:3117":["practical propositions (g cognitions), as opposed to the speculative, do not belong to logic.",[]],"refl:3118":["Practical propositions as to form: of these logic treats and distinguishes them from theoretical ones.*\nPractical propositions as to content: of these morals treats and distinguishes them from speculative ones.\n(g are sources of practical propositions.)\n*(g these do not say: what belongs to the *obiect*, but rather: through which action a certain *obiect* is possible. Thus not the relation of cognitions, but of freedom to an *obiect* of cognition, which is possible thereby. practical propositions are either rules (conditioned) or commands (unconditioned *imperativi*).)",[]],"refl:3119":["/\nThe human being is a rational being. Bodies are divisible.",[]],"refl:3120":["/\nHuman beings are human beings (g entirely empty). Human beings are rational creatures with earthly organic bodies.\nOn L §. 314 “*iudicium ex parte identicum*” [judgment identical in part]:\nHuman beings are rational beings.\nOn the penultimate sentence of L §. 314:\nWhat is possible has a ground.",[]],"refl:3121":["Virtue is an aptitude. Whoever wants to become virtuous must acquire an aptitude.",[]],"refl:3122":["Virtue:\n3. Whoever is to exercise virtue must be free.",[]],"refl:3123":["Whoever wants to exercise virtue must acquire a freedom in actions,\nlawfulness of these,\naptitude.\nFurther L 89 beside L §. 317 nos. 1-3:\nWhoever wants to make a circle must move a straight line about a fixed point.",[]],"refl:3124":["Small brooks are the beginnings of great rivers.\nMathematical method.",[]],"refl:3125":["*identitas implicita vel explicita; posterior tavtologia* [implicit or explicit identity; the latter tautology].",[]],"refl:3126":["All *principia* are either formal or material. The latter either analytic or synthetic; [not a] only with empirical concepts, as in mathematics, can synthetic principles occur. Analytic principles arise through the *praedicat* always being thought in the concept of the *subiect*, but [ver] *implicite*; synthetic ones, through its belonging to it.",[]],"refl:3127":["/\nanalytic propositions contain *praedicata logica*, synthetic ones: *determinationes*. The *subiecte* of the former are concepts (*conceptus reflexi*), of the second *conceptus abstracti*. *e.g.* 1. A body is extended. 2. a body has attraction.\n(s Example of an analytic proposition.\nEvery body is extended: (s of the synthetic — every body is heavy.)\nEvery x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, to it extension (b) also belongs.\nOf a synthetic one:\nEvery x to which the concept of body (a+b) belongs, to it attraction (c) also belongs.\nconsequently both propositions represent the *identitaet* of the *praedicat* with the *subiecte* x. The first, however, at the same time with its mark a+b, the second, however, not with the same. The synthetic propositions augment cognition *materialiter*, the analytic *formaliter*.)",[]],"refl:3128":["Every judgment means as much as: everything to which the *notion* of the *subiect* belongs, to it the *praedicat* also belongs. This can happen in that the *praedicat* is identical with the [subi] *notion* of the *subiect*, as in analytic propositions, or also not, as in synthetic ones. The |\n*subiect* is something x. The *notion* of it is S, the *praedicat* P; thus is breaks off.",[]],"refl:3129":["Judgment is a means to the distinctness of concepts. Now if this distinctness is not attained, *e.g.* human being is human being, then the judgment is empty.",[]],"refl:3130":["There are empty propositions of [skill] intelligibility, of skill, of prudence and of morality. Those of skill: when one gives as the means what was properly the merely paraphrased *qvaestion* of the problem; those of prudence: when one commands what everyone wants anyway; those of morality: when one takes admonitions for moral instructions, without showing how one is to promote their exercise.\n(s Empty judgments are either empty of sense or empty of fruit. The latter are tautological.)",[]],"refl:3131":["The boundary of *analysis* is tautology (*battologie*).",[]],"refl:3132":["(s Synthetic (g intuitive) judgments *a priori*.)\nAxioms are *iudicia intuitiva a priori*. (g The principle of contra|diction\nis *iudicium discursivum*.) *Principia formalia sunt axiomata, materialia sunt propositiones elementares* [formal principles are axioms, material ones are elementary propositions].\n(s Analytic *principia* are not axioms, because they are discursive.\nSynthetic ones too are axioms only when they are intuitive. Thus philosophy has no axioms, but rather acroamatic *principien*.)",[]],"refl:3133":["A *postulat* is properly a practical immediately [nec] certain proposition. But one can also have theoretical *postulate* (g on behalf of practical reason), namely a theoretical *Hypothesis* necessary with a practical aim of reason, such as that of the existence of God, of freedom and of another world. The practical propositions are objectively certain; subjectively they can only become practical insofar as that *Hypothesis* lies at the ground.",[]],"refl:3134":["practical *postulate* and discursive cognition. E.g. That what it is duty to do can also happen.",[]],"refl:3135":["Axiom is an immediately certain intuiting judgment *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3136":["/\nAnalytic judgments all rest on identity.\n*Iudicium inane* [empty judgment].",[]],"refl:3137":["Empty of sense or empty of understanding (s[thought] empty of consequence); the first are of *qvalitatibus occultis*, the second tautological judgments.",[]],"refl:3138":["Intuiting judgments, when they comprise abstracted concepts in themselves, are not sensations. Only individual things are sensed.\nFurther L 89:\nI am cold (g warm), iron is heavy. [Light makes bodies visible] is a posterior judgment.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\n2×2=4.\nFurther likewise from ψ−θ:\neither of empirical or of pure intuition.",[]],"refl:3139":["On the penultimate sentence of L §. 319:\nIn the sun there are spots.\nFurther L 89:\nMediate experiences are provable. *e.g.* Iron is heavy, gold the heaviest.",[]],"refl:3140":["*iudicia intuitiva sunt synthetica, e.g. intuitus puri.*\n*analytica sunt discursiva, vel demonstrabilia vel indemonstrabilia.* [intuitive judgments are synthetic, e.g. of pure intuition. analytic ones are discursive, either demonstrable or indemonstrable.]",[]],"refl:3141":["So that it become distinct. *e.g.* One does not see mountain and valley in the moon, but rather one infers them.\n*caveatur vitium subreptionis* [let the fault of subreption be guarded against].\nFurther L 90 from β1−ε2 beside L §. 320 nos. 2, 3:\nHeat extends bodies.",[]],"refl:3142":["I do not experience that gold is denser than iron, but rather that it is heavier.\nFurther L 90 from β1−ε2:\nNot divisibility.\nL 90' from β1 on L §. 321 sentence 2:\nI do not sense that the moon has no light of its own.",[]],"refl:3143":["First of some.\nall bodies become denser through cold.\nOn L §. 322 no. 2a, b, c:\nBodies.\nThe cohesion of the parts, fire.\nNecessary.\nFurther L 90 from β1−ε2 beside L §. 322 no. 2a, b, c:\nTriangle has 3 angles which contain 360°; thus all.",[]],"refl:3144":["All things (g of the world) are alterable.\nFurther L 90 from β1−ε2:\n*e.g.* That the human being is mortal.\nβ1. L 90'. On L §. 323 final sentence:\n“Air is elastic”: is a posterior judgment.",[]],"refl:3145":["A judgment from mere perceptions is not really possible except only through my declaring my representation as perception. I, who perceive a tower, perceive in it the red color. But I cannot say: it is red; for that would be not merely an empirical judgment but also a judgment of experience, i.e. an empirical judgment through which I get a concept of the *Object*. E.g. “On touching the stone I sense warmth” is the former: but “the stone is warm” is the second. — It belongs to the latter that I do not reckon what is merely in my *Subject* to the *Object*; for a judgment of experience is the (g perception) from which a concept of the *Object* |\narises. *e.g.* Whether bright points move in the moon, or in the air, or in my eye.",[]],"refl:3146":["A judgment of perception* is merely subjective; an (g objective) judgment from perceptions is a judgment of experience.\n*(g “I sense warmth in touching the stove” is perception; “the stove is warm” is judgment of experience.)",[]],"refl:3147":["Virtue is attained through practice.\nFurther from β1−ε2:\nOf the triangle *theorema Pythagoricum minus* [lesser Pythagorean theorem].\n*Corollarium* I. thus no triangle can have more than 1 right angle.\nII. Thus if two angles have been given, the 3rd is given.",[]],"refl:3148":["The method of philosophy is zetetic, of mathematics heuristic. Hence the form of the edifice with its subdivisions, *e.g.* *Definitio, Axioma* etc etc., in mathematics does not fit philosophy.",[]],"refl:3149":["or to ground something old on a new proof.\nβ1−ε2. L 91'. Opposite L §. 326 no. 2a:\nGod is not material.",[]],"refl:3150":["to make a definition.",[]],"refl:3151":["How one can funnel learning in.\nOn L §. 329 no. 1 from β1−ε2:\nTo raise water so that it [use] drives mills.\nβ1. L 92. On L §. 329 no. 3:\nTo burn with ice.\nFurther L 92':\n*e.g.* the shooting star.\nOn L §. 329 no. 4:\nThunder.\nThe cheerful hour.",[]],"refl:3152":["How to make an airship.\nHow one is to deny oneself philosophically. When one avoids a smaller good in order to attain a greater one.\nFurther from β1−ε2:\nTo bring water to spring up.\nOn L §. 330 no. 2 from β1:\n*medicin*. Gunpowder.\nAir gun.",[]],"refl:3153":["e.g. to explain how the human soul arises.\nOn L §. 331 second half:\nIf I ask: how a sensible, a spiritual gratification arises, then I can also say: how a gratification in general arises.\nIf I know: how to make a crane, a winch, then I also know: how to make a machine in general that lifts loads.\nFurther L 93 beside the end of L §. 331 from γ−λ (? ν? ρ??):\nTo make a burning mirror.",[]],"refl:3154":["To make a clock.\nFurther L 93':\nWith the northern lights the light. The position. The time. The motion.",[]],"refl:3155":["When it is composite.\nβ1. L 93'. On L §. 333 no. 1:\ne.g. how one is to love the [neighbor] enemy. 1. the human being. Then the enemy.\nOn L §. 333 no. 2:\nIf one knows how gratification arises, then one also knows how the rational one arises.",[]],"refl:3156":["To prevent earthquakes.\nOn L §. 334 no. 2:\nE.g. to test the parts of the blood.\nFurther L 93' from β1−ε2:\nTo make water spring.\nβ1. L 93. On L §. 334 no. 3:\nDifferent kinds make many solutions.\nFurther L 93' from β1−ε2:\nTo lift a load, if it also moves the same quickly.",[]],"refl:3157":["E.g. happiness through the extirpation of the affects.\nL 93'. On L §. 335 no. 2:\nE.g. the Platonic republic. the Abbé St. Pierre's proposals.\nL 94'. On L §. 335 no. 3:\nE.g. To drive away fever with little slips of paper.\nOn L §. 335 no. 4:\nEpicureans. *ede, bibe, lude* [eat, drink, play].",[]],"refl:3158":["Pray. not sufficient for a temporally happy life.\nOn L §. 336 no. 2:\nWhen for the attainment of happiness a perfect wisdom is prescribed.\nOn L §. 336 no. 3:\nNot in the whole of physics the magnifying glasses.\nFurther L 94 from β1−ε2:\nNot everywhere water mills.",[]],"refl:3159":["The rabbinic [stuff] for philosophy.\nFurther L 94 from β1−ε2:\n*e.g.* Old assaults on fortresses.",[]],"refl:3160":["When the thing to be explained is taken as the cause.\n*vis plastica*. (s instinct, *horror vacui*, sense of truth. (g idiosyncrasy.) The manifold feelings of Home: feeling of right. feeling of honor, sympathetic [feeling]. The *consensus* of the nerves of the physicians. The *miasmata*. — Sympathy. Antipathy. Oil and water.)\nβ1−ε2. L 94. On L §. 338 sentence 2:\nAlchemistic.",[]],"refl:3161":["What is the cause, distinct from all self-interest, of moral satisfaction? The moral feeling.",[]],"refl:3162":["*qvalitates occultae** are tautological explanations of appearances, just as empty resolutions of problems are tautological imperatives.\nMorals [of exerc] applied.\n*(s is the tautology in (citation) determination of the cause.)",[]],"refl:3163":["In logic *principium rationis*.\nFurther L 94 from ψ−θ:\nEpisodic part.",[]],"refl:3164":["One must borrow. but only that which has previously been proved. also not what is borrowed from this, what one wants to infer from it. For example: I want to prove the immaterial nature of the soul and assume immortality as a lemma.",[]],"refl:3165":["Utility. Elucidation. History. Objections.\nFurther L 94 from ψ−θ:\nalways, however, inessential part.",[]],"refl:3166":["In the *consequentiis immediatis* there is no middle concept.",[]],"refl:3167":["are not consequences.\nFurther ψ−θ:\n*Conseqventiae immediatae*.",[]],"refl:3168":["*Conseqventiae immediatae*. Show merely the form of the inference or the consequence of one judgment from the other (g without *subsumtion*). |\nWhen the *subsumtion* of a concept [the] through the power of judgment is added, then one concludes the cognition of another thing from a given cognition. *e.g.* “All human beings are mortal, consequently some [are] mortal ones are human beings” only indicates that the *subsumtion* under the *praedicat* yields a *particularitaet*, hence no syllogism *ab universali*.",[]],"refl:3169":["Inferences of the understanding cannot be transformed into syllogisms otherwise than through tautological *subsumtionen*.\nThe consequence of one judgment from the other, from which it is distinguished merely as to form. As to content or matter, however, the same.\n(s Difference. 1. Merely as to quantity. 2. [merely] as to quality (g and quantity (perhaps)). 3. of the relation of the *Subiect* to the *praedicat* with retention of the quality. 4. of the very same relation with alteration of the quality, but retention of the quantity.)",[]],"refl:3170":["95'.\nL 95:\nThe *conseqventiae immediatae* as immediate, i.e. inferences of the understanding (g They proceed merely from categorical judgments) go through all classes of the categories.\nThose *per iudicia subalternata*\nbelong to quantity.\nThose *per iudicia opposita*\nbelong to quality.\nThose *per conversionem iudicii*\nbelong to relation.\nThose *per contrapositionem iudicii*\nbelong to modality: to make an apodictic judgment out of an assertoric one.\nL 95':\nAll bodies are divisible (assertoric). What therefore is not divisible is not a body (apodictic judgment, because it contradicts itself to be a body and not divisible, and a proposition whose opposite contradicts itself is apodictic.)\n(s One can also use an empirical proposition as an example. *e.g.* All human beings are mortal. For, this presupposed, the proposition: what is not mortal is not human being, is expressed apodictically.)",[]],"refl:3171":["Under the universal the particular and individual ones are all contained,\nbut not under the particular all individual ones.\nTo L §. 342 conclusion Kant adds:\n3. One cannot infer: if the universal judgment is false, then the particular and individual judgments are also false; for the *praedicat* can be merely a particular concept in regard to the *subject*. *e.g.* it is false: all learned men are pious, but true that some are.\n4. Not from particular judgments and their truth to universal ones.",[]],"refl:3172":["*particulariter negans*.\nOn “E”:\n*vniversaliter negans*.\nOn “I”:\n*particulariter affirmans*.\nBeside L §. 343 sentence 2 from µ−φ:\nAccording to the *principio exclusi Medii*.",[]],"refl:3173":["With individual contradictory judgments the very same *subject* must be there: this world is the best.\nFurther L 95':\nIf *judicia communia* are to contradict one another, then the one must universally affirm and the other particularly deny, or universally deny and the other particularly affirm.\nall virtues of the heathens are true virtues: some not.",[]],"refl:3174":["One judgment affirms of a concept what the other denies, but not in the degree in which that one affirms and denies it. or in a greater one.",[]],"refl:3175":["Some human beings are pious, some not pious.\nFurther L 96 from κ−θ:\nnot enough for conflict.",[]],"refl:3176":["Scoffers at religion, some clergymen are hypocrites, therefore it is false that many would not be hypocrites.\nOn L §. 344 no. 2:\nIf it is false that some are pious: then all human beings must |\nbe godless; for then the restriction is taken away from the *praedicat*, and then it says: all human beings are (g not) pious, and: some are pious.",[]],"refl:3177":["All human beings are learned, no human being is learned.\nsome —— —\nsome not.\nOn L §. 345 no. 2:\nFor what is denied of all is also denied of some.\n*e.g.* all human beings can err,\nno human being can err.\nsome human beings cannot err.\nκ−θ. L 96. Beside L §. 345 “universal — be” (l. 30f.):\ntoo much for conflict.\nµ−φ? (η?) L 96. On L §. 345 no. 2:\ntoo much for conflict.",[]],"refl:3178":["Where I cannot say: *Aut Aut*, i.e. where both can be true or false, there is no true *opposition*.",[]],"refl:3179":["We have to do only with the conversion of categorical judgments.",[]],"refl:3180":["The *metathesis terminorum* or the transposition is called conversion only when the quality remains the same.\nIt is *contrapositio* when merely the quantity remains the same.",[]],"refl:3181":["but considered merely as affirmative judgments only the particular ones can.\nFor the *praedicat* is *conceptus latior*; thus only some of those which are contained under it can belong to the *Subject*.",[]],"refl:3182":["If I say: “some human beings are learned”: then this means as much as: “they occupy a part in the *sphaera* of the learned”; thus a part of these learned is the same as human beings. This conversion is an identical proposition.",[]],"refl:3183":["One can infer thus:\nTo all those to whom the [subiect] *praedicat* of the negative judgment belongs, the *Subject* is contrary. *e.g.* No human being is infallible, and none who is infallible is a human being.",[]],"refl:3184":["in that they are altered into particular ones. All triangles are figures, some figures are triangles.",[]],"refl:3185":["[All] In all affirmative judgments the *subiect* is particular in regard to the *praedicat*; for because this is *notio latior*, [is the sub] the *subiect* signifies only some of those things which are contained under the universal concept of the *praedicat*. Hence human beings are some of the mortals, i.e. some mortals are human beings; on the other hand the mortals are in everything that is called human being.",[]],"refl:3186":["The *Praedicat* is [bet] *conceptus latior*; thus only some of it is contained under the *notion* of the *Subiect*.",[]],"refl:3187":["some human beings are not learned. Some are unlearned. thus some unlearned are human beings.\notherwise not: some human beings are no learned human beings. and some learned human beings are no human beings.\nOne can always infer thus: to some to whom the *praedicat* does not belong, [are] the *subject* belongs.",[]],"refl:3188":["All human beings are rational beings. [Some who] What is not a rational being is no human being.",[]],"refl:3189":["Opposition [*Entgegenstellung*].\nFor if the *praedicat*, as that which contains the *subiect* under itself, hence the whole sphere, is denied, then a part of it is also denied, i.e. also the *Subject*.",[]],"refl:3190":["Just as concepts, so too judgments are combined.\nThe proofs with the proved propositions.\nL 99'. On L §. 353 “*nexus veritatum*”:\nIs called an inference. All animals infer. When my master looks for the stick, he wants to strike; *atqvi*: *ergo*.\nA human being who keeps to the 12th mealtime hour.",[]],"refl:3191":["Difference from the aesthetic.",[]],"refl:3192":["Why is the world the best? Answer: because God chose it.\nOn L §. 355 sentence 2:\n1. The condition. 2. The derivation from this condition.",[]],"refl:3193":["Only *Maior*; for *Minor* is *Subsumtio*.",[]],"refl:3194":["Opposite 704 19:\n*Conseqventia immediata est [transitus a iudicio derivatio] deductio iudicii ab alio absqve iudicio intermedio.* [Immediate consequence is the [transition from a judgment derivation] deduction of a judgment from another without intermediate judgment.]",[]],"refl:3195":["/\nAll syllogisms contain the consequence of one judgment from the other by means of a universal *principii*, which is the major premise. Hence they are also called inferences of reason, because the consequence takes place *a priori*, i.e. according to a universal principle.\nThe syllogism premises a universal rule and a *subsumtion* under its condition. One thereby cognizes the *conclusion a priori* not in the individual, but as contained in the universal and as necessary under a certain condition.\nThat everything stands under the universal and is determinable in universal rules is the *principium rationalitatis sive necessitatis*.",[]],"refl:3196":["The syllogism is the consciousness of the necessity of a cognition through the *subsumtion* (g of the condition) of it under a universal rule.\n*Maior* is the universal rule that says something under a condition. *Minor* the *subsumtion* under the condition of the rule. The *conclusio* says just that of the subsumed concept which the rule said of the condition.",[]],"refl:3197":["Syllogism is the mediate inference of a proposition from a universal rule.\nThe mediating proposition is (g *subsumtion*) that which subsumes something under the condition of the rule. Matter and form —\nThey are categorical, hypothetical or disjunctive. Those alone have *maiorem, minorem* and *medium terminum* and are the matter of the others.",[]],"refl:3198":["Syllogism is a [cognition] judgment *a priori* through *subsumtion* of [its] its condition under (g the condition) of a universal rule.",[]],"refl:3199":["Division of syllogisms into categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.\nFor all rules (judgments) contain (g objective) unity of consciousness of the manifold of cognition, hence a condition under which one cognition belongs with the other to one consciousness.\nThe rule is *Major*. The *subsumtion* of a cognition under its condition *minor* etc etc. There are only three conditions of this unity.\n*Subiect* of the *inhaerentz* of the marks.\nGround of the *dependentz* of one cognition on the other.\nCombination of the parts in a whole.\nLogical division.\nHence there are so many *maiores propositiones*.\n(s The categorical syllogisms alone infer *per medium terminum* (g in them alone is *maior propositio* assertoric, in the |\nothers problematic), the others only through the *subsumtion* of a proposition represented in *maiori* problematically, in *minori* assertorically.)",[]],"refl:3200":["1) Inferences of the understanding (s are immediate inferences) infer from the universal to the particular or from the particular to the particular (s but immediately), never, however, from the particular to the universal, because they are to yield determining judgments.\n2) Inferences of the power of judgment go from the particular to the (s empirically-) universal (s are ways of progressing from *individuis* to *generibus*.), from some things which belong to a certain species, to all, or from some properties in which things of one and the same species agree, to the remaining ones, insofar as they belong to the same *Princip*. They are nothing but ways of coming from particular concepts to universal ones, thus of the reflecting (s not determining) power of judgment, hence not to determine the *obiect*, but only the way [about] of reflection about it, in order to attain to acquaintance with it. (s are inferences for attaining to provisional, not to determining judgments. — Analogy and induction.)\nAre the inferences of the power of judgment immediate inferences? No, a *Princip* of the power of judgment lies at their ground: namely that much will not agree in one without a common ground, that therefore what belongs to it thus will be necessary from a common ground. (s — Analogy, induction.)\n(s 3. Syllogisms are (g mediate) inferences from the universal to the particular *per iudicium intermedium*.)",[]],"refl:3201":["Syllogism is the cognition of the necessity of a proposition through the subsumption of its condition under a [univer] given universal rule. — What stands under the condition of a rule (g the *Subject* of the *Conclusion*), that also stands under the rule itself (g the *Praedicat* of the *Conclusion*).",[]],"refl:3202":["A rule is an *assertion* under a universal condition. The relation of the condition to the *assertion*, namely how the latter stands under the former, is the *exponent* of the rule.\nThe cognition that the condition [ta] (somewhere) takes place is the *subsumtion*.\nThe combination of that which has been subsumed (g under the condition) with the *assertion* of the rule is the conclusion.\nSyllogisms are also as manifold (as to their *conclusionen*) as judgments (affirmative, universal etc.).",[]],"refl:3203":["Because the *praedicat* is for the most part more universal, consequently greater. *e.g.* All vice makes human beings unhappy.\nOn L §. 357 “*terminus medius*”:\nFor the smaller or greater principal concept cannot be the proof.",[]],"refl:3204":["Provable judgments are never convincingly true without the condition.",[]],"refl:3205":["I go from the universal to the particular, consequently from P to the *subiect*, *per notam intermediam*, thus: P. M. S.",[]],"refl:3206":["*Major propositio* is not so called because the *terminus major* is in it, but because it is the rule. For the difference of the *terminorum* takes place only in categorical syllogisms.",[]],"refl:3207":["Both together make the whole syllogism.",[]],"refl:3208":["Either when a premise is false or 2.\nOr the conclusion does not flow from the premises. *Homo habet pedes, Asinus* — — —.\nWhen both are false.",[]],"refl:3209":["From false judgments something true can never be correctly inferred, but indeed from their partial truth. *e.g.* All souls of human beings are immortal;\n— Angels are souls of human beings;\n*Ergo* ...\n*Conditio veritatis*: they are spirits.",[]],"refl:3210":["The argumentation can be correct (form), although from incorrect grounds (matter).",[]],"refl:3211":["/\n*ratiocinium est vel purum vel hybridum (in qvo conclusio immediata immixta est)* [a ratiocination is either pure or hybrid (in which an immediate conclusion is mixed in)].",[]],"refl:3212":["Uncertain syllogisms.\nOn L §. 361 sentence 2:\nWhy one believes that the logical order is not necessary.",[]],"refl:3213":["is a *principium formale* of propositions, not of inferences.",[]],"refl:3214":["What is valid of all human beings, that is valid of some and of one.\nψ−θ. L 101. On L §. 363 “the inference — particular” (l. 23):\nIs valid in all inferences.",[]],"refl:3215":["{S16_716.SHG}\nIn this figure  lie two kinds of inferences:\nAll C are B; All A are C; consequently all A are B. 1. figure.\nAll A are C; all C are B; consequently some B are A. 4th figure.\n Herein lie the following:\nAll C are B; some A are C. *Ergo*: some A are B.",[]],"refl:3216":["The logical relation of all [jud] concepts is that the one is contained under the *sphaera notionis* of the [subiects] other:  The |\nmetaphysical relation consists in whether the one is combined with the other synthetically or analytically:\nsynthetic\nanalytic",[]],"refl:3217":["*Nota notae est nota rei ipsius* (g is the *principium* of categorical inferences): is this *dictum* and at the same time the ground of the *prima figura*.",[]],"refl:3218":["The *princip* of categorical syllogisms is the *dictum de omni et nullo*: *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius: repugnans notae* etc etc.\nThat of the hypothetical is: *a ratione ad rationatum valet conseqventia, a negatione rationati ad negationem rationis itidem*.\nThat of the disjunctive is: *a contradictorie oppositorum negatione vnius* (g *ad affirmationem alterius*) *valet conseqventia et [viceversa] a positione vnius* etc. (not as in the hypothetical, where [only (g the) conv] only one kind of inference held in the conversion).",[]],"refl:3219":["Three *Principien* for the 3 kinds of inference: 1. *Principium contradictionis*. 2. *rationis* 3. *exclusi medii* — —",[]],"refl:3220":["The three sides of a triangle are the sufficient ground of the angles.\nthus I can say: where the sufficient ground is encountered ....\nOn L §. 364 sentence 2 “then — false”:\nfor otherwise, were it true, then 2 sequels opposed to one another would be true at the same time.\nBut where the sufficient ground is not, there one cannot always say that the sequel is not. For the sufficient ground can be twofold. All human beings have sensations; there I thus cannot infer: what is no human being has no sensations.",[]],"refl:3221":["All members of the *disjunction* except one, taken together, make up the *contradictorie oppositum* of this one; thus a *dichotomie*, where it holds: if one of the two is true, then the other is false, and conversely.",[]],"refl:3222":["All human beings are sinners; some sinners are human beings; No one who is no sinner is a mere human being.",[]],"refl:3223":["/\nAll human beings can err;\nLeibniz was a human being;\nThus Leibniz could err.",[]],"refl:3224":["Take the *medium terminum* as *Subject* and affirm of it the *praedicat* of the *conclusion*. Take the *subject* of the *conclusion* and affirm of it the *medium terminum*. |\n|\n|\n|\n|\n| there, wherein the ground is encountered, is also the grounded. On L 102 after “have in common” (l. 26) the following words, stemming from σ−φ (? ω?), are to be inserted:\nThe extraordinary ones have in their *praemissen* one or more judgments in common.\nβ1. L 102'. Opposite L §. 368 “the — pro” (l. 27f.):\nIn all *ratiociniis* this must be.",[]],"refl:3225":["1. There are only 2 *praemissae* in which the *medius terminus* is contained.\n2. that *praemissa* in which *terminus medius est loco subiecti* is *maior*, and the *praedicat* is affirmed of it.",[]],"refl:3226":["No thinking *substantz* is composite.\nAll bodies are composite.\nThus no body is a thinking [being] substance.",[]],"refl:3227":["All [fallen] natural human beings sin.\nSome natural human beings are [learned ration] prudent.\nThus [sin] some prudent ones sin.\nFurther L 103':\nNo triangle has [4] more than 3 angles.\nsome triangles are [right-angled figures] equilateral figures.\nThus some equilateral figures have not more than 3 angles.\nFurther L 102 from ψ−χ (? µ??):\nAll human beings are [faulty] imperfect.\nSome human beings are virtuous.\nSome virtuous ones are imperfect.",[]],"refl:3228":["*Omnis spiritus est substantia.\nOmnis substantia habet vim agendi.\nQvoddam, qvod habet vim agendi, est spiritus.*\nFurther ψ−χ (? µ??):\n*Reductio.*\n*Omnis substantia habet vim agendi.\nOmnis spiritus est substantia.\nErgo: Omnis spiritus habet vim agendi.\nErgo: Qvoddam, qvod habet vim agendi, est spiritus.*\nFurther ψ−χ:\nIf the *reduction* were to take place through the [invers] *conversion* of the *praemissen*:\n*Qvaedam substantia est spiritus.*\n[*Omnis substantia habet*] *Omne, qvod vim agendi habet, est substantia.*\n*Ergo*: then nothing would follow.\nFurther ψ−θ:\n*Qvicqvid non est substantia, non est spiritus.\nQvicqvid non habet vim agendi, est non substantia.\nErgo qvicqvid non habet vim* etc etc., *non est spiritus.*",[]],"refl:3229":["In the final sentence of L §. 372 Kant adds the following letters: above “stupid”: P, above “learned”: M, above “learned ones”: M, above “pious”: S, above “pious ones”: S, above “stupid ones”: P.\nBeside the final sentence of L §. 372 and the beginning of L §. 373 stand the following schemata (probably also stemming from ι-φ):\nPM\nMP /\nMS\nSM /\nSP\nSP",[]],"refl:3230":["If, as here, *maior* is *negans*, *minor particulariter affirmans*, then the *praemissen* can be converted. If it is affirmative, then *metathesis praemissarum* must take place.",[]],"refl:3231":["In the 4th figure it says: the *praedicat* depends on the *medio termino*, the *medius terminus* depends on the *Subiect*, thus the *Subiect* on the *Praedicat*, which is false; rather it must say: the *praedicat* on the *subiect*, and this *proposition* must afterwards be converted.",[]],"refl:3232":["In the fourth figure it is inferred: The *praedicat* depends on the |\n*medio termino*, the *medius terminus* on the *subiect* (of the *conclusion*), consequently the *Subiect* on the *praedicat*, which does not follow at all, but rather at most its converse. [It] In order to make this possible, *maior* must be made into *minori* and *vice versa* and the *conclusion* converted, because with the former alteration *minor terminus* is altered into *majorem*.",[]],"refl:3233":["(s The *reduction* takes place either *per metathesin terminorum* or *praemissarum*.)",[]],"refl:3234":["It is necessary for the *synthesi* [not merely] that the *praedicat* of the *conclusion* be in the first *praemissa*, and in this *synthesi* lies the sole ground without which it would be arbitrary, and then more than four figures (g namely 8 *per metathesin*); but this *synthesis* absolutely requires that the *praedicat* of the *conclusion*[d] first be predicated of the *medio termino*, but this of the *subiect* in *minori*.",[]],"refl:3235":["/\n└",[]],"refl:3236":["The *subiect* is always particular or *inferior* in regard to the *praedicat*. Thus the *praedicat* must be taken universally in the *maiori*.\nIn the second figure P is particular. It must thus be able to be raised to universality, become *superior*.\nIn the third S is universal; it must be able to be lowered to *particularitaet*.",[]],"refl:3237":["“*Maior universalis, Minor affirmans*” signifies: that the *praedicat* of the *conclusion* must be *conceptus [maior] superior* than the *medius terminus*. (s The *Subiect* depends on the *praedicate* by means of the *medii termini*.)",[]],"refl:3238":["The proposition “*ars non habet osorem*” etc etc. makes it that one cannot reject this *artificium characteristicum*.",[]],"refl:3239":["*medius terminus* must compare the place of a *nota intermedia*, thus stand first *loco subiecti*, second *loco praedicati*. Hence 1st figure lies *tacite* at the ground of all *syllogismen*.\n(g NB. *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius*: is the *princip* of the first figure and yet at the same time of all syllogisms.)",[]],"refl:3240":["/\n*ratiocinium vel purum vel hybridum. [Conclusio] Conseqventia immediata per metathesin terminorum vel conversionem vel contrapositionem.*",[]],"refl:3241":["A fox is a 4-footed animal.\nHerod was a fox.\n*Ergo* ...\n*In prima propositione proprie, in secunda improprie.*\n[Faith justifies without works.\nFaith]\nThe place in which I am, in it is no other.\n(g This room is) The place in, I am. [is the room.]\nThus in this room is no other.\nσ−φ? (ω?) L 103. On L §. 374 “In — three” (729 22-24):\nThe syllogism otherwise goes on 4 feet.",[]],"refl:3242":["Because otherwise there would be 4 *termini*.\nAll affects good in themselves become, through the weakness of human beings, [corrupted sinf] faulty.\nParental love is an affect good in itself.\nThus parental love in itself becomes faulty through the weakness of human beings.",[]],"refl:3243":["No human being is a [beast] irrational animal.\nNo ox is a human being.\nThus no ox is an irrational animal.\nFurther L 104 from β1−ε2:\n*ex puris negantibus nihil seqvitur*.",[]],"refl:3244":["(s Example of the *iudicio infinito*:)\nWhat is not composite is not corporeal.\nThe soul is not composite.\nThus it is not body.",[]],"refl:3245":["For according to the rule of regular inferences one must infer from a universal to the universal or particular.\n*e.g.* Some sensing beings are human beings.\nSome irrational animals are sensing beings.\nThus some irrational animals are human beings.\nFurther L 104 from ε2−ψ (? β1??):\n*ex puris particularibus nihil seqvitur*.",[]],"refl:3246":["According to the rule: what is universally denied of a concept is also denied of what belongs under it.\nNow this [concept] thing belongs under the concept; thus it is denied of it.\nFurther L 104 from µ−φ:\n*Conclusio seqvitur partem debiliorem.*",[]],"refl:3247":["To all that belong under [this] a concept, this or the opposite pertains.\nNow some things belong under the concept, thus ...\n*e.g.* All persistent believers become blessed.\nSome human beings are — — —\n*Ergo* ...",[]],"refl:3248":["All 4-cornered right-angled figures are parallelograms.\nA figure that has 4 equal angles is a 4-cornered right-angled figure.\nConsequently a figure that has 4 angles is a parallelogram.",[]],"refl:3249":["All virtues make me happy.\nThe philosophical virtues are virtues.\nConsequently they make me happy.",[]],"refl:3250":["If I say that he does not belong to it, then the denial of the *praedicat* does not follow thereby. *e.g.* all angels are [spirits] rational; human beings are not angels, thus not rational.\nL 105'. On L §. 383 conclusion:\nWhoever is not virtuous does not become happy.\nNow no vicious one is virtuous.",[]],"refl:3251":["For if this were particular, then the conclusion would also have to be so.\nOn L §. 384 “thus must — be” (l. 29):\nfor were it also a particular, then *ex puris particularibus* ...\nFurther L 105' opposite L §. 384 sentence 2 “is it — be” (ll. 28f.):\nSome human beings are godless.\nAll believers are human beings,\nconsequently all godless.\nL 105. On L §. 384 end:\nGod is almighty.\nChrist is God.",[]],"refl:3252":["*qvia conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem.*\nβ1−ε2. Next to L §. 386 sentence 2:\nUniversal\naffirmative.\n— — —\nnegative.\nParticular\naffirmative.\n— — —\nnegative.",[]],"refl:3253":["No composite being is imperishable.\nAll matter is a composite being.\nThus is\nNo matter [is] imperishable.",[]],"refl:3254":["All beings that think are spirits.\nSome substances are thinking beings.\nSome substances are spirits.",[]],"refl:3255":["(s In *prima*. (g *Medius terminus*) The place of a *notae intermediae*.)\nIn *secunda figura*: *Major vniversalis, conclusio negativa*.\nWhat justifies pleases God.\nAll good works [justify] please God.\n*ergo* — —\nÙ Some silver is money.\nÖ\nÜ No cup is money.\nÜ\nÛ Thus no cups are\nØ\nIn *tertia*: *Minor affirmans, conclusio particularis*.\nAll spirit is substance.\nNo spirit is a body.\nThus some bodies are not substances.\nEach spirit is simple.\nEach spirit is substance.\nThus each substance is simple.\nin *qvarta*: [*maj*] *si major affirmans, minor vniversalis*.\n(s *si major universalis, conclusio* (g *erit*) *negatiua*.)\n*si minor affirmans, conclusio particularis.*\n*si conclusio negativa, major vniversalis* (s drops out).\nEvery spirit is substance.\nSome substance is body.\nSome body is [*subst*] spirit.\n(s *conclusio vel negativa vel particularis*.*)\n*(s That follows according to the preceding, because the *major*, as in the 2nd figure, must be converted, and the *minor*, as in the third, also must be converted. The first, however, is possible only if the *major* is negative (s because a universal proposition is to follow). If it is affirmative, then a *metathesis praemissarum* must take place. (For otherwise the *major* would become particular.) The second only if the *minor* is *affirmans*; for this gives through conversion an affirming proposition.)\n(s Thus in none of the three remaining figures a universal affirming conclusion.)",[]],"refl:3256":["/\n(s Of 64, 28 drop out, 36 remain.)\n*modi possibiles*: 64.*\n│\nA a a\n│\ne, a a\n│\ni a\n│\no a\n│\n│\n— a e\n│\na e\n│\n│\n│\n│\na i\n│\na i\n│\n│\n│\n│\na o\n│\na o\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne a\n│\ne e a\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne e\n│\ne e e\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne i\n│\ne e i\n│\n│\n│\n│\ne o\n│\ne e o\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni a\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni e\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni i\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\ni o\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no a\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no e\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no i\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\no o\n│\n│\n│\n│\n*modi* 10: *utiles*.\n*deinde* [16].\nI.**\nBarbara, Celarent, Darii, Ferio.\nII. cesare, camestres, festino, barocco.\nIII. darapti, felapton, disamis, datisi, bocardo, ferison.\nIV. Calemes, bamalip, dimatis, fesapo, fresisom.\n*per regulam*: “*conclusio seqvitur partem debiliorem*” *excluduntur* 24;\n“*ex mere negativis nihil seqvitur*”: *excluduntur* 16.\n(s 19. Possible kinds of inference.)\n*(s Of these [54 drop out, namely] 28 (g drop out), because the conclusion would have to follow *e puris negativis* or *ex puris particularibus* — [18] 36 remain. Of these 18 drop out in which *conclusio* not *seqvitur partem debiliorem*, 8: in which a *conclusio negativa* would follow *ex puris affirmantibus praemissis*; thus 10 remain.)\n**(s Here only the three *vocales* are to be attended to; hence *disamis* and *dimatis* are equally correct. and *camestres*, *Calemes*. *Consonans initialis monstrat, ad quemnam modum primae figurae sit reducendus syllogismus* = *barocco ad barbara*. — *Caeterae*: *S vult simpliciter verti etc etc.*)\n*S. vult simpliciter verti,*\n*P uero per accid.,*\n*M. vult transponi,*\n*C per impossibi e duci.*\nCam\n│\n│\nEveryone devoted to virtue turns away from sensitive appetites.\nEstr:\n│\n│\n│\n│\nNo one who leads a soft and voluptuous life turns away from sensitive appetites.\nes\n│\n│\n│\n│\n│\nNo one who leads a soft and voluptuous life is devoted to virtue.\nCel\n│\n│\n│\n│\nNo one who turns away from sensitive appetites leads a soft and voluptuous life.\nar\n│\n│\n│\nEveryone devoted to virtue turns away from sensitive appetites.\nent.\n│\n│\n│\nNo one devoted to virtue leads a soft and voluptuous life.\nBar.\n│\nEvery wise man is blessed.\noc\n│\n│\nSome learned man is not blessed.\nco\n│\n│\nSome learned man is not wise.\nBar[ba]\n│\nEvery wise man is blessed.\nbar\n│\nEvery learned man is wise.\na\n│\nEvery learned man is blessed.\n(s On the *fabric* of syllogisms. One always seeks ultimately to make reason technical, so that, in subjecting it to the handling of the senses, one may be certain regarding errors.)",[]],"refl:3257":["The rule of the second figure is: whatever a mark of a thing contradicts, that contradicts the thing itself. Here I must first convert and say: whatever a mark contradicts, that contradicts this mark, or I must convert the *conclusionem*: Whatever a mark of a thing contradicts, that the thing itself contradicts, consequently it contradicts the thing.\nWhat is (not true) does not stand in the Bible.\nEverything absurd is (not true).\nNothing absurd stands in the Bible.\n*Consequentia*: Nothing that stands in the Bible is absurd.\nWhat does everything that stands in the Bible contradict? the absurd.",[]],"refl:3258":["The rule of the third figure is: what pertains to or contradicts a mark, [wiede] pertains also etc etc. to some under which this mark is contained. Here I must first say: it pertains to all that are contained under this mark.",[]],"refl:3259":["105.\nL 105':\n(g The *maior* is the rule; the *minor* can be thought either through the subsumption under the condition of the rule or through the negation of the conditioned or of the *praedicat* of the rule. From this latter, however, one can infer only if I convert the negative proposition.)\nFirst it must be proved that in all syllogisms the *maior* must be *universalis*, the *minor affirmans*, consequently that the first figure lies at the ground of all others and the others must be brought to it through *conseqventias immediatas* or *metathesis praemissarum*.\nFor in all other syllogisms the *medius terminus* does not serve as *nota intermedia*.\nThe remaining figures infer only in that the *medius terminus* receives such a place in the propositions that from it, through *conseqventias immediatas*, its place according to the first figure can arise. They are *ratiocinia impura, mixta cum conseqventiis immediatis*.\n(s Rules of all figures: *ex puris* [*affirm:*] *negativis et particularibus nihil seqvitur. Conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem.*\n(s *Non sunt plures quam 3 termini.*)\nRule of the first figure: *maior universalis, minor affirmans*.)\nIn the second figure the *minor* stands right; thus the *maior* must be converted, and indeed so that it remains *universalis*. This is possible only if it is universally negating (s *per conversionem*), or, if it is affirming, if it is contraposed; in both cases the conclusion becomes negative (*sequitur partem debiliorem*).\nIn the third figure the *maior* stands right; the *minor* must be converted, yet so that an affirming proposition arises from it. This, however, is possible only in that the affirming proposition is particular, consequently the *conclusio* is *particularis*.\n(s In the fourth, if the *maior* is (g universally) negating, it can be converted *simpliciter*; likewise the *minor* as particular; thus the *conclusio* is negative. If the *maior* is universally affirming, then it can either only be converted *per accidens* or be contraposed; thus the conclusion is either particular or negative; |\nL 105: |\nin the fourth figure, if the conclusion is not to be converted: *PS* into *SP*, then a *metathesis praemissarum* or *conversio* of both must take place.)\nL 105':\nThe *ratiocinia* in the remaining figures are not merely unnecessary, but spurious (*ratiocinia spuria, genuinis opposita*); for they are *mixta*.",[]],"refl:3260":["The composite syllogisms consist of composite premises.\n*sunt vel conditionales vel disjunctivae vel copulativae.*\nIf the world has a beginning, then it is not God.\n*atqvi* — — —",[]],"refl:3261":["*a positione antecedentis ad positionem consequentis.*\nOn L §. 393 no. 2:\n*a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis.*\nFurther L 107':\nÙ If God has created the world, then it is [without flaws] beautiful.\nÖ\nÜ Now it is not without flaws.\nÜ\nÛ Thus — —\nØ\nIf animals think, then they have reason.\nNow they have no reason, thus they do not think.",[]],"refl:3262":["*non a remotione antecedentis ad remotionem consequentis,*\n*nec a positione consequentis ad positionem antecedentis.*\nIf the world is God, then it is the best world; *atqvi non*: *ergo*.\nIf the earth is an animal, then it has motion; now it has motion ....",[]],"refl:3263":["In hypothetical syllogisms the *consequentz* of two propositions from one another is expressed in the *maiori* [in categorical ones are bey and in *minori*], of which the first is a *praemisse*, the second a conclusion. The *minor* is a transformation of the problematic *condition* into a categorical proposition. One sees from this that it is not a *ratiocinium purum* but *exponibile*. Namely through a particular *ratiocinium*.",[]],"refl:3264":["/\nA hypothetical syllogism consists of 2 propositions, consequently it is not a *ratiocinium*; for whoever says: “God is just — consequently he punishes the persistently evil,” says through the word “consequently”: that, if the first proposition is true, [thus] then so is the second; I can say just the same with immediate inferences. And thus the hypothetical syllogisms are only *conclusiones immediatae demonstrabiles* (*ex antecedente et conseqvente*) either *qvoad materiam* or *formam*.\nIf I say: God is just, thus he is not unjust. etc etc.\n(s The hypothetical syllogisms have no *medium terminum*, but the *consequentz* of one (g categorical) proposition from the other is only indicated.)",[]],"refl:3265":["Every syllogism is to be a proof;\nnow the hypothetical carries with it only the ground of proof;\nconsequently it is not a syllogism.\nIn the hypothetical proposition one thinks only that one proposition follows from the other; in a hypothetical inference, that [one] the one (*antecedens*) is also true [or the]. Because, if the *antecedens* [is denied] is negative: *e.g.* The world is not necessary (consequently [has] it is also not eternal), the *proposition* does not thereby become negative, so this is not a particular *Modus*. For the premise: “if the world is eternal, then it is also necessary,” is already contained in the sequel.",[]],"refl:3266":["/\nOn the logical principle,\nin hypothetical syllogisms\nnot to be able to infer from the sequel to the determinate\nground,\nbecause more than one ground can be for the same sequel.\nWhence does one take this proposition? To be sure the proposition *unius ejusdemqve causati plures possunt esse causae* is correct, but it can be cognized only through experience or, as in mathematics (if it is a question only of ground and sequel), in pure intuition, but not from the mere concept of this relation. — The answer is: in the hypothetical syllogism the major premise is the universal rule, the minor premise [there] contains the particular case under that rule, the conclusion the [sequel of the] application of the universal proposition to the case standing under it.\nThat I thus cannot conversely infer from the sequel to a determinate ground is grounded not on the proposition *vnius eiusdemque caussati etc etc.*, but on this, that I cannot infer from the particular case [to] to the universality of the rule.",[]],"refl:3267":["The human soul is either simple or composite. Now it is not composite, thus simple.\n(g false:)\nThe souls of animals are either spirits or bodies; now they are not spirits; thus — —",[]],"refl:3268":["Holy Scripture is either a divine book, or there are untruths in it.\n*Atqvi* it is a divine book.\nOn L §. 396 no. 2:\nGod has either made the world, or it is eternal.\nNow it is not eternal; thus he has made it.\nFurther opposite L §. 396 “are false” (l. 33) etc.:\nTo this is opposed the categorical, if [is assumed or] the member that is assumed or removed is assumed as the *medio termino*.\nOf a copulative syllogism the *major* is always negating. *e.g.* No one serves God and the devil at the same time; *atqvi* the Americans serve the devil, thus not God.",[]],"refl:3269":["All disjunctive syllogisms of more than two *membris disiunctionis* (s dichotomy of a concept) are properly polysyllogistic. For every true *disiunction* can only be *bimembris*, and the logical *division* is also *bimembris*, but the *membra subdividentia* are, for the sake of brevity, placed under the *membra dividentia*.",[]],"refl:3270":["If God does not punish the (g persistently) evil, then he must either approve their evilness or not have the power to punish.\n*atqvi* the latter is false, thus also the former.\n(g If Christ has not risen, then) The witnesses of the resurrection [have] either have been able to tell the truth and not willed to, or willed to and not been able.",[]],"refl:3271":["They infer from one judgment and a logical rule.",[]],"refl:3272":["*Crypticus per transpositionem praemissarum.*\n*Crypticus per omissionem vel enthymema*\n— *contractus*,\n*si conclusio cum medio termino.*",[]],"refl:3273":["Or one only adds the *medium terminum*. This world is the best, because it was created by the highest being.",[]],"refl:3274":["I am a human being; what befalls others can strike me too.\nFurther from ω (? φ??):\n*Circulus in probando* — and *petitio principii*.",[]],"refl:3275":["Sight represents present things to me Hearing likewise Smell — — — — — — — }\n}\n} thus all senses represent present things to me.\nE.g. all human beings die.\nThe planets attract one another, thus all heavenly bodies.\nOn L §. 401 no. 3:\nLeibnitz can err, consequently all philosophers can err.\nIn the higher concept must be the sufficient ground why one attributes something to the lower.\n(g Other hidden inferences:)\nIn place of a principal concept one can posit the logical alteration of it. Whoever loves his enemy is virtuous. Now Cajus does good to his slanderer, thus he is virtuous.",[]],"refl:3276":["/\nEvery syllogism must yield necessity. Hence induction and analogy are not syllogisms, but presumptions (s inferences of the power of judgment, namely for it) or also [Erfa] empirical inferences.",[]],"refl:3277":["From the particular one infers to the universal in empirical sciences, in which experiences are to become *principia*; for without their yielding universal propositions, they cannot be used for inferring. Thus empirical principles are at all times true *per inductionem* alone and say: that what holds of as many of a certain genus as I know will also hold of the remaining ones.\nFrom the part of a whole concept one cannot infer to [whole] the rest; but, if everything that I perceive of things [that] of one genus [as belonging to the genus] agrees with one another, then they will also agree in regard to the rest which [to] was perceived as at all times combined with those determinations. Inference from analogy.",[]],"refl:3278":["Extraordinary inferences go from the particular to the universal: |\ninduction and analogy; but then they are not true inferences of reason, but presumptions of it.",[]],"refl:3279":["Analogy proceeds according to the rule: *similium eadem* (g *par*) *est ratio*. (s The inference would be strict if the ground of their similarity lay in the concept of their [whole] essence.)",[]],"refl:3280":["Induction (s extends the empirically given from the particular to the universal in regard to many objects, analogy the given properties of a thing to several of the very same thing) makes what he knows of things of a genus universal and extends it to things of the same genus which he also does not know (synthetic).\nAnalogy gives [that which] [what he knows of one thing, to others in which he does not know it] to things of one genus in which [he] one knows something agreeing, also the rest which he cognizes in one, but not in others. *e.g.* Earth and moon.",[]],"refl:3281":["On the necessity (g [of empirical universality]) of general propositions (s through induction), i.e. properly particular propositions that in use hold *instar vniversalium*. They are rules that admit an *exception*, although rarely [or], like grammatical ones, because they are empirical. |\nIf no *exception* has yet occurred, then it is with right presumed that the ground of the truth lies in the constitution of the thing and not merely the circumstances of its perception.\nWe cannot do without such general propositions.",[]],"refl:3282":["110'.\nL 110:\nInduction infers *a particulari ad universale* (g through induction one obtains general, not universal propositions) according to the principle of universalization.\nAnalogy from partial* similarity of two things to total according to the principle of specification.\n(g Through analogy: on the whole, but not wholly similar things.)\nL 110':\n(* g One in many, thus in all: induction. Much in one (which is also in others), thus also all the rest in the same: Analogy. )",[]],"refl:3283":["L 110:\ninduction: one mark in many — *ergo* in all of the same kind; yet here attention must be paid to the condition.\nAnalogy: many marks in one, thus also all the remaining ones that are connected with the same condition.",[]],"refl:3284":["/\n1. What pertains to many things of one genus pertains also to the remaining ones (g all).\n2. If (s so) much (s in) the things of one genus (s as we can perceive) pertains [then pertains] and they agree therein, then the rest also pertains to them which we know in some of this genus and do not perceive in the given ones.\nThe last inference goes from something which [in] a thing has in common with others to the rest which it will also have in common with those.\n(s If many (g all of one genus, as far as I know) agree in something, then all.\nIf two things agree in much (g everything, as far as I know): then in everything.)",[]],"refl:3285":["According to analogy we bring things empirically under a genus.\nOne cannot according to analogy infer *ultra tertium comparationis*.\nEverything depends on how to draw universal judgments from experience, hence not *a priori*: either from many to all things of a kind, or from many determinations of a thing to the remaining ones of it. That the moon has inhabitants, not: that they look as we do. That they have eyes.",[]],"refl:3286":["Logical universality says something of genus or species and |\nnot of all individuals in an aggregate of them; for the latter yields only particular propositions. As: all planets of our system are dark bodies.\nEmpirical universality is only [*gen*] *analogon* of the logical.",[]],"refl:3287":["The power of judgment is twofold: the determining or reflecting power of judgment. The former goes from the universal to the particular, the second from the particular to the universal. The latter has only subjective validity. — (Inference according to analogy (g and induction) is logical presumption.)\n(s They are principles of the [empirical] procedure in the expansion of our cognition through experience.\nWe can indeed think a being (which is incomprehensible to us) according to analogy, but not infer [according to] from a relation of given things to the same relation of unknown beings.)",[]],"refl:3288":["these can be either divisions *a priori* [of a concept] or empirical division.",[]],"refl:3289":["an empirical universality.",[]],"refl:3290":["All human beings are mortal (g all planets dark bodies): an inference from induction.\nFor the sake of the identity of the *medii termini*. analogy.",[]],"refl:3291":["[From *particula*] From a part of the *sphaera* of a concept to the whole *sphaera*. — From a part of the concept to the identity of the whole concept, i.e. holds also of the rest. *e.g.* gravitation, suns do not move around dark bodies.",[]],"refl:3292":["We infer according to analogy only to rational moon-inhabitants, not to human beings; thus with analogy only the identity of the ground, *par ratio*, is required.",[]],"refl:3293":["/\nImmortality according to the analogy [of the appropriateness] of the complete development of the natural predispositions [to] of each creature.",[]],"refl:3294":["If in things that which we become aware of in them [with as belonging to their generic concept to the unity of the] must be thought as belonging to one and the same genus, then the rest too which is required for the very same genus, although we do not become aware of it in them, can be presumed of them. E.g. mushrooms agree with vegetables in regard to growth; thus according to the analogy with [plants] them they will also agree with them in this, that they propagate themselves through seed. But I cannot infer that, because animals [according to], as far as we know them, have circulation of their juices, plants are also so constituted. For they are in [*Species*] genus distinct in that which concerns the ground of the given determination, since the former take nourishment into themselves voluntarily.\nWe can therefore indeed think to ourselves (g the causality) of a cause of the world according to the analogy [with] (g of the constitution) of a cause in the world, [but] namely in the way of being a cause as human beings (namely through understanding) are cause of a clock, but not infer from the future constitution of the things of the world to that causality. For in the first case we have only similar relations, in the second a similar thing — but God and causes in the world are entirely heterogeneous things.\nTo represent something to oneself according to an analogy — to infer.",[]],"refl:3295":["Sophistries.",[]],"refl:3296":["The right eye is not necessary for seeing. nor the left. thus neither.",[]],"refl:3297":["No artist is born. Some human beings are artists ....\nIf you say: “you lie,” and in saying this etc.\nWhoever can get through the whole world with Latin can come to every place in the world.\nNow the morass is a place in the world.\n— — — —",[]],"refl:3298":["Whoever drinks well sleeps well; whoever sleeps well does not sin (g while he sleeps); whoever does not sin is saved; therefore whoever drinks well is saved.\nL 111. On the example in L §. 404 no. 3:\nin the major premise without, in the minor premise with closer determination.",[]],"refl:3299":["Because Paul warns against philosophy.",[]],"refl:3300":["The liar.",[]],"refl:3301":["if it is not true, then either the dogs as well as the others have no reason; 2. that besides the dogs still other four-footed animals have reason.",[]],"refl:3302":["Sophist who had a lawsuit with his pupil.\nEither I win the lawsuit: then I need give you nothing. Or I lose it: then I also need give you nothing.",[]],"refl:3303":["Dialectic: art of illusion.\n— — mere form is deceptive, not merely of syllogisms, but of the — — stellung. *e.g.* Many grounds heaped up, of which none is any good. Similarities with — —, instead of using analogies. Agreement of others instead of proofs.",[]],"refl:3304":["/\nAbaelard. there flies an ox etc etc.",[]],"refl:3305":["The logic of the Megarian sect (*Euclidis non geometrae*).\n*Sophistici ratiocinandi modi*:\n1. *Mentiens*. If you say that you lie, and you speak (s objectively) the truth, you lie (s subjectively). therefore you lie that you lie, i.e. you do not lie.\nEpimenides says that all Cretans are liars; but he himself is a Cretan, therefore he lies, and the Cretans are truthful; but he himself is a Cretan, therefore (s and since he is truthful) he is to be believed, and the Cretans are liars.\nSeneca. My whole life lies: refute it, direct it to the truth, if you are sharp.\nPhiletas by these subtleties was made thin and slender, so that, lest he be carried off by the wind, he was compelled to put leaden little balls beneath.\n2. *Electra*. (Agamemnon's daughter did not recognize Orestes her brother standing by.)\n3. *Obvelatus*. Do you know your father? Yes. But if I place a veiled man near you? Do you know this one? Not at all. But he himself is your father; therefore you know and at the same time do not know.\n4. *Acervus* (*Sorites*). This dream is probable; why not also (g very) verisimilar? why not so much so that it is with difficulty distinguished from the true? finally: that nothing comes between this and that?\nDoes one grain make a heap? Do two, do three? therefore that a heap finally comes to be, a single grain brings about.\n5. *Cornutus*. But you have not lost horns, therefore you have them.\n6. *Calvus*. from which hair lost does someone begin to be counted bald?\nIf something moves, it moves either in the place in which it is, or in which it is not.\nThe *sophisma aceruus* always takes place where we want to make a specific difference through mere magnitude. There must be a *Punctum flexus contrarii* in the progression, where the one direction ceases and the other begins. Thus avarice cannot be explained by all too great thrift etc etc. Rich and poor are distinguished in that the former exceeds prosperity or has more than he needs, but the latter is below it.\n*Summum ius summa iniuria* — against the stale principles of equity — or the principle: to prefer what is useful to the most to what is right.\n*Fiat iustitia, pereat mundus* — it will not perish thereby.\nto be all too kind (not all too clever).",[]],"refl:3306":["/\nthrough subordination.\nNext to L §. 406 “not — immortal” (ll. 22-24):\nthrough coordination: if the *major* is a copulative proposition.",[]],"refl:3307":["Ù All triangles have three angles.\nÖ\nÜ all right-angled triangles are triangles.\nÜ\nÛ — — — —\nØ\n[All figures that have no more than three angles.]\nEverything that is divisible takes up a space.\nThe soul takes up no space.\nThus it is not divisible.\nWhat is not divisible is imperishable.",[]],"refl:3308":["*cui adjicitur probatio.*\nFurther:\n4. What makes me more perfect, to that I am obligated; for that is [the] (g the rule of the) nature of the free will. Now virtue makes me more perfect, because it is in accordance with the laws of nature. Thus I am obligated to virtue.",[]],"refl:3309":["The soul is a simple thing.\nEverything simple is indivisible.\nEverything indivisible imperishable.\nEverything imperishable endures always.\nThus the soul endures always.",[]],"refl:3310":["(s *fiat major* I.\n*major*\nII.\nA spirit that has no obscure representations at all has also no confused ones. whoever has no confused ones has nothing but distinct ones. whoever has nothing but distinct ones — — —, has the most distinct. whoever has the most distinct cognition —: he knows all possible things. Whoever these, he is omniscient. whoever is omniscient is infinite. whoever is infinite is God. *ergo*. )\nOne makes it thus:\n1.\nOne takes the judgment that one wants to prove and proves it *in prima figura*.\n2.\nThe *minorem* and so on.\nThe last is the first prosyllogism.\n3.\nAll judgments that occur twice are left out.\nExcepted in the first prosyllogism.\n4.\nOne begins from the *minori* of the first *praemissarum*.\n5.\nThe *majores* follow one another.\n6.\nThe conclusion has the subject of the first premise and the predicate of the last.\nFurther L 113 from σ−χ? (ξ?) (ω?):\nChain inference.\n*acervus*.",[]],"refl:3311":["*omnes praemissae vniversales affirmativae et vniversales.*",[]],"refl:3312":["The chain inference through categorical propositions, i.e. predicate. The chain inference through hypothetical propositions, i.e. *consequentz*. {\n{\n{\n{\n{just as the chain-rule calculation. *praedicatum praedicatorum sibi subordinatorum est etiam praedicatum subiecti*; or *rationatum rationatorum sibi subordinatorum est etiam rationatum principii*.",[]],"refl:3313":["E.g. the principle of sufficient ground.\nOn L §. 410 “*saltus illegitimus*”:\nThere is order in the world; there must therefore be an author located outside the world.",[]],"refl:3314":["/\nA freethinker must not be refuted from Holy Scripture.\nA Catholic not from the apocryphal books.\nOn L §. 411 “*circulus in probando*”:\nThe actuality of God from Scripture.\nFurther L 113' from ψ−θ:\n(s *Circulus vitiosus*:) e.g. The proof that duty rests on happiness, and afterwards: that one cannot find oneself happy without being conscious of virtue.\n(s There one must presuppose duty before the [duty] desire for happiness.\nOr if one proves the Godhead from the ends of the world and afterwards concludes that everything in the world is wise because it belongs to the divine ends.)",[]],"refl:3315":["If one proves the immortality of souls. and wants to prove their imperishability.\nL 113. On L §. 412 “*probatio plus probans*”:\nE.g. that we should not take our lives, because we did not give it to ourselves.",[]],"refl:3316":["Without order there is no perfection.\nFurther:\nWhoever does not have insight into the rules of order does not have insight into order itself.",[]],"refl:3317":["E.g. how to give a speech.\nTo teach the catechism.\nσ−χ? ω? L 114. On L §. 415 “common method of teaching”:\npopular.\nOn L §. 415 “rational method of teaching”:\nscholastically correct.\nψ−θ. Between L §. 415 and 416:\n*Methodus naturalis, artificialis.*",[]],"refl:3318":["Wit, imagination, reason.",[]],"refl:3319":["Scientific or free method of teaching.",[]],"refl:3320":["/\n(s *Locus communis*: commonplace.)\n*De locis* or *topica*. *Loci* are the general heads of arguments or the names of the genera under which they are found. *Loci gramatici*: *ab Etymologia*. 2. *Logici*. *a definitione, a genere et specie, ab oppositis*. 3. *Metaphysici*: *a toto ad partes*, axioms concerning efficient causes. 4. *Ethici*: ultimate ends, the becoming and the honorable, likewise in questions of fact. 5. *Physici*: from the ends of nature.",[]],"refl:3321":["Method of teaching is only the form of a whole of cognitions according to logical perfection.",[]],"refl:3322":["Regularity of practice is either —\nMethod: A [determinate] rule (g *a priori* of) of the practice of cognitions; constitutive rule or prescription; or Manner: A way of uniform conduct without [rule] prescription.\nIn everything that must relate to an idea there is method. Consequently in every doctrine, but not in conversation.",[]],"refl:3323":["/\nOn the last proposition of L §. 421:\nAll cognition and a whole of it must be [according to] in conformity with a rule. Rulelessness is at the same time unreason. But this rule is either of manner (free) or of method (constraint). Cognition as science [stands] must be arranged according to a method. For this is a whole of cognition as system and not merely as aggregate. Herein the scholastically correct is the ground.\n(s For science requires a systematic cognition, hence one composed according to considered rules.)",[]],"refl:3324":["The grounds of every method relate:\n1. to the object;\n2. to the subject;\n3. to the cognitive power with which the object is compared;\n4. [for the person and his station, who teaches] to the science or particular kind of cognitions to which something belongs. e.g. One must distinguish the objective from the subjective, that which is true in the idea from that which happens, the object of mere reason from the empirical, in general each part of cognitions from that which is of another kind. *Philosophia pura*.",[]],"refl:3325":["Method is unity of a whole of cognition from principles. thus it is at all times scientific; it makes an aggregate into a system and common cognition into science\nPopularity requires only manner, i.e. unity according to empirical laws of social intercourse.\nPractical logic is guidance about method and its diversity in general.",[]],"refl:3326":["The theory (g [doctrine] *doctrin*): which contains the conditions of the perfection of a cognition.\nThe method: which expounds the way to arrive at it. The latter also abstracts from the content.\n*Modus*, the manner, differs from *methodo* in that the latter is a *modus* from *principien*, the former only from empirical grounds. *e.g. svaviter in modo, fortiter in re.* Investigations of method are the last and most difficult.",[]],"refl:3327":["Method is really the rule of *praxis* (exercise), insofar as [it] one is conscious of it. At first one thinks tumultuarily, thereafter regularly, finally methodically. Method is the last and most important thing of thinking.\n(s The way of teaching is methodical or tumultuary. The former systematic or rhapsodistic (s — aphoristic).)",[]],"refl:3328":["Scholars [are] must teach not merely pupils but also scholars. For a scholar must learn from others. This happens |\nthrough books. A scholar thus has only two paths. He is either also a teacher with respect to other scholars (or at least can be one) or indeed a teacher with respect to the *idioten*, but a pupil with respect to other scholars.\nA teacher who is himself only a pupil is no real scholar. For if he cannot himself extend science, then he is not in possession of the methods, and he lacks indeed not acquaintance, but yet insight.\nA teacher who writes no books, and indeed for scholars, is no public person in the field of learning. Hence to write for the public and not at all for scholars, because this *publicum* does not hang together, is just the same as keeping an inn as against him who serves the police.",[]],"refl:3329":["Method is not always necessary, and then the manner is rhapsodistic. *e.g.* little stories, similes etc. The pieces of a system without elaboration give aphoristic method.\nPopularity consists in the accommodation and condescension to common cognition and common (g interest) attention. It is the greatest perfection of a fine mind, but only then, when it is combined with thoroughness.\nThe rules of popularity are still a philosophers' stone for logicians. (s It also has a particular method, which is really the end of popularity and from which its cognition begins.)",[]],"refl:3330":["The way of expounding the thoughts of one's own mind to oneself and of fittingly bringing them forth is the *modus proponendi*.",[]],"refl:3331":["On »Der andere Haupttheil« (77617):\nGeneral doctrine of method. Not practical logic.",[]],"refl:3332":["114'.\nL 114:\nLogic has two parts: 1. a theoretical one, the doctrine of elements of the understanding in general; 2. (g practical) the doctrine of method. Both irrespective of the *obiect*, also of the origin and of the *principien* of cognition.\nThe doctrine of elements is a. Analytic, b. Dialectic. The doctrine of method treats only of the [manifold] way to connect the manifold of cognition into a science. It is either doctrinal or polemical. This logic is *logica pura*. The anthropological *logica* is *applicata*. |\nL 114': |\nOn prejudices. On persuasion. On the interest in cognition in itself: the logical one of the understanding, the aesthetic one of sensibility.",[]],"refl:3333":["/\n*Modus*, way of teaching, is either *modus aestheticus*: manner, or [*scholasticus*] *logicus*: method (s *Forma systematis*). the former is (s with regard to) popularity, the latter (s with regard to) scholastic method (s perfection).\nPractical logic treats merely of the form of a science in general and of the presentation of it. Thus of method.\nMethod can also be called way of thinking (g according to form, not according to principles.) in distinction from the mode of thinking.",[]],"refl:3334":["Popularity concerns either the method or the presentation.",[]],"refl:3335":["To think without method is tumultuary.\nTo think with an [only concealed] empirical method (g but not to present): rhapsodistic.\nWith an order according to method, but without [combination i.e.] transitions, since it constitutes no *qvantum continuum*, but *discretum*: aphoristic.\nwith method of thinking and of presentation, at the same time of connection: systematic.",[]],"refl:3336":["/\n(g The way of expression) Presentation or style is distinguished from method (g the way of the combination of thoughts.). Both according to *principien*.",[]],"refl:3337":["The word \"way of teaching\" is unsuitable. It should be called merely systematic way of thinking. Since now in one's own thinking one passes over much that belongs to the interconnection and the *totalitaet* of a system, [so] by contrast in teaching one is compelled to take account of it, the systematic form is called way of teaching.",[]],"refl:3338":["Systematic presentation is to fragmentary Methodical presentation is to tumultuary\n}\n} opposed.\n}\nThe fragmentary[metho] way of teaching which is nevertheless (g at the same time) methodical — is aphoristic.\n(fragmentary and rhapsodic are one and the same.)",[]],"refl:3339":["Everything that is contingent is alterable; the fortune of human beings is contingent, therefore alterable.",[]],"refl:3340":["One must according to that think through no great doctrinal edifice [draw]. A beginner must not learn it in such a way.\nOn L §. 423 no. 1:\n*inventio* .\nThen I know which premises will be necessary for the sequels.\nOn L §. 423 no. 3:\nLet one imprint upon oneself the propositions and then the grounds.\nOn L §. 423 no. 4:\nFirst the conclusion, from it the proof.",[]],"refl:3341":["*e.g.* clear ones before distinct ones.\nOn L §. 425 no. 2:\n*Axiomata, postulata, lemmata.*",[]],"refl:3342":["Synthesis and analysis is either of coordination: when the whole is thought before the parts or conversely, or of sub|ordination:\nwhen the ground is thought before the sequel and the universal before the particular, the *abstractum* before the *concretum*.",[]],"refl:3343":["All cognition is as to content historical or rational.\nThe method of the latter is synthetic or analytic. The latter is the method of discovering, the former of grasping. If analysis (especially in religion) has gone before (which must always happen with concepts), then synthesis must yet finally follow, so that a system comes to be. (s Astronomy first through appearances, then according to a system.)",[]],"refl:3344":["Empirical natural science: analytic, rational: synthetic.\nMorals, that there is one: analytic.\nIn history to begin from chronology is synthetic. — In morals from freedom and natural necessity. — In explanation of sayings.",[]],"refl:3345":["It is still always the question whether just as much learning is necessary in order to know how human beings are to live pleasingly to God |\n(g so that, if all Greek ceased, no human being would know how he is to be saved), or in order that their rights among one another be allotted (g and) administered to human beings. In both cases no other rules need to be given than those which each one's natural understanding teaches him when he acts or represents to himself from his actions a right against others. Deep, hidden grounds of right, [to] which no human being lets occur to him in his civil actions, cannot, even if they lie therein, be of help to him. If the current rules of reason cannot [to] determine what is to be done, then one can be in the wrong merely because one is not learned or has not asked a learned man. The positive laws would have to constitute an entirely separate collection. In the lawsuit the parties must not be regarded as learned men, consequently the advocate (g who only puts their claim in order) must not represent them as a learned man, i.e. rationalize more finely than they can. [Each] One must adduce the ground of right on which he has grounded his claim, the other justify his entitlement. Neither rationalize about right in general, but only present what his disposition in the legal transaction was, and his opinion of the right, the judge however present the ground of right of the decision. What is useless in all this philosophy must determine. Everything here comes down to this, that actions are brought under rules, and indeed universal practical rules. A distinction is always to be made between universal civil right and that of the arbitrary constitution. There is also a distinction to be made between the use of learning, which sharpens the judgment of the judge, and the ground of right on which the parties can rely.\nSince no one can complain that his right is not administered to him if he receives satisfaction without delay and partiality with respect to the concepts that he makes for himself of his right (and which at the same time his opponent makes for himself), but the public nevertheless applies no learning in order to contrive its concepts of right, and he too [who] who contrives a ground of right that does not lie in common concepts already uses intrigues: legislation needs no learning, still much less the administration of *iustice*. Where a particular constitution of the state or the locality* requires positive laws (of which there must nevertheless be few), there the *principien* must serve as precepts. Otherwise unlearned peoples would not be able to administer any right.\n*(g or where cunning and the uncertainty of the proofs of the *pactorum* are to be forestalled.)\nThere are two kinds of concept of right, of which the one requires learning and pondering, the other not at all, but only an understanding practiced in business. The latter concept is inferred from the conditions of the unity of the will of the participants; the former from the conditions of the distribution of right or determination of the difference of the manifold, (g which) under a rule (g stands). (If someone demands something out of his right, then he must name it, and also be able to suppose that his counterpart must have thought the same thereby. Consequently he does not demand that something be worked out for him according to concepts unknown to him, but only that his ground of right be compared with that of the other. The law must thus refer to the concrete but universal concepts of right.)\nThe *iustitia distributiva* presupposes that every right must be able to be proved in whose possession one has not been prior to all *Laesion*. The *commutativa*, however, etc etc. *e.g.* If someone lends something and harm arises from it. Whether the advocate is such an intermediary to whom the party can hand over his case in order to seek a right in it, and how the law may forestall this assaying art of the advocate. With the commutative it comes down to what right follows from the relation of the parties |\nalone. With the distributive: what follows from the relation to the whole.",[]],"refl:3346":["Law is either a necessary one (g law *stricte*) or a contingent one; the latter is called statute and has the universal advantage as its aim. The laws (*leges stricte tales*) must be taken not [w] from principles of the unity of the system, but of the will.",[]],"refl:3347":["Whether legislation must not direct itself according to the principle of the *iustitia commutativa* or determine it?\nto the common judgment of right is suited the unity of the *principii*, but not the plurality of the restricting conditions from the kinds of right (*personel* or *reel*). Sale breaks lease. *Vindicatio ablati.* He who has acquired what belongs to another without his fault can indeed not gain, but must also not suffer harm.\nOne *principium* is: one can acquire from another *facto iusto* only as much as the other declares. Thus the seller before the *tradition* not the security of what he sells, without having stipulated it for himself; the *commodatarius* not, beyond the use, the release from all risk on account of the thing itself.\nThe representation that each makes for himself of right in his juridical actions is the agreement of the other under known conditions. The common understanding indeed cognizes principles of right, but not the sources of right. *Principia constitutiva* (g or rather *principia regulativa*, not *constitutiva*), which prescribe what is right, but not *genetica*, which set out the ground of rightfulness. *e.g. casum sentit dominus* in the case of what has been lent. Also the latter cognition is doubtful.",[]],"refl:3348":["What has arisen from a contract can thus easily be brought to common *principia*.\nSystem of a popular cognition of right distinguished from the speculative. The popular concepts of right that contradict the speculative ones must first be sought out.",[]],"refl:3349":["*Jus reale* is an ideal obligation of a thing toward the *dominum*.",[]],"refl:3350":["To every right corresponds an obligation of others not to hinder him in the exercise of it. But because all human beings have a right |\nto acquire *per occupationem* or *translationem* (g just as the owner himself), there corresponds on the side of the owner to this right an obligation to distinguish his property recognizably and securely, and not of others to investigate this distinction or the *titulum* of the *possession* of a third.\n[Suppose] Thus the *dominus* has no right except against the *primum laedentem*; the others justly ignore the *titulum possessionis* of the latter. It is either the *domini* fault or misfortune if [he] after (g unlawfully) interrupted possession the thing is transferred in right form.\nIt is to be proved that each in the *permutatione iurium* has a natural right to ignore the *titulum possessionis* of the other, indeed, that he is not authorized to inquire after it, before he is agreed The *iniustus possessor*. Otherwise it would follow that each who is to transfer his right must first prove that he has one.",[]],"refl:3351":["The right that someone acquires from another depends solely on the *modum translationis* of the thing, and not on the right that the latter has to the thing. Otherwise we would be able to acquire no thing *originarie*, because there there is as yet no right to the thing. Thus someone can indeed give another not more (thing) than he has, but can yet constitute a right that he does not have, because the other acquires through his *acceptation* and does no one wrong, although the former does someone wrong.\nHe has [a] right to transfer a thing of which it remains undetermined |\nhow he came by it. I can sell something that another has given me for sale as my own.",[]],"refl:3352":["Right can be taken negatively; then it signifies merely: not wrong. This is how it stands with the *ius reale*. But it is also affirmative; then it refers to obligation and is *ius personale*.",[]],"refl:3353":["One must always distinguish what belongs in itself to perfection and what is required for its execution. The latter must be left to last. In fairness the judge should judge from the *facto* and not from the alleged grounds of right of the advocates; for these can be false, but the case nevertheless right. Although such a way of judging is burdensome, it is nevertheless perhaps not impossible.",[]],"refl:3354":["One can divide all laws into right-determining and provisory ones. The latter must be entirely separated from the former. Thereby the one for whom the government provides obtains no right and must not profit. Further, laws that belong to the ease of the determination of right; these must be simple.",[]],"refl:3355":["In the sale of a thing there are three pieces: 1. The seller, |\n2. the thing, 3. the buyer. In the contract no wrong occurs between them; the seller does not do the thing wrong. This has no obligation against which the former compelled it to pass over to another. Nor does he do the buyer wrong, and the latter *vice versa* not. This is an *actus originarius* and not *derivativus* (except when the buyer knows that the former is *non dominus*). To say: that the buyer must derive his right from the right of the seller makes the difficulty infinite, if one takes the right of the seller so that he does not act contrary to the obligation toward any other through the sale and injures no other thereby. But as regards the *actum translatorium*, he has the thing in hand, and no one may take it from him, except the owner. This one, however, is not in play. thus among these three the seller actually transfers his right, which is visible. But if the thing has its *existentz* only from the (g determinate) soil, from which it cannot be moved without perishing, then the buyer must first inquire after the right. For since the soil can never actually be possessed and thus has no real *dominum*, but can only be used, [has] it can belong only to him who through his possession (g and original *occupation*) can do no wrong, i.e. to the *societaet*, which has the power to administer right among itself.\nWith respect to things one is never in the wrong, let them belong to whom they will, but certainly against the owner. This one also has only just such a right to the thing, but the *prioritaet* which binds all others to do no injury to his right.\nWith respect to me even the thief has a right to the possession of the thing which he offers me for sale. I would do him wrong as soon as I take it from him by force. He gives up this right to my advantage, and I acquire. What wrong he may have done to another is not my affair but the other's. My *actus* is rightful. I have dealt with the human being and know of no other.",[]],"refl:3356":["/\nNatural right is that which concerns merely the rule of the community of human beings with one another, through which one grants the other a right; the artificial and transcendental is that which would derive all rights from the original origination. The latter contradicts itself. For then no one can communicate a secure right to another, because he can never prove originally how he himself came by it.",[]],"refl:3357":["The judge should 1. as inquirer proceed analytically, in order [first] to obtain from all *momentis in facto* an idea of the *Facto*. But since much in it can be indifferent with respect to the disputed right, at least juridically, he must compare all *varia Facti* with the *iure (cuiusvis partis) controverso*, in order to select the *momenta Facti* from them. [Then must] Thus he must nevertheless first weigh the *ius praetensum*, in order [to know] to determine *a priori* what is requisite for it. *e.g.* What belongs to a partnership contract and the continuation of it of the one of the partners. This must be settled beforehand universally with the judge, and the parties must either appeal about it to the legislative commission or now engage, according to all these *momentis in iure*, to bring forward the *momenta in facto*.\n2. as judge he must proceed synthetically: according to the *iustitia distributiva* [th] indicate those *praestanda* which each of the disputants [had] must perform according to the right of the other or for which he must render *satisfaction*.",[]],"refl:3358":["Rational science is either authentic or doctrinal. The |\nfirst is itself lawgiving [the] (legislatory), the other merely law-knowing (*legisperitia*).",[]],"refl:3359":["Whoever acts naturally acts according to rules, but of which he is not conscious.",[]],"refl:3360":["Natural or artificial.\nNatural or contrary to nature.",[]],"refl:3361":["/\nThe natural method must be opposed not to the artificial but to the contrived. For to this belongs the most art, to be quite adequate to nature.\nThe natural method is the popular one.",[]],"refl:3362":["All method is art and not nature.\nThe (g contrary to purpose) dispensable art is *affectation*.\nPopularity requires merely manner.",[]],"refl:3363":["School method.",[]],"refl:3364":["*Methodus scientifica vel popularis.*\nThe former removes everything heterogeneous, the latter takes it along.\nThe former begins from elements, the latter from the customary.\nThe former aims at thoroughness, the latter: entertainment.",[]],"refl:3365":["Art and science (being able and knowledge).\nArt and handicraft: as science and common cognition.",[]],"refl:3366":["/\nOne pursues an art either as free art or as trade (bread-art).",[]],"refl:3367":["*methodus vel scholastica (g scientifica) vel popularis;*\n*prior vel naturalis vel popularis.*",[]],"refl:3368":["The scientific way of teaching.\nOn L §. 428 no. 2 »methodus tabellaris«:\nDisposition.\nFurther L 117':\nDichotomy.",[]],"refl:3369":["/\n*Methodus erotematica.*",[]],"refl:3370":["[See image above.]\nClear\n[Distinct] Obscure Confused | distinct.\n│\n│\n│ Marks\n│\n│\n│\n│\nSufficient:\nclear\nInsufficient\nThe marks\nare clear, distinct\nobscure: indistinct\nMarks distinct: complete indistinct: incomplete\nPropositions.\ntheoretical\npractical\nindemonstrable: demonstrable indemonstrable: demonstrable through\nPrinciples through one inference: corollaries through several: theorems postulates several inferences: problems",[]],"refl:3371":["Mathematical method.",[]],"refl:3372":["*e.g.* If you come upon a statue that is very beautiful, do you not say that it was a good artist who made it?\nYou thus presuppose that an artist must have made it.\nAre statues not all the more perfect, the more they resemble human beings?\nare human beings breaks off.\nFurther L 118 from ψ−θ beside »einander folgen — veranlassen« (ll. 22f.):\nsystematic or aphoristic.",[]],"refl:3373":["dogmatic or critical (to that end skeptical).\nFurther L 119 from β1− ρ3 beside »einander — Gedanken« (ll. 25f.):\nLeibniz's Theodicy.",[]],"refl:3374":["One drafts rhapsodistically and orders methodically.",[]],"refl:3375":["/\nIn *theologia Naturalis* the concept of God.\nin Logic: of learned cognition that is not merely learned.",[]],"refl:3376":["The historical way of teaching (s of history) is pragmatic when it has yet another aim than the scholastic, is not merely for the school but also for the world or for morality.",[]],"refl:3377":["History of states.",[]],"refl:3378":["Just as in the way of life.\nThose who have accustomed themselves to the mathematical method.",[]],"refl:3379":["*A colloquy is\n1, catechetical, mechanical (s learning by heart) or judicious: teacher toward pupil. (g epistolary way of teaching.)\n2, dialogical, where one draws out the thoughts from the other and lets oneself be examined reciprocally.\n(s Systematic or aphoristic. erotematic.)\n(* s is either merely one-sided (g in teaching (otherwise it is examination, interrogation)) erotematic: [(doctrinal) or] catechetical, [or Exam] or reciprocal: dialogue. methodical dialogue.) )",[]],"refl:3380":["(g playful.) humorous. gallant. courtly. ponderous. well-read. notes. brain-racking etc etc.",[]],"refl:3381":["*Methodus acroamatica*: where [others only listen to the presentation] one\n(s /\\ / \\ / \\ Historical or rational; the latter: mathematical or philosophical; synthesis — analysis. Mixed must be separated. alone speaks; erotematic: where two converse. The latter either dialogical or catechetical. The dialogical with respect to the | )\npupil is Socratic; the dialogue is either mechanical or judicious; the former: *catechesis*.",[]],"refl:3382":["The dialogical (g and erotematic method), Socratic and catechetical.\nThe thoughts are not his own which the pupil recites catechetically; it is work of memory.\nSocratic is: where the pupil questions the teacher (who is nevertheless really the pupil); is to be applied only in cognition of reason from mere concepts, since we develop these.\nIf the teacher questions the pupil, then they must be matters of memory; for the pupil can after all disclose no insights to him. (g catechetical.)\nFinally dialogue is: when they converse and reason [like] (thus not in question and answer), as: Conversations in the Realm of the Dead. Fasmann, Littelton.",[]],"refl:3383":["The Socratic dialogue is no conversation, because one is always regarded as teacher. In conversation no one is teacher or pupil, but they are *in commercio* of thoughts.",[]],"refl:3384":["The erotematic method is either examinatory (g where one seeks not at the same time to instruct someone through questions, but questions out what was learned elsewhere) or catechetical (g where the teacher himself [through] in his questions wants to instruct the pupil) or dialogical.\n(s The catechetical holds only for empirical cognitions, the dialogical for rational ones, both only for philosophical ones.)",[]],"refl:3385":["Erotematically one can teach no otherwise than through the Socratic dialogue. Through the common *catechesis* one cannot teach, but only question out what one has taught acroamatically.\nThe Socratic dialogue teaches through questions, in that it teaches the pupil to know his own *principien* of reason and sharpens his attention to them.",[]],"refl:3386":["In dialogue (g through which I teach) I put questions to the understanding; hence the other must also be able to ask questions in return.\nIn *catechesis* I put them merely to memory, in order to hear again what |\nhas been taught. *Catechesis* is either mechanical or judicious.\nWhether in the *catechesis* of the doctrine of religion the historical [doctrines] or merely historically cognized propositions are to be presented as matters of faith or as mere history or tradition does not belong here.",[]],"refl:3387":["or critique.",[]],"refl:3388":["*Institution* is either doctrine (positive) or discipline (negative). The *organon* of the latter is critique.",[]],"refl:3389":["Insofar as it is regarded as an *object* of cognition. otherwise it is a skill to prove cognized truths.\nAll sciences have been doctrines. *e.g.* theology. Methodical minds came. Finally sciences.",[]],"refl:3390":["*e.g.* Baumelle.",[]],"refl:3391":["Whoever thinks methodically can present systematically or fragmentarily.\nThe outwardly fragmentary but in itself methodical presentation is aphoristic.",[]],"refl:3392":["False meditating. Borrowing postils.",[]],"refl:3393":["/\nSynthetic, analytic.",[]],"refl:3394":["Otherwise one thinks nothing.",[]],"refl:3395":["Thinking along with an author.* Thinking after. Thinking ahead.\nThinking along does not proceed well without a thinking over and thinking after, namely of the whole before the parts, because the parts in dogmatic cognitions are determined only in the whole and also\ncan be made distinct by themselves only from the whole. Explanations of sayings are therefore faulty when they are micrological.\n*(s Hence obscuring of the witty notion, so that we have something to unriddle. One has enjoyment in conversation only insofar as one gets occasion to speak oneself.)",[]],"refl:3396":["One must [tum] draft according to an idea, seek out the manifold tumultuarily and combine according to a method",[]],"refl:3397":["not rhapsodistically.\nTo think methodically *a priori*.",[]],"refl:3398":["On L 436:\n1. To understand oneself, what one wants.\n2. Not only to dissect one's thoughts, but to hold them together: system.",[]],"refl:3399":["/\nCorrectness of language is to be distinguished from fine speech or style.",[]],"refl:3400":[") η?? L 122.\nSpeaking and discoursing.",[]],"refl:3401":["[Presentation] Aggregate has manner and style. System: method.",[]],"refl:3402":["The speech which refers everything to logical perfection, or which refers everything to the entertaining play of sensibility.",[]],"refl:3403":["Wealth of ideas through the wealth of languages.\nFurther L 123' from ψ−θ:\nThe [learned] scholastic and popular presentation. The latter is the work of a genius.",[]],"refl:3404":["Solemn speeches have no technical terms.\nLearned language.\nGreek [from] technical terms.",[]],"refl:3405":["Hunter.\nFurther L 123 from ρ2−ψ (? ι2−λ?):\nproper and technical expressions befit a connoisseur: mining. Machines. Handicraft.",[]],"refl:3406":["Quick, hasty, nimble and fast.\nCourageous, vigorous, valiant.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\n*synonyma*: when many words one meaning,\n*homonymon*: when one word has many meanings. (s as *acies*.) (s poverty of language.)\nFurther from ω (φ??):\nAppropriate expression.\nEconomy of words in a language in general is: when one also expresses composite concepts [from] through words for the partial concepts; the false wealth of the Arabic language. — But economy of words in a speech makes partly obscure, partly emphatic.",[]],"refl:3407":["The three persons of the Godhead have one essence.",[]],"refl:3408":["Eye.",[]],"refl:3409":["The [familiar] customary use determines the meaning of words. One must introduce no peculiar meanings of old words, nor new |\nwords instead of old. *Verba valent sicut numi*; *a nomos*: law.",[]],"refl:3410":["One must introduce no new language, avail oneself of the expressions of the ancients.",[]],"refl:3411":["Not to fetter oneself to terminologies and to formulas.",[]],"refl:3412":["Crab.",[]],"refl:3413":["Most obedient servant.\nIt is worth being able to do.",[]],"refl:3414":["transs:\nL 124'. On L §. 449 no. 2:\nSubstance. Instinct.\nψ−θ. Opposite L §. 449 »druck — Be« (l. 33):\nEpicurus's Canonic.\nω? (φ?) ξ?? On L §. 449 »conceptus deceptor« (l. 19):\n*e.g.* the smallest time, greatest velocity. — Entelechy.\nβ1. On L §. 449 no. 3:\n*e.g.* that one calls the representations of animals thoughts.",[]],"refl:3415":["Nature. Force.\nFurther opposite »Ein — fa« (l. 22) from γ−φ:\nInstinct.",[]],"refl:3416":["Of blind chance.\nOn L §. 451 no. 1:\nThinking; then discoursing.",[]],"refl:3417":["The germ of non-ground.\nFurther from ω (? ξ−φ?):\nLogomachy, often pretended.\nβ1. On L §. 452 »Wortkrämerei«:\nAttractive force.\nFurther from ψ−θ:\nLogodaedaly.\nFurther beside L §. 452 sentence 3 from ρ−θ:\nThe aesthetic or logical word-mongering.\nFurther beside L §. 452 sentences 3 and 4 from ρ−θ:\nWord-picking in interpretation.\nWord-forging.\nwhittling.",[]],"refl:3418":["Heaviness. Weight.\nBeside L §. 453 »Bedeutung — dunkele« (l. 29.), probably on the beginning of sentence 3:\nThe word can be clear. *e.g.* conversion.\nOn L §. 453 sentence 4 first half:\n*e.g.* mystery.\nOn L §. 453 sentence 4 second half:\nMonads. Harmony.",[]],"refl:3419":["Nature of the human being. Color.",[]],"refl:3420":["Otherwise he puts his words on screws.",[]],"refl:3421":["Nature.",[]],"refl:3422":["Logic is a science of the rules of learned cognition and of learned presentation.",[]],"refl:3423":["tropic use of words.\nL 128'. β1. Opposite L §. 460 »von — könnte« (ll. 36f.):\nClarity of cognition. The light of cognition.\nOpposite L §. 460 »nen — Er« (l. 31):\nobscure cognition.",[]],"refl:3424":["It is up to me whether I use the word \"understanding\" as the entire power of cognition or merely as the faculty of cognizing distinctly.",[]],"refl:3425":["*iudicium assertorium*.",[]],"refl:3426":["It is not necessary that each concept have a particular expression. *e.g.* There is a God.\nOn the penultimate sentence of L §. 462:\nThe Germans have no wit.\nOn L §. 462 final sentence:\nOr understand the judgments designated by the expressions.",[]],"refl:3427":["*Syllogismus est ratiocinium formale.*",[]],"refl:3428":["The crab is an animal; now a certain disease is a crab (cancer) ....\nFurther L 129' from ξ−χ:\namphiboly\n( s Instead of *malum*: *pravum* The [good] evil — ill.\nAppearance — illusion. )",[]],"refl:3429":["Method and style.\n*stilus vel eruditus vel popularis.*",[]],"refl:3430":["What method is in thinking, style is in the designation of thinking for others. For myself I need no style.\n(s Whether purity and elegance in a dead language are so necessary in philosophical works? *purismus*.)",[]],"refl:3431":["*modus aestheticus in [communicandis perceptio] declaratione mentis suae.*\nThe way of making one's thoughts conform to the subjective conditions of the communication of them. — Style in paintings.\nStyle in building.\nTo stylize is to affect.",[]],"refl:3432":["Not affected.",[]],"refl:3433":["Learned distinctness: distinguished from the common.\nThe learned style must not be ornate (*pretieux*).",[]],"refl:3434":["Intervals and resting points.\nOn L §. 468 »punctum«:\n*Interpunctio*.",[]],"refl:3435":["In learning, purity and wealth not in synonymous but in proper words.",[]],"refl:3436":["Logikalisch. Physikalisch. Vernunftlehrerisch. (s *longimetrie*.)\nOn L §. 471 no. 2:\nIchheit.\nOn L §. 471 no. 4:\ncomplicirt. Fenster.\nOn L §. 471 no. 5:\nendelich, eilfertig.\nOn L §. 471 no. 7:\nDependentz. Substantz.",[]],"refl:3437":["Purists.",[]],"refl:3438":["God said: let there be light!\nWhere one thinks merely learnedly, one must not think periodically.",[]],"refl:3439":["Flowery words, unusual constructions.",[]],"refl:3440":["When we present something, we want to produce a learned cognition.\nHe who produces it is the teacher; he in whom it is produced: pupil.\nOral or written presentation. He who learns from an oral presentation: listener; he who from a written one: reader.",[]],"refl:3441":["On »von — Rede« (83821):\nTo discourse like a book or to write like chatter.",[]],"refl:3442":["The way of teaching is distinguished from presentation. The latter from style.\ndidactic, polemical way of teaching.",[]],"refl:3443":["The understanding (g of the pupil) must not be burdened by the presentation, but strengthened, thus be practiced in thinking for itself.",[]],"refl:3444":["We need words in order to become intelligible not only to others but to ourselves. This faculty of the use of words is language, and children learn to speak. By contrast, the skill [to] of speaking in interconnection about an object is the faculty of discoursing, which children do not attain at all and women scarcely.\nThis faculty is now either articulateness or fine speech or eloquence. The first is the wealth of discoursing (g without concerning oneself with interest), being able to discourse much and in manifold ways about a thing; loquacity is the inclination to speak much; whether about a single thing or about many things without interconnection is all one.\nArticulateness is the foundation; one must have a store in order [to discourse] to be able to discourse well. Fine speech is to discourse suitably both to the object and to taste. This is the condition under which alone articulateness takes place, and is style. It concerns more the quality [and is] than the prolixity. By contrast, eloquence, *eloqventz*, is an art of talking over and dialectical, hence harmful. Also rhetoric both in the state and in sciences [to] has always brought ruin upon religion, philosophy: Demosthenes, Carneades, Cicero. By contrast, the decline of fine speech (barbarous style) also either indicates that of the sciences as effect or produces it as cause; *e.g.* Scholastici, later Romans.",[]],"refl:3445":["Prolixity of matters or of words.",[]],"refl:3446":["*Laus Asini. De sandaliis Hebraeorum.*\nTo hold no great *Panegyricos* for any school monarch.",[]],"refl:3447":["One must not exceed the truth in a funeral oration.",[]],"refl:3448":["He is understood when one combines with his speech just the same series of thoughts that he has combined with it. He must either explain which concepts he combines with the words, or use the common expressions.",[]],"refl:3449":["The confused Not as to him who has seen and sensed it.",[]],"refl:3450":["He appeals to authority.\nOn L §. 488 no. 3:\nThrough prejudices; *e.g.* papists.",[]],"refl:3451":["The utility of each thing.",[]],"refl:3452":["Joking. *Pathema*.",[]],"refl:3453":["With different listeners, in edifying (g practical) speeches condescension is needed, in [unt] learned ones coascension.",["condescension, coascension: condescendentz, coascendentz.","learned ones: Gelehrten, either the adjective (with Reden) or the noun 'scholars'."]],"refl:3454":["Refutation. Before it, resolution of the illusion. Cause of the dispute is removed.",[]],"refl:3455":["First it must be shown how far the true still goes in the [Ged] assertions of the opponent; second: wherein the illusion consists that misleads him into error, and [woh] whence it could have arisen for him or even for everyone else.\nOne refutes the man or a proposition, not because the former presents it, but because it contains illusion.\nThe refutation [ist] should be a step toward unification, and indeed by means of the correction of the cognitions on both sides.",[]],"refl:3456":["The presentation is dogmatic or polemical. The means is critical, where one anticipates the opponent.",["means: Mittel, which can also be 'the mean' (the middle).","where: da, which can also be read causally, 'since'."]],"refl:3457":["Opposite L 138 line 1:\nThat the earth moves. Joshua.",[]],"refl:3458":["*discussio quaestionis dubiae*. [Unt] Dispute, in order to come to agreement.",[]],"refl:3459":["This world is the best. 2 meanings.\nFurther L 138 from ρ−θ:\nThere are none that last long.",[]],"refl:3460":["The author gives a lection and contralection.",["lection, contralection: Kant's lection (Lektion: 'lesson' or 'reading') and its counter-formation."]],"refl:3461":["Leibniz *in Mysterio SS. Trinitatis*.",["*in Mysterio SS. Trinitatis*: 'in the mystery of the Most Holy Trinity'."]],"refl:3462":["Is not always permitted.",[]],"refl:3463":["Let one assume it and show its absurd consequences.\nOpposite L §. 504 »wir — Feh« (84930-85016):\nDoubter. — — —\nHe who denies the principle of sufficient reason.",["it ... its: ihn ... seine, which could also be 'him ... his'.","consequences: Folgen; reason: Grund (not Vernunft)."]],"refl:3464":["*argumentatio ex concessis*.\nTo L §. 504 sentence 2, specifically to »*argumentatio ad hominem*«:\nκαθ ανθροπον.\nκαθ' αλητειαν\nFurther from ψ−θ:\n*ad crumenam*, wager.\n*ad verecundiam*, appeal to great men.",["*argumentatio ex concessis*: 'argumentation from what is conceded'; *ad crumenam*: 'to the purse'; *ad verecundiam*: 'to modesty'.","καθ ανθροπον, καθ' αλητειαν: so spelled, for κατ' ἄνθρωπον, 'according to the man', and κατ' ἀλήθειαν, 'according to the truth'."]],"refl:3465":["*fallacia ignorationis elenchi*.\nTo L §. 505 end:\n*methodus Socratica*.",["*fallacia ignorationis elenchi*: 'fallacy of ignorance of the refutation'."]],"refl:3466":["or not so unconditionally: restriction.",["restriction: Kant's Latinate restriction, not Einschränkung."]],"refl:3467":["God chooses the best that is possible.\n[Das Beste] All possible best is: therein no evil.\nTherefore there is in that which God chooses no evil.\nIn whichever world the most perfections are to be encountered, that one is the best. Now in this one the most are to be encountered.\n}Restriction: and the greatest.\n} other restriction: absolutely no\n}imperfections.",["that is possible: das moglich, with no verb.","restriction: Einschränkung."]],"refl:3468":["If I assert: Everything composite consists of monads, then the other says breaks off.\nI assert: Some good does not move the will. The other objects: What we represent to ourselves as good, that we desire; then I answer with the distinction that the living and dead representation must be distinguished.",["breaks off: bricht ab, apparently the editor's remark that the sentence breaks off.","living and dead representation: singular Vorstellung with plural verb müßen."]],"refl:3469":["If one says that erudition improves the will: then the instance of evil scholars is given against it.\nAnswer of Villiers to a priest.\nOpposite L §. 508 sentence 2:\nIf one says that religion promotes the welfare of the republic.",["instance: instantz (Latin instantia).","erudition: Gelehrsamkeit; scholars: Gelehrten."]],"refl:3470":["/\nIn the last sentence of L §. 508 Kant has added a 1 before »wenn« (line 33), a 2 before »dass« in line 34, a 3 before the second »oder« in line 34, a 4 before »oder« in line 35. To this sentence refers the following remark on L 140':\n1. If someone against my proposition, that all finite spirits have bodies, brings forward the instance that therefore the souls of the deceased too would have to have bodies, then I accept it.\n2. If someone were to say that therefore God too has a body.\n3. If I had said: all finite spirits have bodies, and that one objected that therefore [wen] God could not have created a spirit-world entirely alone, then I say: all finite spirits in this world.\n4. One does not, for the sake of brevity, enter into the instance.",["against: wieder (i.e. wider); instance: instantz (instantia).","entirely alone: gantz allein may go with spirit-world or with God."]],"refl:3471":["It is the right of retaliation.\ne.g. Those who attack the system of predetermined harmony because it overturns freedom.",["predetermined harmony: vorbestimmten Harmonie, not the usual vorherbestimmte ('pre-established').","overturns: umstoße, subjunctive (reported reason)."]],"refl:3472":["he brands them with the most odious names.\nHe reproaches them with the desire for innovation.\nFurther from ξ−φ:\n*argumentum ab adio*.\nFurther from ρ−θ:\n*argumentum a tuto*.",["*ab adio*: so written, presumably for *ab odio*, 'from hatred'; *a tuto*: 'from what is safe'."]],"refl:3473":["controverting for the sake of truth; disputing for the sake of being in the right, i.e. of the upper hand in dispute.\n(s eristic. Quarreling.)",["disputing: disputiren (Latinate); dispute: streit.","being in the right: Rechthaberey."]],"refl:3474":["/\nadvocate's proof: that which draws advantage from the bad defense of the other and from that which he precipitately concedes (pompadour).",[]],"refl:3475":["Hennigs.\nNext to L §. 518 »dogmatica — systematische« (lines 19-22):\ndoctrinal (s historical or dogmatic)\npolemical\nCritical (s Bayle)\nskeptical",[]],"refl:3476":["*Mega biblion: mega kakon*.\nGreat charnel house: library.\nTo interpret the author to his advantage, because he himself cannot speak.\nThe end is reason and its growth.",["*Mega biblion: mega kakon*: Greek, 'big book: big evil'."]],"refl:3477":["Regard to the content.\nRegard to the presentation (method).\nRegard to the clothing (style.).\nScholastic or popular, urbanity.\nnot to speak like a book; not to write as one chatters.\n1. System or rhapsody.\n2. Elegance of the system.\nLiterary (g Learned) [scholastisch] or pragmatic.",["Regard to: Absicht auf (the idiom 'with regard to'); Absicht is otherwise 'aim', 'intention'.","presentation: Vortrag; clothing: Einkleidung."]],"refl:3478":["/\nEvery book [ist] should be: 1. Thorough; 2. Exhaustive; 3. Enticing through novelty, importance, agreeableness. 4. Eloquent. 5. contain something lasting.\nIn ω (? φ?) Wichtigkeit, 4., 5 — enthalten were struck through and the following remarks added to the left of the Refl.:\n1. Scholastic perfection. [2. Interessant] 2. aesthetically interesting. 3. practically interesting.\nFrom ω (φ?) also comes the following sentence, standing under line 4:\nProhibition of books and eradication means to eradicate the soul of human beings from the earth.",["Thorough: Gründlich; Exhaustive: Ausführlich."]],"refl:3479":["Popularity does not consist in this, that the scholastic perfection be set aside, but only that the clothing not let it be seen as the scaffolding (just as one draws lines in pencil, on which one writes, and afterwards wipes them away). Everything scientific must be in conformity with the school; but the technical in it must not be visible, but rather condescension to the power of grasping and the accustomed expressions.",["in conformity with the school: schulgerecht.","condescension: condescendenz; power of grasping: Fassungskraft."]],"refl:3480":["/\nOpposite the heading of the 4th section, above No. 3477, probably \nThe skepticism of (g ancient learned) history (g or humanistics) according to Hardouin's opinion.",["probably: wohl; the editorial line breaks off after it."]],"refl:3481":["Instrument of prudence.",[]],"refl:3482":["Rhapsody without method.",[]],"refl:3483":["*Literatus* is after all at least refined. not *eruditus*.\nCulture civilizes.\nHume's judgment that the true scholar is at least an honest man.",["civilizes: civilisirt, which could also be the participle 'civilized'."]],"refl:3484":["With erudition not to forget healthy understanding and with the school not the good manners of the world.\n*doctor umbraticus* (s *Graeculus*).",["not to forget ... not: nicht stands twice, zu vergessen only once, at the end.","*doctor umbraticus*: 'teacher in the shade'; *Graeculus*: 'little Greek'."]],"refl:3485":["One must show more *bon sens* than erudition.",[]],"refl:3486":["Naturalist* (s in fencing) in comparison with the school-scholar.\n*(s is: one who has not gone through the school.)\nNaturalists in metaphysics and morality are chatterers.\n(s The school consists in discipline, to grind off the crudeness, and doctrine, to equip with cognition.)",["doctrine: Doctrin (Latinate), not Lehre.","gone through the school: die Schule gemacht, literally 'made the school'."]],"refl:3487":["and of popular writings.\nOne must read many kinds of things and choose few things for oneself as a model. (s Mere reading is enjoying without digesting; it is gorging. Hence *Helluo librorum*.)",["and of popular writings: the passage begins mid-sentence; populairer Schriften is genitive.","*Helluo librorum*: 'glutton of books'."]],"refl:3488":["One must read some books of genius often.",[]]};
